window.PROSE=window.PROSE||{};Object.assign(window.PROSE,{"janet-jackson-rhythm-nation-1814":["A concept album in two halves. The first is industrial dance music about the state of the world: Rhythm Nation, State of the World and The Knowledge are clanging, metallic tracks about poverty, drugs, illiteracy and racism, stitched together by short interludes. The second half turns to love and pleasure with Miss You Much, Escapade and the slow Come Back to Me. The sound throughout is dense, percussive and militarily precise.","Her label wanted a sequel to Control; Jackson and Jam and Lewis, working again in Minneapolis, made a record about social problems they were seeing on the news. It came with a long black-and-white film whose drilled, uniformed choreography became as famous as the music. The album produced seven top-five American singles across 1989, 1990 and 1991, a record at the time.","The title track is built partly on a sample from Sly and the Family Stone's Thank You (Falettinme Be Mice Elf Agin), tying its call for unity to a 1969 funk anthem of the same spirit. The number 1814 is the year the words of the American national anthem were written. Black Cat, a hard-rock track in the middle of the album, is one Jackson wrote alone.","Some reviewers found the social message vague next to its sheer commercial power; others were surprised a pop star of her rank would try it at all. Its reputation has grown steadily. It is now heard as one of the most ambitious big-budget pop albums of its decade, and its sound and staging run through the work of Beyoncé and many who followed.","Start with the title track and pay attention to the clanks, sirens and sampled noises in the mix; this is dance music built from scrap metal. Then watch the film, because the choreography is part of the work. Compare with Marvin Gaye's What's Going On for an earlier, gentler soul record about the same state of the nation."],"janet-jackson-the-velvet-rope":["A long, dark, intimate album about depression, self-worth, sexuality and loss, laid out like a night inside a private club. Short interludes connect songs that range from the trip-hop groove of Got 'til It's Gone to the house music of Together Again and the slow, whispered title track. Jackson's voice is often recorded close and soft, and the production by Jam, Lewis and René Elizondo is thick with atmosphere.","Jackson had just signed one of the largest recording contracts in history with Virgin, and was, by her own later account, in the grip of a serious depression. The album addresses it openly, alongside domestic abuse, homophobia and AIDS. In the late 1990s few mainstream pop stars wrote so directly about mental illness or about gay lives.","Got 'til It's Gone is built on a sample of Joni Mitchell's Big Yellow Taxi, with Mitchell's own voice repeating a fragment through the track, and a guest verse from Q-Tip of A Tribe Called Quest. Together Again, which sounds like the happiest record on the album, is in fact an elegy for friends lost to AIDS; the joy is a memorial.","Reviews were largely strong, but it sold less than its predecessors and some listeners were put off by its frankness. Its standing has climbed since, and it is now often called her best album and a forerunner of the confessional, genre-crossing R&B of the 2010s. Its treatment of sexuality has made it a touchstone for queer listeners.","Play it straight through, in the dark, as a single sequence; the interludes matter. Listen to Got 'til It's Gone next to Joni Mitchell's original to hear what a sample can do to a song's meaning. Then set Together Again beside it and notice how the tempo hides the grief."],"tlc-crazysexycool":["The trio's second album moves from the bright, cartoonish hip-hop pop of their debut to something slower, smokier and more grown-up. T-Boz's low, husky voice leads Creep and Red Light Special; Chilli's lighter tone takes other songs; Left Eye's rapid rap verses cut across them. Waterfalls, with its live horns and warning stories, is the centre. Interludes, including guest spots from Atlanta rappers, give it the feel of a mixtape.","Made in Atlanta for LaFace, the label run by L.A. Reid and Babyface, with producers including Dallas Austin, Jermaine Dupri, Sean Combs and the local team Organized Noize. The group were in turmoil: Lisa Lopes was facing criminal charges, and within a year of its enormous success the three filed for bankruptcy, having earned remarkably little from their contracts. It was the moment Atlanta became a centre of American R&B.","The title divides the group into three parts, T-Boz the cool, Chilli the sexy and Left Eye the crazy, and the album is built around those roles. Listen to Creep for the muted trumpet sample that loops through the whole song; it gives a song about infidelity a jazz-club melancholy.","It sold over ten million copies in America and won the Grammy for best R&B album. It has since been credited with defining the sound of 1990s female R&B groups and, through Organized Noize, with bringing the Atlanta sound of OutKast into the mainstream. Its story of commercial triumph and financial ruin is now a standard case in debates about artists' contracts.","Begin with Creep, then Waterfalls, then Red Light Special, to hear how differently three producers used the same three voices. Pay special attention to T-Boz: her low alto was unusual at the top of the charts. Follow it with Destiny's Child's The Writing's on the Wall to hear who learned from it."],"tlc-waterfalls":["A mid-tempo song with a warm, live-sounding groove, wah-wah guitar and a brass section, carrying two grim little stories. In the first a young man is drawn into selling drugs and is killed; in the second a man with HIV passes it on. Between them the chorus advises staying within your limits, and Left Eye's rap verse turns the message toward hope and self-belief.","Produced by Organized Noize, the Atlanta team behind OutKast, it was released in 1995 as the third single from CrazySexyCool and spent seven weeks at number one in America. Its video, directed by F. Gary Gray, used then-expensive digital effects, with the singers standing on water, and won the MTV award for video of the year. AIDS was still, in 1995, a leading cause of death for young Americans.","The sound is closer to 1970s Southern soul than to 1990s drum machines: Organized Noize built it around live instruments, including a horn line that could sit on an old Stax record. That warmth is what lets such a bleak song become a singalong. Listen also to how the two story verses are sung in the third person, keeping the singers as witnesses rather than preachers.","It was greeted as a rare hit that carried a public-health message without sounding like one, and it is still one of the best-known songs of the decade. Some critics have found the moral simplistic. It is routinely cited as proof that mainstream R&B in the 1990s could engage directly with the crises of its audience.","Listen once for the horns and the bass and once for the stories. Then play Curtis Mayfield's Pusherman from Super Fly, another soul record about the drug trade, and notice how both wrap hard content in an irresistible groove. Watch the video once; it is part of how the song was received."],"tlc-no-scrubs":["A crisp, mid-tempo dismissal of men who have nothing to offer but want everything. Chilli sings the lead over a clipped, stuttering beat and a looping minor-key guitar figure, and the chorus lands like a flat refusal. Left Eye adds a rap verse in some versions. It is short, precise and relentlessly catchy.","The lead single from FanMail, the group's first album after their bankruptcy and a long break. It was written by Kandi Burruss and Tameka Cottle of the Atlanta group Xscape with the producer Kevin 'She'kspere' Briggs, who were then shaping late-1990s R&B with a run of songs about money and self-respect. It spent four weeks at number one in America and won two Grammys.","Listen to the gaps in the drum pattern: Briggs leaves beats out and pushes others early, a stop-start style that Timbaland and Darkchild were also developing and that would define R&B around 2000. The same writing team wrote Destiny's Child's Bills, Bills, Bills the same year, and the two songs are close cousins in both sound and attitude.","It provoked answer records from male rappers, and debate about whether it was empowering or simply snobbish about money. It has become one of the defining songs of late-1990s R&B, much covered and still a staple of any party that plays music from the period.","Play it next to Destiny's Child's Say My Name and notice the shared jittery rhythm. Then listen only to the guitar loop: it is the song's melancholy undertow, and it keeps the track from sounding merely smug."],"destinys-child-the-writings-on-the-wall":["The Houston group's second album, full of tight four-part harmony and quick, clipped vocal lines that fall across jittery programmed beats. Bills, Bills, Bills, Bug a Boo, Jumpin', Jumpin' and Say My Name are the hits; slower songs let the group's gospel-trained harmonies spread out. Beyoncé Knowles leads most tracks, with Kelly Rowland, LaTavia Roberson and LeToya Luckett close behind.","Managed by Knowles's father, Mathew, the group had been rehearsing since childhood. The album was made with a roster of the most sought-after R&B producers of the moment, including Kevin 'She'kspere' Briggs, Rodney Jerkins and Missy Elliott. Before it had finished its run, two of the four members had left in a bitter dispute and been replaced, one of the most public line-up changes in pop.","The album is framed as a book of rules for relationships: a spoken opening sets it up, and many track titles carry a mock-scriptural commandment in brackets. Listen to how the vocal lines are written almost as percussion, with syllables packed tightly and cut short; this rapid-fire singing became a signature of Beyoncé's later style.","It sold many millions and made the group the leading R&B act of the turn of the century. Critics at the time were friendly but not reverent. The album is now heard as the foundation of Beyoncé's solo career and as a summary of late-1990s R&B production at its sharpest.","Start with Bills, Bills, Bills and Say My Name, then go back and listen only to the harmonies. Compare with TLC's CrazySexyCool: similar Southern roots, but here the singing is the rhythm section. Then jump to Solange's A Seat at the Table to hear where the family took it."],"destinys-child-say-my-name":["A suspicious lover interrogates a partner on the phone, over one of the most intricate beats of its time. Rodney Jerkins' production keeps shifting under the singers: the drums skip and stumble, the chords change colour, and the song's sections feel as if they have been cut and re-spliced. The harmonised chorus is a simple demand, repeated until it sounds like an accusation.","The fourth single from The Writing's on the Wall, released early in 2000 in the middle of the group's line-up split. The video introduced Michelle Williams and Farrah Franklin as new members, and the departing LaTavia Roberson and LeToya Luckett reportedly learned of their replacement from it. The song reached number one in America and won two Grammys.","Listen to the harmony under the verses: Jerkins moves the chords in unexpected directions, which is why the song feels unsettled even when the beat is steady. The track's most distinctive sound, a sharp clicking percussion, sits almost where a hi-hat would be but never quite behaves like one.","It was praised immediately as one of the most inventive R&B productions of the era, and it has stayed on critics' lists of the best songs of the 2000s. The drama around its video has become part of its story, and part of the myth of Beyoncé's rise.","Listen with headphones to the drum programming alone; it is doing something new every few bars. Then compare with TLC's No Scrubs from a year earlier, and with the slower, broken-down beats of Frank Ocean or SZA a decade later."],"dangelo-brown-sugar":["Ten warm, slow-burning songs played mostly by D'Angelo himself on Fender Rhodes electric piano, organ and bass, over drums that nod to hip-hop without its hardness. His voice layers on itself in close harmony, murmuring and falsetto by turns. The title track opens the album with a lazy, rolling groove; Lady, Me and Those Dreamin' Eyes of Mine and a cover of Smokey Robinson's Cruisin' follow.","D'Angelo, from Richmond, Virginia, was twenty-one, the son of a Pentecostal minister, with a gospel-trained ear and a hip-hop generation's taste in drums. His manager and label head Kedar Massenburg used the phrase 'neo-soul' to sell him and, soon, Erykah Badu. The term stuck to a whole movement. Contemporary R&B was then dominated by slick, heavily produced ballads; this sounded handmade.","The title track was co-produced with Ali Shaheed Muhammad of A Tribe Called Quest, and the rhythm feel of that group's records runs underneath the album. Listen to the background vocals: D'Angelo stacks his own voice in the manner of Marvin Gaye's 1970s records, so that a choir of one surrounds the lead.","It sold well, earned Grammy nominations and was received as a return to the virtues of 1970s soul. Critics now tend to hear it as a starting pistol: without it the neo-soul of Badu, Maxwell, Lauryn Hill and Jill Scott would sound quite different. Some argue that the neo-soul label always undersold how new it was.","Play the title track and follow the Rhodes piano, which holds the whole song together. Then put on Marvin Gaye's Let's Get It On and hear the family resemblance in the layered voices. Move on to Voodoo, five years later, to hear the same man take everything further."],"dangelo-voodoo":["Thirteen long, loose, deeply funky tracks recorded live by a small band. The drums lag behind the beat, the bass pushes against them, and D'Angelo's voice is often buried in the mix like another instrument. Playa Playa opens with a slow, swampy groove; Devil's Pie, Left & Right, The Line, Chicken Grease and Untitled (How Does It Feel) follow, alongside a cover of Roberta Flack's Feel Like Makin' Love. It is hazy, sensual and hard to hurry.","Made over about four years at Electric Lady Studios in New York, the studio Jimi Hendrix built, with a loose collective later called the Soulquarians: Questlove on drums, James Poyser on keys, Pino Palladino on bass, J Dilla's ideas about timing, and guests including Roy Hargrove. The sessions involved endless jamming on old Prince and Parliament records. The album went to number one in America and won a Grammy.","The rhythmic trick is everywhere and easy to miss: the drums, bass and voice each sit at slightly different points around the beat, a deliberately 'drunk' feel borrowed from J Dilla's programmed beats and played by humans. Listen to the snare on Left & Right, which arrives late every time. Devil's Pie was produced by DJ Premier, the one track built on a sample in the old hip-hop manner.","Early reviews were mixed to strong, with some listeners finding it too murky and slow. Its standing has risen every year since, and it is now widely named among the best albums of its decade. Musicians study its rhythmic feel the way jazz players study Kind of Blue. Its long shadow was lengthened by D'Angelo's fourteen-year silence afterwards.","Put it on loud, not quiet, and listen to the drums and bass on Playa Playa rather than the singing. Once the lag in the rhythm clicks, the whole record opens. Then play Prince's Sign o' the Times era and Sly Stone's There's a Riot Goin' On if you know them, and Robert Glasper's Black Radio after."],"dangelo-untitled-how-does-it-feel":["A slow, simmering ballad that builds for seven minutes from a soft falsetto plea to a raw, full-throated climax. The guitars and keyboards churn gently, the drums stay steady, and D'Angelo's layered voices rise until the last minutes, where the song breaks into something close to a scream. It is an unashamed homage to Prince's slow jams.","Written with Raphael Saadiq and released from Voodoo in 2000. Its video, directed by Paul Hunter, is a single continuous shot of D'Angelo, apparently naked, singing to the camera. It made him a sex symbol overnight. He later said that the attention and the expectation it created made performing unbearable, and it is widely seen as one reason he withdrew from public life for over a decade.","The song is built to sound like Prince at every level: the falsetto, the guitar tone and the arc from whisper to shriek. D'Angelo has described it as a tribute. Listen near the end for the moment the band drops almost out and the voice carries the song alone before the last surge.","It won the Grammy for best male R&B vocal performance and remains his best-known song. Its video is still cited in discussions of how the music industry treats Black male sexuality, and of how an image can overwhelm an artist. Musically it is admired as a masterclass in slow build.","Listen to it all the way through without skipping; the last ninety seconds are the point. Then find a live version from the 2000 tour to hear how the band stretched it. Compare with Al Green's Call Me for another soul singer who could make restraint burn."],"dangelo-black-messiah":["A thick, murky, guitar-heavy funk album that sounds as if it were recorded inside a haze. D'Angelo's voice is again half-buried, the guitars grind and chime, and the rhythms lurch. 1000 Deaths opens with sampled speech and a heavy, distorted groove; Really Love turns to a soft acoustic ballad with flamenco-tinged guitar; The Charade and Another Life take the soul tradition back to its gospel and protest roots.","After fourteen years of silence, the album was released with almost no warning in December 2014, brought forward in response to the protests in Ferguson and after the death of Eric Garner. It is credited to D'Angelo and the Vanguard, his touring band, with Questlove, Pino Palladino and co-writers including Q-Tip and Kendra Foster. It won the Grammy for best R&B album.","D'Angelo wrote a note explaining that the title does not refer to him but to people, to a collective of those rising up. The Charade's references to being denied breath were widely heard as pointing at Eric Garner's final words, though the song was written before his death. Listen for the vintage recording gear: much of it was tracked on analogue tape.","Reviews were rapturous and it appeared on almost every critics' list of the year. Some listeners found it too murky after the clarity of Voodoo. It is now heard alongside Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly and Solange's A Seat at the Table as part of a wave of Black American protest albums in the mid-2010s.","Start with Really Love for its warmth, then go back to 1000 Deaths for its fury. Listen to it next to Marvin Gaye's What's Going On and Curtis Mayfield's Curtis, whose tradition of soul as social commentary it knowingly continues."],"erykah-badu-baduizm":["A slow, jazzy, deliberately unhurried debut. Badu's voice, thin, grainy and oddly phrased, floats over upright-sounding bass lines, dry drums and soft keyboards. On & On opens with a spare, looping groove and a lyric full of mystical numerology; Next Lifetime, Appletree and Otherside of the Game follow. The record sounds closer to a small jazz club than to 1990s R&B radio.","Badu, born Erica Wright in Dallas, had worked as a teacher and performed in Dallas clubs before Kedar Massenburg, D'Angelo's manager, signed her. Several tracks were recorded in Philadelphia with members of the Roots. The album entered the American chart near the top and won the Grammy for best R&B album, confirming neo-soul as a movement rather than a single artist.","On & On draws on the teachings of the Five-Percent Nation, a Black nationalist offshoot of the Nation of Islam whose vocabulary of numbers and 'knowledge of self' runs through 1990s hip-hop; the song can seem abstract until you know that. Listen also for the flute and the way her phrasing lags and bends the beat, which drew instant comparisons to Billie Holiday.","Critics were taken with her at once and the Billie Holiday comparison followed her everywhere, sometimes to her annoyance. The album's reputation is secure; debate is mostly about whether her later, stranger records surpass it. Many consider it the defining neo-soul debut.","Start with On & On, then Otherside of the Game, and listen to how much space the band leaves. Then play Billie Holiday's God Bless the Child and hear where the comparison came from, and where it stops."],"erykah-badu-mamas-gun":["Badu's second studio album is warmer, looser and more varied than her first. Penitentiary Philosophy opens with rock-tinged funk; Didn't Cha Know rides a woozy, hazy loop; Bag Lady advises letting go of emotional baggage; Cleva celebrates plainness. It ends with Green Eyes, a ten-minute suite in three parts that moves from sparse jazz balladry to full band as it traces the stages of heartbreak.","Made at Electric Lady Studios in New York with the same Soulquarians collective who were finishing D'Angelo's Voodoo: Questlove, James Poyser, J Dilla and others. The overlap in players, rooms and months was deliberate; the collective traded ideas across albums. It was released on Motown in late 2000, and Bag Lady became a major hit.","Didn't Cha Know was produced with J Dilla and built on a sample from the jazz-fusion group Tarika Blue; its stumbling drum feel is his signature. A.D. 2000 is a quiet elegy for Amadou Diallo, the unarmed man shot by New York police in 1999, though the song never states the facts outright.","It was well received and is now often ranked above her debut as her finest album. Green Eyes is admired as one of the most ambitious pieces of songwriting in the neo-soul movement. It is regularly paired with Voodoo as the twin peak of the Electric Lady sessions.","Play Green Eyes last and all the way through; its three sections are best heard as one act. Then play Didn't Cha Know alongside D'Angelo's Left & Right to hear the shared rhythmic feel of the collective."],"erykah-badu-new-amerykah-part-one":["A dense, psychedelic, politically charged album built largely from samples and beats by hip-hop producers. Amerykahn Promise opens with a brassy 1970s funk cover; The Healer drifts over a slow Madlib beat; Master Teacher and Soldier look at Black American life, faith and survival; Honey closes in a lighter mood. It sounds like a radio dial turning through a troubled country.","Made in the last year of the George W. Bush presidency, with producers including Madlib, 9th Wonder and the Los Angeles team Sa-Ra Creative Partners. Badu recorded much of it on a laptop and had embraced the beat-driven, sample-based underground of the 2000s. It is dedicated in part to J Dilla, who had died in 2006; Telephone addresses him.","Amerykahn Promise is a cover of The American Promise by the group RAMP, a 1970s band linked to Roy Ayers, so the album opens with a borrowed voice from Black radicalism of an earlier decade. The Healer names hip-hop as bigger than religion or government; listen for Madlib's hazy, unquantised drums, a direct inheritance from Dilla.","It was widely praised as her boldest album and one of the most adventurous major-label records of its year. Some fans missed the song-shaped warmth of her earlier work. It now reads as a forerunner of the sample-rich, socially minded Black music of the 2010s, from Kendrick Lamar to Solange.","Treat it as a mixtape and listen straight through. Start again at The Healer and notice how little happens, and how much that little does. Then go to De La Soul's 3 Feet High and Rising for an earlier record built on the same collage principle."],"maxwell-urban-hang-suite":["A concept album that follows a single love affair from first meeting to a proposal of marriage. Maxwell sings mostly in a soft, airy falsetto over live, gently swinging grooves: warm bass, muted guitar, Fender Rhodes and discreet horns. Sumthin' Sumthin', Ascension (Don't Ever Wonder) and Whenever Wherever Whatever are the best known. The whole is continuous, adult and deliberately unhurried.","Maxwell, from Brooklyn, of Puerto Rican and Haitian descent, was in his early twenties. The album was finished in 1994 but shelved for over a year by Columbia, who were unsure how to sell it. Among its collaborators was Stuart Matthewman of Sade, and Leon Ware, who had written and produced Marvin Gaye's I Want You. When finally released it went platinum.","Leon Ware's presence is the key: Sumthin' Sumthin' carries the lush, sensual texture of the Gaye album he made in 1976, and the whole record is in a sense a sequel to it. Matthewman's involvement links it directly to Sade. Listen to the transitions between songs: the story is told as much by moods as by words.","Critics welcomed it as a romantic, musicianly alternative to the hard R&B of the mid-1990s, and it was nominated for a Grammy. It is now counted, with Brown Sugar and Baduizm, as a founding neo-soul album. Maxwell's long gaps between records have kept its reputation high.","Listen straight through, in order, as a story. Then play Sade's Love Deluxe and Marvin Gaye's Let's Get It On to hear the two parents of this sound. A good system helps; the bass and the Rhodes carry the warmth."],"lauryn-hill-the-miseducation-of-lauryn-hill":["An album that moves freely between singing and rapping, hip-hop beats and live soul, reggae and gospel. Lost Ones is a hard, rapped opening; Ex-Factor a slow ballad of a relationship coming apart; To Zion a song to her newborn son with Carlos Santana on guitar; Doo Wop (That Thing) a street-corner warning set to horns and harmonies. Between songs, a teacher talks with a classroom of teenagers about love.","Hill, from New Jersey, had been the singer and one of the rappers of the Fugees, whose album The Score had been one of the biggest records of 1996. She made this, her only solo studio album, partly at Bob Marley's Tuff Gong studio in Kingston, Jamaica, while pregnant and after the group had begun to fall apart. It won five Grammys, including album of the year, the first for a hip-hop record.","The title alludes to Carter G. Woodson's 1933 book The Mis-Education of the Negro, about how schooling shaped Black Americans' sense of themselves. The classroom interludes were recorded with real students. The musicians who worked on it, credited as New Ark, later sued for credit as co-writers; the case settled. D'Angelo sings with her on Nothing Even Matters.","It was acclaimed at once and has never really been doubted. Its standing grew further because Hill never made a follow-up studio album, and it is now a fixture on all-time lists. The credit dispute remains a live question in how its authorship is described.","Play Ex-Factor for the singing and Lost Ones for the rapping, then To Zion for everything at once. Listen through the classroom interludes, which frame the album as lessons. Afterwards play Aretha Franklin's Amazing Grace to hear where the gospel in her voice comes from."],"lauryn-hill-doo-wop-that-thing":["A brisk, bouncy warning in two halves: the first verse cautions young women against men who want only one thing, the second turns on young men who care more about looks and money than character. Hill raps the verses in a quick, conversational flow and sings the chorus over a rolling piano, punchy horns and 1950s-style doo-wop backing harmonies. It sounds joyful and old-fashioned at once, and that sweetness is what lets the lecture land.","The first single from The Miseducation of Lauryn Hill. It entered the American chart at number one, a rare feat at the time, and won Grammys for best R&B song and best female R&B vocal performance. Its video shows two block parties side by side in split screen, one in 1967 and one in 1998, with Hill performing in both.","The title is an ironic nod to doo-wop, the street-corner harmony style of the 1950s, and the backing singers use its vocabulary while the verses speak with 1990s directness. The split-screen video makes the point explicit: the same problems, thirty years apart. Listen for the moment the two halves of the arrangement, old and new, lock together.","Critics liked it immediately, and its debut at the top of the chart proved that a song with an explicit moral could also be a commercial hit. It is now regularly named among the best songs of the 1990s. Some listeners find its moralising stern, and its even-handedness, scolding both sexes in turn, has been read both as fairness and as conservatism. Its musical wit, fusing a 1950s harmony group with a 1990s rap verse, is rarely questioned.","Watch the video once, then listen with eyes closed and hear the two eras in the music. Then go back to The Supremes' Where Did Our Love Go to hear the tradition she was pointing at."],"alicia-keys-songs-in-a-minor":["An R&B debut built around the piano. Keys plays on almost every track, from the classical-sounding introduction to the gospel chords of Fallin' and the soft balladry of A Woman's Worth. Her voice is husky and strong, and the drums often have the thick, dusty feel of hip-hop. She also covers Prince's How Come You Don't Call Me.","Keys, from Hell's Kitchen in Manhattan, trained as a classical pianist and signed her first record deal as a teenager with Columbia. When that deal stalled, Clive Davis took her to his new label J Records. She appeared on the Oprah Winfrey Show before the album came out, and it sold over twelve million copies worldwide. It won five Grammys, including best new artist.","The opening track, Piano & I, is Keys playing a phrase from Beethoven's Moonlight Sonata before the beat drops in; the whole album is framed as the story of a classical pianist finding her way into soul. The title is a pun: A minor is a key, and she was a minor when she wrote many of the songs.","It was a huge commercial success and well reviewed, though some critics thought it earnest and derivative of the 1970s. It is now seen as the moment neo-soul crossed into the pop mainstream, and as the record that made the piano central to R&B again.","Start with Piano & I and then Fallin', and listen for the classical training in her left hand. Then play the first movement of the Moonlight Sonata itself, and afterwards Stevie Wonder's Songs in the Key of Life, to which the album owes a great deal."],"alicia-keys-fallin":["A slow, swaying gospel-blues ballad in a lilting triple feel. Keys plays a rolling piano figure that repeats almost unchanged, and sings over it in a husky voice about a love that keeps pulling her back despite herself. The arrangement is spare: piano, drums and backing voices that rise in the choruses. By the end she is almost shouting.","The first single from Songs in A Minor. Clive Davis promoted it with an unusual appearance on the Oprah Winfrey Show before release, and it rose to number one in America, where it stayed for six weeks. It won the Grammy for song of the year, a rare honour for an R&B ballad by a new writer.","Listen to the piano: it keeps circling round the same few minor chords, a pattern that owes as much to blues and old gospel as to modern R&B. The rhythm has the slow triple sway of a 1950s soul ballad, which is why the song sounds older than it is. Its opening seconds were designed to announce 'a pianist', not 'a producer'.","It was praised as a return to real musicianship in pop R&B at a moment when the charts were dominated by producers' beats, and a twenty-year-old playing her own piano on television became part of the story. Some critics found it dramatic without being deep, all build and no surprise. It remains her signature song and a standard for singing contests, which has done its reputation no favours, though her own live versions keep it raw.","Listen to the piano alone, then to the voice alone; they are two different songs of equal weight. Then compare it with Etta James's I'd Rather Go Blind, another slow minor-key song of helpless love."],"jill-scott-who-is-jill-scott":["A warm, conversational debut that moves between singing, spoken poetry and scat. Scott's voice is full and flexible, sliding from a murmur to a roar within a line. A Long Walk describes a simple date in loving detail; Gettin' in the Way is a firm warning to a rival; The Way and He Loves Me (Lyzel in E Flat) are soft, slow songs of contentment. The grooves are live and relaxed.","Scott came from the Philadelphia spoken-word scene, where poets and musicians performed together in small clubs. She had co-written The Roots' You Got Me, which won a Grammy with Erykah Badu singing her part. This debut was produced largely by DJ Jazzy Jeff's company A Touch of Jazz, in Philadelphia, and released on the new independent label Hidden Beach.","He Loves Me (Lyzel in E Flat) is one of the rare pop songs to name its own key in the title, a nod to the jazz and classical music around which Scott grew up. Listen for how often her lines move from singing to speaking in mid-sentence: the album is poetry performance as much as soul.","It was received warmly and sold well, and it is a defining record of the Philadelphia strand of neo-soul. Some critics found its domestic, everyday focus refreshing, others slight; its reputation has settled on the side of warmth and craft.","Start with A Long Walk, then He Loves Me, and notice how much of each song is spoken. Then play Erykah Badu's Baduizm and Lauryn Hill's Miseducation for the neo-soul circle she belonged to."],"frank-ocean-channel-orange":["A sprawling debut album of character sketches and confessions. Thinkin Bout You is a falsetto love song over sparse synthesisers; Super Rich Kids portrays bored, wealthy teenagers; Pyramids is a ten-minute journey from ancient Egypt to a modern strip club; Bad Religion is a confession to a taxi driver, set against organ and strings. Ocean's voice moves from croon to falsetto to a nearly spoken murmur, and the arrangements are lush but never crowded.","Ocean, raised in New Orleans and a member of the Los Angeles collective Odd Future, had already made a name with a mixtape and songwriting for others. Days before release in July 2012, he published an open letter describing his first love as a man. At the time, R&B and hip-hop had few openly queer stars, and the letter gave the album a context it could not escape.","The title refers to synaesthesia: Ocean has said he associated the summer he first fell in love with the colour orange. Listen to how the album is framed by television sounds, with channels switching between tracks, as if you were flicking through stations. Bad Religion and Forrest Gump address love for a man without saying so outright.","Critics greeted it with near-unanimous acclaim and it won the Grammy for best urban contemporary album. It is now seen as the album that opened R&B up to wider subjects, stranger structures and queer lives, and as the start of the alternative R&B era.","Start with Thinkin Bout You, then Bad Religion, then Pyramids. Listen to how often the songs end in a different place from where they started. Then go back to Stevie Wonder's Innervisions, another album of character sketches in soul form."],"frank-ocean-pyramids":["A ten-minute song in two halves. The first is a pulsing, synthesiser-driven tale of Cleopatra stolen from her palace; midway, the music drops away and returns as a slow, woozy R&B groove set in a modern-day strip club called the Pyramid, where a woman who shares Cleopatra's name works. The tempo, the texture and the whole century change; the story continues. It ends with a long guitar solo.","Released as a single shortly before Channel Orange, and the album's clearest statement of ambition. Long R&B songs had been rare on the radio since the 1970s, when Isaac Hayes and Stevie Wonder stretched tracks past ten minutes; this one refused radio length outright at a time when streaming was only beginning to make such refusals affordable. Ocean produced it with his collaborator Om'Mas Keith, and John Mayer adds the closing guitar.","The two halves share a name and a woman: the song is a comparison between two kinds of queenship, one ancient, one debased. Listen for the arpeggiated synthesiser in the first half, which owes a lot to 1980s dance music and to Kraftwerk's machine pulses, before the second half lurches into slow soul.","It was admired as one of the boldest singles of its year and is often cited as the moment Ocean showed his range, turning an R&B single into something closer to a short film. Some critics found it overlong and the Egyptian half more concept than song. Over time the second half, slow and sordid, has come to be heard as the real heart of it. It remains one of the defining long-form songs of 2010s R&B.","Listen through without skipping to the switch at about the midpoint, which is the point. Then compare with Stevie Wonder's long, multi-part songs from the 1970s, and with Kraftwerk's Computer World for the synthesiser pulse."],"frank-ocean-blonde":["A drifting, fragmented, often drumless album of memories and longing. Nikes opens with pitched-up vocals over a soft beat; Ivy is a guitar ballad about a fading first love; Self Control, Solo and White Ferrari are hushed and open-ended, often with guitar or organ alone. Ocean's voice is sometimes sped up, sometimes low and close. Many songs end without resolving; the album closes with the gospel-tinged Futura Free.","Ocean released it himself in August 2016, a day after delivering a visual album, Endless, to fulfil his contract with Def Jam, and so owned it outright. He had spent four years making it, with contributors including Beyoncé and André 3000, largely out of view. The title on the cover reads Blond, and the difference between the two spellings is often read as an allusion to gender.","White Ferrari quietly borrows lines and melody from the Beatles' Here, There and Everywhere, and its writers are credited. Listen also for the spoken interludes, including a voicemail from Ocean's mother warning against drugs, and for how few tracks have steady drums: the missing pulse is the album's most radical choice.","It was acclaimed at once and topped many best-of-the-decade lists. Ocean declined to submit it for Grammys, criticising the awards' treatment of Black artists. It is now widely considered the defining album of alternative R&B and one of the most influential albums of the century so far.","Listen late at night, in order, with headphones. Start with Ivy, then Self Control, then White Ferrari. After White Ferrari, play the Beatles' Revolver and find Here, There and Everywhere to hear how completely he transformed it."],"solange-a-seat-at-the-table":["A soft-spoken, deeply personal album about Black identity, anger and healing. Cranes in the Sky lists the ways the singer has tried to escape sadness, over a gentle groove and strings; Don't Touch My Hair is a quiet refusal of other people's intrusions; Mad and F.U.B.U. address rage and ownership. Solange's voice is light and airy, and the grooves are slow, warm and full of space.","Solange, Beyoncé's younger sister, had been making music for a decade with modest success. She recorded much of this in New Iberia, Louisiana, with Raphael Saadiq co-producing. Released in September 2016, in the middle of the Black Lives Matter protests, it went to number one in America. Cranes in the Sky won a Grammy.","The album is threaded with spoken interludes from her parents, Tina and Mathew Knowles, and from Master P, the New Orleans rapper and label founder, who talks about independence and ownership. These voices give the album the feel of a family conversation. Cranes in the Sky was written some eight years earlier and held back until it fit.","It was met with near-unanimous praise and appeared at the top of many best-of-the-year lists. Critics noted that its anger is delivered almost in a whisper, the opposite of the anthemic protest song, and that choice is what most reviews returned to. It was released months after Beyoncé's Lemonade, and comparison was inevitable. It is now seen as a major statement of the mid-2010s, and as the moment Solange stepped fully out of her sister's shadow.","Start with Cranes in the Sky, then Don't Touch My Hair. Listen to the interludes; they are part of the argument. Then play Frank Ocean's Blonde, released the same year, for another quiet, bold R&B record."],"sza-ctrl":["A candid, funny and anxious album about being young, insecure and in love. Supermodel opens with guitar and a list of grievances; Love Galore, Drew Barrymore, The Weekend and 20 Something are slow, woozy songs about jealousy, self-doubt and wanting to be chosen. SZA's voice is loose, slurring and conversational, and the beats are soft, spacious and often guitar-led.","SZA, born Solána Rowe in St Louis and raised in New Jersey, had been signed to Top Dawg Entertainment, Kendrick Lamar's label. The album was delayed for years while she argued with the label. When it arrived in June 2017 it was an immediate success and earned five Grammy nominations.","The album is threaded with spoken interludes from SZA's mother and grandmother, who talk about control, and whose voices frame the album's question: how much of life can anyone control? Listen to the guitar: many songs are built around it rather than keyboards, an unusual choice in modern R&B.","It was met with acclaim and topped many best-of-the-year lists. Critics admired its candour and its refusal to present the singer as glamorous. It is now seen as one of the defining R&B albums of the late 2010s, and a blueprint for the confessional, genre-blurring R&B that followed.","Start with Supermodel, then Drew Barrymore and The Weekend. Listen for the spoken interludes. Then compare it with Frank Ocean's Channel Orange, whose openness about the singer's inner life it continues."],"the-weeknd-house-of-balloons":["A mixtape of dark, slow, reverb-soaked songs about parties, loneliness and excess. The Weeknd's high, pleading voice floats over cavernous drums and blurry synthesisers. High for This, What You Need and The Morning are the best known; the title track rides a sample from a British post-punk band. The mood is hazy, seductive and uneasy.","Abel Tesfaye, a Toronto singer born to Ethiopian immigrant parents, released this for free online in March 2011 with producers Doc McKinney and Illangelo. He gave no interviews and hid his face, so the music spread through blogs and a recommendation from Drake. The mixtape was later reissued as part of a compilation called Trilogy. Drug use and casual sex are its constant subject, treated with numbness rather than celebration.","The title track samples Siouxsie and the Banshees' Happy House, and The Party & The After Party uses Beach House's Master of None; alternative rock samples in R&B were rare, and they give the album its dreamlike haze. Listen for how the reverb swallows the drums, making even the fastest tracks sound half-asleep.","It was acclaimed by online critics and made the Weeknd famous almost overnight. Along with Frank Ocean's early work, it is credited with defining alternative R&B. Some listeners have found its view of women and excess troubling, a debate that has followed his career.","Listen late at night, with headphones, from start to finish. Pay attention to the samples and how they are bent into R&B. Then compare with Sade's Diamond Life, a very different take on seduction, and with Frank Ocean's Channel Orange."],"the-weeknd-blinding-lights":["A bright, driving synth-pop song with a pounding drum machine and a looping keyboard hook. The Weeknd sings of racing through a city at night to reach someone he needs. The sound is pure 1980s: gated drums, shimmering synthesisers and a relentless pulse. It is short, catchy and always moving forward.","Released in November 2019 as a single from the album After Hours, with production by Max Martin and Oscar Holter. It became one of the biggest songs of the century, spending ninety weeks on the American chart and topping Billboard's list of the greatest Hot 100 songs of all time in 2021. It also became one of the most-streamed songs in history.","Listen to the drum machine: it has the sharp, gated sound of 1980s pop, and the keyboard hook recalls a-ha's Take On Me. The song is part of a larger story on After Hours, in which the Weeknd plays a character spiralling through a night in Las Vegas, and the video shows him bloodied and dazed.","It was a huge commercial success and widely admired as a polished pop song; arriving just before the pandemic lockdowns, its nocturnal rush through empty streets took on an unintended resonance. Some critics found it more nostalgic than original. Its chart records have since made it a reference point in debates about how streaming measures popularity. It is now seen as the moment the Weeknd moved fully from alternative R&B into global pop.","Listen to the drum machine and the synthesiser hook, then play Kraftwerk's Computer World to hear where that machine pulse began. Then compare it with House of Balloons to hear how far the Weeknd travelled in eight years."],"sugarhill-gang-rappers-delight":["Fifteen minutes of three young men from New Jersey rapping in turn over a live band's replay of Chic's Good Times: bass, hi-hat, a scratchy rhythm guitar, almost nothing else. Wonder Mike opens with the hip hop, the hippie chant; Big Bank Hank and Master Gee follow with boasts, a joke about a terrible dinner at a friend's house, and a Superman routine. Nobody sings a note. The groove simply keeps going and the voices keep passing the baton.","Sylvia Robinson, a singer turned label owner, had heard rapping at a New York club and decided to put it on a record. The Bronx MCs who invented the form worked live and saw little reason to make records, so Robinson auditioned three men she found around Englewood, New Jersey, one of them rapping while working at a pizzeria. Her house band played the Chic groove rather than sampling it, because sampling barely existed in 1979.","Big Bank Hank's verse was not his own. He had been working with Grandmaster Caz of the Cold Crush Brothers and used lines from his notebook; the boast that spells out C-A-S-A-N-O-V-A is Caz's own name, and Caz was never credited or paid. Nile Rodgers and Bernard Edwards, hearing their bassline coming out of a club, took action and were added to the songwriting credit.","Bronx originators were scornful: outsiders with no standing had beaten them onto vinyl. It sold enormously anyway and travelled far past New York. Judgment has settled on two claims that are both true, that it was not the first rap record and that it was the one that made the rest possible. The Chic settlement also set early terms for how borrowed music would be paid for.","Find the full-length twelve-inch rather than the radio edit; endurance is the point. Notice how the band stiffens and loosens behind the voices in a way no drum machine would. Then play Chic's Good Times straight afterwards and hear how much emptier the rap version deliberately leaves the middle of the mix, so that speech has room."],"grandmaster-flash-adventures-on-the-wheels-of-steel":["Seven minutes of one man cutting between records in real time, with no band and almost no rapping. Flash drops the needle on a groove, snatches it back, stutters a single word into a rhythm, and slams into a different record mid-bar. Fragments of Chic, Blondie's Rapture, Queen's Another One Bites the Dust and his own group's records collide and hand off to each other. The join is always audible, and that is the music.","By 1981 Flash had spent years building a technique in Bronx parks and community rooms: a cue system so he could hear the next record before the crowd did, and a way of marking the label with crayon to find a break instantly. Sugar Hill let him document it. The takes had to be played straight through, because multitrack editing would have defeated the purpose, and a turntable record had no commercial precedent at all.","Listen for the moment Blondie's Debbie Harry names Flash in the lyric of Rapture, which he then cuts up: the record is quoting a record that is quoting him. A children's spoken-word disc also turns up in the collage, chopped into nonsense. Everything you hear is the sound of vinyl being physically dragged, so the pitch bends slightly on almost every entry.","Radio had nowhere to put it and it sold modestly. Critics at the time treated it as a novelty document of a party trick. It is now read as the founding statement of turntablism, the argument that a record player is an instrument and that existing recordings are raw material. DJ battle culture, scratch records and the entire sampling aesthetic descend from these seven minutes.","Count the sources on a first pass and then stop counting and listen to the rhythm of the cuts instead: Flash is phrasing, not just switching. Compare it with Cage's radio piece to hear two different centuries deciding that found sound is material, then play a modern DMC battle routine to hear where the sport went."],"grandmaster-flash-the-message":["A slow, heavy mid-tempo groove, a rubbery synthesizer figure looping under it, and a voice describing broken glass, rats, urine on the stairs and a repossessed car. The refrain, don't push me, 'cause I'm close to the edge, arrives like a held breath. Melle Mel's closing verse follows a child from birth to a prison cell in about a minute. The track ends not with music but with a spoken scene of the group being stopped by police.","Sugar Hill's percussionist Ed Fletcher, known as Duke Bootee, brought most of the words and the track; Melle Mel supplied the last verse and the delivery. Several of the Furious Five wanted nothing to do with it, believing audiences came to dance and boast, not to hear about the rent. Flash himself is barely present. New York in 1982 was cutting services, and the South Bronx had become national shorthand for urban collapse.","The final thirty seconds are a drama, not a song: the crew greet each other on a corner, a squad car pulls up, and the record fades on them being put in it. Nothing is explained. Notice too that the famous refrain is sung, not rapped, by Fletcher, which is why the hook sits so oddly against the verses around it.","It was the first rap record widely treated as journalism, and it gave critics who had dismissed the form a way in, which annoyed people who had liked hip-hop when it was only about dancing. The complaint that it invented a market for misery is as old as the record. So is the answer: it also proved rap could carry an argument.","Play it after Rapper's Delight to feel the temperature drop. Follow the last verse only, from the birth of the child to the cell, and time it. Then listen to how little the backing track changes across seven minutes, and how that refusal to develop becomes the point: nothing here moves."],"grandmaster-flash-white-lines":["A bassline that climbs and drops in a four-note hook carries the whole record; over it Melle Mel warns about cocaine while sounding thrilled by it. Backing voices chant the title and a hissed freeze, rock. Two worlds alternate in the verses, a dealer in a Cadillac who beats the charge and a kid who gets eight years for a small amount. The groove never lets up for either of them.","By 1983 Flash had sued Sugar Hill and left; the record still went out under his name, which he objected to. Cocaine had moved from glamour to epidemic in New York, and the anti-drug song was becoming a commercial category. Sugar Hill's house band, as usual, replayed rather than sampled the borrowed part, a habit that would shortly become an expensive legal problem for the label.","That bassline comes from Cavern by Liquid Liquid, a downtown New York post-punk group; Sugar Hill's musicians copied it without clearance. The lawsuit that followed helped destroy both Liquid Liquid's label and Sugar Hill itself. Listen also to how the chorus voices are pushed almost into the red, a distortion that was cheap studio compression rather than a choice, and became part of the record's sound.","It was a hit in Britain long before America caught up, and its warning has always been undercut by how good the track feels, a tension critics have argued over ever since. Grandmaster Flash's absence from a record bearing his name remains a standing example of how labels treated artists in the era. It has been sampled, covered and re-recorded continuously.","Start with the bass and try to ignore the words for a chorus, then let the words back in and notice the mismatch. The twelve-inch version has room the edit does not. Play Liquid Liquid's Cavern afterwards to hear the same figure played by a very different band in a very different room."],"afrika-bambaataa-planet-rock":["No band, no guitars, no live drums: a Roland drum machine ticking out a hard, dry pattern, a synthesizer melody riding above it, a vocoder chanting party people, and the Soulsonic Force calling a crowd to the floor. The middle breaks down into handclaps and orchestral stabs before the machine snaps back. It sounds less like a song than like a transmission, and the voices behave as if they are answering it.","Bambaataa had been a Bronx gang leader who turned his organisation into the Universal Zulu Nation and his record collection into a philosophy: anything with a break was fair game, rock, calypso, television themes, German electronics. Producer Arthur Baker and keyboardist John Robie built the track with him in New York, at the moment the TR-808 drum machine had become cheap and unfashionable, which is exactly why it was available.","The melody is lifted from Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express and the rhythm from their Numbers, welded together deliberately rather than quoted in passing. Kraftwerk were not asked, and the settlement came later. Listen for the 808's bass drum, which is not a sample of a drum at all but a tuned sine tone decaying, the sound that would define dance music for the next forty years.","It was a huge club record instantly and a foundational one in hindsight: Detroit techno and Miami bass both point back to it, and so does most electro. Bambaataa's own standing is now contested, after allegations of sexual abuse made public in 2016 led the Zulu Nation to remove him from his role, and writers have had to separate the record's importance from the man's.","Play Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express first, then this, and hear a cool European machine record turned into a hot American dance record by little more than tempo and attitude. Focus on the handclap breakdown, where the track almost stops. Then jump forward to early Detroit techno and notice how little had to change."],"afrika-bambaataa-looking-for-the-perfect-beat":["Eight minutes built from the same machines as Planet Rock but arranged like a suite: sections arrive, take over, and give way, with synth fanfares, sirens, a vocoder chorus and stretches where only the drum machine and a bass pulse remain. The Soulsonic Force chant the title as a mission statement. Where the earlier record had one idea repeated, this one keeps promising a bigger one around the next corner.","Tommy Boy wanted a follow-up, and Arthur Baker and John Robie were by then fluent in the equipment rather than improvising with it. Electro had become a genre within a year, with records coming out of New York, Miami and London, and the competition was to be more extravagant. Twelve-inch singles aimed at club DJs allowed lengths and structures that radio would never have permitted.","The track keeps deferring its own arrival, which is the joke in the title: the perfect beat is always the next section. Notice how the vocoder voice is used as an instrument in the arrangement rather than as a singer, dropping in for a bar and vanishing. The sirens are synthesized, not sampled, which is why they bend in ways a real siren never would.","It never matched Planet Rock commercially and has always lived in its shadow, though DJs and producers often prefer it for the arrangement. Critics who found electro thin in the mid-eighties have largely come round, hearing in it the template for a dance record built as a sequence of drops rather than verses and choruses.","Listen for structure rather than hooks: mark where each new section enters and see how rarely anything returns. Play it against Planet Rock to hear ambition replacing shock. Then follow the thread to Miami bass and early Detroit records, which took the drum machine and threw the fanfares away."],"run-dmc-sucker-mcs":["A drum machine, a handful of scratches, and two voices. There is no bassline, no chords, no chorus in the usual sense. Run and DMC shout in hard, clipped phrases and finish each other's lines, one voice landing on the last word of the other's. Three minutes, over almost before it has settled. After the layered party records of the previous four years it sounds like someone has switched the lights on.","Joseph Simmons and Darryl McDaniels were middle-class kids from Hollis, Queens, not South Bronx veterans. Run's brother Russell Simmons and producer Larry Smith put them on Profile, a small New York independent. The B-side of their first single, this was built on a hard programmed rhythm rather than a disco band, in a year when the available machines finally made that cheaper and louder than hiring players.","The lyric is a list of ordinary life, a Cadillac, a college, a bedroom, delivered with the aggression usually reserved for boasting about wealth. Listen to the trading: Run and DMC frequently overlap by a syllable, an effect they rehearsed rather than stumbled into, and which almost no earlier rap record attempts. The scratches are the only element that is not a machine.","It is routinely called the first new school record, the moment hip-hop stopped dressing like disco and started sounding like itself. Old school performers heard it as crude; younger MCs heard a blank page. Every stripped-back rap production of the following decade, and a good deal of the argument that less instrumentation means more force, starts here.","Play it immediately after Rapper's Delight and count the instruments in each. Then listen only to the vocal interplay, following where one voice hands over to the other. The best way to hear its influence is to put on a bare Def Jam record from three years later and notice that nothing essential has been added."],"run-dmc-run-dmc":["Under half an hour, nine tracks, and almost no decoration. Hard programmed drums, scratching from Jam Master Jay, and two voices barking in unison or in relay. It's Like That lays out unemployment and war in flat declaratives with a shrug of a refrain. Rock Box drops a distorted electric guitar into the middle of a rap record, doubling the drum machine's aggression. Hard Times and Jam-Master Jay keep the same skeletal frame.","Profile could not afford, and Russell Simmons did not want, a session band. The group came from Hollis, Queens, dressed in what they already wore, black hats, Adidas with no laces, leather, and refused the sequins and spacesuits of the previous generation. Rock radio was closed to rap; putting a guitar on Rock Box was partly a musical choice and partly a way of forcing a door.","The guitar on Rock Box was played by a session musician, Eddie Martinez, not by anyone in the group, and it is mixed as a rhythm instrument rather than a lead, sitting exactly where a horn stab would go. Notice that Jam Master Jay's scratching is arranged as punctuation at line ends, not as a solo: the DJ is treated as a third voice in the conversation.","It became the first rap album to sell in the hundreds of thousands and to be discussed as an album rather than a collection of twelve-inches. Rock critics used Rock Box as permission to write about hip-hop, which some in the scene resented as approval on the wrong terms. The record's plainness, once taken for poverty of means, is now heard as its argument.","Listen to It's Like That and Rock Box back to back: the same machine, two different strategies for getting heard. Keep an ear on how much silence the mix leaves around each drum hit. Then play Raising Hell to hear the same group with Rick Rubin making the emptiness even bigger."],"run-dmc-raising-hell":["Twelve tracks of enormous, dry drums and two voices at full volume. Peter Piper rides a loop of tubular bells against a hard break; My Adidas turns a pair of shoes into a chant; It's Tricky borrows a new wave guitar riff and rides it; Walk This Way drags a rock band into the room. Proud to Be Black closes with a history lesson. The whole record is louder, cleaner and funnier than anything rap had made.","Rick Rubin, then a student running Def Jam out of a dormitory, co-produced with Russell Simmons and pushed the group towards fewer elements played bigger. Rap was still assumed to be a singles form with no album audience. The record's success, a multi-platinum seller and a Rolling Stone cover, ended that assumption and made hip-hop a business that major labels could no longer pretend not to see.","The bells that run through Peter Piper come from Bob James's instrumental Take Me to the Mardi Gras, a break already famous in the parks long before it reached a record. My Adidas was not a paid endorsement when it was written; the sponsorship deal came afterwards, when the company's representatives watched an arena full of people hold their shoes in the air.","It was the first hip-hop album to reach a mass rock audience, and the crossover brought the familiar complaint that the music had been diluted for white listeners. Time has largely dismissed that: the record is harder, not softer, than its predecessors. What is still argued is whether Walk This Way opened a door for rap or simply rescued Aerosmith.","Begin with Peter Piper for the bells and the trading, then My Adidas for the chant. Notice throughout how much of the arrangement is silence between hits. Compare the drum sound here with the same group two years earlier: fewer elements, far more room, and every one of them enormous."],"run-dmc-walk-this-way":["The guitar riff from Aerosmith's 1975 record starts alone, then a drum machine lands on top of it with twice the weight. Run and DMC trade the original's tongue-twisting verses in hard alternating phrases while Steven Tyler sings the chorus and Joe Perry plays over them. Two bands are in the room at once and neither yields; the track works because the riff was already a rhythm loop before anyone rapped on it.","Rick Rubin proposed it. Run and DMC knew the Aerosmith break from park DJs and had rapped over it for years without knowing the band's name, and they initially thought recording it was embarrassing hillbilly music. Aerosmith, by 1986, were a spent commercial force. The video, in which a wall between a rock rehearsal room and a rap session is knocked down, did as much work as the record.","Listen to how Run and DMC deliver the original lyric: they keep Tyler's syllable count but flatten the melody out of it entirely, which exposes how close his phrasing already was to rapping. Note also that the drum machine does not follow the live drums, it replaces them, so the rock band is effectively playing over a hip-hop rhythm rather than the other way round.","It went top five, put rap on rock radio and MTV for the first time in force, and revived Aerosmith's career outright. Purists on both sides called it a stunt. The rap-rock genre it licensed produced a great deal of bad music over the following fifteen years, which has made some critics colder on it, but the collision itself still sounds deliberate rather than accidental.","Play Aerosmith's original first and listen only to the drums, then hear how the remake removes their swing and replaces it with a grid. Watch the video once for the argument it makes visually. Then try Licensed to Ill, made months later, where the same producer takes the joke much further."],"ll-cool-j-radio":["A teenager's voice, enormous drums and very little else. I Can't Live Without My Radio is a boast about a boombox delivered as if the boombox were a weapon; Rock the Bells rides a hammering pattern and a scratched bell; I Need a Beat is almost all space. The album is short, fast and monomaniacal, and the rapping is unusually agile, sliding between double-time bursts and slow, taunting lines.","James Todd Smith was sixteen when Def Jam, then run by Rick Rubin from a New York University dormitory with Russell Simmons, released his first single. Radio was the label's first album. Rubin's production philosophy was subtraction: a drum machine turned up until it distorted, one or two other elements, nothing to hide behind. The back cover credit reads that the record was reduced, not produced.","That credit, reduced by Rick Rubin, is the whole method stated in two words. Listen to how often a track drops to voice and kick drum alone for a bar before the rest slams back; the effect depends on the listener expecting something to fill the gap. The boombox on the cover is not decoration either, this is music mixed to sound correct out of a single plastic speaker on a street.","It sold strongly and established both an artist and a label. Critics noticed that a sixteen-year-old could carry an album alone, which nobody had proved. LL's later move into ballads and pop divides opinion about his legacy, but the argument that he was the first great solo technician on the microphone usually starts with this record.","Start with Rock the Bells, at volume, and pay attention to the drum machine's decay: the sound is as much room as hit. Then follow the voice alone, noting how he changes speed within a single verse. Compare with Run-D.M.C.'s debut, where two voices do what one is doing here."],"ll-cool-j-mama-said-knock-you-out":["The title track opens with a shouted warning not to call it a comeback and then never lets go: a stuttering drum loop, a low horn stab, and a voice pushed to the edge of hoarseness. Around it the album alternates snarling battle records like The Boomin' System and Murdergram with slower turns such as Around the Way Girl, where charm replaces threat. Marley Marl's drums are wetter and heavier than the dry Def Jam sound LL began with.","LL's previous album had been badly received and he had been booed by a hometown crowd, an unusual public humiliation for an artist still in his early twenties. He went to Marley Marl, the Queensbridge producer whose sampled and layered drums had reshaped New York rap. The competition by 1990 included Rakim, KRS-One and Public Enemy, and a reputation built in 1985 counted for nothing.","The title is literal: it comes from his grandmother, who told him to knock them out when he complained about being written off. The vocal on the title track is deliberately kept slightly out of control, with breaths and cracks left in, where his earlier records were clean. Notice that the hook is not sung at all, it is shouted, which was still unusual for a lead single.","It restored him commercially and critically and won a Grammy, and the title track became shorthand for any return from the dead. Critics who had filed him under teen novelty had to revise. The album is long and uneven, and the ballads still divide listeners, but nobody argues about the first three minutes.","Begin with the title track and listen for the moment the voice tears. Then play The Boomin' System for Marley Marl's drums, which are the real story: dense, compressed, with the room baked in. Set it beside Radio from five years earlier to hear how much the technology of sampling changed what a producer could do."],"public-enemy-yo-bum-rush-the-show":["Slower and heavier than the rap around it, with a booming low end and guitar that snarls rather than riffs. Chuck D's baritone is already fully formed, unhurried and declamatory; Flavor Flav circles him with yelps and interjections that turn statements into arguments. You're Gonna Get Yours is a car record with menace in it, Public Enemy No. 1 sets out the group's name and stance, Timebomb ticks. The album moves at a deliberate, bulldozing pace.","Chuck D had been a graphic design student and radio DJ on Long Island, older than most rappers and initially reluctant to sign. Def Jam wanted the voice. The group arrived with an apparatus already designed: a logo of a figure in a rifle sight, a security detail called the S1Ws who drilled on stage, and a clock the size of a dinner plate around Flavor Flav's neck. None of it was conventional showbusiness.","The tempos are notably slower than contemporary hip-hop, a decision the Bomb Squad reversed completely on the next record; hearing them side by side shows a production team working out what it wanted. Listen for how Flavor Flav is mixed, usually slightly off-centre and behind Chuck, so his comments sound like heckling from the wings rather than a second lead.","Reviews were respectful rather than excited, and sales were modest; the consensus was that something formidable had arrived but had not yet gone off. That judgment still holds, which is why the record is more often cited than played. Its reputation improved once critics went back to find where the group's political framing began.","Play Public Enemy No. 1 for the voice alone, which is already the most authoritative in rap. Notice the weight of the low end on a good system. Then go straight to It Takes a Nation of Millions and hear the tempo jump by a third and the samples multiply; the same group, one year, an entirely different noise."],"public-enemy-it-takes-a-nation-of-millions":["A wall of sirens, shrieking saxophone loops, scratching and hard drums, faster than any rap album before it. Chuck D declaims over the top in a voice pitched to carry across a stadium; Flavor Flav interrupts, mocks and occasionally takes a whole track. Bring the Noise, Don't Believe the Hype and Rebel Without a Pause come at you almost without gaps. Black Steel in the Hour of Chaos slows everything to a lone piano loop and tells a prison-escape story.","The Bomb Squad, Hank Shocklee, Keith Shocklee, Eric Sadler and Chuck D, built the tracks by layering dozens of short samples until the result behaved like distortion. Sampling technology had just become affordable and clearance law had not caught up, so a producer could use forty sources on one track. The politics came from Chuck's reading of Black nationalism, aimed at an audience that mainstream radio was not serving.","The album opens with a live recording made at the Hammersmith Odeon in London, so the first thing you hear is a British crowd. The screaming horn loop that powers Rebel Without a Pause comes from The Grunt by The J.B.'s, pitched up until it is nearly unbearable. The piano under Black Steel is lifted from Isaac Hayes's Hyperbolicsyllabicsesquedalymistic, on Hot Buttered Soul.","It was received almost immediately as a landmark and has stayed near the top of critical lists ever since. The controversies are separate from the music: Professor Griff's antisemitic statements the following year, and the argument over whether the group's rhetoric survives close reading. Producers point out that no record like this can legally be made now, because clearing its samples would be impossible.","Listen to Rebel Without a Pause once for the sax loop and once for everything underneath it. Then play Black Steel for contrast, a single piano and a story. If you know Reich's Different Trains, made the same year from sampled speech, play them in sequence: two very different people discovering that recorded voice is musical material."],"public-enemy-fight-the-power":["A marching drum loop, layers of horn and guitar fragments stacked until they buzz, and Chuck D calling out over the top with Flavor Flav answering. The chorus is a chant rather than a melody. Midway through, the verses turn from general defiance to specific targets, dismissing Elvis Presley and John Wayne as heroes who were never theirs. A saxophone wails across the last section. Five minutes that never stop accelerating in feel, though the tempo never changes.","Spike Lee asked the group for an anthem for Do the Right Thing, a film about a Brooklyn block on the hottest day of the year that ends in a police killing and a riot. In the film it blasts from a character's boombox for most of the running time, becoming the thing the argument is about. It arrived in a summer of high-profile racial violence in New York.","The title is taken from the Isley Brothers' 1975 song of the same name, a soul record with the same refusal in it. Branford Marsalis's saxophone was played live into the collage rather than sampled. Listen to how the Bomb Squad tune their loops slightly against each other so the texture beats and shimmers; nothing in the track is quite in tune with anything else, and that friction is deliberate.","The Elvis and John Wayne lines drew furious responses and are still the most quoted in the group's catalogue; Chuck D's defence was that he was describing whose heroes they were, not denying their talent. The song has since been used at protests across four decades, which some read as vindication and others as domestication. It remains the group's most widely known recording.","Watch the opening credits of Do the Right Thing first, then listen to the track alone and notice how much it still implies a street. Focus on the saxophone entry near the end. Then play the Isley Brothers' song of the same title to hear a demand made in 1975 in a completely different voice."],"public-enemy-fear-of-a-black-planet":["Twenty tracks, many under two minutes, running into each other with radio chatter and snatches of speech between them, so the record plays like a broadcast rather than a sequence of songs. 911 Is a Joke is Flavor Flav alone, funny and furious about ambulances that do not come. Welcome to the Terrordome is the densest thing the Bomb Squad ever built. Burn Hollywood Burn brings in guests to attack film casting. Fight the Power closes it.","The group made it while under sustained attack, following Professor Griff's antisemitic remarks to a newspaper and the firing, rehiring and disbanding that followed. Chuck D's lyrics respond directly to the press coverage. Sampling lawsuits were beginning in earnest, and this is close to the last major record made under the old assumption that anything on a record could be used.","The short tracks and the linking chatter are structural: the album is designed to be heard whole, with no gaps to skip into, and several pieces make no sense on their own. Listen for how often a sample is used only for its texture, a half-second of guitar or crowd noise, rather than for a recognisable hook. Welcome to the Terrordome reportedly carries around twenty separate sources.","It sold better than its predecessor and was praised as an achievement in production even by people who objected to the lyrics, some of which, in Terrordome, were widely read as repeating the offence the group was defending itself against. Chuck D has disputed that reading. Judgment now tends to hold the production as extraordinary and the politics as a genuine and unresolved problem.","Listen to the first third without pausing, so the transitions do their work. Take 911 Is a Joke as the way in, since Flavor Flav's comic timing carries an argument the serious tracks state flatly. Then compare the density with Yo! Bum Rush the Show: three years, and the same group has gone from a groove to a collage."],"eric-b-and-rakim-eric-b-is-president":["A slow, walking bassline, a hard unhurried drum pattern, scratching at the line ends, and a voice that does not raise itself once. Rakim enters with a calm announcement that he came in the door, said it before, and never lets the volume or the tempo of his delivery vary. Where every other rapper in 1986 shouted on the beat, he speaks across it, placing rhymes inside lines rather than only at their ends.","Eric Barrier was a DJ from Queens looking for an MC; William Griffin, a teenager from Wyandanch on Long Island who had taken the name Rakim, was a saxophone player and a student of the Nation of Gods and Earths. They recorded for a tiny label before 4th & B'way picked it up. The sampler was new enough that a loop still counted as a whole production idea.","The bassline is lifted from Over Like a Fat Rat by Fonda Rae, looped almost without alteration, which is why the track breathes like a band rather than a machine. Listen to where Rakim's rhymes fall: several land in the middle of a line, so the ear hears a pattern with no obvious downbeat. He also breathes in the wrong places on purpose, cutting a phrase where a shout would have landed.","MCs recognised it instantly as a change of standard, and the years afterwards are full of rappers describing the day they first heard it. Critics were slower, because nothing about the record announces itself. It is now the usual starting point for any argument about who changed rap technique, and the B-side, My Melody, is often preferred by specialists.","Play it after any 1985 rap single and listen to the difference in volume and heart rate. Then follow only the rhymes and try to hear where they land relative to the drums. The Fonda Rae original is worth a listen straight afterwards, to hear what a full arrangement sounds like before it was reduced to a loop."],"eric-b-and-rakim-paid-in-full":["Loops of old soul and funk, cut close and left largely alone, with scratching for punctuation and a voice that stays level throughout. I Ain't No Joke and My Melody are technical displays delivered without a raised syllable. I Know You Got Soul rides a bassline with a horn stab. Eric B. Is President is included. Two instrumentals give the DJ the floor. Nothing on the record is cluttered, and the spaces are as calculated as the words.","Recorded quickly and cheaply in New York by a duo with no track record, at the point when the Akai sampler put the contents of a record collection under one pair of hands. Rakim's vocabulary comes partly from the teachings of the Nation of Gods and Earths, common in Queens and Long Island, which is why so much of the imagery is about knowledge, precision and self-command rather than money or violence.","I Know You Got Soul takes its title, hook and feel from Bobby Byrd's record of that name, and the title track's bassline comes from Dennis Edwards's Don't Look Any Further. Listen to how Rakim's syllables sit slightly behind the drum, never quite on it, which is what makes an unhurried delivery sound like pressure rather than laziness.","The album's influence was immediate among rappers and slower among critics, but it has ended up on nearly every list of the essential hip-hop records. A British remix by Coldcut, which spliced the title track with Ofra Haza's Israeli vocal, became famous and was disliked by the duo, who felt it turned their record into something else; it is now studied as an early example of the remix as authorship.","Start with I Know You Got Soul, then I Ain't No Joke, and keep an ear on the pauses: Rakim leaves more silence than anyone before him. Play Bobby Byrd afterwards to hear the soul record underneath. The Coldcut seven-minute remix is worth hearing once, as an argument the artists lost."],"eric-b-and-rakim-follow-the-leader":["Faster and busier than the debut, with more instruments and more moving parts: bass guitar, keyboard stabs, layered breaks. The title track opens with a rumbling low figure and Rakim describing a journey out past the planets. Microphone Fiend sets an addiction metaphor over a hard swinging loop; Lyrics of Fury is a display of sustained rhyming at speed. The voice is the same instrument as before, only now it has more to push against.","Success had moved the duo from an independent to a major label with a budget, and rap production was changing monthly as samplers gained memory. Rakim was by now the MC other MCs measured themselves against, which raised the technical stakes on every verse. The album arrived in the same extraordinary year as It Takes a Nation of Millions and Slick Rick's debut.","Notice how many verses have no chorus to land on: Rakim will rhyme continuously for a minute and a half, breaking only for a scratch. The title track's imagery of travelling beyond the solar system is not decoration but a running conceit about leaving competitors behind. Listen also to the bass, which is played rather than sampled on several tracks, a sign of the bigger budget.","It sold well and was widely admired, though some listeners missed the starkness of Paid in Full, and the debate about which is the better record has never resolved. Its status among rappers is undisputed: Microphone Fiend and Lyrics of Fury are among the most quoted and rewritten verses in the form.","Take Microphone Fiend first for the swing, then the title track for the sustained rhyming. Try following one verse with the lyric in front of you to see how the internal rhymes stack. Then return to Paid in Full and decide for yourself whether more production helped."],"boogie-down-productions-criminal-minded":["Cheap, hard and fast. Scott La Rock's tracks are mostly a drum machine, one loop and a scratch, and KRS-One rides them in a hectoring Jamaican-inflected sing-song that slips between rapping and toasting. South Bronx is a geography lesson delivered as a threat. The Bridge Is Over is almost nothing but a piano figure and a voice demolishing rivals by name. 9mm Goes Bang tells a shooting story over a reggae rhythm.","KRS-One was living in a Bronx shelter where Scott La Rock worked as a counsellor. The record was made for a tiny label with almost no money. It is also a battle document: Queensbridge's Juice Crew had released a record that KRS read as claiming hip-hop began in Queens, and most of this album is the answer. Four months after its release, Scott La Rock was shot dead.","The piano loop that carries The Bridge Is Over is a lift from a Billy Joel song, played at a jaunty tempo entirely at odds with the contempt in the lyric. Note how often KRS drops into a Jamaican cadence: his father was Trinidadian and he had absorbed the sound-system tradition, which is where the practice of naming and destroying an opponent on record actually comes from.","It is the founding document of the recorded rap battle, and also of the argument about whether gunplay on a record cover is reportage or marketing; the sleeve, with the pair posed with weapons, has been cited in both directions for decades. Scott La Rock's murder changed the reception overnight and pushed KRS towards the teaching persona of the next record.","Play South Bronx and The Bridge Is Over together for the full argument, then find MC Shan's record that provoked them. Listen to how little is on any of these tracks. The reggae inflection is clearest on 9mm Goes Bang, which explains more about the album's rhythm than anything else on it."],"boogie-down-productions-by-all-means-necessary":["The same lean production, now turned towards instruction. My Philosophy sets out a manifesto against rappers who say nothing, over a loping break and a wandering bassline. Stop the Violence answers the killing of Scott La Rock without naming him much. Illegal Business connects the drug trade to the people policing it. KRS-One's delivery still swings between lecture, toast and taunt, and he refers to himself throughout as the Teacher.","Made within a year of his partner's murder, for a bigger label, by a man who had decided that being the best battle rapper was not enough. Crack had transformed New York's street economy and the political response was mass incarceration. KRS began appearing at universities, an unusual move for a rapper in 1988, and started the Stop the Violence Movement with other New York artists.","The cover deliberately recreates a famous photograph of Malcolm X holding a rifle and looking out of a window, with KRS in his place; the title alters Malcolm's own phrase. Listen for how much of the album is addressed to other rappers rather than to a general audience, which is why the tone is so often corrective rather than persuasive.","It was taken as proof that hip-hop could argue rather than only assert, and it helped establish the conscious rap category that later artists have both claimed and resented. KRS-One's habit of certainty has drawn steady criticism, and some of the album's positions have aged poorly, but the willingness to change position in public is part of the record's character.","Start with My Philosophy, one of the most quotable verses in the form, and follow the argument rather than the hooks. Then play Criminal Minded from the year before to hear the same voice with a completely different purpose. Stop the Violence is best heard knowing who is absent from the record."],"de-la-soul-3-feet-high-and-rising":["Twenty-four tracks including a running game-show skit in which the group are asked absurd questions. The samples come from everywhere: a children's educational song, soft rock, French language instruction records, psychedelic soul. Three voices, none of them aggressive, talk over each other and finish each other's jokes. Me Myself and I bounces on a fat funk loop; Eye Know whistles; Say No Go turns a pop hit inside out. It is the first rap album that is funny on purpose and all the way through.","Three teenagers from Amityville on Long Island, working with Prince Paul of Stetsasonic, who produced the record as a collage and treated the studio as a place for sketches. Rap in 1989 was hardening into militancy and gangster narrative; this arrived in bright colours with flowers on the sleeve and a coined slogan, the D.A.I.S.Y. age, which the group spent the next two years denying meant they were hippies.","The chorus of The Magic Number is taken from Schoolhouse Rock's Three Is a Magic Number, a children's television song; Eye Know whistles over Steely Dan's Peg; Say No Go is built on Hall and Oates's I Can't Go for That; and Me Myself and I rides Funkadelic's Knee Deep. The Turtles sued over an unlicensed fragment, a case that helped establish that every sample must be cleared.","Reviews were rapturous and the hippie label stuck anyway, to the group's lasting irritation. Its real legacy is procedural: the Turtles settlement changed the economics of sampling permanently, and the album itself was unavailable on streaming services until 2023 because the original clearances did not cover formats nobody had imagined. A generation therefore knew it only by reputation.","Let the skits play rather than skipping them; the album is designed as a broadcast. Start with The Magic Number and Eye Know. Then try tracing three or four samples to their sources and notice that almost none are funk records, which is precisely what made this sound new. Compare with Paul's Boutique, made the same year on the opposite coast."],"de-la-soul-de-la-soul-is-dead":["Longer, darker and more elaborate than the debut, with a framing device in which a group of kids find the album on cassette and mock it between tracks. Ring Ring Ring is an exasperated comedy about answering machines and demo tapes. Millie Pulled a Pistol on Santa tells a story of sexual abuse and revenge without a single joke. Prince Paul's production is denser, the humour is sourer, and the sleeve shows a broken flowerpot.","Two years of being called hippies, dressed in daisies by magazines, and expected to be cheerful had curdled. The album is partly an act of image destruction, made while the Native Tongues circle around them, A Tribe Called Quest, Jungle Brothers, Queen Latifah, was at its height. Sample clearance costs had risen sharply since the debut, which is audible in how much of the record is played rather than lifted.","The kids in the interludes eventually dismiss the tape and throw it away, which is the album's own review of itself. Millie Pulled a Pistol on Santa is narrated in the ordinary comic voice used everywhere else on the record, which is what makes the ending land so badly on a first hearing; nothing in the music warns you.","Critics admired it and many fans found it exhausting at seventy-four minutes. It has risen steadily in reputation since, particularly for its willingness to break its own franchise, and Millie is now widely cited as one of the first rap narratives about abuse told from a bystander's position. The debut is better loved; this one is more often called braver.","Do not treat it as a singles record. Play the first twenty minutes with the skits intact, then Millie Pulled a Pistol on Santa on its own, twice. Afterwards return to 3 Feet High and Rising and hear how the same three voices sound when they still expect to be liked."],"a-tribe-called-quest-peoples-instinctive-travels":["Loose, bright and wandering. Q-Tip's nasal, conversational voice carries most of it, with Phife Dawg appearing only occasionally. I Left My Wallet in El Segundo is a shaggy travel story; Bonita Applebum is a slow, blatant seduction over a soft psychedelic loop; Can I Kick It? rides a famous bassline and a call-and-response the group barely bother to vary. Nothing is hurried, and the drums sit low in the mix where other records put them front and centre.","Four friends from St. Albans, Queens, still teenagers, part of the Native Tongues circle around De La Soul and the Jungle Brothers. Their label had a hit-making department and no idea what to do with an album that contained no obvious single. Sampling was still cheap enough for a group with no budget to build a record from records, though not for much longer.","Can I Kick It? is built over the bassline of Lou Reed's Walk on the Wild Side; Reed is reported to have taken the entire publishing, so the group earned nothing from their best-known early song. Listen to how thin the drums are compared with the New York records around them, a deliberate softness that the next album would reverse completely.","It was received as charming and slight, an assessment that hardened once The Low End Theory appeared and made it look like a sketch. Critics have since come back to the looseness, hearing an alternative to hardness that no other group in 1990 was offering. Bonita Applebum has drawn later scrutiny for its lyrics, which is part of any honest account of it.","Take Bonita Applebum first for the texture, then Can I Kick It? for the bassline, then Lou Reed's original. Notice how much space the mix leaves at the bottom. Play The Low End Theory afterwards and hear a group deciding that bass is the whole point."],"a-tribe-called-quest-the-low-end-theory":["Almost nothing but bass, drums and two voices. Most tracks have three or four elements at most: an upright bass figure, a dry kick and snare, a fragment of horn, and Q-Tip and Phife Dawg trading verses in completely different timbres, one nasal and reflective, the other clipped and combative. Check the Rhime is a rooftop argument between friends. Scenario ends the record with a posse of guests and the most explosive verse on it.","The group rebuilt after a debut that had been liked rather than respected, with Phife stepping up from a guest to a full partner. Engineer Bob Power gave the record a clarity unusual for hip-hop then. Jazz was the deliberate reference point, not as decoration but as a claim of lineage, at a moment when rap's commercial centre was moving towards harder and louder productions.","Ron Carter, who played bass with Miles Davis's second great quintet, plays live on Verses from the Abstract; he agreed only after being assured the lyrics would be clean. The album's mixing is the real trick: by removing mid-range clutter the producers made two voices and a bass sound bigger than a wall of samples. Scenario's closing verse belongs to Busta Rhymes, then nineteen.","It was immediately and almost unanimously praised, and has stayed on best-of lists ever since. The jazz rap label it helped create was soon overused, applied to any record with a saxophone sample, and the group disliked it. What is still discussed is how much the album's clean low end shaped the sound of nineties New York production generally.","Listen on something with real bass; the record is unintelligible on a phone speaker. Start with Buggin' Out for the interplay between the two voices, then Verses from the Abstract for Ron Carter. Then play a Miles Davis quintet record from the sixties and hear where the group thought they were standing."],"a-tribe-called-quest-midnight-marauders":["Warmer and more tuneful than its predecessor, with a synthesized female voice introducing tracks like a museum audio guide and commenting on the tempo. Award Tour is a travelogue with a bright keyboard hook; Electric Relaxation is a slow, sly seduction over a soft electric piano loop; Oh My God brings in Busta Rhymes to shout across a stuttering beat. Phife is at his sharpest here, and the two voices are balanced as equals throughout.","Recorded as New York hip-hop entered its most competitive stretch, with Wu-Tang Clan's debut appearing the same month and Nas six months away. The group were now established enough to be a reference point rather than a novelty, and the sleeve, a grid of dozens of rappers' and DJs' faces, was an explicit claim about belonging to a lineage rather than standing outside it.","The linking voice is credited as a tour guide and gives the album's sampling rate and running commentary, a small joke about the record as a piece of equipment. Listen for how the two MCs hand over mid-line rather than at verse ends, a technique they had used sparingly before and now deploy constantly. Q-Tip produced most of it himself.","It sold better than anything they had made and is the album most often named as their best by listeners who came later, while critics tend to split between this and The Low End Theory. Its reputation has grown with the years, partly because the drums and keyboard textures became the default sound of warm East Coast production for a decade.","Play Award Tour and Electric Relaxation back to back for the two moods. Then listen to how Q-Tip and Phife divide a single verse between them. If you want to hear its children, put on a mid-nineties Pete Rock or J Dilla production and count the similarities."],"beastie-boys-licensed-to-ill":["Three nasal New York voices shouting over enormous drums and metal guitar. Rhymin and Stealin opens on a thunderous rock break with a pirate chant; Paul Revere runs a drum machine pattern backwards under a tall tale about the Wild West; No Sleep till Brooklyn is a heavy metal parody that works as heavy metal; Fight for Your Right is a beer commercial that became an anthem. The record is loud, juvenile and impeccably timed.","A New York hardcore punk band turned rap group, working with Rick Rubin, who liked both genres and heard no reason to separate them. Def Jam's distribution deal with Columbia gave it a reach no rap record had had. It became the first rap album to top the American album chart, sold to suburban teenagers in enormous quantities, and turned three art-school kids into a touring scandal.","The drums under Rhymin and Stealin are Led Zeppelin's When the Levee Breaks, and the riff behind them is Black Sabbath's Sweet Leaf; Kerry King of Slayer plays the solo on No Sleep till Brooklyn. Paul Revere's beat is a drum machine pattern recorded and then reversed, which is why it lurches rather than swings.","It was enormously successful and widely deplored, and the group's own later view was that the misogyny and the frat-party persona had been a joke that the audience took literally and that they had helped make real. Adam Yauch apologised in print years afterwards. Critics still disagree about whether the record's craft can be separated from what it licensed.","Play Paul Revere for the reversed beat and the storytelling, which is the best thing here. Then Rhymin and Stealin for the sheer size of the drums. Afterwards go to Paul's Boutique and hear the same three people three years later, having decided to be something else entirely."],"beastie-boys-pauls-boutique":["A record built almost entirely out of other records, hundreds of them, layered three and four deep so that a single bar may contain a drum break, a bassline, a guitar squeal and a half-second of dialogue from four different decades. The three voices trade lines in a constant stream of references. It closes with B-Boy Bouillabaisse, a suite of nine short pieces run together, which functions as a summary of everything the album has been doing.","The group had left Def Jam after a dispute, moved to Los Angeles and signed to Capitol, a label that expected another Licensed to Ill. They worked instead with the Dust Brothers, whose existing instrumental tracks were already dense collages the group rapped over rather than trimmed. Nobody was clearing much of anything in 1989, and the window in which this was affordable closed within a year.","The drums the group rap over on The Sounds of Science come from the Beatles, and several more Beatles fragments run through the record. Listen for how often a sample is used for a single hit or breath rather than a loop, so the texture keeps flickering. The album cover is a panoramic photograph of a real Manhattan street corner, printed as a wraparound so the shop of the title only appears when the sleeve is opened out.","It sold poorly, the label effectively abandoned it, and the group were written off. Within five years it was being called one of the great sample records; within twenty it was standard on best-album lists. It is also cited as an argument against sample clearance law, since a record of this density could not be licensed today at any realistic price.","Do not try to identify everything on a first listen; let the density be the experience. Start with Shake Your Rump, then Shadrach. Afterwards, one of the many published sample maps will double the record. If you know Berio's Sinfonia, play its quotation-stuffed third movement next and enjoy the family resemblance."],"slick-rick-the-great-adventures-of-slick-rick":["An English accent, several character voices, and a set of stories told in the third person with a moral at the end. Children's Story follows a robbery from bravado to a body in the street in three minutes, the narrator speaking as if reading to a child at bedtime. Mona Lisa is a comic pick-up scene. Hey Young World is an address to listeners younger than the narrator. The production is light and springy, and everything serves the telling.","Richard Walters was born in London and moved to the Bronx as a child, which is why he sounds like nobody else on a rap record. He had become famous in 1985 as MC Ricky D on a single with Doug E. Fresh before signing to Def Jam. Rap in 1988 was dominated by political declamation and technical display; a storyteller in gold chains and an eyepatch was a different proposition entirely.","He performs the whole cast: victim, robber, police, bystander, shifting register mid-line, which is why the stories work without any narration telling you who is speaking. Children's Story ends on the narrator's voice returning to the bedtime frame, so the violence is bracketed by a lullaby. The eyepatch covers an eye blinded in infancy by broken glass; it is not a costume choice.","It sold well and made him one of rap's most imitated voices, and Children's Story has been quoted, sampled and remade continuously since. His conviction and imprisonment in the early nineties, and a long immigration fight afterwards, interrupted the career at its peak, which has made the album feel like a complete statement by default. Critics now treat him as the form's foundational narrator.","Listen to Children's Story once for the story and once for the voices, counting the characters. Then Mona Lisa for the comedy. If you want to hear where this belongs in a longer tradition, play a narrative ballad like Schubert's Erlkonig, where one voice also plays every part in a chase that ends badly."],"slick-rick-la-di-da-di":["One voice rapping and one mouth making the drums. There is no band, no sampler and no turntable: Doug E. Fresh supplies the rhythm entirely with his mouth while Slick Rick, then billed as MC Ricky D, narrates a morning in the life of a young man who wakes, bathes, dresses, and is pursued down the street by a woman and her mother. It is funny, unhurried, and five minutes long with no chorus.","Released as the flip side of The Show on a small New York label, and an instant street hit. Human beatboxing was a live technique born of not owning equipment, and by 1985 it was a recognised discipline with its own stars. The pairing of a London-raised storyteller with the most celebrated beatboxer in New York was arranged by their label rather than by long friendship.","Listen to how Doug E. Fresh's beat changes to follow the story, dropping out for a punchline and thickening when the chase starts; the drum part is a performance, not a loop. Rick's use of a sung fragment as a refrain, and his habit of voicing the women's dialogue in a different register, both become standard practice in rap storytelling afterwards.","It is among the most sampled, quoted and directly recreated records in hip-hop, borrowed in full by later artists and used as the basis for hit singles. Critics at the time treated it as a novelty because of the beatboxing. Later writing has taken it more seriously, as the record where rap narrative and rap comedy arrive fully formed with no production to lean on.","Listen once for the story and once for the mouth drums alone, ignoring the words. Then find Snoop Dogg's version from 1993, which keeps the structure and changes almost nothing else, to hear how completely later rappers absorbed it. Any decent remaster is fine; the original was recorded cheaply and sounds it."],"dr-dre-the-chronic":["Dr. Dre's first solo album is an hour of slow, rolling grooves built from seventies funk, rebuilt rather than simply looped: session players replay bass lines and keyboard parts, and a high, whining Moog lead floats over the top like a siren heard from a car window. A young Snoop Doggy Dogg is on most of the tracks, his drawl as much a part of the sound as the synthesizer. Skits and interludes stitch the songs into a single long ride through Los Angeles.","Dre had left N.W.A and Ruthless Records in a bitter contract dispute and co-founded Death Row Records with Suge Knight; no major label would touch the venture until Interscope agreed to distribute it. The album appeared in December 1992, months after the Los Angeles riots that followed the Rodney King verdict, and it carried the city's anger and its hedonism side by side. Several tracks are aimed squarely at Dre's former partner Eazy-E.","Listen to how little is actually sampled. Where the East Coast chopped records, Dre hired musicians to replay Parliament-Funkadelic and Leon Haywood figures, which gave him cleaner bass, room to change keys and fewer legal headaches. The chanted hook of 'Let Me Ride' is an interpolation of Parliament's 'Mothership Connection (Star Child)'. The portamento synth line, not the drums, is the true signature: it recurs across the record like a leitmotif.","The Chronic sold millions, won Dre a Grammy for 'Let Me Ride', and moved hip-hop's commercial centre from New York to California for several years. Critics praised its sound almost without reservation; its content, heavy with guns, weed and contempt for women, drew the same objections that followed gangsta rap generally. Later writers treat it as the founding document of G-funk and as a studio achievement that producers still study.","Start with 'Nuthin' but a 'G' Thang' and 'Let Me Ride' back to back and listen to the bass, which sits lower and rounder than anything on an East Coast record of the same year. Then follow the Moog whine through the album. Play it loud in a car if you can: it was mixed for exactly that. Afterwards try Herbie Hancock's Head Hunters for the synth-funk ancestry."],"dr-dre-nuthin-but-a-g-thang":["A four-minute duet in which Dr. Dre and Snoop Doggy Dogg trade verses over an unhurried, sunlit groove: a lazy electric-piano figure, a deep bass, handclaps and that high synthesizer whistle. Snoop's voice arrives first, conversational and sly, almost sung, and Dre answers in a flatter, harder tone. There is no dramatic build; the song simply rolls, which is the point. It is a party record about cruising through a Los Angeles afternoon.","Released as the lead single from The Chronic in late 1992, it introduced most listeners to Snoop, then an unknown from Long Beach. Its video, a backyard barbecue in South Central, sold a vision of the neighbourhood that had been on the television news for riot footage a few months earlier. The single became a top-ten pop hit and carried G-funk onto radio stations that had never played West Coast rap.","The groove is built on Leon Haywood's 1975 soul record 'I Want'a Do Something Freaky to You', slowed and smoothed until the original's lounge-soul keyboard sounds like it is melting in the heat. Listen for how Snoop places his syllables behind the beat, a fraction late, which makes everything sound relaxed even when the words are not. That rhythmic placement became as influential as the production.","It was an immediate hit and has never really left rotation; critics' lists of the greatest hip-hop singles almost always include it near the top. The debate it prompted was less about the music than about what its easygoing tone normalised. Musicians tend to single out the chemistry between the two voices, the rapper as bandleader and the protégé as soloist.","Listen once for Snoop alone: the pitch of his voice rises and falls almost like a melody. Then listen for the bass, which never hurries. Compare Grandmaster Flash's 'The Message' to hear how much the sound of a street record changed in a decade, and follow it with Snoop's own Doggystyle."],"dr-dre-2001":["Dre's second solo album trades the lazy funk of The Chronic for something colder and more monumental: stark piano riffs, orchestral stabs, tight live bass and drums so crisp they sound carved. 'Still D.R.E.' rests on a three-note piano figure that everyone recognises in two seconds; 'The Next Episode' swings on a spy-film guitar and horn; 'Forgot About Dre' is a rapid-fire showcase for Eminem. Guests carry much of the rapping while Dre acts as the architect.","By 1999 Dre had left Death Row, founded Aftermath, and seen a first compilation flop badly enough that critics wondered whether he was finished. He had also just signed and produced Eminem. 2001 was his answer, recorded with a team of co-producers and keyboard players at a time when digital recording was replacing tape, which helped give the album its unusually clean, separated sound.","'The Next Episode' is built on David McCallum's 1967 instrumental 'The Edge', a piece of orchestral library-funk by the actor best known from The Man from U.N.C.L.E. Listen too for how much of the album is played rather than sampled, with bass and keyboards recorded live and then quantised. Jay-Z helped write Dre's verses on 'Still D.R.E.', a fact that was barely hidden at the time.","The album sold enormously and re-established Dre as the most sought-after producer in rap, a position he held for the next decade. Reviewers praised the sound and were cooler about the words, much of it ghostwritten and often crude. Its piano-led minimalism shaped much commercial rap of the early 2000s, from 50 Cent to Eminem's own records.","Put on 'Still D.R.E.' and count how few elements there are: piano, drums, bass, voice. That restraint is the style. Then hear 'The Next Episode' for the swagger, and compare both with The Chronic to hear how the same producer moved from warm to steely in seven years."],"snoop-dogg-doggystyle":["Snoop Doggy Dogg's debut, produced by Dr. Dre, takes The Chronic's sound and makes it looser, sleepier and more playful. The grooves are soft Parliament-style funk, heavy on bass and handclaps, with sung hooks from the Death Row family. 'Gin and Juice' is a lazy house-party anthem, 'Who Am I (What's My Name)?' a self-introduction over a funk chant, and 'Lodi Dodi' a loving remake of an old New York story rap. Snoop's voice, languid and nasal, holds it together.","The album came out in November 1993 amid enormous anticipation and entered the album chart at number one, unheard of for a debut rap record then. Snoop was simultaneously facing a murder charge over a shooting in Los Angeles, of which he was acquitted in 1996, and the case hung over the album's reception. Death Row was at its peak of commercial power and notoriety.","'Lodi Dodi' is a near line-for-line remake of Slick Rick's 1985 'La Di Da Di', with Snoop adjusting the story to his own world: an unusual act of homage in a genre that prized originality. 'Who Am I (What's My Name)?' borrows the chant and bass of George Clinton's 'Atomic Dog', and the whole album's sense of humour owes much to Clinton's cartoon funk.","Reviewers loved Snoop's voice and found the music, sometimes, too close to The Chronic. Its misogyny was criticised then and is harder to ignore now. Yet the record remains one of the best-selling rap debuts and is usually ranked alongside The Chronic as the core of G-funk. Snoop's relaxed delivery influenced a generation of rappers who realised you did not have to shout.","Start with 'Gin and Juice' and 'Lodi Dodi', then play Slick Rick's 'La Di Da Di' right after to hear how a Long Beach teenager reworked a Bronx classic. Listen for the hooks sung by guest vocalists: this album brought soul singing back into gangsta rap."],"2pac-me-against-the-world":["2Pac's third album is his most inward: an hour of slow, mournful soul-based beats over which he talks about fear, grief, depression, poverty and the likelihood of an early death. There are party moments, but the tone is set by 'So Many Tears', 'If I Die 2Nite' and 'Dear Mama', a tribute to his mother. His voice is urgent and hoarse, pushing slightly ahead of the beat, as if he were short of time.","Recorded in 1994, a year in which 2Pac was shot five times in a New York studio lobby and convicted on a sexual abuse charge, the album was released in March 1995 while he was in prison. It went to number one on the album chart, the first time an artist had topped it while incarcerated. His paranoia about who had set up the shooting would soon fuel his feud with the Notorious B.I.G.","Listen to how often the beats reach for gospel and quiet-storm soul rather than funk: the producers were building a church-like frame around a confession. The title track's theme of isolation is answered, deliberately, by 'Dear Mama' and 'Temptations', where he turns outward to family and desire. The album is sequenced as a mood cycle rather than a set of singles.","Critics at the time called it his best and most mature record, and many still do, preferring it to the louder All Eyez on Me. It is often cited as the moment gangsta rap admitted to its own despair. Debate continues over how much its introspection was art and how much a man in crisis, and over whether the two can be separated.","Play it from the start, in order, preferably at night. Notice how the tempo rarely rises and how often a sung chorus relieves the rapping. Then compare Marvin Gaye's What's Going On, another soul record about fear and faith that turns private grief into public testimony."],"2pac-dear-mama":["A thank-you letter from a son to his mother, told in three verses over a warm, slow soul groove with a sung refrain. 2Pac describes growing up poor with a single parent, his own adolescent anger, her addiction and her recovery, and his gratitude, without softening any of it. The music is gentle, glowing electric piano and a sighing chorus, which makes the frankness of the words all the more striking.","His mother, Afeni Shakur, had been a member of the Black Panther Party and was acquitted in the Panther 21 trial while pregnant with him. The single came out early in 1995, just before Me Against the World, while 2Pac was about to go to prison, and it became one of the biggest hits of his career. It gave a public that knew him from headlines a very different picture.","The groove borrows from Joe Sample's 'In All My Wildest Dreams', a jazz-soul piano piece, and the Spinners' 'Sadie', itself a soul song addressed to a mother. So the sample is a quiet quotation: a son's tribute built on an older tribute. Listen too for how the verses move chronologically, from childhood to the present, like a short memoir.","It was widely praised on release, even by critics hostile to 2Pac's other work, and in 2010 the Library of Congress added it to the National Recording Registry, a rare honour for a rap single. Some listeners find it sentimental; most hear it as a model of how hip-hop could handle family love and damage in the same breath.","Listen for the moment each verse turns from the hard fact to the gratitude. Then play the Spinners' 'Sadie' to hear the source, and the Temptations' 'Papa Was a Rollin' Stone', a darker soul song about a parent, as a companion piece."],"2pac-california-love":["A celebration of the state, not a love song: a booming piano riff, a stomping beat, and Roger Troutman's robotic talk-box voice singing the title hook. Dr. Dre opens with a verse and 2Pac follows with the victory lap. It is loud, blunt and joyful, built for stadiums and car stereos rather than close listening, and one of the most recognisable openings in nineties pop.","This was 2Pac's first single after Suge Knight posted his bail and signed him to Death Row in late 1995, and it announced the label's new star. Its video, directed by Hype Williams, staged the two rappers in a post-apocalyptic desert modelled on Mad Max. The feud between Death Row and New York's Bad Boy Records was intensifying, and the song's West Coast pride was read as a salvo.","The piano figure comes from Joe Cocker's 1972 'Woman to Woman'. The talk-box voice belongs to Roger Troutman of Zapp, whose electro-funk records were a crucial ingredient in G-funk; bringing him in was Dre's way of acknowledging the debt. Two versions exist: the original single and a darker remix on All Eyez on Me.","It topped the Billboard Hot 100 in 1996 and was nominated for Grammys; it is now a staple of California sporting events and a regular entry on best-of-decade lists. Critics tend to rate it as a thrilling record rather than a deep one. It marks the moment G-funk became mainstream pop music.","Wait for the talk-box and notice how it turns a human voice into a synthesizer, the very sound that links Zapp's funk to Kraftwerk's robots. Compare the single mix with the album remix, then hear Afrika Bambaataa's 'Planet Rock' for the electro strand in the family tree."],"2pac-all-eyez-on-me":["A double album of more than two hours, recorded at speed after 2Pac's release from prison. It swings between party records such as '2 of Amerikaz Most Wanted' with Snoop Doggy Dogg, the wistful 'I Ain't Mad at Cha', the triumphant 'Ambitionz az a Ridah' and many tracks of defiance aimed at enemies real and imagined. The production, by Dr. Dre, Johnny J, Daz Dillinger and others, is glossy G-funk throughout.","Released in February 1996 as one of the first double albums of solo rap, it fulfilled much of 2Pac's Death Row contract in one go. He recorded compulsively, sometimes several songs a night. By September he had been shot in Las Vegas and died six days later, at 25, which turned the album into a last will almost overnight. The East–West rivalry was at its most toxic.","'I Ain't Mad at Cha' was recorded, by most accounts, on the very day he left prison, and its video, released after his death, shows him as a ghost looking down on his friends. Listen to how many tracks imagine his own funeral or legacy: the premonitions on this album are not hindsight, they were already there.","Commercially enormous and certified diamond, it was received as a blockbuster rather than a masterwork: critics admired the energy and grumbled about length and repetition. Opinion has moved in its favour, partly through nostalgia, partly because its best tracks have lasted. Arguments continue over whether Me Against the World or this is his defining statement.","Do not attempt it in one sitting at first. Start with 'Ambitionz az a Ridah', 'I Ain't Mad at Cha' and '2 of Amerikaz Most Wanted', three different 2Paks. Then play Me Against the World to hear the private man before the public persona took over."],"notorious-big-ready-to-die":["Biggie Smalls's debut is structured as a life story: an opening skit takes him from birth through childhood to prison release, the album passes through crime stories, bedroom comedy and radio hits, and it ends with a suicide note set to music. In between are 'Juicy', 'Big Poppa', 'Gimme the Loot' and 'Things Done Changed'. His voice, rich and unhurried, glides over the beats with conversational ease and an effortless sense of rhythm.","Christopher Wallace grew up in Clinton Hill and Bedford-Stuyvesant, Brooklyn, sold drugs as a teenager and was signed by Sean 'Puffy' Combs, first at Uptown Records and then at Bad Boy, the label Combs founded. Released in September 1994, the album pulled New York rap back toward the charts at a moment when G-funk dominated. Producers included Easy Mo Bee, DJ Premier and Combs's own team.","The album is split between two philosophies. Hard, dusty tracks like 'Gimme the Loot', where Biggie voices two characters by changing his pitch, sit next to glossy hits built on well-known R&B: 'Big Poppa' is set on the Isley Brothers' 'Between the Sheets'. Combs pushed for the hits; the tension between street record and pop record is audible, and it works.","Critics recognised immediately that Biggie was an exceptional technician and storyteller, and the album is routinely ranked among the best in hip-hop. The darker tracks were criticised for their violence; the closing track's portrait of depression was, for its time, remarkably frank. After his murder in 1997 the album's title and ending took on a meaning that has shaped every listening since.","Listen to 'Gimme the Loot' for the two-voice trick, then 'Juicy' for the rags-to-riches arc, then 'Everyday Struggle' for the weariness underneath. Compare Slick Rick, whose storytelling and character voices Biggie clearly admired. Then hear it as a whole, from the birth skit to the final track, because the sequencing is the story: a life laid out in order, with its ending announced in the title."],"notorious-big-juicy":["A rags-to-riches autobiography in three verses: the child who read rap magazines and taped radio shows, the young man hustling to pay the rent, the star who can now buy his mother a house. The groove is bright and gliding, a sampled early-eighties R&B keyboard line with a sung chorus. Biggie delivers it with a grin, dedicating the song at the start to everyone who doubted him.","Released in August 1994 as the lead single from Ready to Die, it was Sean Combs's calculated attempt to put a hardcore Brooklyn rapper on pop radio, and it worked. The song is also a love letter to hip-hop's own history, name-checking the magazines and pioneers of Biggie's childhood. It became the anthem of a new, upwardly mobile era in rap.","The sample is Mtume's 1983 hit 'Juicy Fruit', a slow electro-soul song by a band led by James Mtume, who had played percussion with Miles Davis in the electric period. So a jazz lineage runs underneath one of rap's biggest pop moments. Listen for Biggie's internal rhymes, which chain several sounds within a single line.","It was a hit and has become one of the most loved rap songs of all: frequently ranked at or near the top of lists of the best hip-hop singles, and heard at graduations and weddings. Some purists at the time thought it too polished; that argument has long since lost. Its narrative arc became a standard hip-hop form.","Follow the three verses as three chapters. Then play Mtume's 'Juicy Fruit' and hear how little was changed, and how much the new words transform it. Pair it with 2Pac's 'Dear Mama', released months later, for the two coasts' great songs of gratitude."],"notorious-big-life-after-death":["A sprawling double album that shows how wide Biggie's range had become: the pop-disco of 'Mo Money Mo Problems', the swaggering 'Hypnotize', the hard New York of 'Kick in the Door', the Midwestern double-time flow borrowed for 'Notorious Thugs' with Bone Thugs-n-Harmony, and West Coast gestures on 'Going Back to Cali'. Between them come crime narratives told with a novelist's control. It opens where Ready to Die ended.","Wallace was shot dead in Los Angeles on 9 March 1997; the album, already finished and titled, came out sixteen days later. Its release in the aftermath of both his and 2Pac's murders made it a national event. It went to number one and sold in huge numbers. Combs's Bad Boy was then the dominant commercial force in rap. Its title, chosen before his death, now reads as grim prophecy.","'Hypnotize' is built on Herb Alpert's 1979 instrumental 'Rise', and 'Mo Money Mo Problems' on Diana Ross's 'I'm Coming Out': proof of Combs's taste for recognisable hits. Listen to 'Notorious Thugs' for the real feat, as Biggie switches from his usual slow glide to the rapid, sung triplet flow of his Cleveland guests without losing clarity.","Reviewers praised the virtuosity and complained about length and filler, a pattern with double albums. Its reputation has grown, particularly for tracks like 'Somebody's Gotta Die' and 'I Got a Story to Tell', which show a storyteller at full stretch. Debate over whether it or Ready to Die is the greater record has no settled answer.","Try 'Hypnotize', 'Notorious Thugs' and 'I Got a Story to Tell' first: three different skills. Then listen to 'Kick in the Door', produced by DJ Premier, to hear him on classic New York ground. Compare 2Pac's All Eyez on Me, the other coast's double album."],"nas-illmatic":["Ten tracks in forty minutes, with no filler: a portrait of the Queensbridge housing projects in Queens by a twenty-year-old poet. Nas's rhymes are dense with internal rhyme and precise images, delivered in a cool, almost detached voice. The beats, by DJ Premier, Pete Rock, Large Professor, Q-Tip and L.E.S., are built from jazz piano and soul fragments over hard, dusty drums. 'N.Y. State of Mind', 'The World Is Yours' and 'Life's a Bitch' are the core.","Nas had been a prodigy since a guest verse on Main Source's 1991 'Live at the Barbeque'; Columbia signed him and assembled the best producers in New York for his debut. Released in April 1994, it earned a perfect rating from The Source. It sold modestly at first, overshadowed by West Coast and Wu-Tang success, but its reputation among rappers was immediate.","Nas's father, the jazz cornettist Olu Dara, plays the melancholy solo at the end of 'Life's a Bitch', a direct link between the album and the jazz it samples. The opening track uses audio from the 1983 film Wild Style, planting Illmatic in hip-hop's own origins. Listen to how each producer's style shifts the mood while Nas's voice stays constant.","Almost immediately it became the benchmark: the record that critics, rappers and fans cite as the perfect hip-hop album, and it is usually ranked at or near the top of lists of the genre's greatest. The only real debate is whether its perfection trapped Nas, whose later records were always measured against it. Few debuts have been dissected so closely, line by line, by later rappers.","Listen straight through; forty minutes is short. Start by following just the words on 'N.Y. State of Mind', then just the drums. Compare Eric B. and Rakim's Paid in Full, the model for Nas's style, and then try Ahmad Jamal, whose piano underpins 'The World Is Yours'."],"nas-the-world-is-yours":["The most hopeful song on Illmatic. Over a looped, rippling jazz piano phrase and hard drums, Nas reflects on ambition, the pressures of the projects and the possibility of rising above them, answering the idea of the title with a question about who the world belongs to. The chorus is a call and response. It is reflective rather than boastful, and the piano gives it a sunlit, wistful glow.","The title comes from the 1983 film Scarface, whose rise-and-fall gangster story was an obsession of nineties rap. Produced by Pete Rock, one of the great crate-diggers of the era, it was released as a single from Illmatic in 1994. Pete Rock's own records with CL Smooth had already shown how jazz and soul fragments could carry emotional weight in hip-hop.","The piano loop comes from Ahmad Jamal's 'I Love Music', a 1970 live performance, lifted from the introduction where Jamal is spinning out a free, rhapsodic figure. Pete Rock trims it to a few bars that seem to breathe. Listen for how the loop's slight irregularity, a live pianist's rubato, makes the track feel less mechanical than most sampled music of the time.","It was not a big hit, but it is among the most admired tracks on the album and among Pete Rock's finest productions. Critics often use it to explain why Illmatic is loved: its mix of hardness and tenderness. It is regularly cited in discussions of jazz's second life inside hip-hop.","Listen once only to the piano: it never quite repeats identically. Then find Ahmad Jamal's live recordings, starting with 'Poinciana', to hear the pianist whose space and restraint made him Miles Davis's favourite and, twenty years later, a producer's treasure."],"wu-tang-clan-enter-the-wu-tang":["Nine rappers from Staten Island on one raw, murky record produced by RZA. The beats sound as though they were made in a basement, and they were: grimy drums, out-of-tune piano loops, soul fragments and snatches of dialogue from kung fu films. Each member has a distinct voice, from Method Man's smoky charm to Ol' Dirty Bastard's howling eccentricity to GZA's precise cool. 'Protect Ya Neck', 'C.R.E.A.M.' and 'Da Mystery of Chessboxin'' are the landmarks.","RZA assembled the group in 1992 with a five-year plan: the collective would make its name, then each member would sign solo deals with different labels while remaining in the group. The independent single 'Protect Ya Neck' led to a deal with Loud Records, and the album appeared in November 1993. Its low-budget rawness stood against the polished sound of Los Angeles.","The kung fu dialogue comes largely from the 1983 Hong Kong film Shaolin and Wu Tang; the group's whole mythology, of chambers, disciplines and rival schools, is borrowed from those films and from the teachings of the Five Percent Nation. Listen for RZA's detuned samples: he often let loops drift slightly out of pitch, creating the album's queasy, haunted feel.","Initial sales were steady rather than spectacular, but its influence was immense: it revived the gritty New York sound, launched a string of classic solo albums, and made the business model of the collective famous. It regularly appears near the top of lists of hip-hop's greatest albums. Critics now treat RZA as one of the genre's great auteurs.","Start with 'Protect Ya Neck' and try to tell the nine voices apart; learning them is half the pleasure. Then 'C.R.E.A.M.' for the soul side. Afterwards, the solo albums: GZA's Liquid Swords and Raekwon's Only Built 4 Cuban Linx. Listen on headphones for the hiss and grit left deliberately in the mix."],"wu-tang-clan-cream":["Two long verses of memory and a chorus that became a slogan. Raekwon recalls a teenage life of petty crime and poverty, Inspectah Deck a youth that led to prison and the lessons drawn from it, and Method Man delivers the hook about money ruling everything. The beat is a slow, melancholy soul piano loop with a mournful sung fragment, under hard drums. It sounds like a lament, not a boast.","The title is an acronym about cash, and the song was released as a single in early 1994. Where much rap of the time celebrated money, Wu-Tang presented it as a trap that shaped every choice in the Park Hill and Stapleton projects of Staten Island. The song was widely heard as a sober account of the crack era's economics.","The piano and wordless vocal come from the Charmels' 'As Long as I've Got You', a little-known 1967 Stax soul record. RZA loops only a few bars, pitch-shifted, so the source sounds like a ghost. Listen to how different the two verses are in pace: Raekwon storytelling, Inspectah Deck compressing more syllables into each line.","It is one of the most admired singles in hip-hop, often ranked among the best of the decade, and its acronym entered everyday speech. Critics note that its strength lies in its restraint: there is no triumph at the end. It is frequently used to illustrate the social realism of nineties New York rap.","Listen for the piano first; it never changes, and the song's sadness lives there. Then concentrate on Inspectah Deck's verse, often called the finest on the track. Compare Grandmaster Flash's 'The Message', the first great rap song about the projects."],"gza-liquid-swords":["GZA's second album, produced by RZA, is the darkest and most austere of the Wu-Tang solo records: slow, heavy beats, minor-key organ and piano, eerie synthesizer drones and swathes of samurai-film dialogue. GZA raps in a calm, measured voice, a chess player rather than a brawler, with dense metaphor and wordplay. 'Liquid Swords', 'Cold World', 'Shadowboxin'' and '4th Chamber' are high points; nearly every Clan member appears.","GZA, the oldest member of the Clan, had recorded a failed debut for Cold Chillin' in 1991. Released on Geffen in November 1995 under the collective's solo-deal strategy, this album came in the same run as Method Man's, Ol' Dirty Bastard's and Raekwon's solo debuts. The period was the peak of Wu-Tang's creative and commercial power. RZA was producing several of these albums at once, yet each has its own colour.","The long opening speech and other dialogue come from Shogun Assassin, a 1980 English-language re-edit of the Japanese Lone Wolf and Cub films, and its story of a father and son wandering through violence frames the album. The cover, by comic artist Denys Cowan, shows a chess game played with swords. Listen for the chess metaphors, which run through the lyrics like a hidden structure.","Reviews were strong and its standing has only risen; many fans and critics rank it as the best Wu-Tang solo album and among the best hip-hop records of the decade. GZA's reputation as the Clan's most cerebral lyricist rests on it. Some listeners find it forbidding; its admirers hear that as the point.","Play it at night and let the film dialogue set the scene. Focus on RZA's use of silence and space. Then compare Raekwon's Only Built 4 Cuban Linx from the same year: one record wintry and philosophical, the other a crime film."],"raekwon-only-built-4-cuban-linx":["A crime film in album form. Raekwon and his partner Ghostface Killah, effectively co-lead, tell interlocking stories of dealing, betrayal and the dream of getting out, with cinematic skits and RZA beats that use lush soul strings and orchestral samples more than on any earlier Wu-Tang record. The language is a thick, coded slang. 'Criminology', 'Incarcerated Scarfaces', 'Ice Cream' and 'Guillotine (Swordz)' are among the landmarks.","Released in August 1995 on the original purple cassette that gave the album its nickname, it arrived as the Wu-Tang solo wave peaked. Its gangster-cinema imagery, drawn from Scarface, The Godfather and Hong Kong action films, helped launch the so-called mafioso wave in New York rap, with Nas, Jay-Z and others soon adopting similar personas. RZA produced every track himself.","Listen to how often the strings and horns sound like soundtrack music, not funk; RZA was building a score as much as a beat. The album's slang, full of Five Percent vocabulary and invented terms, was difficult even for American listeners, and part of the pleasure is decoding it. Ghostface's abstract, stream-of-consciousness verses would soon grow into his own solo style.","Hailed on release and now consistently ranked among the finest rap albums, it is often called the pinnacle of the Wu-Tang solo catalogue. Its influence on the mafioso style of the later nineties was enormous. Critics debate whether that influence was entirely healthy, since imitators took the glamour without the storytelling.","Listen to 'Criminology' and 'Ice Cream' first, then play the album through as if watching a film. Compare Curtis Mayfield's Super Fly and Isaac Hayes's Shaft, the soul soundtracks behind the crime-cinema imagination this record inherited. Notice how the skits work like scenes between songs, and how often Ghostface steals the spotlight."],"mobb-deep-the-infamous":["Queensbridge seen from street level by two teenagers: Prodigy and Havoc rap about survival, paranoia and violence in flat, cold voices, with none of the glamour of the mafioso records. The beats, mostly by Havoc, are sparse and frightening: detuned piano, eerie jazz fragments, heavy drums with space around them. 'Shook Ones (Part II)', 'Survival of the Fittest' and 'Eye for an Eye' are the core; Nas, Raekwon and Ghostface guest.","Mobb Deep's first album had failed; released in April 1995, this second one, recorded when the two were barely twenty, made them the voice of Queensbridge after Nas. Q-Tip of A Tribe Called Quest helped with production and mixing. The mid-nineties were a period of high crime in New York, and the record documents it without editorialising. Its bleakness was a direct response to what the two saw daily.","Havoc often built loops from obscure jazz records, pitched down until they sound almost unrecognisable: the album's chill comes from that deliberate degradation. Listen to the relationship between the two rappers: Prodigy vivid and fatalistic, Havoc blunter. Prodigy lived with sickle-cell disease his whole life, and illness and mortality shadow his verses.","It was an instant classic among New York listeners and is now counted among the definitive East Coast records of the decade. Some critics found it bleak to the point of nihilism; others heard it as the most honest reportage of the period. Its influence on hardcore rap production is considerable.","Start with 'Shook Ones (Part II)' and 'Survival of the Fittest', and notice how much of the menace comes from what is missing, not what is there. Then compare Illmatic: the same projects, the same year, a far different mood."],"mobb-deep-shook-ones-part-ii":["Five minutes of dread. A smeared, dissonant piano phrase and a low hum loop under hard drums, while Prodigy and Havoc warn off pretenders who talk tough but would not survive the real thing. The famous opening verse by Prodigy is delivered almost quietly, which makes it more threatening. The beat is one of the most recognisable in hip-hop and has been reused endlessly.","A remake of a less successful single, 'Part II' was released early in 1995 and became the anthem of The Infamous. In 2002 it reached a much wider audience when it played during the final rap battle of the film 8 Mile. The song's picture of fear masking itself as toughness spoke to a New York still gripped by crime and heavy policing.","The piano comes from Herbie Hancock's 'Jessica', a gentle ballad, pitched down and cut so that its consonances turn sinister. It is a striking case of a sampler changing a piece's emotional meaning entirely. Listen for the faint hiss of the original vinyl under the loop. The low hum under the piano is part of the menace.","Widely ranked among the greatest hip-hop singles, it is often cited by producers as the pinnacle of the dark East Coast style. Its lyrics are violent, and critics disagree about whether they describe or glamorise. Its beat has been rapped over, freestyled over and battled over so often that it functions as a kind of folk standard.","Listen once to the beat alone, then find Hancock's 'Jessica' and marvel at the distance travelled. Then compare Hancock's own Maiden Voyage, whose modal harmonies share the same haunted spaciousness in a gentler key. Notice how quiet Prodigy stays: menace without volume."],"outkast-southernplayalisticadillacmuzik":["OutKast's debut sounds like nothing from either coast: slow, humid, played-live Southern funk with wah-wah guitar, organ, live bass and loose drums, the Organized Noize production team treating hip-hop like a soul band would. André and Big Boi, still teenagers, trade verses in thick Atlanta accents about Cadillacs, weekends and the Georgia police. 'Player's Ball' and 'Git Up, Git Out' mark the range from party to sermon.","Signed to LaFace, the Atlanta label of L.A. Reid and Babyface, the duo came out of a collective known as the Dungeon Family, which recorded in a basement studio. The album appeared in April 1994. At the 1995 Source Awards in New York, amid boos when the duo won best new group, André declared that the South had something to say, a moment now seen as a turning point.","Listen for how little of the music is sampled: Organized Noize hired live players, giving the record the loose feel of Southern soul from Stax and Hi rather than the clipped loops of New York. 'Git Up, Git Out', featuring Cee-Lo Green, is a sermon about ambition and idleness that shows the moralist side of André long before his later experiments.","It sold well and was received warmly but with some condescension from East Coast media, which treated Southern rap as a regional curiosity. Its standing has risen steadily, and it is now counted as the founding album of a Southern hip-hop mainstream that eventually overtook both coasts. André's Source Awards speech is now quoted as prophecy.","Listen for the live band, especially the bass and organ. Then play Al Green or Curtis Mayfield to hear where the warmth comes from, and follow with Aquemini to hear how far the duo travelled in four years. Notice the accents too: nobody on either coast sounded like this."],"outkast-aquemini":["OutKast's third album is a sprawling, adventurous record that blends funk, gospel, blues, psychedelia and electro with rapping of great virtuosity. 'Rosa Parks' stops for a stomping harmonica breakdown; 'SpottieOttieDopaliscious' is a slow horn-driven reverie with spoken narration; 'Da Art of Storytellin'' tells two tender, tragic stories; 'Liberation' is an eight-minute spiritual. The contrast between André's experimental impulses and Big Boi's grounded flow drives it.","Released in September 1998, it followed ATLiens, where the duo had begun producing themselves. The title combines their star signs, Aquarius and Gemini. By now Southern rap was selling heavily, but OutKast were treated as art-rap outliers. Rosa Parks sued over the use of her name in the single, a case eventually settled in 2005. The duo now produced much of their music themselves.","The harmonica breakdown in 'Rosa Parks' is a deliberate nod to country blues and church revival, sounds rarely heard in rap. 'SpottieOttieDopaliscious' is built on a slow, drunken horn line and a spoken, almost Southern-Gothic narrative about a night in a club. Listen for Raekwon on 'Skew It on the Bar-B': an East–West–South truce in miniature.","Critics received it as a classic almost at once, and The Source gave it five mics. It has come to be regarded as one of the greatest hip-hop albums and proof that Southern rap could be both popular and experimental. Some fans still prefer its mix of adventure and grounding to the wilder Stankonia.","Start with 'Rosa Parks' for joy, then 'SpottieOttieDopaliscious' for mood, then 'Da Art of Storytellin' (Pt. 1)' for narrative. Compare Stevie Wonder's Innervisions for another Black American album that treats a record as a whole world. Give it time: the long tracks reward patience."],"outkast-stankonia":["OutKast's most explosive record. 'B.O.B.' hurtles at drum-and-bass tempo with distorted guitar, a gospel choir and rapid-fire verses; 'Ms. Jackson' is a gentle, rueful apology to the mother of an ex-partner; 'So Fresh, So Clean' is smooth funk. Elsewhere there is psychedelic rock, slow jams and electro. The duo produced most of it themselves with Earthtone III, and the energy is restless and playful.","Released on 31 October 2000, the album followed the duo's buildup of their own Atlanta studio, Stankonia. 'B.O.B.' came out before the September 2001 attacks but became tied to the Iraq wars by its title's reference to Baghdad. The album won the 2002 Grammy for best rap album, and 'Ms. Jackson' became a huge crossover hit. It was their commercial peak until then.","'B.O.B.' runs at roughly 155 beats per minute, borrowing the tempo of British drum and bass, a genre almost unknown in American rap. Listen at the end for the gospel choir and the wailing guitar solo, pulling the song from rave to church to Hendrix in a minute. Listen too for how 'Ms. Jackson' sets an apology over a lullaby-soft groove, a rare tenderness in rap about breakups.","Critics loved it; it topped many year-end polls, and 'B.O.B.' is often ranked among the finest songs of the decade. Its genre-blurring prefigured the duo's split into two solo discs on Speakerboxxx/The Love Below. Some fans felt it traded Aquemini's coherence for spectacle; that is the main dissent. Its reputation has only grown since.","Play 'B.O.B.' loud, then 'Ms. Jackson' right after, and marvel that the same duo made both. Then go back to Afrika Bambaataa's 'Planet Rock' to hear the electro roots of their restlessness. Notice how the duo treat genre as a palette rather than a boundary, then compare Missy Elliott's 'Get Ur Freak On'."],"missy-elliott-supa-dupa-fly":["Missy Elliott's debut, produced with her childhood friend Timbaland, sounds like it arrived from the future: stuttering, syncopated drum programming, gurgling synthesizers, odd vocal ad-libs and huge amounts of empty space. Missy moves between rapping and singing, playful and warm. 'The Rain (Supa Dupa Fly)', 'Sock It 2 Me' and 'Beep Me 911' are the landmarks, with guests including Aaliyah, Busta Rhymes and Lil' Kim.","Missy and Timbaland, both from Virginia, had already written and produced for Aaliyah and Ginuwine, creating the lopsided R&B that dominated the late nineties. The album came out in July 1997 on Missy's own Elektra imprint. Hype Williams's video for 'The Rain', with Missy in an inflated black plastic suit shot through a fish-eye lens, made her an instant visual icon.","'The Rain' is built on Ann Peebles's 1973 Memphis soul hit 'I Can't Stand the Rain', whose ticking electric percussion Timbaland loops almost unchanged: Southern soul becomes futurism. Listen for Timbaland's trademark sounds throughout, such as baby gurgles, cricket chirps and human beatbox, used as drums. Missy herself wrote most of the hooks.","It was hailed immediately as a leap forward in sound and design, and Missy became one of the most distinctive and influential figures in pop. Some reviewers felt the songs were thinner than the production. Its reputation has grown with the recognition that Timbaland's rhythms reshaped both R&B and hip-hop for a decade.","Start with 'The Rain' and hear how the Peebles loop drives it. Then listen to the drums on any track and try to count them: they skip and stutter. Compare Afrika Bambaataa's 'Planet Rock' for an earlier electro vision of Black futurism."],"missy-elliott-get-ur-freak-on":["A dance record built on a nagging five-note riff played on a plucked single string, over tabla and stuttering electronic drums. Missy's delivery is gleeful and teasing, full of shouts, grunts and bursts of speed, and the whole thing has the manic energy of a rave in a street market. It sounds unlike any hip-hop single before it, and the sound of the riff alone announces it.","Released in 2001 as the lead single from Miss E... So Addictive, it was produced by Timbaland at the height of his fame. Its South Asian elements anticipated a wave of Western producers borrowing from bhangra and Bollywood; a remix with Nelly Furtado followed. It won Missy a Grammy for best rap solo performance. The video, with its crawling bodies and green light, was equally strange.","The riff is played on or imitates the tumbi, a one-string Punjabi instrument associated with bhangra, and the tabla patterns are close to North Indian playing. Timbaland had absorbed these sounds from Indian film and bhangra records. Listen for the handful of Japanese words at the very start, a jolt of global collage before the beat drops.","It was a critical and commercial triumph and is often ranked among the best singles of its decade. Later writers have also questioned the borrowing of South Asian sounds without credit to their sources, part of a wider debate about appropriation in Timbaland's work. Its sound has aged remarkably little.","Listen for the tabla and the one-string riff, then play some classic bhangra or Hindustani tabla to hear what Timbaland borrowed. Pair it with OutKast's 'B.O.B.' from a year earlier: two Southern futurists pulling in sounds from everywhere. Then notice how much space surrounds the riff."],"fugees-the-score":["The Fugees' second album blends hip-hop beats, soul, reggae and folk guitar with the contrasting voices of Lauryn Hill, Wyclef Jean and Pras Michel. Its hits were covers: 'Killing Me Softly', Roberta Flack's song set over a bare hip-hop beat and sung by Hill, and Bob Marley's 'No Woman, No Cry'. Originals such as 'Ready or Not', 'Fu-Gee-La' and 'The Mask' show the trio's fluid mix of rap and song.","Released in February 1996 after a debut that had sold poorly, it became one of the best-selling hip-hop albums, won two Grammys and made Hill a star. Wyclef and Pras were of Haitian descent, and the group's name was a reclaiming of 'refugee', a slur used against Haitians in New York. Its success showed rap could be mellow, musical and global.","'Ready or Not' samples Enya's 'Boadicea', a New Age vocal piece, a surprising source for a hip-hop hit. 'Killing Me Softly' was written by Charles Fox and Norman Gimbel and first recorded by Lori Lieberman before Roberta Flack's famous version. Listen for the kitchen-sink sounds and street recordings woven between tracks.","Critics welcomed its warmth and originality; purists grumbled that its hits were covers. It remains one of hip-hop's great crossover albums and set the stage for Hill's solo work. The trio split soon after, and its reunions were brief. Critics now also credit its mixture of Haitian, Caribbean and American influences as a quiet political statement about migration and belonging, well ahead of its time.","Listen to 'Ready or Not' and 'Killing Me Softly' first, then 'The Mask' to hear the trio as rappers. Follow with The Miseducation of Lauryn Hill to hear where Hill went next. Then listen to Roberta Flack's version of 'Killing Me Softly' to hear how much Hill kept and what she changed."],"jay-z-reasonable-doubt":["Jay-Z's debut is a cool, detailed study of hustling as a business: plush, jazzy soul samples, laid-back beats and a rapper whose flow is relaxed, witty and precise. 'Dead Presidents II', 'Can't Knock the Hustle' with Mary J. Blige, 'Brooklyn's Finest' with the Notorious B.I.G. and 'D'Evils' are the standouts. The mood is less violent than reflective, ambivalent about the life it describes.","Shawn Carter, from the Marcy projects in Bedford-Stuyvesant, Brooklyn, was in his mid-twenties and unable to find a label deal, so he founded Roc-A-Fella Records with Damon Dash and Kareem Burke. Released in June 1996, the album was distributed by Priority. Producers included DJ Premier, Ski and Clark Kent. It came at the height of the mafioso wave launched by Raekwon.","'Dead Presidents II' is built on Lonnie Liston Smith's 'A Garden of Peace', a gentle spiritual-jazz piece, and its hook takes a vocal fragment from Nas: an early sign of the rivalry to come. Listen to how Jay-Z raps around the beat with little effort, rarely stressing the downbeat, giving the flow its conversational ease.","It sold modestly at first but is now widely considered his best album and one of the essential New York records of its decade. Critics debate whether his later commercial peaks or this intimate debut is the real Jay-Z. It is often grouped with Illmatic and Ready to Die as the canon of nineties New York.","Start with 'Dead Presidents II' and 'Brooklyn's Finest', where Jay-Z and Biggie trade verses. Listen for the piano and horn samples, which give it a jazz-club feel. Then compare Raekwon's Only Built 4 Cuban Linx for a grittier take on the same themes."],"gang-starr-daily-operation":["Gang Starr's third album is the purest statement of the duo's method: Guru's calm, monotone voice, smooth and authoritative, over DJ Premier's chopped jazz and soul samples, hard drums and scratched choruses. 'Take It Personal', 'Ex Girl to Next Girl', 'Soliloquy of Chaos' and 'I'm the Man', featuring the young Jeru the Damaja, show the range. The sound is sparse, jazzy and absolutely precise.","Guru, from Boston, and Premier, from Houston, met in New York in the late eighties. By 1992 Gang Starr were leaders in the jazz-rap movement alongside A Tribe Called Quest and De La Soul; Guru would soon launch Jazzmatazz, recorded with live jazz musicians. Released in 1992, the album came as Premier began producing for others, a role that would make him the most admired beatmaker in New York.","Listen for Premier's signature chorus technique: instead of a sung hook, he scratches together fragments of other rappers' lines, creating a collage that doubles as a history lesson. His drums are programmed slightly off the grid, which gives them their famous swing. Guru's voice, a flat tenor, was described by fans as the perfect match for jazz.","It was praised on release and is widely considered one of the finest records of the jazz-rap era, although Gang Starr's later Moment of Truth sometimes overshadows it. Premier's production has been influential far beyond the duo: his method of chopping and scratching became a template for East Coast hip-hop.","Listen to 'Take It Personal' and focus on Premier's drums. Then compare A Tribe Called Quest's The Low End Theory, another jazz-rap album of the same moment, and hunt out the jazz records these producers mined: Bill Evans, Ahmad Jamal, Miles Davis."],"geto-boys-mind-playing-tricks-on-me":["A song about paranoia, depression and guilt. Over a slow, looped soul guitar figure, Scarface, Willie D and Bushwick Bill each describe a mind unravelling: sleeplessness, visions, suspicion of friends and a hallucinated fight. The tone is weary and haunted, the music soft and melancholy, which makes the subject all the more disturbing. It sounds like nothing else from its year.","The Geto Boys were from Houston, signed to James Prince's Rap-A-Lot Records, and notorious for their violent lyrics; their earlier album had been refused by its distributor. 'Mind Playing Tricks on Me', from We Can't Be Stopped (1991), became their biggest hit and brought Southern rap onto national radio before OutKast. The album cover showed Bushwick Bill after a shooting incident.","The guitar comes from Isaac Hayes's 'Hung Up on My Baby', from his 1974 soundtrack Tough Guys, one of Hayes's orchestral soul pieces. Listen to Scarface's verses, which are the song's emotional core: they hint at the frank treatment of mental illness that would mark his later solo work. The loop barely changes.","It was a hit and remains one of the most admired Southern rap songs, frequently ranked among the best hip-hop singles of all. Critics have singled it out as an early and frank treatment of mental illness in rap. Its influence on the psychological storytelling of later artists is widely acknowledged.","Listen for the guitar loop and how its gentleness frames the unease. Then play Isaac Hayes's Hot Buttered Soul to hear the orchestral soul world behind it, and follow with 2Pac's Me Against the World for another record of paranoia and dread."],"ugk-ridin-dirty":["UGK's third album is slow, heavy Texas soul-funk: bluesy guitars, deep bass, organ and swinging drums, played live more often than sampled. Pimp C, who produced most of it, sings and raps in a nasal drawl; Bun B raps with measured precision. 'One Day', about losing friends and family, 'Murder', 'Diamonds & Wood' and '3 in the Mornin'' are the core. Interludes feature phone calls from a friend in prison.","Pimp C and Bun B came from Port Arthur, Texas, a refinery town near the Louisiana border. Released in July 1996 on Jive, the album had no radio hit and little promotion, yet sold well through Southern word of mouth. It was a major influence on the Houston scene, and its slow tempos anticipated the chopped-and-screwed style of DJ Screw.","Pimp C studied music at school and played keyboards and bass himself, which explains the unusually sophisticated harmonies and live arrangements. Listen for the blues and gospel inflections, Southern roots that set UGK apart from the funk-loop approach of both coasts. The prison phone calls give the album a sober, documentary frame.","It was little noticed by national critics at the time but has come to be regarded as one of the definitive Southern rap albums and a foundation of the region's sound. Pimp C's death in 2007 prompted reassessment. Critics now rank it with OutKast's early albums as the heart of nineties Southern hip-hop.","Start with 'One Day' and 'Murder', and listen for the live guitar and organ. Play it slowly, in a car if possible, and notice the unhurried tempo. Compare the Geto Boys and OutKast's debut for the Houston and Atlanta sides of the story."],"eminem-the-marshall-mathers-lp":["Eighteen tracks, most produced by Dr. Dre with Mel-Man or by Detroit's Bass Brothers, and a sound that is surprisingly bare: a hard drum loop, a bouncing keyboard figure, a lot of empty space for the voice. The rapper plays several people at once — the leering comedian of The Real Slim Shady, the furious celebrity of The Way I Am, the doomed fan of Stan — and the whole record works as a hall of mirrors about what fame does to the man inside the persona.","The Slim Shady LP had made Eminem a star a year earlier, and a white rapper from Detroit's east side selling to suburban teenagers had become a moral panic of its own. This follow-up, released by Dre's Aftermath label in May 2000, answered the outrage directly and sold about 1.76 million copies in its first week in the United States, a record for a rap album at the time. It was made in the last months before file-sharing began to hollow out album sales.","Listen to how many tracks are really about the listener: the skits, the answering-machine messages, the fan letters, the parents and politicians named in The Way I Am. The album keeps staging its own reception. And beneath the jokes, the rhyme technique is extraordinary — long strings of internal rhymes that land on every stress in the bar, a density few rappers had attempted at pop tempo.","It won the Grammy for Best Rap Album and was nominated for Album of the Year, while GLAAD and others protested its homophobic language and one track's graphic domestic violence fantasy; Eminem's duet with Elton John at the 2001 Grammys was staged as a reply. Critics still argue whether the persona excuses the content. Its technical brilliance and its influence on a generation of rappers are not in dispute.","Start with Stan and The Way I Am, the two tracks where the craft and the self-portrait are clearest, then play The Real Slim Shady to hear the same man as cartoon. Notice how Dre's beats leave the voice almost unaccompanied. Afterwards, go back to Dr. Dre's 2001 from the year before, where Eminem is a guest in the same sonic world."],"eminem-stan":["A song in letters. Over a sampled loop of the English singer Dido's Thank You — her voice drifting in for the chorus — and the sound of rain, thunder and a scratching pen, a fan writes to his idol three times, each letter angrier and more unhinged, until he drives off a bridge. In the final verse the star finally writes back, kindly and too late, and realises mid-letter who the fan was.","Produced by the veteran New Jersey producer The 45 King and released on The Marshall Mathers LP, it became a single late in 2000 and reached number one in Britain. Eminem was then receiving the kind of obsessive fan mail the song describes, and the track is partly a warning about taking his violent persona literally. Dido appeared in the video, and the song made her a star in the United States.","Listen to how the three letters are performed: the same rapper voices the fan, but the pitch, pace and breathing change with each verse, until the words are almost shouted over the loop. The reply verse drops back to Eminem's ordinary voice. The fan's name has since become a word — to stan is to be an obsessive supporter — and the Oxford English Dictionary added it in 2019.","Widely ranked among the greatest hip-hop singles, it earned praise even from critics hostile to the rest of the album for its narrative control. Some hear its ending as the album's moral centre; others note that it depicts the fan's violence against his pregnant girlfriend with the same cinematic relish it claims to deplore. Elton John's performance of it with Eminem at the 2001 Grammys fixed its place.","Listen once simply as a short story, following the four letters. Then listen again for the sound design: the rain, the pen, the car, the splash, each placed like a film's foley. Compare Dido's full recording of Thank You to hear how little of it the producer needed."],"eminem-lose-yourself":["A muted, palm-damped electric guitar riff, a spoken introduction, and then a relentless, rising build: piano chords hammered on every beat, drums entering, three long verses with no let-up. The song narrates a young rapper choking at a battle, then getting one more chance, and its hook is an exhortation to seize the single moment. It is built like a sports anthem and has been used as one ever since.","Written for 8 Mile, the 2002 film in which Eminem played a lightly fictionalised version of himself on Detroit's battle-rap circuit, it was reportedly written and recorded in breaks during filming, with production by Eminem, Jeff Bass and Luis Resto. It spent twelve weeks at number one in the United States and in 2003 became the first rap song to win the Academy Award for Best Original Song; Eminem did not attend the ceremony.","The music barely changes chords; its tension comes from layering and from the voice, which gets faster and more crowded with syllables as each verse goes on. Listen for the moment at the start of the final verse where the arrangement briefly drops away — the only real breath in five minutes. Eminem finally performed the song at the Oscars in 2020, seventeen years late.","Instantly canonical as a motivational anthem, it has been praised as the purest distillation of Eminem's craft without the shock tactics, and dismissed by some as his most conventional record. The Oscar win was read as a turning point in the establishment's acceptance of hip-hop.","Notice the structure first: a guitar that never resolves, piano that grows heavier, verses that accelerate. Then follow the story line by line. Compare Run-DMC's Walk This Way for an earlier fusion of rap and rock guitar, and watch the battle scenes in 8 Mile for the world it came from."],"kanye-west-the-college-dropout":["A debut built from soul records sped up until the voices squeak — the sound nicknamed chipmunk soul — with live strings, a gospel choir and comic skits about a school that tells students to stay in line. Kanye raps as the ordinary middle-class kid in a genre of hustlers: about student debt, consumer insecurity, faith, and the car crash that nearly killed him. Jesus Walks marches over a stomping gospel chant; Through the Wire was rapped through a jaw still wired shut.","By 2003 Kanye was a sought-after producer for Jay-Z's Roc-A-Fella label, but executives doubted a producer in a pink polo shirt could sell as a rapper. After a near-fatal car accident in October 2002 he recorded Through the Wire weeks later, sampling Chaka Khan's Through the Fire, and the label relented. The album appeared in February 2004 as mainstream rap was dominated by gangster personas.","All Falls Down borrows its melody from a Lauryn Hill song, re-sung by Syleena Johnson because the original could not be cleared. Listen too for how the drum sounds are softer and rounder than in most rap of 2004, closer to a 1970s soul session, and how often the samples are allowed to finish their phrases rather than being chopped into loops.","It won the Grammy for Best Rap Album and topped many critics' lists for 2004; reviewers heard it as a sign that hip-hop's centre could accommodate doubt, religion and self-mockery. Some later listeners find the skits long. It is now seen as the launch of the most consequential producer-rapper career of the century.","Start with Jesus Walks and Through the Wire, then Spaceship and All Falls Down. Listen for the pitched-up soul voices that sit in the background of nearly every track. Pair it with Jay-Z's The Blueprint, where the same sampled-soul style first reached the charts."],"kanye-west-late-registration":["The second album keeps the soul samples but dresses them in orchestral colour: strings, harpsichord, French horns, marimba and brass arranged with the film composer Jon Brion. Touch the Sky rides a slowed-down horn fanfare from Curtis Mayfield; Gold Digger turns a Ray Charles sample, sung by Jamie Foxx, into a comic hit; Diamonds from Sierra Leone sets Shirley Bassey's Bond theme against the trade in conflict diamonds. Hey Mama and Roses are tender family portraits.","Kanye brought in Jon Brion, known for his work with Fiona Apple and his film scores, and the two shaped the arrangements through 2005. The album appeared that August; days later, during a televised benefit for Hurricane Katrina, Kanye went off script to say that President Bush did not care about Black people, and the record played against that argument.","Listen for how often the orchestra enters only for a bar or two — a flourish of strings or a harpsichord line — rather than playing through, so the tracks feel like soul records visited by a chamber band. Touch the Sky uses Mayfield's Move On Up at a slower tempo, which makes its horns sound heavier and more ceremonial than the original.","It won the Grammy for Best Rap Album and was widely named album of the year by critics; some rate it Kanye's most complete record, others find it a lavish transitional step between The College Dropout and later reinventions. Gold Digger became his biggest single to that point.","Play Touch the Sky straight after Curtis Mayfield's Curtis to hear the source. Then listen to Diamonds from Sierra Leone for the collision of a Bond theme and politics, and to Roses for the strings at their most intimate. Wear headphones: the arrangements are full of small instrumental entries."],"kanye-west-808s-and-heartbreak":["Almost no rapping. Kanye sings, his voice run through Auto-Tune so that every wobble becomes a robotic slide, over the cavernous booms of a Roland TR-808 drum machine, cold synthesizer pads and pounding taiko drums. The songs — Say You Will, Heartless, Love Lockdown, Street Lights, Coldest Winter — are about loneliness, a broken engagement and grief, sung by a man who sounds numb. It is spare, slow and deliberately anti-triumphant.","In November 2007 Kanye's mother, Donda West, died after cosmetic surgery; soon after, his engagement ended. He recorded the album quickly during 2008, partly in Honolulu, drawing on 1980s synth-pop and new wave as much as rap. Auto-Tune was then a novelty associated with T-Pain's party hits; using it as the sound of grief was a startling choice from the biggest rapper in the world.","Coldest Winter borrows its melody from Tears for Fears' Memories Fade, and the whole album's minor-key synth palette owes more to British 1980s pop than to hip-hop. Listen to how Auto-Tune is pushed to exaggerate the voice's cracks rather than hide them: the machine becomes an expression of emotional flatness, not a correction. Love Lockdown alternates near-silence with thunderous drums.","Reviews were divided in 2008; many rap fans felt betrayed. Within a few years it was recognised as one of the most influential records of its time: Drake, Kid Cudi, The Weeknd, Frank Ocean and the whole wave of melodic, emotionally exposed rap and alternative R&B grew from it. The argument now is less about its merit than about how much it enabled.","Start with Love Lockdown and Heartless, then Street Lights. Listen for the space around the voice, and how few elements each track uses. Follow it with Drake's So Far Gone from the next year to hear the influence arrive almost at once."],"kanye-west-my-beautiful-dark-twisted-fantasy":["A maximalist album about excess, fame and self-loathing, stacked with choirs, prog-rock samples, strings and a crowd of guests: Jay-Z, Nicki Minaj, Rick Ross, Bon Iver's Justin Vernon, Rihanna, Pusha T and others. Power stomps over chanting and a King Crimson sample; All of the Lights piles up horns and a dozen voices; Runaway opens with a single piano note repeated and ends in a long, distorted, wordless solo on a vocoded voice.","After Kanye interrupted Taylor Swift at the 2009 MTV Video Music Awards he was widely vilified and withdrew to Hawaii, where he gathered producers, rappers and writers for months of communal sessions in Honolulu. He previewed songs through free weekly downloads, the GOOD Fridays series, and released the album in November 2010 alongside a thirty-five-minute film, Runaway.","Power samples the 1969 King Crimson track 21st Century Schizoid Man; Lost in the World builds on Bon Iver's Woods, and the album closes with Gil Scott-Heron reciting his poem Comment No. 1. Listen at the end of Runaway: the vocoder solo is Kanye's own voice, deliberately smeared into unintelligibility, a three-minute confession without words.","It received near-universal acclaim, including a rare perfect score from Pitchfork, and won the Grammy for Best Rap Album but was left out of the Album of the Year shortlist, which became a lasting grievance. It regularly appears near the top of decade and all-time lists; the debate now is inseparable from reassessments of its maker.","Hear it in order, with headphones. Start with the chant that opens Dark Fantasy, then Power and Runaway. Play King Crimson's In the Court of the Crimson King afterwards to hear where Power's riff and scream come from, and compare the stripped-down Yeezus three years later."],"kanye-west-yeezus":["Ten tracks in forty minutes of grinding industrial synthesizers, acid-house squelches, screams and sudden silences. Daft Punk co-produced the opening On Sight and Black Skinhead, with its glam-rock stomp; New Slaves attacks consumerism and prison profiteering; Blood on the Leaves sets a sample of Nina Simone singing Strange Fruit against a booming brass hook; the closing Bound 2 swerves into a sweet 1970s soul sample, as though the rest had been a nightmare.","Recorded partly in a Paris loft with a large team of producers including Daft Punk, Hudson Mohawke, Arca and Chicago drill artists, the album was stripped down in its final weeks by Rick Rubin, brought in as executive producer. It was released in June 2013 with no conventional singles or cover art — just a clear jewel case sealed with red tape.","Strange Fruit, the anti-lynching song sampled on Blood on the Leaves, is set under lyrics about a failed relationship, a juxtaposition many found jarring — and perhaps intended to be. Listen for the abrupt cuts: tracks change beat, drop to silence or collapse into noise mid-phrase, the editing itself becoming a rhythm. Bound 2 samples the Ponderosa Twins Plus One.","Critics largely admired its nerve; many fans and radio did not. Its abrasive minimalism was a direct rebuttal of the lavish Fantasy, and it opened space for noise and experimental electronics in mainstream rap. Its use of Strange Fruit remains disputed.","Play loud. Start with Black Skinhead and New Slaves, then Blood on the Leaves. Listen for how little is going on in each track, and how violently it changes. Then play Billie Holiday's Strange Fruit and Kanye's own Late Registration to feel how far he had travelled."],"kendrick-lamar-good-kid-maad-city":["Subtitled a short film, the album follows a seventeen-year-old Kendrick through one day and night in Compton: a crush, a borrowed van, peer pressure, a robbery, a friend shot, and finally a prayer. Voicemails from his parents stitch the scenes together. The music moves from hazy, woozy West Coast beats (Money Trees, Swimming Pools) to the split-screen menace of m.A.A.d city and the twelve-minute Sing About Me, I'm Dying of Thirst.","Kendrick had built a following through Top Dawg Entertainment's independent releases and the album Section.80 (2011); Dr. Dre's Aftermath co-signed him and released this, his major-label debut, in October 2012. It deliberately positioned him as heir to Compton's rap history while reversing its point of view — from gangster to observer.","The album is arranged as a flashback: the closing prayer is the moment the rest of the story has been leading towards, and the final track, Compton, with Dr. Dre, jumps forward to the adult star. Swimming Pools, a radio hit, is an anatomy of peer pressure and drinking disguised as a party song — one voice in the verse is his conscience.","Widely acclaimed, it was treated as an instant classic and compared with Nas's Illmatic as a coming-of-age portrait of one place; its commercial and critical success made Kendrick the defining rapper of the decade. Grammy voters preferred Macklemore for Best Rap Album, a result later widely regretted, including by Macklemore.","Listen in order, like an audiobook, and follow the voicemails. Start with Money Trees if you want one track. Then compare Illmatic and Dr. Dre's The Chronic, the albums it both honours and answers. Notice how the beats darken as the night goes on."],"kendrick-lamar-to-pimp-a-butterfly":["Sixteen tracks played largely by live musicians — Thundercat's melodic bass, Terrace Martin's alto saxophone, Robert Glasper's keyboards, Kamasi Washington's string arrangements, Flying Lotus and Sounwave's production — ranging across free jazz, P-Funk, spoken word and gospel. The album argues with fame, depression, American racism and Black self-image, from the funk strut of King Kunta to the drunken self-lacerating u, the protest chant Alright and the confrontational The Blacker the Berry.","After the success of good kid, Kendrick visited South Africa, including Robben Island, and returned while protests over police killings were spreading across the United States. He spent months with a Los Angeles circle of jazz-trained musicians rather than beatmakers. The album was released in March 2015, a week earlier than announced; George Clinton appears on the opening Wesley's Theory.","A poem is assembled one line at a time across the album: Kendrick recites a little more of it at the end of several tracks until, in Mortal Man, he reads it complete — and then appears to interview Tupac Shakur, using a 1994 radio interview with the Swedish broadcaster Mats Nileskär. The album ends with a question to the dead rapper that goes unanswered.","Reviewers called it the most ambitious rap album in years; it won the Grammy for Best Rap Album, was nominated for Album of the Year, and routinely heads polls of the decade's best albums. Some listeners find it dense and demanding, and its jazz turn helped revive public interest in Los Angeles' young jazz scene.","Give it a full, uninterrupted hour and twenty minutes. Start with King Kunta and Alright, then u and Mortal Man. Listen to the bass throughout. Then hear Kamasi Washington's The Epic, made by some of the same players that year, and Marvin Gaye's What's Going On."],"kendrick-lamar-alright":["Produced by Pharrell Williams and Sounwave, the track opens with a spoken passage and a sung refrain from Pharrell before Kendrick's verses weave in and out of a jittery, jazz-inflected beat with saxophone lines threading through. The song holds despair and resilience side by side: temptation, police violence and inner demons are all named, and the chorus answers them with a plain promise that things will, in the end, be alright.","Released on To Pimp a Butterfly in March 2015 and as a single that summer, it arrived during the protests that followed police killings of unarmed Black Americans in Ferguson, Baltimore and elsewhere. Kendrick performed it at the 2015 BET Awards standing on a graffiti-covered police car, which drew criticism from some commentators.","Within months, protesters were chanting its chorus at demonstrations, beginning with a widely filmed moment at Cleveland State University in July 2015; it became an unofficial anthem of the Black Lives Matter movement without having been written as one. Listen for how much of the track is Pharrell's calm singing, against which the rapping sounds urgent.","It won the Grammys for Best Rap Performance and Best Rap Song and is often ranked the most important protest song of its decade, compared with Public Enemy's Fight the Power and Curtis Mayfield's civil-rights hymns. The criticism it drew from conservative television hosts became part of its story.","Listen first for the chorus and imagine it chanted by a crowd, then go back for the verses, which are darker than the hook suggests. Compare Curtis Mayfield's People Get Ready and James Brown's Say It Loud as earlier songs that crowds adopted."],"kendrick-lamar-damn":["Fourteen tracks with one-word, capitalised titles — BLOOD., DNA., YAH., ELEMENT., FEEL., LOYALTY., PRIDE., HUMBLE., LUST., LOVE., XXX., FEAR., GOD., DUCKWORTH. — played tighter and harder than To Pimp a Butterfly, with trap drums, sudden beat switches and guests from Rihanna to U2. DNA. flips halfway into a frantic new beat; HUMBLE. is a stark piano-and-bass hit; FEAR. is a long, slow meditation on dread.","Released in April 2017, after the sprawl of Butterfly, it was made with producers including Mike Will Made-It, Sounwave, 9th Wonder, The Alchemist and James Blake. In 2018 it won the Pulitzer Prize for Music, the first time the prize went to a work outside classical music and jazz; the committee called it a virtuosic song collection unified by vernacular authenticity and rhythmic dynamism.","The album is a loop about chance and fate. It opens with a staged shooting and closes with DUCKWORTH., a true story: the label head Anthony Tiffith once nearly robbed a fast-food restaurant where Kendrick's father worked, and spared him — the song then rewinds to the album's start. A later edition reverses the running order, and the album reads differently backwards.","It was a critical and commercial triumph and won the Grammy for Best Rap Album. The Pulitzer set off a debate about whether the prize was catching up with American music or conferring classical respectability on a form that needed none; most musicians welcomed it.","Start with DNA. and HUMBLE., then FEAR. and DUCKWORTH. — then listen to the whole album in reverse order to hear the alternative story. Compare To Pimp a Butterfly for the opposite method: live band instead of tight programming. Pay attention to YAH. and ELEMENT., where the beat drops out or changes under him, and to how often the same phrases and questions return from track to track, tying the album together like leitmotifs."],"madvillain-madvillainy":["Twenty-two tracks in forty-six minutes. Madlib's beats are brief, crackly loops drawn from jazz, Brazilian pop, soundtracks, old cartoons and radio drama; MF DOOM raps over them in a conversational murmur, rarely bothering with a chorus, stringing together puns, comic-book references and dense chains of rhyme. Tracks end abruptly, beats mutate mid-song, and short instrumentals interrupt. Accordion, All Caps, Figaro and Rhinestone Cowboy are the signposts.","Madlib, a producer on the Los Angeles label Stones Throw, and DOOM, reviving his career in the mask, began working together around 2002. An unfinished version leaked online in 2002, which reportedly discouraged them; they reworked much of it before the album's release in March 2004. Some of the beats were reportedly made on portable equipment while Madlib travelled in Brazil buying records.","Listen for the samples of old radio serials and superhero cartoons that introduce the villain persona — the album is structured like a comic book, with narration between scenes. Accordion is built on a loop by the electronic musician Daedelus. Many songs are shorter than two and a half minutes: brevity itself is the aesthetic, the opposite of the long, chorus-driven radio track.","Modest sales on release, but immediate critical acclaim; its reputation has grown steadily, and it is now routinely cited as the definitive underground hip-hop album, a touchstone for rappers and producers from Earl Sweatshirt to Tyler, the Creator. There is little debate beyond whether any follow-up could have matched it — none came.","Listen straight through; it is barely longer than a vinyl LP. Pay attention to the beats first on one pass, then to the rhymes on another. Afterwards play De La Soul's 3 Feet High and Rising and J Dilla's Donuts for kindred sample-collage records."],"mf-doom-mm-food":["A concept album loosely organised as a meal, with track titles that are food puns — Beef Rapp, Hoe Cakes, Kon Karne, Deep Fried Frenz, One Beer, Kookies. Most of the beats are DOOM's own, under his producer alias Metal Fingers: jaunty soul, soft-rock and easy-listening loops, some cheerfully cheesy. Over them he raps about friendship, rivalry and the rap business with deadpan wit, and several interludes are built from children's story records.","Released in November 2004 by the Minneapolis label Rhymesayers, the same year as Madvillainy and as his Viktor Vaughn and King Geedorah projects had kept him prolific. DOOM had re-emerged from years of obscurity after KMD's collapse and his brother's death, now in a metal mask, performing sometimes through stand-ins — a refusal of celebrity that became part of the legend.","The interludes lift dialogue from old Fantastic Four children's records, placing the rapper within the Marvel universe his persona came from. Listen to how often the samples come from gentle, unfashionable sources — soft-rock ballads, cartoon themes — and how DOOM's voice, heavy and laconic, turns them sinister or funny. One Beer was produced by Madlib.","Praised on release as DOOM's most personable solo album, it has become a cult favourite, often recommended as the easiest way into his catalogue. Some critics find the food conceit thin; admirers counter that the conceit is merely a frame for the rhyming.","Start with Hoe Cakes and Deep Fried Frenz, then Beef Rapp. Listen for the multi-syllable rhymes stacking up at the ends of lines. Compare Madvillainy, released months earlier, to hear the same rapper over someone else's beats. Listen, too, for the children's-record interludes and how they set up the next track like a comic-book panel, and try following a single verse on paper to see how far the rhyme chains run."],"j-dilla-donuts":["Thirty-one instrumental pieces in forty-three minutes, most under two minutes, made from chopped fragments of soul, funk and pop records. Voices stutter, drums lurch against the loops, sirens wail, and tracks end mid-phrase or melt into the next. There is no rapping; the sampled singers do the talking, in sentences cut up and reassembled. Workinonit, Don't Cry and Last Donut of the Night are the landmarks.","Dilla made much of Donuts while seriously ill with a rare blood disorder and lupus, some of it reportedly in a Los Angeles hospital bed with a small sampler and portable record player. Stones Throw released it on 7 February 2006, his thirty-second birthday; he died three days later. That timing has shaped how it is heard ever since.","The album begins with a track called Donuts (Outro) and ends with Welcome to the Show (Intro), so it loops back on itself like the ring in its title. Listen for fragments that seem to speak to his situation — phrases about crying, time and goodbyes, cut from love songs by the Jackson 5, Dionne Warwick and others — though how intentional this was is debated.","Little noticed outside hip-hop at first, it is now regarded as the high point of instrumental hip-hop and one of the most influential albums of its decade; producers from Flying Lotus to Knxwledge, and jazz drummers such as Chris Dave, trace their feel to it. The romantic deathbed myth has sometimes overshadowed the craft.","Play it through once without stopping; it is shorter than most albums. Listen for the drums that land slightly off where you expect them. Then hear D'Angelo's Voodoo and Erykah Badu's Mama's Gun, where the same loose feel was played by live musicians."],"jay-z-the-blueprint":["Jay-Z at his most relaxed and commanding, rapping over warm, looped soul records: the Jackson 5 on Izzo (H.O.V.A.), Bobby Bland on Heart of the City, the Doors and David Bowie on Takeover. Most of the beats are by the young Kanye West and Just Blaze, with Timbaland and Bink!; the only guest rapper is Eminem, on Renegade, which he also produced. Song Cry is a tender breakup confession; Takeover is a surgical attack on rivals.","Recorded in a few weeks in the summer of 2001 while Jay-Z faced criminal charges, and during a feud with Nas and Mobb Deep's Prodigy, it was released on 11 September 2001, the day of the attacks on New York. It sold strongly regardless. The style of pitched-up soul samples it popularised shaped mainstream hip-hop for years.","Takeover's jab at Nas provoked Ether, one of the most famous replies in rap, and the rivalry ran for years before the two reconciled. Listen for how the samples are left almost whole — the soul singers' own phrases carry the hooks — a sound that made Kanye's name as a producer before he ever rapped on an album.","Acclaimed on release and consistently placed among the best rap albums of the decade; many consider it Jay-Z's finest after Reasonable Doubt. It is often credited with shifting commercial rap from glossy club production back towards soul sampling. Later critics singled out its unhurried confidence, the sense of a rapper who no longer needed to prove anything, and the Takeover and Ether exchange is still studied as the high-water mark of the battle record played out on major-label albums.","Start with Heart of the City and Song Cry, then Takeover. Listen to how the voice sits calmly in the pocket of the beat. Follow it with Nas's Illmatic and Kanye's The College Dropout, the rival and the protégé. Pay attention to U Don't Know, where Just Blaze's sped-up soul loop and horns build a victory march, and to how rarely Jay-Z raises his voice anywhere on the album."],"jay-z-the-black-album":["Presented as a retirement album, it gives each track to a different star producer: Just Blaze's triumphal brass on December 4th and Public Service Announcement, Kanye's soul on Encore, Timbaland's minimalism on Dirt Off Your Shoulder, the Neptunes on Change Clothes and Allure, and Rick Rubin's rock guitar and crashing drums on 99 Problems. December 4th, named after his birthday, weaves in his mother's voice telling his life story.","Released in November 2003, it was followed by a farewell concert at Madison Square Garden filmed as Fade to Black. The retirement lasted three years. The record appeared as file-sharing and mixtape culture were reshaping hip-hop, and the label released its a cappella vocals — an invitation to remixers. Jay-Z gathered beats from producers across the industry, using a different one for nearly every song, and the album's sequence moves deliberately from childhood memories to career summary, like a spoken autobiography.","Those a cappellas were taken by Danger Mouse, who set them to samples of the Beatles' White Album to make The Grey Album (2004), an unauthorised mash-up that EMI tried to suppress and that became a landmark of internet remix culture. Listen too to 99 Problems, where Rubin, who produced Run-DMC and the Beastie Boys, brings back the 1980s rap-rock sound.","Widely praised as a strong farewell and a showcase of producers; 99 Problems became one of his signature songs, and critics have analysed its verse about a traffic stop in law-school classes on search and seizure. Some reviewers found the retirement framing self-regarding.","Start with December 4th and 99 Problems, then Encore. Notice how each producer's signature shapes the voice differently. Compare Run-DMC's Walk This Way and the Beastie Boys' Licensed to Ill for Rubin's earlier rap-rock. Then hear the whole album as a curated anthology of 2003 production styles."],"jay-z-4-44":["Ten tracks in thirty-six minutes, all produced by No I.D. from carefully chosen soul samples. Jay-Z, in his late forties, raps plainly about marital infidelity, therapy, money, race and inheritance. The Story of O.J. uses Nina Simone's Four Women to talk about Black wealth; Smile, built on Stevie Wonder's Love's in Need of Love Today, includes his mother Gloria Carter reading a poem about coming out as gay; the title track is an extended apology to his wife.","Released in June 2017 initially through Tidal, the streaming service he part-owned, it was widely heard as a reply to Beyoncé's Lemonade (2016), which had depicted a marriage damaged by betrayal. No I.D., a Chicago producer who had mentored Kanye West, pushed Jay-Z towards introspection and away from his usual guest-laden approach.","Listen to the samples as commentary: Nina Simone's song of four Black women's lives, Stevie Wonder's plea for love, Hannah Williams and the Affirmations' Late Nights and Heartbreak on the title track. Each is chosen for what its original lyric says, not only its sound. The album's brevity is itself a statement from a rapper once known for excess.","It drew Jay-Z's best reviews in over a decade and eight Grammy nominations, though it won none. Some found its advice on generational wealth tone-deaf; many heard it as a model of how a rapper might age honestly. With Lemonade and Everything Is Love it forms a public marriage trilogy.","Play it after Lemonade and hear the two sides. Start with The Story of O.J. and 4:44. Then play Stevie Wonder's Songs in the Key of Life to hear the source of Smile. Listen for how sparse the drums are, leaving the samples and the voice exposed, and how short the whole record is."],"drake-so-far-gone":["A free mixtape that sounds like a studio album: dim, muffled synthesizers and slow drums by Noah \"40\" Shebib and Boi-1da, with Drake half-rapping, half-singing about ambition, fame and women who got away. Best I Ever Had became a radio hit; Successful, with Trey Songz and Lil Wayne, and Houstatlantavegas set the mood of late-night melancholy. Several tracks rap over borrowed beats, including one from Kanye's 808s.","Drake, a former child actor from Toronto, released it free on his blog in February 2009. It spread so fast that labels competed for him; he signed with Lil Wayne's Young Money that summer. An abbreviated EP version was sold later. It was among the first mixtapes to launch a major star in the blog era, just as streaming and social media were displacing the old label pipeline.","Listen for how the production sounds as if it is heard through a wall — 40 filters out the high frequencies so the beats feel submerged and nocturnal. That filtered sound, not the lyrics, is the album's signature, and became the template for a decade of Toronto-born R&B and rap, from The Weeknd to PartyNextDoor.","Reviewers noticed at once that it took Kanye's 808s & Heartbreak into more commercial territory; some dismissed the emotional openness as whining, others heard a new vocabulary for rap. Its reputation now rests on its influence more than on any single track.","Start with Successful and Houstatlantavegas. Listen to the texture of the keyboards before the words. Then play 808s & Heartbreak and The Weeknd's House of Balloons to hear the circle of influence. Notice how often the hook is sung rather than rapped, and how slow the tempos are."],"drake-take-care":["Eighty minutes of hushed, late-night introspection. 40's production is muted piano, pillowy synthesizers and slow drums; Drake raps and sings about loneliness in success, old relationships and regrets. Marvins Room is a drunk phone call set to barely-there keyboards; the title track with Rihanna is a dance-pop duet; Headlines and The Motto are swaggering rap singles; Crew Love introduced The Weeknd to a mass audience.","Released in November 2011 on Young Money and Cash Money after his first album Thank Me Later, it was made partly at the Los Angeles studio once owned by Marvin Gaye, which gave Marvins Room its name. The title track was built from Jamie xx's remix of Gil Scott-Heron's I'll Take Care of U, an older soul-blues ballad.","Listen to how much of the album is sung rather than rapped, and how often the voice is doubled, whispered or pitched down — the voice becomes one more soft texture in the mix. The album also features a brief appearance from Stevie Wonder on harmonica, on Doing It Wrong.","It won the Grammy for Best Rap Album and is widely considered Drake's best record, the one that fixed the template of his career. Some critics still find the self-pity cloying; others argue that its vulnerability made space for emotional candour across rap.","Start with Marvins Room and Doing It Wrong at night, then Crew Love. Listen for the empty space in the mixes. Follow with Sade's Love Deluxe, which Drake admires, and Frank Ocean's Channel Orange from the next year. Listen for the long, slow outros where the beat carries on after the voice stops, a 40 hallmark, and compare the loud singles Headlines and The Motto with the quiet album tracks around them."],"tyler-the-creator-flower-boy":["A sunny, lush, self-produced album of warm synthesizer chords, strings, jazz harmonies and soft sung hooks, over which Tyler raps about loneliness, driving, growing up and longing. See You Again, with Kali Uchis, floats on bright keyboards; Who Dat Boy is a grinding, horror-film counterpoint; Garden Shed, Boredom and 911 / Mr. Lonely are introspective and melodic. It sounds like a summer drive with the windows down and a lot on your mind.","Tyler first found fame with the Odd Future collective and records designed to shock. By 2017, with Frank Ocean, Steve Lacy and Kali Uchis among his collaborators, he had moved towards the jazzy, harmonically rich pop of his idols Pharrell Williams and Stevie Wonder. Released by Columbia in July 2017, it was widely read as a coming out, with several songs alluding to attraction to men.","Listen to the chords: Tyler, self-taught, favours stacked jazz harmonies and unexpected key changes more typical of 1970s soul than of rap. Garden Shed works as an extended metaphor — a hidden place where feelings are stored — and sets Estelle's singing against a slow guitar solo; the metaphor lets the song speak by suggestion rather than statement.","Critics hailed it as a breakthrough and a reinvention; it was nominated for the Grammy for Best Rap Album, losing to DAMN. It is now seen as the hinge of his career, and of a wider openness about sexuality in hip-hop.","Start with See You Again and Garden Shed, then Where This Flower Blooms. Listen for the key changes. Compare Frank Ocean's Blonde, made by his Odd Future colleague, and Stevie Wonder's Innervisions for the harmonic inheritance. Listen to 911 / Mr. Lonely for the way the song splits into two moods halfway through, a trick Tyler uses often, and notice how much of the album is instrumental colour rather than rapping."],"tyler-the-creator-igor":["A breakup album told by a character, Igor, sung mostly in a pitched and distorted voice, over fuzzy synthesizers, blown-out bass and soul samples. It moves from infatuation (EARFQUAKE, with its lilting keyboard hook) to jealousy and obsession (NEW MAGIC WAND, a grinding distorted rant) to acceptance (GONE, GONE / THANK YOU and ARE WE STILL FRIENDS?). There is little conventional rapping; it is closer to a psychedelic soul record.","Released in May 2019, it came with Tyler's own warning to listeners not to expect a rap album. He produced, arranged and wrote nearly everything himself; EARFQUAKE was reportedly first offered to Justin Bieber and Rihanna. It won the Grammy for Best Rap Album, a category Tyler publicly criticised as a box into which Black artists were put regardless of genre.","A BOY IS A GUN* is built on the same Ponderosa Twins Plus One soul record that Kanye West used on Bound 2, heard very differently. Listen too for how the voice changes pitch across the album: as the character loses control, the vocals become more distorted, and they grow clearer as he lets go.","Widely praised as his most coherent album; its Grammy win and his speech about genre labels sparked a debate about how the industry categorises Black artists. Some listeners miss the rapping; many hear it as the culmination of the melodic turn.","Listen straight through, as a story. Start with EARFQUAKE if you want one song, then NEW MAGIC WAND. Play Kanye's Yeezus to hear the shared sample in its first setting, and Prince's Dirty Mind for another one-man-band funk album. Notice how the tracks bleed into one another without gaps, so the album plays like one long suite, and listen for the moment on GONE, GONE / THANK YOU when the fuzz lifts and a clean melody takes over."],"run-the-jewels-run-the-jewels-2":["Thirty-nine minutes of pounding, distorted, bass-heavy beats by El-P, over which he and Killer Mike trade verses at full volume. The mood swings from exuberant menace (Oh My Darling Don't Cry, Blockbuster Night Part 1) to direct politics: Early describes a police stop from both sides of the car, and Lie, Cheat, Steal attacks the powerful. Zack de la Rocha of Rage Against the Machine guests on one track.","The duo released the album free online in October 2014, amid the protests in Ferguson, Missouri, after the police killing of Michael Brown. On the night the grand jury declined to indict the officer, Killer Mike gave an emotional speech from the stage in St. Louis before a show, which spread widely and fixed the album to the moment.","Listen for El-P's production detail: layers of fuzz, dub-style delays and industrial clatter, closer to a noise-rock record than to a sample-based beat. Early is a dialogue, with the singer BOOTS voicing a fearful bystander. The duo later released Meow the Jewels, a remix album made from cat sounds, as a crowdfunding joke that raised money for charities.","It topped many critics' lists for 2014 and made the duo, both veterans in their late thirties, into festival headliners. It is often compared to Public Enemy for its political energy; some find the sonic assault relentless. Its politics sounded prophetic as protests spread through 2015, and later critics have argued it marked a moment when independent, free-release rap could command the kind of attention once reserved for major-label albums.","Start with Oh My Darling Don't Cry, then Early. Listen for the contrast between El-P's New York clip and Killer Mike's Atlanta drawl. Then play Public Enemy's It Takes a Nation of Millions to Hold Us Back. Pay attention to Blockbuster Night Part 1 for the sheer drum sound, then to Crown, a quieter confession where the aggression turns inward."],"danny-brown-atrocity-exhibition":["A disorienting, anxious album: detuned horns, lurching drums, clattering noise, psychedelic rock and Detroit dance samples, produced mostly by the British producer Paul White. Danny Brown's high, strained voice spirals through addiction, paranoia and depression, sometimes comic, often frightening. Ain't It Funny is a queasy carnival; Really Doe features Kendrick Lamar, Ab-Soul and Earl Sweatshirt over a tolling bell; When It Rain is a Detroit ghettotech rush.","Released in September 2016 on Warp, the British electronic label of Aphex Twin, it signalled Brown's move away from rap's commercial mainstream. He was then in his mid-thirties, touring constantly and, by his own account, struggling with substances and his mental health. Paul White, a London producer known for psychedelic, sample-heavy instrumental records, had worked with Brown before, and the pairing pushed the album away from Detroit rap conventions towards rock, noise and dub.","The title is borrowed from Joy Division's song Atrocity Exhibition, the opening track of their album Closer, which itself took it from J. G. Ballard's experimental novel. Listen for the post-punk inheritance: jagged guitars, dub-heavy bass and claustrophobia. Brown has named Joy Division, Talking Heads and Radiohead as inspirations for the record.","Many critics named it one of the best albums of 2016, praising its daring and the way it made rap sound like no other rap. Some found it too harrowing to return to; it is increasingly seen as a key record of experimental hip-hop in the 2010s.","Start with Really Doe and Ain't It Funny, then When It Rain. Listen for the horns, which never quite sit in tune. Afterwards, play Joy Division's Closer and the Velvet Underground's White Light/White Heat for the darker lineage. Notice the tempo: many tracks feel slightly too fast or too slow, deliberately uncomfortable. If it overwhelms, start with the calmer Rolling Stone and work outwards from there."],"little-simz-grey-area":["Ten tracks in thirty-five minutes, built with the producer Inflo from live bass, drums, guitar and strings rather than samples. Offence and Boss are confident, punchy openers; Selfish, with Cleo Sol, and Flowers, with Michael Kiwanuka, are slower and reflective; Venom is a furious answer to men who underestimate her, over horror-film strings. The album is about being in one's mid-twenties — neither young nor settled — and the conflicts that brings.","Little Simz, a north Londoner of Nigerian heritage, released it in March 2019 on her own label, Age 101, after years of independent mixtapes and albums. Inflo, a childhood friend, was then working with the secretive collective Sault. The record was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize and won the Ivor Novello award for Best Album.","Listen for how rarely the music loops: parts change under her verses as a band would, and several tracks end in extended instrumental codas. Venom's orchestral stabs recall a 1970s thriller score. The phrase grey area refers to the in-between state she describes — a life no longer simple enough for black-and-white answers.","It was widely hailed as the best British rap album of its year and the one that made her a major figure; critics praised the concision and the live-band sound. It is often compared to Lauryn Hill's Miseducation for its mix of confidence and self-examination.","Start with Offence and Venom, then Selfish. Listen for the live bass. Follow it with her Sometimes I Might Be Introvert, which takes the same partnership to orchestral scale, and Lauryn Hill's The Miseducation of Lauryn Hill. Listen closely to the drums, which sound like a real kit in a room, and to 101 FM, a nostalgic portrait of growing up on London estates with pirate radio and video games."],"little-simz-sometimes-i-might-be-introvert":["A cinematic album that opens with Introvert, a fanfare of brass, strings and choir like a film's title sequence. Across nineteen tracks Inflo's arrangements range from orchestral grandeur to Afrobeat (Point and Kill, with Obongjayar), 1970s soul (Woman), and quiet piano; interludes narrated by the actor Emma Corrin frame the record like a fairy tale. The subject is her own life: family, fame, grief, her absent father, her introversion.","Released in September 2021 during the pandemic, the album's acronym, SIMBI, is her own name. Inflo, now famous through the anonymous collective Sault, arranged it with an orchestra and choir. It won the Mercury Prize in 2022, confirming her as the leading British rapper of her generation. It was recorded with a large studio orchestra and choir, an unusual expense for a self-released rap record, and the title reflects how she has described herself: an introvert who comes alive only on stage.","I Love You, I Hate You addresses her estranged father directly, and the orchestration pulls back to let the verse speak. Little Q is a two-part portrait of a cousin who survived being stabbed, told first in his voice and then in hers. Listen for how the interludes move from fairy tale to intimate counsel, a narrator speaking as an inner voice.","Critics called it a British counterpart to To Pimp a Butterfly for its ambition and its orchestral-soul palette. Some found its length and grandeur overwhelming; most praised its sustained focus. It is widely seen as a landmark of UK hip-hop.","Start with Introvert for the fanfare, then Woman and Point and Kill. Listen for how the orchestra enters and exits. Compare Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly and Lauryn Hill's Miseducation. Headphones help: the arrangements are full of small orchestral details beneath the voice."],"kae-tempest-everybody-down":["A novel told in twelve tracks: Becky, Harry and Pete, three young people in south-east London, meet, fall in love, deal drugs and get caught in something larger than themselves. Tempest raps and speaks each chapter in a breathless, rhythmic London voice over spare, bright electronic beats by Dan Carey, with synthesizer riffs that grow track by track. Each song advances the plot, and the album is best heard as a single story.","Tempest, already known as a poet and playwright, had won the Ted Hughes Award in 2013 for Brand New Ancients. Released on Ninja Tune's hip-hop imprint Big Dada in May 2014, the album was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize. Tempest later expanded the same characters into the novel The Bricks That Built the Houses (2016).","Listen for how the beats are constructed by Carey in his south London studio with analogue synthesizers and drum machines, often playing live with the voice rather than looping. The characters recur from track to track, and the voice shifts register for each of them as the story turns darker.","It was praised as a new kind of rap record, closer to spoken-word theatre, and compared with The Streets' A Grand Don't Come for Free; hip-hop purists sometimes doubted whether it counted. It established Tempest as a crossover figure between poetry and music.","Listen in order, as a story. Start with Marshall Law and Lonely Daze. Follow the characters' names. Afterwards read the novel, or play Let Them Eat Chaos for the next chapter of Tempest's method. Notice how the synthesizer lines build across the record."],"kae-tempest-let-them-eat-chaos":["One long poem in thirteen parts. At 4.18 a.m. on a London street, seven neighbours lie awake in seven flats — a nurse, a heartbroken young man, a builder, a woman grieving a friend — each given a track, until a storm brings them all out into the street. Dan Carey's music is spare synthesizer pulse and slow-building drums. The climax, Tunnel Vision, turns into an urgent address about climate, inequality and indifference.","Released in October 2016, months after the Brexit referendum and weeks before the American election, it was also published as a book. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize in 2017. Tempest, a performer of great intensity, often played the whole piece live without a break. The title plays on the phrase attributed to Marie Antoinette, turning a remark about indifference to hunger into a comment on political distraction and the chaos of modern life.","Listen for how the album is structured as a single panning camera shot, moving from window to window. Some tracks are almost pure speech; others lock into a beat. The opening track sets the whole thing in cosmic scale — the earth seen from far away — before zooming onto one street.","Critics praised its ambition and topicality; some found it didactic. It is often compared to Marvin Gaye's What's Going On as a panorama of a society in distress, and to Under Milk Wood for its structure. It featured on many British critics' lists for 2016, and its live performance, an uninterrupted hour, drew praise as theatre as much as concert.","Listen late at night, in one sitting, as if it were a radio play. Pick a character to follow. Then play Marvin Gaye's What's Going On to hear a different panorama of a troubled nation. Notice how the beat drops out entirely in some passages, leaving the voice alone, and how the storm arrives in the music before it is named in the words."],"noname-telefone":["A short, gentle mixtape of warm electric piano, soft drums and gospel-tinged backing voices, produced by Chicago friends including Phoelix, Cam O'bi and Saba. Noname's delivery is quiet and rapid, a poet's cadence, and the subjects are serious beneath the softness: childhood in Chicago, police violence, grief, depression, and, on Bye Bye Baby, a pregnancy lost. Diddy Bop recalls childhood summers; Casket Pretty confronts young death.","Released free in July 2016 after long delays, it came out of a Chicago scene of young rappers and musicians around Chance the Rapper, Saba and Smino, many from the city's youth poetry programmes. Noname had built an audience from guest verses rather than any record of her own. She had performed slam poetry as a teenager, and the youth open-mic programmes of Chicago public libraries and arts centres shaped her quiet, dense style; Telefone took several years to finish.","Listen to the backing singers and keys: many songs are closer to a church or jazz lounge than a rap studio, and the joyful arrangements often sit under the saddest words — childlike melodies over mourning. The title evokes a phone conversation; the songs are meant to feel like intimate calls.","Critics praised its warmth and its poetry; it placed on many year-end lists despite being a free mixtape. It established Noname as a leading voice of Chicago's new rap, and some compared her to Erykah Badu for her mix of softness and pointedness.","Start with Diddy Bop and Casket Pretty. Listen for the contrast between sweet music and serious words. Then play Erykah Badu's Baduizm and her own Room 25. Listen with the words in front of you the first time; her delivery is quick and quiet, and the lines reward close attention to their images."],"noname-room-25":["Eleven tracks of live, jazzy instrumentation — piano trio, strings, flute, soft backing choirs — produced mainly by Phoelix. Noname's delivery is still quiet, but sharper and funnier than before: she writes about moving to Los Angeles, sex, Black womanhood, politics and self-discovery. Self is a witty opening statement; Blaxploitation and Prayer Song are political; Ace, with Smino and Saba, is playful.","Released independently in September 2018, it followed Noname's move from Chicago to Los Angeles and her first adult relationship, according to her interviews. She would later found Noname's Book Club and increasingly turn her attention from music to activism. Phoelix, a Chicago producer and multi-instrumentalist, again led the arrangements, with live strings and a small jazz band recorded rather than programmed, and guests from her Chicago circle, including Smino and Saba.","Listen to the bass and drums: the band plays like a jazz trio behind a singer, pushing and pulling tempo, and some tracks change groove mid-song. The title refers to a room she lived in; the album is about what happens in private spaces. Some tracks end with long instrumental passages, jazz tags rather than hip-hop fades.","It was widely named among the best albums of 2018, often praised as the best rap album by a woman that year. Critics highlighted its intelligence and musicianship; some noted the quietness might make it easy to underrate. Later assessments have treated it as a high point of the Chicago jazz-rap scene of the late 2010s, and her later ambivalence about the music industry has only added to its standing.","Start with Self and Blaxploitation, then Montego Bae. Listen for the band. Compare A Tribe Called Quest's The Low End Theory for an earlier jazz-rap fusion, and Little Simz's GREY Area. Notice how the drummer shifts the groove under her, and how often she lands a joke at the end of a serious line."],"kraftwerk-autobahn":["The title track fills the whole first side: some twenty-two minutes of a motorway journey rendered in sound. A car door slams, an engine turns over, a horn sounds, and then a steady, rolling pulse sets off under a sunny major-key tune on the Minimoog, with voices chanting 'Wir fahr'n, fahr'n, fahr'n auf der Autobahn'. Passing traffic whooshes by in stereo. Side two holds shorter pieces, including the night-time 'Kometenmelodie' pieces and the pastoral 'Morgenspaziergang', with flute and birdsong.","Ralf Hütter and Florian Schneider had made three records of loose, organ-and-flute improvisation in the Düsseldorf Krautrock scene. For their fourth they bought a Minimoog and recorded with producer Conny Plank at his farmhouse studio near Cologne, joined by Klaus Röder and Wolfgang Flür. West Germany's motorways stood for postwar prosperity, and the group wanted a German modern music not borrowed from Anglo-American rock. An edited three-minute single found unexpected radio success in Britain and the United States.","The painter Emil Schult, who designed the cover, helped write the words, and the chanted refrain is often heard as a joke on the Beach Boys' 'Fun, fun, fun'. Listen for how little the harmony moves: the long track is built from a handful of chords, and its sense of travel comes from changing textures, a new synth line or filter sweep every minute or so, like scenery through the window.","Many British and American reviewers treated it as a novelty, a German oddity with a catchy hook. Within a decade the judgment had reversed: musicians from David Bowie to the Detroit techno producers cited it, and it is now usually named as the hinge where Kraftwerk left Krautrock behind. Debate continues over how much credit Conny Plank's engineering deserves, since the group later minimised his role.","Give the title track its full length, ideally on a drive or with headphones, and notice the stereo cars passing. Then compare the single edit to hear what was lost. The 2009 remaster is clean and punchy. Follow it with Trans-Europe Express to hear the pulse become fully mechanical, and with Stockhausen's Gesang der Jünglinge to hear what the German electronic avant-garde had already done with tape."],"kraftwerk-trans-europe-express":["Seven tracks in two halves. Side one is a postcard of old Europe: the wistful 'Europe Endless', the eerie 'Hall of Mirrors' and the stiff-limbed 'Showroom Dummies'. Side two is one continuous rail journey: 'Trans-Europe Express' sets a clanking, metallic rhythm going, it flows into the harsher 'Metal on Metal', and then into 'Franz Schubert' and a short coda. The recognisable sound is the train itself, a pounding electronic beat with a melody that falls and rises like a whistle.","By 1977 the line-up was Ralf Hütter, Florian Schneider, Karl Bartos and Wolfgang Flür, working at their own Kling Klang studio in Düsseldorf. The album was an argument for European identity at a time when pop meant Britain and America; the TEE was a real network of luxury international trains. Punk was raging in London; Kraftwerk answered with suits, short hair and total control. Bowie and Iggy Pop, both in Berlin, were friends and admirers.","The lyric of the title track names Iggy Pop and David Bowie, whom the group had met. Listen too for the album's circularity: 'Europe Endless' opens it and the brief 'Endless Endless' closes it with a ghostly reprise, so the journey ends where it began. In 1982 Afrika Bambaataa and the Soulsonic Force rebuilt the title melody into 'Planet Rock', one of the founding records of electro and hip-hop production.","It sold modestly in Britain and America on release, and some reviewers found its coolness sinister. Its afterlife is enormous: through 'Planet Rock' it reached the Bronx, through Detroit radio it reached Juan Atkins and Derrick May, and through Bowie it reached British post-punk. Critics now often call it the album where electronic pop became a complete aesthetic, image and sound together.","Start with side two, the rail sequence, as one piece: the title track, 'Metal on Metal' and 'Franz Schubert'. Listen for the moment the beat turns from mechanical to almost industrial. Then play 'Planet Rock' to hear what the Bronx did with it, and Honegger's Pacific 231 for a much older musical locomotive. The German-language edition, Trans Europa Express, is worth comparing."],"kraftwerk-the-man-machine":["Six tracks, tighter and more danceable than anything the group had made. 'The Robots' opens with a vocoder voice declaring 'We are the robots' over a jerky, sequenced bass line. 'Spacelab' and 'Metropolis' are glittering instrumentals; 'The Model' is a three-minute pop song about a fashion model, with a cheerful synthesizer riff; 'Neon Lights' is a long, slow, glowing hymn to a city at night; the title track closes on a chanted mechanical groove.","Recorded at Kling Klang in Düsseldorf, it arrived as disco ruled the clubs and Giorgio Moroder's machine-driven productions were topping charts; Kraftwerk were listening. The cover, red and black with the four members in identical shirts and ties, borrowed the style of the Russian constructivist El Lissitzky. The group had begun presenting themselves as robots, later using mannequins as stand-ins, turning the human-machine question into both theme and theatre.","'The Model' was a minor track at first; reissued as a single in 1981 it reached number one in Britain, years after it was recorded. Listen for the Russian words whispered in 'The Robots', a nod to the constructivist theme. 'Metropolis' takes its name from Fritz Lang's 1927 film, whose robot Maria is a direct ancestor of the group's persona.","Reviewers who had mocked Kraftwerk as cold began to hear humour in the deadpan. The album is now regarded as the blueprint for British synth-pop: the Human League, Depeche Mode and Orchestral Manoeuvres in the Dark all took from it. Some fans prefer its sleek pop focus; others miss the long, landscape pieces of the earlier records.","Begin with 'Neon Lights', the most tender thing the group recorded, and notice how few elements it uses. Then play 'The Robots' loudly for its bass line. Compare with Yellow Magic Orchestra's Solid State Survivor, made a year later in Tokyo, and with Moroder's work of the same years. The 2009 remaster is the usual choice."],"kraftwerk-computer-world":["Seven tracks about the arrival of computers in everyday life. The title song lists 'interpol and Deutsche Bank, FBI and Scotland Yard' in a flat voice; 'Pocket Calculator' is played partly on a toy calculator; 'Numbers' is a stark rhythm track with a synthetic voice counting in several languages; 'Computer Love' sets a lonely-hearts lyric to one of the group's loveliest melodies. The whole record is lean and percussive, its beats sharper and more syncopated than before.","It took three years, as Kraftwerk rebuilt their studio to be portable for touring. Home computers were just appearing, and data surveillance was a live political issue in West Germany. Meanwhile, the Detroit DJ the Electrifying Mojo was playing Kraftwerk on the radio alongside funk, and in New York electro producers were listening closely. The record reached a new, Black American dance audience the group had never directly addressed.","The syncopated drum pattern of 'Numbers', combined with the melody of Trans-Europe Express, is the skeleton of Afrika Bambaataa's 'Planet Rock', and Juan Atkins and Derrick May, founders of Detroit techno, have named this album as a key influence. Listen for the counting in 'Numbers': it runs through German, French, Japanese, Russian and more, a small world tour of machine voices. Coldplay later built 'Talk' on the 'Computer Love' melody, with credit.","Reviews were respectful rather than excited; the record sold solidly without matching the singles success of the previous one. Its reputation has grown steadily, especially in the United States, as critics traced the line from 'Numbers' through electro and Miami bass to techno. It is now often called Kraftwerk's most prophetic album, predicting both the personal computer and the anxieties of data.","Play 'Numbers' first, loud, and try not to move. Then 'Computer Love' for the melody. Follow with 'Planet Rock' and an early Juan Atkins record to hear the inheritance directly. The 2009 remaster keeps the dry, close sound; the 1991 re-recordings on The Mix are a different, glossier experience."],"wendy-carlos-switched-on-bach":["Ten pieces by Johann Sebastian Bach realised entirely on a Moog synthesizer: the Sinfonia from Cantata No. 29, the Air on the G String, three Two-Part Inventions, 'Jesu, Joy of Man's Desiring', 'Wachet auf', two preludes and fugues from The Well-Tempered Clavier, and the whole Third Brandenburg Concerto. Each contrapuntal line has its own colour, some reedy, some bell-like, some buzzing, so the polyphony becomes vividly clear, as though an organ had been taken apart and every stop given its own speaker.","The Moog could play only one note at a time and drifted out of tune as it warmed up. Carlos and her producer Rachel Elkind, with musicologist Benjamin Folkman, built each piece line by line on an eight-track tape machine in a small New York studio, over months. Columbia expected a curiosity. Instead it became one of the best-selling classical records ever made and won three Grammy Awards, putting the word 'Moog' into popular vocabulary.","In the Third Brandenburg Concerto, Bach left only two chords between the fast movements, where players traditionally improvise; Carlos filled the gap with a short electronic improvisation of her own, the most audibly 'synthesizer' moment on the record. Listen for the touch-sensitivity problem too: the early Moog had no dynamics from the keyboard, so every crescendo and accent had to be shaped by hand with knobs and envelope settings.","Glenn Gould praised it warmly, and many classical listeners heard Bach's inner lines more clearly than ever before; some purists called it a gimmick, and rock fans were surprised to find they liked Bach. It triggered a wave of cheaper 'Moog plays the hits' records that did its reputation no favours for a while. Today it is recognised as the record that made the synthesizer a serious instrument in public eyes.","Start with the Third Brandenburg, then play a period-instrument recording straight after and notice what each makes clearer. The Air on the G String shows her lyrical side. Seek the remastered edition overseen by Carlos herself, released in the Switched-On Boxed Set. Compare Stockhausen's Kontakte to hear what the classical studio was doing with electronics at the same time."],"wendy-carlos-a-clockwork-orange":["Music for Stanley Kubrick's film of Anthony Burgess's novel, with an expanded album of Carlos's full-length versions. It opens with Purcell's Music for the Funeral of Queen Mary, turned into a slow, eerie synthesizer processional. Beethoven's Ninth Symphony, the hero Alex's obsession, appears with its choral finale sung by a vocoder, a machine voice singing Schiller's words. The original 'Timesteps' is a long, dark, searching piece, and Rossini's William Tell Overture is sped up to comic mania.","Kubrick heard Carlos and Rachel Elkind's work after Switched-On Bach and invited them onto a film already built around classical music. The novel's theme, a violent youth conditioned by the state to feel sick when he hears Beethoven, suited a score where familiar music is made strange by machines. Only parts of the music reached the finished film, so Carlos released a fuller album. It was released under the name Walter Carlos.","The vocoder, which splits a voice into frequency bands and uses them to shape a synthesizer, had begun as a telephone and wartime encryption technology; this was among its first uses in music, years before Kraftwerk made it famous. Listen for 'Timesteps', which Carlos had begun as an independent piece before the film project came along, which is why it feels less like a film cue and more like a concert work.","The film was hugely controversial, which drew attention to the score. Classical listeners debated whether synthesizer Beethoven was sacrilege or a sharp critique of how culture is consumed; film critics saw how the machine timbres mirrored Alex's conditioning. The full-length album has come to be valued as Carlos's most personal early statement, and 'Timesteps' as a real composition.","Start with 'Timesteps' for Carlos's own voice, then the vocoder Beethoven, and play the ending of the real Ninth Symphony next to it. Watch the film's opening credits to hear the Purcell over the red screen. The Wendy Carlos's Complete Original Score edition includes material the film did not use."],"wendy-carlos-sonic-seasonings":["A double album of four long pieces, one per season, each filling an entire side at around twenty minutes. Spring, Summer, Fall and Winter blend recorded natural sounds, rain, surf, birds, insects, wind and distant thunder, with slow synthesizer textures that sometimes imitate those sounds and sometimes float a melody above them. Nothing is dramatic: the pieces drift, change weather and fade, closer to a landscape seen from a window than to a composition that argues.","Carlos and Rachel Elkind made it in New York while working on commercial and film projects after the success of Switched-On Bach. Columbia released it as a double album in 1972, when there was no category for it; environmental recordings of surf and forest were selling as relaxation records, and minimalism was growing in downtown New York. Vivaldi's Four Seasons is the obvious ancestor in title, though not in sound.","Listen for the places where you cannot tell the recorded world from the synthesizer: a wind that is actually filtered noise, a bird call that is an oscillator. Carlos designed the synthetic sounds to sit inside the natural ones rather than above them, a quieter use of the Moog than anything on her Bach records, and in 'Summer' a gentle melody surfaces only after minutes of pure atmosphere.","It puzzled reviewers and sold modestly compared with the Bach records. Its reputation rose once Brian Eno named ambient music later in the decade; critics now regularly cite it as a proto-ambient, proto-new age record, arriving several years before Discreet Music. Some listeners find it too gentle; others treat it as the first ambient album in all but name.","Put on 'Spring' at low volume while you read or cook, and let it recede; then come back and listen hard to one five-minute stretch. Compare Eno's Music for Airports and Suzanne Ciani's Seven Waves. The Sonic Seasonings reissue from East Side Digital adds a later companion piece."],"delia-derbyshire-doctor-who-theme":["A galloping bass line, a swooping melody that climbs like a voice in a wind tunnel, and hissing bursts of filtered noise. It lasts a couple of minutes and has never been forgotten. Every sound was made without a synthesizer or keyboard: the bass is a single plucked string, recorded and then transposed to each pitch by changing tape speed; the melody is an oscillator shaped by hand; the washes are white noise swept through filters.","Ron Grainer wrote the theme for a new BBC children's science-fiction series, first broadcast in November 1963, handing over a single page with the tune and descriptions like 'wind bubble' and 'cloud'. Delia Derbyshire realised it at the BBC Radiophonic Workshop in Maida Vale, with Dick Mills assisting, cutting every note from tape and splicing them by the hundreds. Television needed sounds nobody had heard before, and the Workshop was built for exactly that.","Grainer is reported to have asked 'Did I write that?' on hearing it, and to have offered Derbyshire co-credit, which the BBC refused because Workshop staff were anonymous. She was not credited on screen. Listen for tiny differences between the bass notes: each pitch was a separate length of tape, cut and joined by hand. The melody's slightly unsteady swoops come from oscillators tuned by hand rather than played from a keyboard.","It was an instant fixture of British television and a favourite of children. Derbyshire's authorship became widely known only late in her life, and she has since become a heroine for electronic musicians, especially women, from Orbital, who played the theme live, to many younger producers. Musicologists now treat the original as a landmark of British tape music; later arrangements, however polished, are generally thought weaker.","Hear the 1963 original first, then any later version, and notice how the modern ones smooth away the handmade wobble. Try to separate the three layers: bass, melody and noise. Follow with Varèse's Poème électronique and Stockhausen's Gesang der Jünglinge, tape-studio works from the continental studios that pointed the way."],"delia-derbyshire-the-dreams":["A radio collage in which ordinary people describe their dreams: of running, falling, drowning, of landscapes and of colour. Their voices are cut, overlapped and repeated, and around them Derbyshire built an electronic dream-space of hums, pulses, glassy tones and rushing noise. It moves through sections on distinct kinds of dream, so the result is closer to a composed piece of music for voices than to a documentary.","The writer Barry Bermange recorded the interviews and devised the idea; it was one of his Inventions for Radio, made with the BBC Radiophonic Workshop for the Third Programme, the BBC's arts channel. Radio in the early 1960s was still the home of adventurous sound work, and the Workshop had been founded in 1958 for exactly such radio drama. Derbyshire, then in her mid-twenties, was fresh from the Doctor Who theme.","Listen for how the edits themselves become rhythm: a phrase like 'running away' is chopped and looped until the words turn into music, the same trick Steve Reich was using at almost the same time in San Francisco. Derbyshire also transposed some voices down so that they merge with the electronic drones, blurring speaker and sound.","It passed almost unnoticed outside BBC radio listeners for decades. Revival came with the rediscovery of Derbyshire's archive after her death in 2001, and it is now valued as a key piece of British tape music and a predecessor of later sound collage and sampling. Some critics hear it as a British equivalent of the Paris musique concrète studio's radio work.","Listen in the dark with headphones, as it was made for radio, not for a concert hall. Follow the speakers' voices as if they were instruments. Compare Reich's early tape pieces and Lucier's I Am Sitting in a Room, where speech also turns into sound, and Luciano Berio's tape work if you know it."],"white-noise-an-electric-storm":["Seven tracks that begin as psychedelic pop songs and end in an electronic nightmare. 'Love Without Sound' opens with sped-up strings and close harmonies; 'My Game of Loving' layers breathy voices into an erotic tape collage; 'Firebird' and 'Your Hidden Dreams' are dreamy songs with strange electronic halos. The long closing piece, 'Black Mass: An Electric Storm in Hell', is pure noise and percussion, a swirl of screams, drums and oscillators.","David Vorhaus, an American bassist in London, heard Delia Derbyshire and Brian Hodgson of the BBC Radiophonic Workshop give a talk and proposed a band. They worked partly in Vorhaus's own small studio in Camden and partly at the Workshop after hours. Island Records had signed them on the strength of a demo and, reportedly, pressed for a quick finish, which is why the last piece was put together fast. It was 1969, the year of psychedelia's peak.","The gentle vocal harmonies are often not multiple singers at all but one or two voices speed-shifted and layered by tape, a technique straight out of the Radiophonic toolkit. Listen for how 'Black Mass' uses real drums alongside electronics, and for the stereo panning throughout: the group planned movements across the speakers rather than fixed placements, which gives the record its unsettled, circling feeling.","It sold slowly but steadily and gained a cult reputation over decades among musicians rather than critics. It has since been reclaimed as a key record of British electronic psychedelia, often cited by later groups who mixed pop song with vintage electronics. Critics sometimes find the songs thin, but the tape techniques remain striking and the closing track anticipates industrial music.","Start with 'Love Without Sound' for the fusion of song and tape, then brave 'Black Mass' at a decent volume. Compare the Beatles' 'Tomorrow Never Knows' era and Silver Apples. Later reissues on Island are cleaner; the original stereo mix is essential for the panning."],"tangerine-dream-phaedra":["Four instrumental pieces, led by the sixteen-minute title track. It begins with murky, shifting electronic tone before a Moog sequencer sets off a bubbling bass pattern that repeats and slowly mutates while Mellotron choirs and flutes drift above. Nothing like a song, it feels like travelling through a dark, glittering space. 'Mysterious Semblance at the Strand of Nightmares' is a slow Mellotron dream; 'Movements of a Visionary' and 'Sequent C'' are shorter, eerier sound studies.","Edgar Froese, Christopher Franke and Peter Baumann had just signed to Richard Branson's young Virgin label and recorded at the Manor, Virgin's studio in rural Oxfordshire. They had bought a Moog modular with a sequencer, an expensive and temperamental instrument. The record reached the British album charts with almost no radio play, carried by word of mouth and the new underground taste for German experimental rock.","The title track was reportedly shaped by accident: the Moog's oscillators drifted in pitch as the machine warmed up, and the group recorded the wandering tuning rather than fixing it. Listen for how the sequencer pattern very slowly changes pitch and filter over minutes, and for the tape-recorder hiss, part of the atmosphere. The whole track is essentially a single improvisation, lightly edited.","Some rock critics found it formless, but it sold unexpectedly well in Britain and was later seen as the founding document of the Berlin School. Its sequencer patterns anticipate trance and ambient techno by two decades, and producers from the early rave scene have named it. Debate continues over whether its looseness is a strength or a limitation compared with Kraftwerk's precision.","Listen to the title track in the dark, from the formless opening until the sequence emerges, and notice when you stop hearing it as repetition. Compare Terry Riley's A Rainbow in Curved Air for the organ-loop ancestor, and Klaus Schulze's Timewind. The remaster in the In Search of Hades box set of the Virgin years is well regarded."],"tangerine-dream-rubycon":["Two long parts, one per side, each around seventeen minutes. Part One opens with gong washes and prepared-piano clatter, then a sequencer pulse arrives and builds to a driving climax before dissolving; Part Two begins with dark, choral Mellotron chords, almost sacred, and then gathers another sequence under guitars and organ. The whole record feels like one slow, flowing current, more focused and more dramatic than Phaedra.","Made by the same trio of Edgar Froese, Christopher Franke and Peter Baumann, again for Virgin, in the wake of Phaedra's surprising success. The group had begun playing concerts in cathedrals in France and Britain, some with audiences of thousands, and their music was increasingly composed for performance rather than purely in the studio. Mid-1970s Britain was hungry for long instrumental albums from Pink Floyd to Mike Oldfield.","The title puns on the Rubicon, the river Caesar crossed on the point of no return, and the record is built like a crossing: a quiet shore, a swelling passage, and a changed landscape on the other side. Listen for the prepared piano, with objects placed on its strings, which links the album to John Cage's technique in a way few rock listeners expect.","It became the group's highest-charting album in Britain and is often named by fans as their best. Critics praise its cohesion over Phaedra's loose experiments. Its structure, long builds and releases over a continuous pulse, has been compared to later trance and to the minimalist process pieces of Reich and Glass, though it is more improvised than either.","Hear the whole of Part One without interruption and notice where the pulse first becomes regular. Then compare Glass's Music in Twelve Parts, from the same period and also built on patterns, but acoustic and exact. The remaster in the In Search of Hades box set of the Virgin years is well regarded."],"klaus-schulze-irrlicht":["Three long pieces of slowly shifting drone, subtitled a quadrophonic symphony for orchestra and electronic machines. There are no synthesizers: Schulze plays sustained organ chords and feeds a recording of an orchestra through filters until it becomes a glowing, unrecognisable haze. The sound swells and fades in waves, sometimes shining, sometimes grimly dark. There is no beat anywhere, only pressure and slow change, like light moving across a room.","Having drummed with Tangerine Dream and Ash Ra Tempel, Schulze went solo in 1971 with almost no money and no synthesizer. He recorded the orchestral material from a student orchestra rehearsal and processed it through damaged equipment in Berlin. It was released on the Ohr label, home of Krautrock's most experimental records, just as West German youth culture was inventing a music outside both Anglo-American rock and the classical avant-garde.","Listen for the orchestra, which you may not recognise at all: in places you can just make out bowed strings, but Schulze filtered them so heavily that the players become part of the drone. The title means will-o'-the-wisp, a ghostly light over marshes, and the record's glimmering, deceptive textures follow that image. Its clouds of sustained sound share something with Ligeti's orchestral writing of the previous decade.","It was barely reviewed outside Germany at first. Later critics have seen it as one of the first dark ambient or drone records, a precursor of later noise and isolationist ambient music. Some listeners find it too static; others hear it as Schulze's purest statement before sequencers took over his style.","Play it loud in a dark room and let the drones fill the space. Listen for the moment, in the first piece, when the organ chord slowly shifts. Compare Ligeti's Atmosphères and Lux aeterna, which share the idea of sound as slowly moving clouds, and Tangerine Dream's Zeit from the same year."],"klaus-schulze-timewind":["Two side-long pieces, each about half an hour. 'Bayreuth Return' starts with a sequencer pattern that builds slowly, then Schulze solos and layers above it in waves of shimmering, droning sound. 'Wahnfried 1883' is darker and more static, a vast, mournful drone that ebbs and swells like an organ in a cathedral. The textures are rich and slightly unstable, the pulses hypnotic, and the length is part of the meaning.","Schulze now had synthesizers and a sequencer, and could at last make the long, pulsing pieces he had been reaching for with organ and tape. The titles are homage to Richard Wagner: Bayreuth is Wagner's festival town, Wahnfried his house there, and 1883 the year of his death. Schulze, like many German musicians of his generation, was working out what could be done with a German Romantic inheritance after the war.","Wagner's ideas of endless melody and slowly transforming harmony lie beneath the whole album; listen for how 'Bayreuth Return' keeps shifting without clear cadences, like a Wagnerian transition that never arrives. Schulze worked largely by improvising over running sequences, and small drifts and irregularities were kept rather than corrected, part of his belief that a performance's life matters more than precision.","It sold well across Europe and was especially admired in France, where Schulze had a devoted following. Critics consider it the album where his mature style arrived. Its Wagner homage has divided listeners, some finding it grandiose, others seeing it as an honest attempt to join electronic music to the European symphonic tradition. Its half-hour spans were later echoed in trance and ambient techno.","Hear 'Bayreuth Return' in one sitting, and notice how the slowly changing sequence keeps you waiting. Then play the Prelude to Wagner's Tristan und Isolde if you know it, to hear the model. Compare Tangerine Dream's Phaedra and Rubycon. Later CD reissues add bonus material from the same period."],"vangelis-chariots-of-fire":["The soundtrack album for Hugh Hudson's film about two British runners at the 1924 Paris Olympics. The title theme, 'Titles', rides on a repeated, slowly pulsing piano figure while the CS-80 plays a broad, brassy melody that swells to triumph. Other pieces are gentler: reflective, chiming, sometimes melancholy synthesizer pieces, with echoing piano. The album closes with an extended version of the main theme.","Hudson wanted modern music for a period film, and Vangelis recorded it alone at his Nemo Studios in London, improvising and building layers. The choice of synthesizers for a film set in the 1920s was bold, but it lifted the film out of its period. The main theme was released as a single and topped the American charts in 1982, and the score won the Academy Award.","Listen for the gentle piano figure underneath the main theme, which keeps going almost unchanged; the drama comes entirely from what the synthesizer layers above it. The CS-80's pressure-sensitive keyboard lets Vangelis swell each note, so the brassy crescendos are played by touch rather than by turning knobs. The anachronism is the point: a 1920s story scored with the sound of 1981.","It won the Oscar for best original score, a remarkable result for an electronic score at the time. Its theme became a cliché, used for any slow-motion triumph, and parodied endlessly; that fame has sometimes obscured its craft. Film-music writers now credit it with opening Hollywood to synthesizer scores through the 1980s.","Hear the main theme first and listen for the piano ostinato, then how the melody rides above it. Then try the quieter tracks for Vangelis's reflective side. Compare his Blade Runner, a year later and very different in mood, and Wendy Carlos's Clockwork Orange for an earlier synthesized film score."],"vangelis-blade-runner":["The score for Ridley Scott's science-fiction film, set in a rain-soaked Los Angeles of 2019. Wide, reverberant synthesizer chords evoke a city of towering buildings; the 'Main Titles' open with booming bells and a slowly rising fanfare; the 'Love Theme' is a sultry saxophone melody over soft synth; 'Tales of the Future' has an Arabic-inflected vocal line. Dialogue from the film threads through the album, and the ending theme drives forward on a pounding pulse.","Vangelis recorded the score at Nemo Studios in London, improvising to the images. For legal and business reasons the original score was not released on record in 1982; an orchestral re-recording by other musicians appeared instead, and fans circulated bootlegs for years. Vangelis finally released his own album in 1994. Early 1980s cinema was discovering synthesizers, and Vangelis had just won an Oscar for Chariots of Fire.","The 1994 album is not simply the film's music: Vangelis re-edited it and wove in lines of dialogue, making it a new work with the film's voices. Listen for the 'Love Theme', played on saxophone by Dick Morrissey, and for the singer Demis Roussos, Vangelis's old bandmate in Aphrodite's Child, on 'Tales of the Future'. The 'Memories of Green' track had appeared on an earlier Vangelis album.","The film was a commercial disappointment at first but became a cult classic, and its score's reputation grew with it. Critics now rank it among the most influential film scores of the 1980s, its sound of rain, neon and loneliness echoed across later science-fiction films and in electronic genres from synthwave to ambient.","Start with 'Main Titles' and the 'Love Theme', then the 'End Titles'. Watch the film's opening with the city at night to hear how score and image fuse. Compare Wendy Carlos's Tron and Glass's Koyaanisqatsi, from the same year. The 25th Anniversary three-disc edition adds unreleased music."],"jean-michel-jarre-oxygene":["Six parts played almost continuously. Part I opens with slowly shifting chords and whooshing filtered noise. Part IV, the famous one, sets a bouncy rhythm-box beat under a simple, catchy synthesizer tune, with swoops and bubbles above. Other parts are dreamy and slow. The sound is warm, spacious and melodic, closer to a pop record than to the experimental music Jarre trained in, but full of textural detail.","Jarre recorded it in a makeshift studio in his Paris flat, on a small budget, and several labels turned it down. Francis Dreyfus's small Disques Motors label released it in late 1976, and it became an international bestseller. Jarre had studied at the Groupe de Recherches Musicales under Pierre Schaeffer, founder of musique concrète, and brought the studio's tricks, filtered noise and tape effects, into a melodic setting.","The rhythm under Part IV comes from a cheap Korg preset rhythm box meant to accompany home organists, the kind of drum machine a serious studio would sneer at. Listen for the wind-and-sea whooshes, made with filtered white noise on the EMS synthesizer, a trick straight from the musique concrète studio. The cover painting by Michel Granger, of an earth peeling to reveal a skull, hints at the ecological theme.","Some critics dismissed it as lightweight, and admirers of the Paris experimental studio saw it as a turn to easy melody. But it sold in the millions and is often cited as one of the best-selling French albums. It opened a mass market for instrumental electronic music, and producers of 1990s ambient house cited its warmth and melody.","Start with Part IV, then go back to Part I to hear the slow opening that sets the mood. Listen for the whooshes and sweeps. Compare Tangerine Dream's Phaedra, darker and less tuneful, and Vangelis's Chariots of Fire. The later sequel albums, Oxygène 7–13 and 3, are interesting but less fresh."],"jean-michel-jarre-equinoxe":["Eight parts forming a continuous piece. The opening is misty and slow; Part IV introduces a pulsing sequencer pattern and a bright melody; Part V has a tune that sounds almost like a folk song. The last parts include a hurdy-gurdy-like fairground melody and a return to the opening mist. Sequencers now drive much of the music, more rhythmic and propulsive than Oxygène, and the textures are richer, with more layers and stereo movement.","After Oxygène's success Jarre had more money and new machines, including a custom sequencer built by his collaborator Michel Geiss. He recorded again in Paris, aiming for a darker, more rhythmic record. In 1979 he performed it at a free concert on the Place de la Concorde in Paris before a vast crowd, the first of his giant city events with lights and projections.","The cover, by Michel Granger, shows crowds of tiny figures with binoculars staring out, a hint that the album is about watching and being watched. Listen in the final parts for the sound of rain and a fairground organ, a return to Jarre's musique concrète training. The fairground tune is gently ironic, a nod to French popular traditions.","It sold well, though less than Oxygène, and critics found it more assured if less surprising. Fans often prefer it for its rhythmic drive, which points toward the sequenced dance music of the following decade. Some find its melodies too sweet; others hear in its pulsing patterns the missing link between the Berlin School and synth-pop. It remains one of his most admired records.","Hear Parts IV and V together to follow the move from pulse to melody, and listen for the rain and the fairground at the end. Compare Kraftwerk's The Man-Machine, released the same year and more severe, and go back to Oxygène to hear how much the sequencer changed his music in two years."],"yellow-magic-orchestra-yellow-magic-orchestra":["The trio's debut, bright and cheeky. It opens with arcade-game bleeps and explosions, then launches into 'Firecracker', a cover of Martin Denny's 1950s exotica tune reimagined as clipped, sequenced synth-pop. 'Tong Poo' is a fast, Chinese-flavoured instrumental; 'La Femme Chinoise' pokes fun at Western clichés of East Asia. The rhythms are exact and bouncy, the melodies catchy, and the whole record is shot through with humour.","Haruomi Hosono formed the group with Yukihiro Takahashi and Ryuichi Sakamoto, with Hideki Matsutake programming a Roland MC-8 computer sequencer, one of the first. It was released on the Alfa label in Tokyo in 1978 and reissued in the United States the next year in a remixed version. Japan was at the peak of its electronics boom, and arcade games were becoming a craze.","The 'yellow magic' of the name answered 'black magic' and 'white magic', a way of claiming a new Asian pop identity. Listen for how 'Firecracker' keeps Denny's exotica tune but removes its jungle sounds, replacing them with video-game noises, a sly comment on how the West imagined Asia. The American mix differs noticeably from the Japanese original.","It sold modestly at first, then boomed after the group's international tour. Its 'Computer Game' opening is often named among the first pop uses of video-game sounds. Hip-hop and electro DJs in New York took notice, and Afrika Bambaataa played 'Firecracker' in his sets. It is now seen as the start of Japanese electronic pop and an influence on video-game music.","Hear 'Firecracker' first, then Martin Denny's original to hear the transformation. Listen for the arcade sounds at the opening and how quickly they give way to a tune. Compare Kraftwerk's The Man-Machine, made the same year in Düsseldorf, and follow with Solid State Survivor. Try both the Japanese and American mixes."],"yellow-magic-orchestra-solid-state-survivor":["Eight tracks, tighter and more song-based than the debut. 'Technopolis' opens with a vocoder chanting 'To-ki-o', setting a fast, bright electronic beat; 'Rydeen' is a galloping instrumental with a sweeping, cinematic melody; 'Behind the Mask' has a stiff robotic groove and a haunting tune. They also cover the Beatles' 'Day Tripper' in a jerky, mechanised style. The playing is precise, but the melodies are warm.","Recorded in Tokyo in 1979 with Hideki Matsutake again programming the computer sequencer. The English lyrics were written by the poet Chris Mosdell. It became one of the best-selling albums in Japan that year and made the three members national celebrities; its success helped launch 'technopop' as a Japanese phenomenon. Tokyo was fast becoming a futuristic, neon-lit city, and the record celebrated it with wit rather than awe.","'Behind the Mask' had a long afterlife: Michael Jackson wrote new lyrics for it during the Thriller sessions, and his version was released only posthumously; Eric Clapton and Greg Phillinganes also recorded it. Listen for how 'Technopolis' treats Tokyo the way Kraftwerk treated the autobahn, as a subject for machine music.","It won strong praise in Japan and growing attention abroad, where listeners had assumed electronic pop was a German speciality. Critics today see it as the peak of the group's pop phase, the moment their humour and precision were in perfect balance. It has influenced British synth-pop, later J-pop and electro, and its bright melodies can be heard behind decades of Japanese game music.","Hear 'Technopolis' and 'Rydeen' first, listening for how the melodies ride above the exact machine rhythm, then 'Behind the Mask' for its stiff, eerie groove. Compare the Beatles' 'Day Tripper' with their cover and notice what the mechanised version reveals about the riff. Then play Kraftwerk's Computer World to hear the two groups in conversation."],"yellow-magic-orchestra-bgm":["A darker, stranger record than its predecessors. 'Ballet' is a slow, sad song with booming machine drums; 'Music Plans' and 'Cue' have muted, melancholy melodies; 'Rap Phenomena' is an early Japanese experiment with rap over electronic beats. The sound is deliberately murky and compressed, with heavy, booming kick drums and clicking percussion. It feels like background music, as the title (an abbreviation for 'background music') implies, turned eerie.","After two cheerful international successes the trio wanted to make something less commercial, and they recorded it in Tokyo early in 1981. The new Roland TR-808 drum machine, released in 1980, sits at the centre of the sound; BGM was among the first records built around it. Western synth-pop was booming, and the group were reacting against being seen simply as entertainers.","The 808, with its deep boom of a bass drum and crisp handclaps, would soon become the sound of hip-hop and electro; listen here for it in an entirely different context, slow and melancholy. The title is ironic: BGM is the Japanese abbreviation for background music, played in shops, and the group made a record that refuses to stay in the background.","Fans who loved the bright earlier hits were confused, and sales were lower. Critics now rate it among the group's most adventurous records, and it is cited for its early and creative use of the 808, before that machine became synonymous with hip-hop. It prefigured the darker, more introspective side of electronic pop in the 1980s, and many listeners now prefer it to the hits.","Start with 'Cue' and 'Ballet', and listen for the 808's deep, booming kick drum under the melancholy melodies. Then try 'Rap Phenomena' as a curiosity. Compare Kraftwerk's Computer World, released the same year, and any early electro record, such as 'Planet Rock', to hear what the same drum machine became in the Bronx."],"brian-eno-discreet-music":["Side one is a single piece, over half an hour long: two short, gentle synthesizer melodies, fed into a pair of tape recorders that repeat them with delays, so they overlap and slowly change against each other, filtered and softened. The result is calm, uneventful and quietly beautiful. Side two is three variations on Pachelbel's Canon, played by a small string ensemble conducted by Gavin Bryars, with the parts slowed, rearranged and overlapped.","Eno released it on his own Obscure label, set up to issue experimental music by composers such as Bryars and Michael Nyman. In the liner notes he explains the idea: lying in bed after an accident, he listened to a harp record played too quietly to hear properly, with rain outside, and heard music becoming part of the environment. He had also learned tape-loop techniques from working with Robert Fripp.","The tape-delay system, two machines passing sound between them, had been used by Terry Riley and was the heart of Eno's collaborations with Fripp. Listen for how the melodies sometimes drop out, change filter, or overlap with themselves, entirely produced by the system rather than composed note by note. Eno's liner notes include a diagram of the system, an invitation to copy it.","It passed almost unnoticed at first, but it is now seen as the first ambient album, the start of a lineage leading to Music for Airports, ambient house and much else. Some find the Pachelbel variations less successful than the title piece; others value them as a gently subversive commentary on overplayed classical music.","Play the title piece at low volume while doing something else, and notice when you start to hear it again. Then hear Pachelbel's Canon in its original form and compare the Eno variations. Follow with Music for Airports and Terry Riley's A Rainbow in Curved Air."],"brian-eno-ambient-1-music-for-airports":["Four pieces, titled only '1/1', '2/1', '1/2' and '2/2'. In '1/1', a few piano notes, played by Robert Wyatt and slowed on tape, repeat and overlap in a calm, spacious texture. '2/1' uses wordless women's voices, each on a tape loop of a different length, so they combine in ever-changing chords. '1/2' combines piano and voices; '2/2' uses soft synthesizer. The whole record is quiet, unhurried, and beautiful to pay attention to or to ignore.","Eno conceived the idea waiting in the Cologne-Bonn airport, disliking the music played there, and wondered what music might suit a place where people might be anxious about flying. He recorded the album partly in Cologne and London. In the liner notes he defined ambient music as music that must be 'as ignorable as it is interesting'. It was installed at LaGuardia Airport's Marine Air Terminal in New York.","The tape loops are of different, unrelated lengths, which is why the voices in '2/1' never repeat the same combination: the system composes a piece that could, in principle, run forever without exact repetition. Listen for the ghostly quality of the piano in '1/1', created by slowing the tape. The idea descends from Satie's 'furniture music' and from Steve Reich's early phasing tape pieces.","Critics at first found it slight, and it did not sell widely. Over time it has become one of the most influential albums of its era, defining ambient music and inspiring genres from ambient techno to modern classical. In 1998 the ensemble Bang on a Can recorded it with live instruments, a sign that it had become a concert-hall work.","Play '1/1' at low volume, and notice how your attention comes and goes. Then listen closely to '2/1' and follow the voices. Compare Satie's Gymnopédies and Reich's Music for 18 Musicians. Bang on a Can's live version shows the music in a new light."],"brian-eno-ambient-2-plateaux-of-mirror":["Ten short pieces for piano by Harold Budd, treated by Eno so that each note hangs in an echoing, glowing halo. Budd plays slowly, softly, with pauses, chords that drift and hover. The sound is so soft and blurred that it sounds as if the piano were made of glass or water. It is lyrical and dreamy, closer to Debussy or Satie than to experimental music, but the treatment makes it strange.","Harold Budd was a California composer who had renounced the avant-garde for what he called soft music, slow and consonant. Eno invited him to make the second record in his Ambient series, with Daniel Lanois engineering. Budd played while Eno shaped the sound around him in real time. The Ambient series was Eno's attempt to make ambient music into a genre shared with other artists, not just one album of his own.","Listen for how the echoes seem to lag slightly behind the notes, as if the piano were in a vast, reflective hall; the effect is electronic, added as Budd played, so he was in effect duetting with his own halo. Much of the music was improvised. The piece titles, like 'First Light' and 'Not Yet Remembered', read like fragments of poems, and set the mood as surely as the sound.","It was seen at first as a slight follow-up to Music for Airports, pretty rather than radical. Critics now value it as a key ambient work and the ancestor of a whole school of treated, reverberant ambient piano heard in film and in modern classical recordings. Some still find it too sweet; admirers answer that its restraint is exactly the point. Budd and Eno collaborated again on The Pearl in 1984.","Play 'First Light' and 'Not Yet Remembered' and listen for how the echoes hang after each chord. Keep the volume low, as with Music for Airports, and then try a second, attentive listen. Compare Satie's Gymnopédies and Pärt's Für Alina, both quiet piano music built on space. Follow with The Pearl for the next step."],"brian-eno-ambient-4-on-land":["Eight pieces, each named for a place, many from Eno's memories of the East Anglian coast where he grew up: 'Lizard Point', 'Dunwich Beach, Autumn, 1960', 'Lantern Marsh'. Unlike earlier ambient records, it is dark and murky: low drones, slithering noises, the croaking of frogs, rustlings and hums, rather than melody. It sounds like a landscape at dusk, imperfectly remembered.","Eno was living in New York, working with Talking Heads and experimenting with sound processing. He described the pieces as attempts to evoke places through recorded sounds, processed and layered, rather than through melody. Contributors include the trumpeter Jon Hassell, whose Fourth World music Eno admired. The record completed the Ambient series, after Laraaji's Day of Radiance as Ambient 3, and pushed ambient music away from calm and toward mystery.","Listen for the found sounds: frogs, rustling, sticks, chains and more, processed until they sound like wildlife or weather. Eno used the studio to make the sounds feel as if they are coming from a distance, like a place in memory. The trumpet in some tracks is Hassell's, heavily treated.","It was admired by critics but did not sell widely. Its reputation has grown, particularly among makers of dark ambient and field-recording music, and it is now often called Eno's most personal ambient record, the one where the idea of music as place is fully realised. Some listeners find it too dark and formless after the gentle clarity of Music for Airports.","Listen at night, in the dark, with headphones, and let the sounds place you somewhere. Start with 'Lizard Point', then 'Dunwich Beach, Autumn, 1960'. Compare Oliveros's I of IV and Feldman's Rothko Chapel, two other ways of making sound feel like a space. Follow with Apollo, the next Eno ambient record."],"brian-eno-apollo":["Twelve pieces made for a documentary about the Apollo moon missions. Some are slow, ethereal washes of synthesizer, evoking the silence of space; others feature Daniel Lanois's pedal steel guitar, with its country-music twang, softened into something dreamy and floating. 'An Ending (Ascent)' is a slow, rising, hymn-like piece of held chords; 'Deep Blue Day' is a gentle slide-guitar melody that seems to float without gravity.","Eno made the album with his brother Roger Eno and Daniel Lanois, the Canadian producer who later worked with Eno on U2's records. It was intended as the soundtrack for Al Reinert's documentary built from NASA footage, which was eventually released as For All Mankind in 1989. Eno wanted to evoke not only the missions but the astronauts' experience, and to avoid the portentous, heroic scoring usual for space footage.","The pedal steel guitar is a country instrument; Eno used it partly because astronauts had taken country music with them into space, so their own taste is folded into the score. Listen for how Lanois's playing blurs into the synthesizers until you cannot tell which is which. 'Deep Blue Day' was later used in the film Trainspotting, in an unexpected and dreamlike underwater scene.","It became one of Eno's most beloved records, and 'An Ending (Ascent)' is widely used in films, documentaries and television, often for moments of grief or wonder. Critics consider it Eno's most emotionally direct ambient work, warmer than the earlier Ambient series. It has influenced film music and ambient pop, and its country-tinged steel guitar opened a lineage of so-called ambient Americana.","Start with 'An Ending (Ascent)' and 'Deep Blue Day', and listen for the pedal steel sliding under the synthesizer. Then hear the album whole, ideally watching For All Mankind. Compare Vangelis's Blade Runner and Glass's Koyaanisqatsi, two other film scores of the early 1980s. The 2019 reissue adds a second disc of new pieces."],"suzanne-ciani-buchla-concerts-1975":["Recordings of two live concerts Ciani gave in New York in 1975, playing the Buchla modular synthesizer alone. The music is improvised and flowing: bubbling, pulsing sequences, sounds that swirl and spin in space, sudden bursts and swoops, and moments of warm, lyrical melody. It sounds nothing like a keyboard instrument; the Buchla has no conventional keyboard, and Ciani treats it as a living, breathing system to be steered in real time.","Ciani had studied with Don Buchla in California and moved to New York, where she funded her work with commercial sound design. In 1975 she played in venues of the downtown experimental scene, including the loft of composer Phill Niblock. The recordings remained unreleased until 2016, when the Finders Keepers label issued them, part of a revival of interest in early female electronic musicians.","Listen for how she keeps sequences running while she adjusts other parts of the patch, making the music change continuously without any stop. The Buchla, unlike the Moog, was designed by its inventor to encourage experimental, non-keyboard music, and Ciani's playing shows why: it sounds more like weather or water than an instrument.","Its release in 2016 caused excitement, revealing a lost piece of history and a performer whose reputation had rested on commercials and new age albums. Critics now see it as proof of Ciani's pioneering role in live electronic performance, at a time when few played synthesizers on stage at all. It has inspired a new generation of modular synthesists, and Ciani herself returned to live Buchla concerts.","Listen with headphones and follow the sounds as they move between the speakers, and notice that nothing ever stops. Compare Morton Subotnick's Silver Apples of the Moon, another Buchla landmark, and Oliveros's electronic work. Then hear Seven Waves to hear how Ciani turned the same instrument toward melody."],"suzanne-ciani-seven-waves":["Seven pieces, one for each wave, each with its own mood. The sound is warm and liquid, with sequences that bubble and flow, lyrical melodies, and textures that seem to wash in and out like water. Some pieces are gentle and dreamy, others brighter and more rhythmic. The melodies have a Mediterranean warmth, and the sounds move across the stereo field.","It was Ciani's first solo album, made in New York after a decade of funding her work through commercial sound design for advertisements, films and games. She now wanted to make music of her own and to show that a synthesizer could sing. The album was later marketed as new age music, a growing genre in the 1980s, which brought her a loyal audience but also critical condescension that lasted decades.","The waves are not only ocean waves: Ciani meant them as waves of energy and feeling, and the seven pieces trace an emotional arc rather than a seascape. Listen for the vocoder in places, turning a human voice into a synthesizer texture, and for sounds that move around you in the stereo field, a legacy of her quadraphonic Buchla concerts.","It found a modest audience at first but led to a long career of albums. Critics in the 1980s often dismissed new age as background music, and Ciani was filed with it. Since the 2010s it has been re-evaluated alongside the rediscovery of her Buchla work, and her early albums are now seen as important, melodic contributions to electronic music by one of its few women pioneers.","Listen to the whole album in order, and notice how the moods change from piece to piece, bright to reflective. Pay attention to how the sounds are placed in space. Compare Wendy Carlos's Sonic Seasonings for another synthesizer view of nature, and Eno's ambient records for a cooler approach to calm."],"laurie-spiegel-kepler-harmony-of-the-worlds":["A short piece of computer music that turns the orbits of the planets into sound. Each planet's orbital speed, as described by Johannes Kepler in 1619, is translated into a sliding pitch, so the planets sing, sliding up and down as they speed up near the sun and slow down far from it. The sound is eerie and beautiful, a slow chorus of wavering tones.","Spiegel made it at Bell Labs, where she was working on computer music. It was chosen for the Voyager Golden Record, a record of Earth's sounds and music launched into space in 1977 on the two Voyager spacecraft, now beyond the solar system. The record, assembled by a team led by Carl Sagan, included music from Bach to Chuck Berry, and Spiegel's piece opened its section of the sounds of Earth.","Kepler, the seventeenth-century astronomer, believed the planets made music, and wrote out their melodies in his book Harmonices Mundi of 1619. Spiegel's piece realises his idea literally, using his data. Listen for the slow, wavering glides: each one is an orbit, rising as a planet races near the sun and falling as it slows, so the piece is a sounding diagram of Kepler's laws.","It is little known as music but famous as part of the Voyager record, one of the most widely discussed recordings ever made. Critics now see it as an early example of data sonification, turning scientific measurement into sound, and as a symbol of Spiegel's pioneering role in computer music at a time when very few women worked in the field.","Listen while imagining the planets circling the sun, and follow the glides as orbits. It is short, so hear it several times. Compare Holst's The Planets for a very different, astrological take on the same bodies. Then hear Spiegel's The Expanding Universe for the music she made with the same Bell Labs tools."],"laurie-spiegel-the-expanding-universe":["A set of pieces of computer music, each generated by Spiegel's algorithms, rules the computer uses to generate and vary music. 'Patchwork' is a lively, folk-like piece of interlocking patterns; 'Appalachian Grove' is gentle and modal, reminiscent of folk fiddle music; the title piece is a slow, spacious, evolving drone. The music is gentle and lyrical, not at all what one might expect of computer music in 1980.","Spiegel made the pieces at Bell Labs between 1974 and 1979, using the GROOVE system, designed by Max Mathews and F. Richard Moore, which let a mainframe computer control analogue synthesizers in real time. It was released on Philo, a folk label, in 1980, an unusual home that suits the music. In 2012 it was reissued in expanded form by Unseen Worlds, bringing it to a new audience.","Listen for how the patterns in 'Patchwork' shift and recombine, like a quilt; each variation is generated by the computer according to rules Spiegel wrote, but she chose and steered the results in real time. The folk colour comes from her background as a guitarist and lutenist. The computer was a partner in improvisation, not a replacement for the composer.","It was little noticed on release outside the small computer-music world. The 2012 reissue brought wide acclaim, and critics now see it as an important early work of algorithmic music that anticipated generative ambient music and later computer composition. Many were surprised by its warmth, which overturned assumptions about how early computer music sounds. It is now cited by a new generation of electronic musicians.","Start with 'Patchwork' and 'Appalachian Grove', and listen for how the patterns shift without ever quite repeating. Then try the title piece for its slow drift. Compare Terry Riley's In C and Steve Reich's Piano Phase, which share the idea of pattern and process, and Eno's Discreet Music, a system of a different kind."],"larry-levan-live-at-the-paradise-garage":["A recording of Larry Levan at work in the booth of the Paradise Garage, issued on two discs. What you hear is not a playlist but a performance: disco, soul and early electronic records flowing into one another, voices rising out of drum breaks, long instrumental passages left to breathe. Levan lets songs run far longer than radio would, repeats sections, and builds the room's energy in slow waves. The thing to recognise is patience: the mix never hurries toward a peak.","The Paradise Garage opened in a former parking garage on King Street in Manhattan in the late 1970s and became the spiritual home of New York's black and Latino gay dance crowd. There was no alcohol licence; people came to dance until morning. Its sound system, built by Richard Long, was legendary, and Levan tuned it obsessively. Disco was at its commercial height in 1979, and was about to suffer a violent backlash in the rest of America; the Garage simply carried on.","Levan treated the sound system as an instrument. On recordings like this you can sometimes hear him cutting the bass or the treble for a few bars, then slamming it back in, a trick that made a packed room scream. Later house DJs copied the move so universally that it is easy to forget someone had to invent it. The way a vocal track suddenly drops to its bare rhythm is also often his own edit, not the record.","For years the Garage survived only as memory and myth, told by people who were there; this release, decades later, let listeners check the legend against the evidence. Some found it less wild than the stories, a reminder that a DJ set is shaped by a room and a night that no recording keeps. Historians now treat Levan, alongside David Mancuso and Frankie Knuckles, as a founder of the modern DJ's art.","Do not skip around. Put it on at length, ideally loud, and notice how the transitions feel rather than which song is playing. Listen for the moments when a record is allowed to strip down to drums before the next one enters. Then hear the Peech Boys' Don't Make Me Wait, which Levan produced, and a Frankie Knuckles record, to hear the Garage's sound travel to Chicago."],"larry-levan-dont-make-me-wait":["A single by the New York Peech Boys, the group Larry Levan formed and produced around the singer Bernard Fowler and keyboardist Michael de Benedictus. It opens with a voice almost alone, then a heavy synthesised bass and bouncing handclaps arrive, drenched in echo. The song is a plea to a lover for patience, but the record's real subject is space: drums that hang in the air, a bass that swells and recedes, sounds that seem to bounce off distant walls. The echoing claps are its fingerprint.","By 1982 disco had been declared dead in the American mainstream, but in New York clubs it was mutating into something sparer and stranger. Levan was hearing Jamaican dub in the same years as synthesisers and cheap drum machines, and the Peech Boys record carries both. West End, a small New York dance label, released it. It was designed, first and foremost, to sound overwhelming on the Paradise Garage's sound system.","Listen to how the echo is used as an instrument rather than a garnish: the handclaps repeat and decay so that the rhythm seems to double itself. This is a dub producer's thinking, borrowed from Kingston studios, applied to a New York dance record. There are several mixes, and part of the fun is hearing how differently the same material can be arranged, with the vocal almost removed or pushed forward.","It was a club hit rather than a pop hit, but it became one of the defining sounds of the post-disco underground, now usually filed as 'garage' after the club that made it famous. Critics have since heard it as a missing link between disco and house, and between dub and dance music. Its bass sound in particular was widely imitated.","Start with the long twelve-inch mix, and wait for the bass. Pay attention to the handclaps and how they seem to ricochet. Then play New Order's Blue Monday from the following year for another record that grew out of the same New York clubs, and Frankie Knuckles's Your Love to hear where this spaciousness went next."],"juan-atkins-cybotron-clear":["An instrumental by Cybotron, Juan Atkins's duo with Rick Davis. A crisp, clattering drum machine sets a quick electro beat; a bubbling synthesised bass locks in underneath; above, a chilly, stately melody moves in long lines, like a signal broadcast across empty space. Sounds pan and flicker between the speakers. There are no vocals, only the sense of a clean, geometric machine-world. It lasts around five minutes and never raises its voice.","Atkins was a teenager in Belleville, outside Detroit, as the city's car industry collapsed. He and Davis, a Vietnam veteran obsessed with futurism, formed Cybotron in 1981 and borrowed the language of Alvin Toffler's The Third Wave, whose phrase 'techno rebels' would give the coming music its name. Kraftwerk were heard constantly on Detroit radio, especially on the DJ Electrifying Mojo's show. Clear appeared on Cybotron's album Enter, on the Californian label Fantasy.","Clear is electro rather than techno in the strict sense, closer to Afrika Bambaataa's Planet Rock than to the four-to-the-floor thump of later Detroit records, yet every idea of techno is already here. Two decades later Missy Elliott built her 2005 hit Lose Control on it, and a new generation heard the melody without knowing where it came from.","On release it was a regional record, admired by DJs and dancers in Detroit and in the electro scene. As histories of techno were written in the 1990s, Cybotron were recognised as the missing ancestor, and Clear is now routinely cited among the key recordings of early electronic dance music.","Hear Kraftwerk's Computer World first, then Clear, and notice what Atkins adds: funk syncopation, a darker bass, a more restless rhythm. Listen on headphones for the stereo movement. Then play Model 500's No UFO's to hear the same mind two years on."],"juan-atkins-model-500-no-ufos":["Recorded under Atkins's alias Model 500, this track pits a nervous, clipped drum-machine groove against a rubbery synthesised bassline and glassy chord stabs. A cool, treated voice drifts in and out, half sung, half spoken, circling the idea of being watched from above and of truths withheld. The rhythm is taut and twitchy, the mood paranoid but danceable. It is the moment electro's sci-fi imagery begins to settle into the sleek pulse that would soon be called techno.","After Cybotron split, Atkins founded his own label, Metroplex, in 1985, and No UFO's was among its very first releases. Owning the label meant owning the music and the name, a principle that became a Detroit creed. The city around him was emptying: factories closing, downtown hollowed out. Atkins, May and Saunderson were making records for a handful of clubs and high-school party crews, and for Chicago, where DJs were hungry for anything new.","Listen to how little the track uses. A few synth voices and a drum machine are made to sound huge by arrangement alone: parts drop out and return, the bass changes its pattern slightly, and the effect is of a machine thinking. The title's refusal of alien visitors reads as a Detroit joke, pointing attention back to earthly power and surveillance.","It sold modestly but spread quickly through Chicago and later Europe, and it is now usually named, alongside Cybotron, as the first true Detroit techno record. Later critics have admired its restraint; it sounds less dated than many flashier electronic records of the mid-1980s.","Focus first on the bassline and how it pulls against the drum pattern, then count how few sounds are actually playing at any moment. Headphones help with the treated voice drifting across the stereo field. Play Kraftwerk's The Man-Machine beforehand to hear what Atkins learned from, and Derrick May's Strings of Life afterwards to hear how quickly the Detroit sound grew more emotional."],"marshall-jefferson-move-your-body":["A pounding drum machine, a bouncing bassline, and then the piano: a rolling, gospel-flavoured riff hammered out in blocks of chords, repeated until it becomes a chant of its own. A voice calls the dancers to move and declares allegiance to house music itself. The track is built from a few elements looped and layered, with breakdowns where the piano stands almost alone. That church-trained piano riff, bright and insistent, is what everyone remembers.","Jefferson worked at the post office in Chicago and started producing after hearing house at clubs such as the Music Box. He bought equipment on credit and taught himself. Chicago house in 1986 was stark and drum-driven, made for DJs rather than radio; Trax Records, run by Larry Sherman, was pressing it cheaply and quickly. The subtitle's claim to be the house music anthem was part boast, part manifesto, and it stuck.","Jefferson has said that other producers told him piano did not belong in house; its presence here, played rather than programmed, was a deliberate stand. Trax was notorious for pressing records on poor, recycled vinyl, so early copies are often noisy, and part of the record's sound in memory is that grit.","It became one of the most played records of the Chicago scene and, once house crossed the Atlantic, of British clubs too. Its piano-led model shaped a whole strand of later dance music, from Italian 'piano house' to countless 1990s hits. Critics now rank it among house's founding texts, though it received little money or attention at the time.","Listen for the moment the piano enters and how it reshapes the whole groove. Then compare Frankie Knuckles's Your Love, darker and hypnotic, and Phuture's Acid Tracks, which Jefferson produced the next year and which could hardly be more different."],"frankie-knuckles-your-love":["A synthesised arpeggio, a pattern of notes cycling up and down, spins endlessly at the centre of this track, over a steady drum machine and a deep, sustained bass. Jamie Principle's voice enters soft and intimate, part confession, part prayer to a lover. The record is slow by later standards and deeply hypnotic; very little changes, and the tension comes from that sameness. The spinning arpeggio, instantly recognisable, is the sound of house at its most romantic.","Jamie Principle, a young Chicago songwriter, wrote and recorded the song in the mid-1980s, and Frankie Knuckles began playing Principle's tape at his club, the Power Plant, long before it existed on vinyl. Knuckles reworked it and it was released on Trax in 1987 under both names. Knuckles had come from New York in 1977 to DJ at the Warehouse, the club whose name gave the music its label, and was by now Chicago's most respected DJ.","Years later the British producers of The Source laid Candi Staton's gospel-soul voice, singing a different song entirely, over this instrumental; the resulting You Got the Love became a British anthem through the 1990s and beyond. Many people who know that hit have never heard the Chicago record underneath it. Listen also for how quietly the track begins; it was built for a DJ to blend in.","Its credit has long been debated, with Principle as writer and Knuckles as producer and champion, and later releases have sometimes shifted the billing. Critically there is little argument: it is regularly named among the finest house records ever made, and its tenderness showed early that house could be about intimacy as much as physical release.","Let it play for several minutes before judging it; its power is cumulative. Listen for how the voice floats over the machine pattern without ever pushing it. Then hear Donna Summer's I Feel Love for its ancestor, and The Source's You Got the Love for its afterlife."],"phuture-acid-tracks":["Twelve minutes of a drum machine and a single little bass synthesiser playing a short looping pattern. But the loop does not stay still: its tone is continually twisted, becoming by turns rubbery, squelching, whining, snarling, as if the machine were alive and struggling. There is no melody in the usual sense and almost nothing else happening. The entire drama is timbre, the colour of one sound mutating in real time. That squelch is the acid sound, and it starts here.","The Roland TB-303 had been sold in the early 1980s as a bass accompanist for guitarists practising alone; it sounded nothing like a bass guitar, flopped, and was dumped cheaply second-hand. Phuture, three young Chicago friends, bought one and discovered that turning its filter knobs while a pattern ran produced strange, vocal sounds. The DJ Ron Hardy played their tape at the Music Box, reportedly several times in one night. Marshall Jefferson helped produce the record for Trax.","Listen for the filter being moved by hand: the changes are not programmed but played, which is why they are so uneven and expressive. The track is almost a live performance on a failed consumer gadget. Many later 'acid' records programmed the same moves perfectly; the slightly clumsy human gestures here are part of its strangeness.","It baffled some dancers at first; others went wild. Within a year the acid sound had crossed to Britain and set off the 1988 'second summer of love', with its smiley-face iconography and moral panic. Phuture's record is now counted among the most influential dance tracks ever made, a founding document of acid house and, through it, of rave.","Listen once while doing nothing else, following only the 303. Notice each time the tone opens or closes. Then play Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians to hear another music where slow change within repetition is the whole point, and Orbital's Chime for where British rave took it."],"derrick-may-strings-of-life":["Released under May's alias Rhythim Is Rhythim, it opens with a piano figure, jagged and urgent, then orchestral string stabs crash in like a film score at its climax. Drums tumble and syncopate beneath rather than simply thumping. The track surges, stops dead, and surges again, with the strings calling out in short, dramatic bursts. It feels less like a dance track than a small symphony for machines. The first entrance of the strings, after the piano, is the unforgettable moment.","May, a friend of Juan Atkins since their teens in Belleville, launched Transmat in Detroit in 1986. He worked with cheap samplers and drum machines, sampling short fragments and rearranging them. Detroit in 1987 was a city in industrial collapse, and May has often described his music as an emotional response to its emptiness and to the machine age. In Chicago and then in Britain, the record was heard as a new kind of house with a heart of pure drama.","The piano figure is often said to derive from an improvisation by the keyboardist Michael James, which May cut into pieces and reassembled; if so, the record is a collage of a live performance rather than something programmed from scratch. Listen too for how often the drums disappear, leaving the strings hanging; those gaps are what make the returns so ecstatic.","It became an anthem in British clubs in 1988 and 1989, sometimes played by DJs who had no idea it came from Detroit. It is now probably the most celebrated Detroit techno record, and critics often point to it as proof that electronic dance music could carry emotion as intense as any orchestral score.","Wait for the strings and let them hit. Then listen again to the piano underneath; notice how uneven and human its rhythm is. Compare Juan Atkins's cooler Model 500 records, then the soaring strings of Underground Resistance's Knights of the Jaguar, a later Detroit echo."],"derrick-may-nude-photo":["A fast, skittering drum-machine rhythm, jittery hi-hats and a busy, bubbling synth line that seems to chase itself in circles. Chords flash on and off. The track is restless and nervy rather than euphoric, built from short cells that interlock and slide against each other, then drop away to reveal a bare groove. There are no words and no obvious chorus, just continual motion. It sounds like a city at night seen from a speeding car.","Nude Photo was one of the first releases on Transmat, the label May founded in Detroit, and he made it with his friend Thomas Barnett. Released under the Rhythim Is Rhythim name, it reached Chicago clubs quickly and then Britain, where the compilation Techno! The New Dance Sound of Detroit in 1988 first packaged the Detroit sound for Europe.","Listen to the percussion as closely as the synths. May programs the drums with a funk drummer's sense of anticipation, pushing hits just ahead of the beat, which is why the track feels so twitchy. The main synth line repeats but its accents shift as other layers enter and leave, so the pattern seems to change even when it does not.","It is less famous than Strings of Life, but many producers and critics consider it the purer statement of May's style, and it is a touchstone for the syncopated, funk-inflected side of Detroit techno. Its influence on British 1990s 'intelligent' techno was considerable.","Focus first on the hi-hats and how they drive the whole thing; tap along and you will find yourself landing slightly early, as May intended. Then follow the main synth line and notice how its accents seem to move as layers enter and leave. Play Strings of Life to hear the same producer in a grander mood, and Kraftwerk's Computer World for the machine aesthetic both grew out of."],"kevin-saunderson-inner-city-big-fun":["Released under the name Inner City, a bright, bouncing synth riff and a crisp drum-machine beat carry Paris Grey's clear, joyous voice. The song is simple: an invitation to join the party. But the backing is pure Detroit, with a hard, rolling bass and synth chords that hit like brass. It is techno reshaped as a three-minute-thrill pop song, and the riff under Grey's first entrance is the thing people hum.","Kevin Saunderson, youngest of the Belleville Three, launched KMS in 1987 and teamed up with Paris Grey, a singer from Chicago. The track was licensed to British labels at the moment house and acid house were exploding in the UK, and it became a British hit in the summer of 1988. For many British listeners it was the first Detroit record they ever heard, though they had no idea where it came from.","The instrumental version, without Grey, sounds far harder and closer to underground techno; Saunderson built a pop song on an underground frame, and the two coexist in the record. Listen for the moment the vocal drops out and the machines take over; the rave generation in Britain danced to exactly that switch.","Some Detroit purists at the time saw Inner City's pop success as a dilution; others saw it as proof that the new music could reach anyone. With hindsight it is widely regarded as a bridge between underground techno and mainstream dance-pop, and Saunderson as one of the most commercially astute of the pioneers.","Hear it next to Strings of Life from the year before: two very different ideas of what techno could be, one orchestral and stormy, one bright and sing-along. Listen under Paris Grey's voice for the hard, rolling bass, which is pure Detroit. Seek out the instrumental mix too, then play Inner City's Good Life, which followed within months."],"kevin-saunderson-inner-city-good-life":["Another Inner City song, this one warmer and more yearning than Big Fun. Paris Grey sings of a better life and a hopeful future over glistening synth chords, a gliding bassline and a steady, relaxed drum-machine groove. The chords shimmer and change underneath her like light on water. The mood is optimistic but tinged with longing, and the upward-lifting chorus is what stays with you.","Following the success of Big Fun, Saunderson and Grey released Good Life late in 1988, and it too became a British hit and an international club staple. It later anchored Inner City's album Paradise. Detroit itself was in deep economic decline; a song dreaming of the good life, made in that city, carries an undertow the lyrics never quite state.","Listen to how the harmony moves under the vocal; the chords are unusually rich for a dance record of the era, sliding through changes closer to soul ballad than to machine music. That harmonic warmth, not just the beat, is why the song has outlasted most of its rivals.","It has become one of the most enduring dance songs of the late 1980s, frequently remixed and revived. Critics now place it among the finest bridges between underground techno and dance-pop, and it helped establish the idea that Detroit could produce hits as well as cult records.","Listen for the chord changes under the chorus; they move more like a soul ballad than a dance record, and they are why the lift feels earned. Notice how relaxed the drum programming is compared with Big Fun. Then play Frankie Knuckles's Tears to hear Chicago reaching for the same tender mood, and Madonna's Ray of Light for how this sound entered 1990s pop."],"kevin-saunderson-reese-just-want-another-chance":["Released under the alias Reese, this is the dark twin of Saunderson's Inner City pop. A stark drum-machine pattern backs a bassline unlike anything before it: two slightly detuned tones rubbing against each other so the sound throbs, growls and seems to rotate in space. A voice fragment pleads for another chance. Almost nothing else happens. That menacing, churning bass is the whole record, and it would become one of the most copied sounds in electronic music.","In 1988 Saunderson was simultaneously scoring hits as Inner City and making heavy underground tracks under other names. Aliases let Detroit producers release more music and experiment without confusing audiences. This record was aimed squarely at club DJs, and its bass sound was designed to feel physical on a big system.","The bass sound became known simply as the 'Reese bass' after this alias. In the early 1990s British jungle and drum-and-bass producers seized on it, and through them it passed into dubstep and countless later genres. Many people have heard a 'Reese' without ever hearing Reese.","It was a modest underground release, and its reputation grew mostly after the fact, as producers traced the lineage of a sound that had become ubiquitous. It is now regarded as one of the most influential basslines in dance music.","Listen on good speakers or headphones, since laptop speakers lose almost everything here, and focus entirely on the bass: notice how it seems to wobble and spin as the two tones drift in and out of line. Then compare the pop sparkle of Inner City's Big Fun, made by the same man in the same year, and The Prodigy's Jilted Generation for the sound's British afterlife."],"frankie-knuckles-tears":["A slow, melancholy house track made with the Japanese producer Satoshi Tomiie and sung by Robert Owens. Soft piano chords, deep bass and a gentle drum-machine groove support Owens's rich, grieving voice, which sings of a love that has ended. The texture is spacious and elegant. Nothing is rushed. Where most dance records aim for euphoria, this one aims for heartbreak, and the piano's sad, circling chords are its signature.","By 1989 Knuckles was back in New York and house had become an international phenomenon, especially in Britain. The London label FFRR issued Tears as part of a wave of American house sold to British buyers. Tomiie was a young producer from Tokyo, and Owens a Chicago singer who had worked with Larry Heard. The track belongs to the 'deep house' current that brought jazz and soul harmonies back into the music.","Listen for how sparse the drum programming is; Knuckles leaves room for Owens's voice rather than filling every gap, a lesson learned from years of mixing soul records on the Warehouse's system. The piano part moves through richer harmonies than most 1980s house, a sign of Tomiie's jazz leanings.","It became a club classic and a template for a more sophisticated, song-based deep house, the strand later carried by producers such as Kerri Chandler. Critics consistently place it among Knuckles's finest productions, and among the records that showed house could express sorrow as convincingly as joy. It also launched Tomiie's long international career, an early sign that house was no longer only an American music.","Play it after Your Love to hear Knuckles's romanticism turn melancholy. Listen to how Owens phrases slightly behind the beat, letting lines trail over the bar, and to how the piano answers him in the gaps. It works best late at night at moderate volume. Then hear Inner City's Good Life for Detroit's warmer, more hopeful answer."],"orbital-chime":["A chiming melodic pattern, bright and bell-like, repeats over a skipping breakbeat and a warm, rounded bassline. The track builds slowly in layers, adding and removing sounds, and moves between rushing euphoria and quieter, reflective passages. It lasts about twelve minutes in its full form and never loses its sense of joyful discovery. The bell-like chime that gives it its name is the sound of British rave at its most innocent.","The brothers Phil and Paul Hartnoll recorded Chime at home in Sevenoaks, Kent, reportedly for next to nothing, and it came out first on a small label before FFRR reissued it in 1990 and it became a hit. They named the duo after the M25 orbital motorway that carried ravers to illegal parties in fields and warehouses around London. It was the high tide of rave, and the Conservative government was already moving against unlicensed parties.","When they performed it on television, the brothers reportedly wore T-shirts protesting the poll tax, a quiet sign of the politics that ran under British rave. Listen too for how much of the track is made from a single melodic cell, changed by adding voices and shifting the rhythm around it, a technique closer to minimalism than to pop song.","It was one of the first British rave records to become a genuine hit and made Orbital's name. It is still regarded as a defining record of its moment, and a proof that rave could be tender and melodic rather than merely hard.","Listen to the full twelve-inch version rather than an edit. Notice when the breakbeat drops out and the chime is left almost alone. Then compare Phuture's Acid Tracks to hear what the Chicago sound became once it crossed the Atlantic."],"frankie-knuckles-the-whistle-song":["A light, airy instrumental built around a playful melody on flute, whistling and fluttering over a smooth house groove. Soft keyboard chords and a gentle, rolling bass fill in beneath. It is cheerful, elegant and uncomplicated, closer to a gentle jazz-funk tune than to the stark machine tracks of early Chicago house. That bird-like flute tune is the whole identity of the record.","The track came from Knuckles's album Beyond the Mix, made for Virgin in the early 1990s after he had moved back to New York and joined David Morales in the Def Mix production team. Major labels were now signing house producers, and Def Mix remixes were polishing pop records for clubs. This record showed what house sounded like when it had money, studio time and live players.","Listen for how much of the track is played rather than programmed; the flute and keyboards breathe in ways a sequencer does not. That shift back toward live musicianship, in a genre born of drum machines, was part of what later came to be called 'soulful house'.","It was a big club hit and remains one of Knuckles's most loved records, though some critics found it too pretty and easy compared with the raw Chicago tracks of the 1980s. Today it tends to be heard as an early example of the polished, musical house of the 1990s.","Enjoy it for what it is: sunny, graceful dance music. Listen for the conversation between the flute and the keyboard, and for the moments the drums fall back and the tune floats alone. Then go back to Your Love to hear how far house had travelled in four years, from a hypnotic machine loop to something close to jazz-funk, and on to Daft Punk's Discovery for its sunny heirs."],"the-prodigy-charly":["Fast, chopped breakbeats, a buzzing synth riff and, cutting through them, a sample of a cartoon cat's meow and a child's voice from a British public-information film. The effect is manic and comic, a nursery rhyme dropped into a rave. The track moves quickly through sections, piling on sound. The meow, instantly recognisable to British listeners who grew up with the films, is its hook.","Liam Howlett made the track in Essex at the peak of the British rave boom, when records were being pressed and sold for weekend parties in fields and warehouses. The sample came from the Charley Says films, short safety cartoons shown on British television in the 1970s in which a cat warned children about strangers and dangers. XL Recordings released it in 1991, and it became a surprise hit.","The cartoon's safety messages, about not wandering off with strangers, took on a knowing double meaning at raves, where drug use was widespread; listeners heard both the childhood memory and the joke. Listen too for the breakbeats: they are chopped and sped up in a style that would soon grow into jungle.","Its success set off a rash of imitations sampling children's television, and critics derided the resulting 'toytown techno' as a cheap novelty. The Prodigy moved quickly away from the style, but the track is now remembered fondly as a period piece and a first sighting of their chaos.","Take it as a snapshot of 1991 rave: listen for the speed of the breakbeats and the delight in collage. Then hear Music for the Jilted Generation three years later to hear how much darker and more serious the band became."],"jeff-mills-waveform-transmission-vol-1":["An album of fast, relentless techno tracks built from pounding drum-machine kicks, clattering hi-hats and short, harsh synth loops that repeat and slowly mutate. There are no vocals and almost no melody; each track is a single idea pushed to its limit through repetition, with small changes creating huge tension. The effect is hypnotic and physical, like being inside a machine running flat out. The hard, dry sound of the 909 drum machine is everywhere.","Mills had left Underground Resistance and was working between Detroit and New York when Berlin's Tresor club and label, based in a vault beneath a former department store near the Berlin Wall's former line, released this album. Techno and the new reunited Berlin were growing up together, and Detroit producers found a devoted audience there. The album marks the moment Detroit techno turned harder and more minimal.","Listen to how Mills uses tiny changes: a loop may repeat for minutes with only a filter slowly opening or a hi-hat shifting position, and your ear starts to hear the change as dramatic. This is a DJ's music; many tracks were designed to be mixed and layered live rather than heard alone.","It became a touchstone for the harder, faster strand of 1990s techno, especially in Europe, and helped cement Mills's reputation as a producer of fierce minimalism. Some listeners find it abrasive and monotonous; admirers hear it as pure, disciplined intensity.","Play it loud and let the repetition work; resist skipping. Pick one sound in a track, a hi-hat or a synth stab, and follow it for several minutes: what seemed static turns out to be constantly shifting. Then compare Steve Reich's Drumming for another music where sustained repetition and gradual change create drama, and Robert Hood's Minimal Nation for the even sparer path it opened."],"underground-resistance-hi-tech-jazz":["Released under the alias Galaxy 2 Galaxy, it is a soaring, melodic techno piece built around a bright synth lead that sings like a jazz horn over lush, shifting chords and a propulsive drum-machine groove. It builds, breaks down, and builds again, with the melody lifting over each new entry of the rhythm. It is uplifting and cinematic rather than hard. That singing lead line, climbing and falling like an improvised solo, is the signature.","Underground Resistance, led by 'Mad' Mike Banks, positioned themselves as a masked, militant collective opposed to the major-label music industry and to the neglect of Detroit. Yet alongside their aggressive records they made music of great beauty, and Hi-Tech Jazz is the most famous. The Galaxy 2 Galaxy project aimed to link techno to the heritage of Detroit jazz and funk, and to Sun Ra's cosmic Afrofuturism.","The title is a manifesto: Banks was arguing that techno belonged to the lineage of black American music, especially jazz, rather than to European electronic music alone. In later live performances the group played the piece with musicians, including a saxophonist, making that connection audible.","It has become one of Detroit techno's most loved records, frequently cited as proof that the genre can be as emotionally expressive as any jazz or soul. Critics have increasingly read UR's work as part of an Afrofuturist tradition that runs from Sun Ra through Parliament.","Listen to the lead melody as if it were a trumpet solo. Then play Sun Ra's Space Is the Place and Parliament's Mothership Connection to hear the cosmic lineage UR claimed, and Strings of Life for another Detroit record reaching for the sky."],"orbital-orbital-2":["Orbital's second album, known by the colour of its cover, flows as a continuous journey through long, evolving tracks. Warm, layered synths, rolling breakbeats and slowly unfolding melodies build from quiet ambience to euphoric climax. Tracks such as Lush 3, Halcyon + On + On and Impact (The Earth Is Burning) move between the dancefloor and contemplation. Halcyon's shimmering, looping vocal sample and bell-like synths are the album's emotional centre.","By 1993 British rave had moved from illegal parties into licensed clubs and festivals, and a new 'intelligent' or 'album' electronic music was taking shape for home listening. The Hartnolls were becoming known as an extraordinary live act, playing sequencers and synthesisers on stage rather than miming. The Conservative government was drafting the law that would target outdoor raves the following year.","Halcyon is named after the tranquilliser Halcion, and the Hartnolls have said it was written for their mother, who had been dependent on it; knowing this, the track's aching beauty takes on a different colour. The album also opens with a phased spoken fragment about time becoming a loop, which links back to their first album; the two records seem to circle into each other.","It was widely praised on release and is now regarded as one of the defining British electronic albums of the 1990s. Their live performance of Halcyon at Glastonbury in 1994 became a landmark in the acceptance of electronic music at rock festivals.","Listen straight through; the album is designed as a single arc. Start with Halcyon if you want one track. Then hear Brian Eno's Music for Airports for the ambient mood, and Steve Reich for the method of slowly layered repetition."],"robert-hood-minimal-nation":["An album of radically stripped-down techno. Each track is built from a few elements: a steady kick drum, a ticking hi-hat, a single clipped chord or synth stab repeating in a tight, funky pattern. Almost nothing else. But the grooves are relentless, and tiny changes in accent and timing keep them alive. The music sounds skeletal, but it swings. Those short, repeating stabs, like a machine hiccuping in rhythm, are the sound of minimal techno.","Hood had been part of Underground Resistance and moved with Jeff Mills toward a leaner sound; Minimal Nation came out on Mills's Axis label. Hood has described minimalism as a response to Detroit itself, a stripping back to what was essential, a funk made from very little. The title named a subgenre that was about to spread across Europe, especially in Berlin and Cologne.","Listen for what is missing: basslines, melodies, breakdowns, vocals. Hood removes almost everything that dance music usually relies on, and yet the tracks remain funky because of precise rhythmic placement. The swing comes from how the hi-hats and stabs sit just off the beat, a drummer's sensibility carried into a machine.","On release it was a cult record; within a few years it was recognised as the foundation of minimal techno, a genre that dominated European clubs in the 2000s. Critics now treat it as one of the most important techno albums of the 1990s.","Pick one track and follow a single element, say the hi-hat, all the way through. Then compare Steve Reich's Clapping Music, another piece that finds drama in almost nothing, and Jeff Mills's Waveform Transmission for the hard sound it grew out of."],"the-prodigy-music-for-the-jilted-generation":["The Prodigy's second album is harder, darker and more aggressive than their early rave singles. Heavy, chopped breakbeats, distorted guitars, snarling synth riffs and sampled voices collide in tracks such as Voodoo People, Poison, No Good (Start the Dance) and the multi-part Narcotic Suite. The mood is defiant and cinematic, closer to rock than to dance-pop. The big, lurching breakbeats, heavy enough to rival a rock drummer, are its signature.","The album was made as the British government pushed through the Criminal Justice and Public Order Act of 1994, which gave police powers against outdoor gatherings with music defined, notoriously, by repetitive beats. Rave culture felt criminalised. Liam Howlett responded with an album that took the side of the ravers, and whose sleeve artwork showed a raver cutting a rope bridge between his world and the police.","The track Their Law, made with the band Pop Will Eat Itself, is a direct protest against the Criminal Justice Act. Listen also for the album's breakbeats: many are borrowed from old funk and rock records, chopped and reassembled so completely that the sources are almost untraceable.","It was widely acclaimed and marked The Prodigy's transformation from a rave novelty act into a serious album band. Many critics now regard it as one of the key British records of the 1990s, and as a bridge between rave and rock that anticipated big beat.","Start with Voodoo People, then play the album in order. Listen for how the guitars and breakbeats interact. Compare Public Enemy's It Takes a Nation of Millions for another protest record built on sampled beats, and The Fat of the Land for what came next."],"underworld-dubnobasswithmyheadman":["Underworld's breakthrough album blends long, hypnotic techno and house grooves with Karl Hyde's murmured, fragmentary vocals. Tracks like Dark & Long, Mmm Skyscraper I Love You, Cowgirl and Dirty Epic stretch out over many minutes, with shimmering synths, pulsing basslines and guitars washed in echo. Hyde's voice drifts through, repeating phrases like overheard conversation. The mood is urban, nocturnal, dreamlike. The half-sung, half-spoken vocal over a relentless groove is unmistakable.","Hyde and Rick Smith had been an unsuccessful synth-pop band in the 1980s before reinventing themselves with the young DJ Darren Emerson. They were also members of Tomato, a London design collective whose visual style shaped the band's look. The album was released on the independent Junior Boy's Own label as British electronic music was moving toward albums and a wider audience.","Hyde's lyrics were often assembled from notes of conversations overheard in the street, snatches of signs and advertisements, and fragments from his notebooks, cut up and repeated. Listen for how the words work as rhythm and colour rather than a story; the effect is of drifting through a city at night catching pieces of other lives.","It was widely hailed as a breakthrough in British electronic music, showing that techno could carry songs, words and emotional depth without losing its hypnotic power. Rock critics who had ignored dance music took notice, partly because Hyde sounded like a singer rather than a sampled diva. It is frequently named among the best albums of the decade, and it opened the way for the band's festival-headlining years.","Start with Dark & Long or Cowgirl and let it run; the tracks need their full length to work. Listen for the voice as another instrument, a rhythm and a colour rather than a story, and for the guitars smeared in echo underneath. Then compare Orbital's Brown Album for a sister record, and Born Slippy .NUXX for where Underworld went next."],"underworld-born-slippy-nuxx":["It opens with washes of shimmering synth chords, slow and ambient; then a pounding, galloping beat bursts in, and Karl Hyde's voice chants and stammers fragments of a night out, repeating and circling. The track keeps building, dropping back to its floating chords, then surging again. It is ecstatic and exhausting at once. The moment the beat crashes in after the ambient opening is one of the most famous in dance music.","It first appeared as a B-side in 1995, then was reissued in 1996 after the director Danny Boyle used it at the climax of his film Trainspotting, about heroin users in Edinburgh. It became a huge hit and a symbol of British 1990s culture. The title reportedly comes from the name of a greyhound on which the band once placed a winning bet.","Hyde's vocal was written as a portrait of drunken self-destruction, the voice of someone lost in a night of heavy drinking; it was never meant as a celebration. Many crowds nevertheless chant its most famous word as a party anthem, which the band have found both funny and troubling. Listen for the desperation under the euphoria.","The song became inseparable from Trainspotting and from the image of 1990s Britain, and is often voted among the greatest dance records. Critics have debated whether its massive popularity obscured its darker meaning, turning a portrait of a man drowning in drink into a stadium singalong. It also fixed a public image of Underworld that the band spent years gently resisting, while still closing concerts with it.","Listen from the start and wait for the beat to drop; the long ambient opening is the setup, not an intro to skip. Pay attention to the voice as a rhythm, stammering and circling rather than singing a tune. Then play Dubnobasswithmyheadman for the album-length version of Underworld's world, and The Prodigy's The Fat of the Land for its louder contemporary."],"jeff-mills-live-at-the-liquid-room":["A recorded DJ set by Jeff Mills at a Tokyo club, where he mixes dozens of techno tracks at astonishing speed. Records are cut in and out every minute or two, layered and flipped, sometimes with a drum machine played live on top. The flow is relentless and dense, a surging wall of rhythm. It is less a playlist than a new composition built in real time from other people's records. The sheer velocity of the mixing is the point.","Mills was famous in Detroit in the 1980s as 'The Wizard', a radio DJ whose rapid mixing amazed listeners. By the mid-1990s he was an international figure, especially in Japan and Europe. Mix albums were becoming a major format, but few captured the athletic, improvisational side of DJing as vividly as this one, recorded at the Liquid Room in Tokyo.","Mills often used three turntables and a drum machine, allowing him to layer records and add his own rhythms; listen for moments when a beat appears that is on no record at all. Several of the tracks in the mix are his own, reworked on the fly.","It is widely regarded as one of the great DJ mix albums, and a benchmark of technical skill. Some listeners find it too relentless for home listening; admirers regard it as a document of DJing as a live art comparable to jazz improvisation.","Listen loud and try to count how often the records change; you will lose count quickly. Notice the moments where two records run together and create a rhythm neither has alone. Treat it like a live jazz album rather than background music. Then compare Larry Levan's Paradise Garage set for a completely different idea of the DJ's art: slow, spacious, patient."],"jeff-mills-the-bells":["A short, relentless techno track built on a pounding kick drum and a bright, ringing synth pattern that sounds like bells or a carillon chiming in endless loops. The pattern repeats insistently, shifting subtly as other layers enter, and the effect is both hypnotic and joyful. It is simple, but the tension between the bell-like melody and the driving rhythm makes it irresistible. The chiming figure is instantly recognisable.","Mills released it on his Purpose Maker label, a vehicle for funkier, more club-functional tracks. By then he was one of the most influential DJs in the world, and The Bells became one of the most played techno tracks of the late 1990s. It fitted his approach: minimal material, maximum impact.","In 2005 Mills performed The Bells with the Montpellier Philharmonic Orchestra, translating the synth pattern into orchestral bells and strings; hearing that version reveals how close the track is to minimalist concert music. Listen for how the pattern changes by adding rather than altering notes.","It became a club staple and is now considered one of the classic techno tracks. Critics have noted its bridge between minimal techno and minimalist composition, and it is often cited as an example of how techno can be both simple and deeply engaging.","Listen to how the bell pattern interacts with the kick drum, and notice that the pattern grows by adding notes rather than changing them, so the melody seems to assemble itself. Then seek out the orchestral version from Mills's Montpellier concert, and compare Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians, and Robert Hood's Minimal Nation for the minimal Detroit sound it grew from."],"daft-punk-homework":["Daft Punk's debut album is raw, looping French house: filtered disco samples, buzzing synths, thumping drum machines and robotic vocoder voices. Tracks such as Da Funk, Around the World and Revolution 909 repeat simple ideas until they become hypnotic. The sound is gritty and playful, made to be played loud in clubs. The filter sweep, in which the sound seems to open and close like a door, is the album's signature.","Thomas Bangalter and Guy-Manuel de Homem-Christo made Homework in Bangalter's Paris bedroom. French house was emerging from Paris clubs and labels such as Roulé and Crydamoure, drawing heavily on Chicago house and disco. The duo took their name from a British music press review that had dismissed their earlier indie band as 'daft punky'. Virgin released the album internationally.","The track Teachers is a roll-call of the Chicago and Detroit producers the pair learned from, a thank-you note to the house and techno pioneers. Listen for the repeated names as a sign of how consciously Daft Punk saw themselves as students of American black dance music.","It was a major critical and commercial success, bringing French house to global attention and helping define the late-1990s dance boom, helped by inventive videos from directors such as Spike Jonze and Michel Gondry. Some reviewers found it too long and repetitive for home listening. Critics now regard it as a key album in the internationalisation of house music, and as the moment Paris joined Chicago and Detroit on the map.","Start with Around the World, then Da Funk. Listen for the filter sweeps: the sound seems muffled behind a door, then the door swings open. Notice how few elements each track uses, repeated with a DJ's patience. Then compare Frankie Knuckles's Your Love for the Chicago sound Daft Punk loved, and Discovery for where they went next."],"the-prodigy-the-fat-of-the-land":["The Prodigy's third album fuses breakbeat electronics with punk and metal aggression. Keith Flint and Maxim snarl over huge, distorted beats, grinding synth riffs and guitar samples in tracks such as Firestarter, Breathe and Mindfields. The sound is loud, abrasive and theatrical, closer to a rock album than a dance record. The combination of sirens, chopped breakbeats and screamed vocals is unmistakable.","By 1997 the Prodigy had become a major international act, and The Fat of the Land went to number one in both Britain and the United States, the height of the 'big beat' and 'electronica' boom. One track's title and video drew protests and bans for their confrontational imagery, adding to the band's notoriety.","Firestarter samples a guitar figure from The Breeders and a fragment from the Art of Noise; listen for the rock sources inside the electronics. Many of the album's guitar sounds are sampled rather than played, a sign of how thoroughly the Prodigy treated rock as raw material.","It was a huge commercial success and critically acclaimed, though some dance purists saw it as a sell-out to rock. It is now regarded as a defining album of the big beat era and as a bridge between electronic dance music and rock audiences.","Start with Breathe, where the call-and-response between the two vocalists sits over a lurching, stop-start beat. Listen for how the beats are built from chopped fragments and how often a 'guitar' is really a sample. Play it loud, as intended. Then compare Music for the Jilted Generation to hear the transformation, and Underworld's Born Slippy for a very different British dance anthem."],"underground-resistance-knights-of-the-jaguar":["Credited to DJ Rolando of Underground Resistance, it is built on a sweeping string-like synth melody, grand and melancholy, over a propulsive, driving techno beat. The melody rises in long phrases, repeated and layered, while the rhythm pushes forward relentlessly. It feels heroic and wistful at once. That soaring string line, sounding like a film theme, is the track's signature.","Rolando Rocha, a Mexican-American DJ from southwest Detroit, made the track as part of UR. The title refers to the Jaguar Knights, an elite warrior order of the Aztec Empire, a reference to his heritage and to UR's warrior imagery. The track became a massive European club hit. UR refused to license it to a major label, which then released a sound-alike version; the resulting dispute became a celebrated case of underground integrity against the industry.","Listen for how simple the melody is; its power comes from repetition and the way it floats over the beat. The track's popularity, and the major label's cover version, became part of its meaning: a battle between the independent Detroit ethos and corporate imitation.","It is regarded as one of the greatest Detroit techno tracks and a late flowering of the genre's melodic side. Critics also see it as a symbol of UR's refusal to compromise, and of the Latino presence in Detroit's music.","Wait for the melody to fully unfold; it arrives in stages, each repetition adding weight. Listen to how the drums never let up underneath, so the grandeur is always moving forward. It rewards a big system or good headphones. Then compare Strings of Life for its Detroit ancestor, Hi-Tech Jazz for another soaring UR record, and Vangelis's Blade Runner for a kindred synthetic melancholy."],"daft-punk-discovery":["Daft Punk's second album is brighter and poppier, drawing on disco, soft rock and 1980s pop. Filtered samples, vocoder vocals, guitar solos and glittering synths fill tracks such as One More Time, Aerodynamic, Digital Love and Harder, Better, Faster, Stronger. The mood is nostalgic and joyful, like a childhood memory of pop music. The heavily processed, auto-tuned vocal on One More Time is the album's signature sound.","The duo described the album as an attempt to recapture the feelings of childhood discovery, when they first heard pop and disco. They adopted robot helmets and a fictional backstory to hide their faces. The album was accompanied by the animated film Interstella 5555, made with the Japanese artist Leiji Matsumoto. One More Time features the vocals of the house singer Romanthony.","The album is built from samples of obscure 1970s and 1980s records, disguised by filtering and chopping: Harder, Better, Faster, Stronger uses Edwin Birdsong's Cola Bottle Baby, and Digital Love draws on George Duke. Kanye West later sampled Harder, Better, Faster, Stronger for his hit Stronger. Listen for the disguised sources.","Initially some critics found it too cheesy and pop-oriented, objecting to the soft-rock guitar solos and the heavily processed vocals that dance purists considered kitsch. Its reputation has grown enormously since. It is now widely considered one of the most influential albums of the 2000s, shaping electronic pop, EDM and hip-hop production, and its embrace of guilty-pleasure sounds now looks prophetic.","Start with One More Time, then Digital Love, and listen for how the vocoder makes a machine sound lovesick. Hear the guitar solo in Aerodynamic, closer to heavy metal than to house. Play the album in order; it flows like a mixtape. Then compare Chic's Good Times for the disco sound that inspired them, and Random Access Memories for their later tribute."],"daft-punk-random-access-memories":["Daft Punk's final studio album abandons samples in favour of live musicians, recreating the lush sound of 1970s disco and soft rock. Nile Rodgers's guitar, Nathan East's bass and top session drummers drive tracks like Get Lucky, Lose Yourself to Dance and Giorgio by Moroder, with guest vocals from Pharrell Williams, Julian Casablancas and Paul Williams. The sound is warm, expansive and meticulous. Nile Rodgers's rhythm guitar on Get Lucky is the album's hallmark.","Recorded in expensive studios in Los Angeles, Paris and New York, the album was a reaction against the laptop-produced EDM that was dominating dance music in the early 2010s. Daft Punk wanted to honour the musicians and studios of the disco era. Columbia released it in 2013, and it won the Grammy for Album of the Year.","On Giorgio by Moroder, the veteran producer tells his life story; the band recorded him with microphones from different decades, so the sound of his voice changes to match the era he is describing. Listen for that subtle shift in tone.","It was a massive commercial success and won major awards, and Get Lucky became one of the most played songs of its year. Some critics felt it was too nostalgic or overproduced, a museum piece for a vanished studio culture. Others heard a sincere argument for musicianship in a laptop age. It is now regarded as a celebration of dance music's history and of studio craft, and as the duo's farewell statement.","Start with Get Lucky, then Giorgio by Moroder, where Moroder's spoken story gives way to a long, building instrumental. Listen for the live drums and Rodgers's choppy rhythm guitar, and hear how the album sounds warmer than a sample-based record. Then compare Chic's Good Times and Donna Summer's I Feel Love for the sources they honour."],"aphex-twin-selected-ambient-works-85-92":["Thirteen instrumental tracks, most of them built from a slow techno pulse, a soft analogue bass line and one or two melodies that loop and drift rather than develop. 'Xtal' opens with a breathy, wordless pad over a lazy kick drum; 'Heliosphan' and 'Ageispolis' are brighter and more mechanical; 'Tha' stretches a murky groove past nine minutes. Everything sits under a faint layer of hiss, so the record sounds less like a club than like a bedroom at three in the morning.","Richard D. James made most of these pieces as a teenager in Cornwall, on cheap and modified synthesizers, recording straight to cassette. They were collected in 1992 by Apollo, the ambient offshoot of the Belgian techno label R&S, just as British rave was splitting into the dancefloor and the living room. Warp's 'Artificial Intelligence' series appeared the same year, and critics began to talk about electronic music made for listening rather than dancing.","The hiss is not a mastering fault that nobody caught; the album was taken from the cassette tapes, and the noise floor is part of the colour. Listen too for how little happens: many tracks repeat a four-bar idea for minutes, changing only a filter or one drum. 'We Are the Music Makers' borrows a line of film dialogue spoken by Gene Wilder, the only human voice on the record that says real words.","Reviewers in 1992 had few categories for it; 'ambient techno' was the label that stuck. Within a decade it had become a fixture on lists of the best electronic albums ever made, and it is often the first record people reach for when explaining what IDM meant before the term existed. The one lasting argument is over the word ambient itself, since most of these tracks keep a steady beat that Eno's ambient music avoids.","Start with 'Xtal' and 'Ageispolis' on headphones at moderate volume, and let the loops settle rather than waiting for a drop. Then compare Eno's Music for Airports, which has no pulse at all, to hear what James added. After that, Boards of Canada's Music Has the Right to Children shows where the same warm, worn sound went next."],"aphex-twin-selected-ambient-works-volume-ii":["Two and a half hours of drones, slow chords and dim, half-heard textures, almost entirely without beats. Some tracks are a single organ-like chord sequence repeated with infinite patience; others are metallic rumbles, distant voices or tones that seem to be leaking from another room. The piece fans call 'Rhubarb', a few soft synthetic chords rising and falling for seven minutes, is the one most listeners recognise, and it could almost be a hymn.","Released on Warp in 1994, when James was already known for abrasive, jokey techno, it confounded almost everyone. The tracks carried no titles, only photographs of textures and objects keyed to a pie chart on the sleeve, and fans later assigned names of their own. James told interviewers that some of the material came to him in lucid dreams, a claim that may be partly mischief but which shaped how the record was heard.","Because the tracks are untitled, the album works like one continuous installation, and it rewards being played in order rather than shuffled. Beneath several pieces are real acoustic sources slowed and smeared beyond recognition. 'Rhubarb' uses almost nothing: a short cycle of chords, no melody to speak of, no change of speed. It is the same wager Satie and Arvo Pärt made, that a few plain sounds left alone long enough will start to feel alive.","The music press was divided in 1994, with some reviewers calling it empty and others hearing a new, darker kind of ambient music than Eno's. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is now often preferred to the first volume by listeners who come from classical minimalism or drone music. Debate continues over how much of the dream story to believe, and over whether the whole thing should ever be heard in one sitting.","Play 'Rhubarb' first, then Pärt's Für Alina, and listen to how both let a few notes ring for as long as they need. Then try a whole disc late at night, lights low, at a volume just above silence. Morton Feldman's Rothko Chapel makes a revealing companion for the darker tracks."],"aphex-twin-richard-d-james-album":["A short record that crams sweet, almost nursery-rhyme melodies against drum programming so fast and fractured it sounds like a breakbeat being fed through a shredder. '4' opens with a floating keyboard tune over a skittering, impossibly busy rhythm; 'Girl/Boy Song' sets a tender string arrangement over jungle drums chopped into tiny slivers. The contrast between the lullaby on top and the chaos underneath is the whole idea.","By 1996 jungle and drum and bass were the sound of London, and James, now living there, took their chopped breakbeats and pushed the editing to an extreme that dancers could not follow. The style became known, half mockingly, as drill 'n' bass. Much of the album was programmed on a computer rather than played on hardware, which let him place drum hits at speeds no drummer or sampler performance could reach. His own grinning face filled the cover.","Several titles are Cornish place names, including 'Carn Marth' and 'Logan Rock Witch', a private map of the landscape he grew up in. Listen past the drums in 'Girl/Boy Song' and the melody is a simple, plaintive tune that would sit comfortably on a music box. In the fastest passages the drum hits are so close together that they stop being rhythm and become pitch, a buzzing tone of their own.","Received as James's most approachable record despite its violence, it sold well and turned the face on its sleeve into a kind of logo. Critics then and now split between those who hear real tenderness and those who hear a sophisticated joke. It is often cited as the moment IDM's technical ambition and its melodic warmth fitted together most tightly.","Begin with 'Girl/Boy Song' and follow only the strings the first time, then only the drums. After that, listen to '4' and notice how the melody never hurries while the percussion races. Compare Nancarrow's Studies for Player Piano, another composer who used a machine to play faster than hands can."],"aphex-twin-windowlicker":["A six-minute track that begins as a slinky, almost R&B groove, with a lazy swung beat and a honeyed synth line, then gradually warps. James's own voice, pitched and sliced until it sounds like a woman cooing, floats in and out. Midway the drums start to stutter, glitch and fold back on themselves, and the smooth surface curdles into something wonky and unsettling before sliding back into the groove.","Released as a single on Warp in 1999, it arrived with a long video by Chris Cunningham that parodied the conventions of hip-hop videos, pasting James's leering face onto a troupe of dancers. The video became a fixture of late-night music television. James had spent the late 1990s trading more and more on his public persona, and this was the peak of that absurdist image-making.","Put the end of the track through a spectrogram, a picture of sound frequencies, and a spiral appears; the B-side, titled with a mathematical formula, hides an image of James's face the same way. So the music contains pictures only a computer can see. The warped vocal is not a sampled singer but James himself, transformed.","It became one of the most widely heard pieces of experimental electronic music of its decade, helped by the video. Some critics hear a satire of commercial R&B; others hear a straightforward love of it bent through James's sensibility. The hidden images have become part of internet folklore, sometimes overshadowing the track itself.","Listen once without the video, following the drums as they move from smooth to stuttering and back. Then watch Cunningham's film. For the lineage, hear Donna Summer's I Feel Love, the machine-made groove this is partly descended from, and Afrika Bambaataa's Planet Rock."],"aphex-twin-avril-14th":["Two minutes of solo piano: a gentle, rocking left hand and a short, tender melody that repeats with small variations and then simply stops. There are no drums, no synthesizers and no processing. The sound is close and slightly muffled, with the mechanism of the instrument audible, as if you were sitting inside the piano. It sits on the double album Drukqs among some of James's most violent drum programming, which makes its quiet all the more striking.","Drukqs, released by Warp in 2001, alternated frantic electronic tracks with pieces for prepared piano and for a Yamaha Disklavier, a piano that can be played by a computer. James programmed the notes rather than performing them, then recorded the real instrument. Many tracks on the album carry Cornish-language titles, and James said the release was hurried along after he left an MP3 player full of unreleased music on a plane.","Because a machine is playing, the dynamics are unusually even and the rubato is written in, not felt, which gives the piece its slightly uncanny, music-box quality. Listen for the soft thuds of the dampers and keys, left prominent in the mix. Kanye West built his 2010 song 'Blame Game' on this music, which introduced it to millions who had never heard of Aphex Twin.","Drukqs was received coolly in 2001, often called overlong and unedited, but this short piano piece escaped the criticism and became James's most widely known composition, used in films, adverts and countless cover versions. Pianists argue over whether to play it with the flat evenness of the original or to add the expressive shaping it seems to invite.","Listen first on headphones to hear the mechanism. Then play Satie's first Gymnopédie and notice the same rocking accompaniment and unhurried melody. Nancarrow's player-piano studies show the opposite use of the same idea, a machine playing what no human could."],"autechre-amber":["Eleven long instrumentals of slow, heavy, softly lit electronic music. The beats are there but restrained, often a muffled kick and a clicking pattern at walking pace; above them hang mournful, slowly shifting synthesizer chords. 'Foil' opens with a clanking rhythm that seems to be searching for its own groove, and 'Nil' closes with a long, warm, sad drift. The overall colour is exactly what the title suggests: something ancient, glowing and preserved.","Rob Brown and Sean Booth, two friends from Rochdale near Manchester, had grown up on electro and hip-hop mixtapes before Warp signed them. Their early tracks appeared on the label's 1992 'Artificial Intelligence' compilation, which framed a new kind of electronic music for home listening. Amber was their second album, and its sleeve showed an eroded rock landscape in Cappadocia in Turkey, a picture of slow geological time.","The rhythms are more complex than they first sound: several tracks layer patterns of different lengths so that the downbeat quietly drifts. Listen also for how often a track stays on one or two chords for its whole length, letting texture rather than harmony carry the change. The hip-hop breakbeat, the duo's first love, is still audible, but slowed down and blurred.","Amber was well received but overshadowed at the time by Aphex Twin's more flamboyant releases. It has since become the Autechre album most often recommended to newcomers, precisely because their later, harder abstraction has not yet arrived. Fans debate whether it is their warmest record or merely their least characteristic one.","Play 'Montreal' and 'Silverside' first; both balance melody and machinery perfectly. Listen in one go, as an album, in the evening. Compare Aphex Twin's Selected Ambient Works 85–92 for a brighter version of the same moment, and Kraftwerk's Computer World for the source of the clean electronic pulse."],"autechre-tri-repetae":["Harder and colder than Amber: ten tracks of metallic, echoing percussion and spare synthesizer lines, the sound of machinery in an empty hall. 'Dael' opens with a lopsided, clanging rhythm that keeps promising to settle; 'Clipper' is a long, grinding study in a single repeated figure; 'Rotar' and 'Leterel' pare the melodies down to a few notes. The music is repetitive in the way a factory is repetitive, but it keeps slightly changing shape.","Recorded in 1995 as Brown and Booth were acquiring more hardware and taking their studio sound in a more industrial direction, it came out on Warp that November. In America the album was expanded with two EPs as Tri Repetae++. British electronic music was splitting into factions, and Autechre were placing themselves firmly on the side of structure rather than the party.","The title suggests threefold repetition, and repetition with tiny changes is the method: listen for the moment a percussion sound shifts in timbre or position while everything else stays still. The reverb is part of the composition, not decoration; many tracks are as much about the space around the sounds as the sounds themselves.","It is now regarded by many fans and critics as Autechre's finest work of the 1990s, the point where they left dance-music conventions behind without yet going fully abstract. Some listeners find it austere and airless; others hear a hard, physical kind of beauty. It is often compared with the process music of Steve Reich.","Play it loud on good speakers rather than headphones so the reverb fills the room. Start with 'Dael' and follow one percussion sound through the whole track. Then listen to Reich's Piano Phase, where the same idea of gradual shift inside repetition is played by human hands."],"autechre-confield":["Nine tracks in which rhythm stops behaving like rhythm. Beats accelerate, decelerate, jam and splinter; sounds bounce around like objects in a closed box. 'VI Scose Poise' opens with a clean, almost pretty melody that is then broken apart by stuttering percussion, and 'Pen Expers' is a relentless, lurching pattern that never repeats exactly. There are no loops in the ordinary sense; the music feels as if it is thinking in real time.","By 2001 Brown and Booth were building their own software environments, using programs such as Max to create systems of rules that generated rhythms and textures as they played. They performed and recorded with these systems, adjusting parameters rather than writing notes. The laptop had just become a viable instrument, and the album arrived at the height of glitch and computer music's crossover into rock and pop circles.","Many of the patterns were not written but generated: the duo set conditions, such as how one drum should respond to another, and let the machine decide. That is why no two bars are quite the same. It is the same basic idea as Xenakis's use of mathematics or Cage's chance operations, arrived at from the dancefloor rather than the conservatory.","Confield divided listeners sharply. Some reviewers called it unlistenable, others the most radical record Warp had released. It is now widely seen as a turning point after which Autechre stood closer to the avant-garde of Stockhausen and Xenakis than to techno. The debate over whether it is music to feel or only to admire continues.","Start with 'VI Scose Poise', whose opening melody gives you something to hold. Treat the rest like a piece of contemporary chamber music rather than a dance record. Pair it with Xenakis's Persephassa or Stockhausen's Kontakte to hear the older lineage of rule-built sound."],"boards-of-canada-music-has-the-right-to-children":["An hour of slow, warm, slightly out-of-tune electronic music built on lazy hip-hop beats, wobbling synthesizer melodies and fragments of children's voices. 'Roygbiv' is a short, bright, instantly memorable tune; 'Turquoise Hexagon Sun' and 'Aquarius' rest on thick, soft drums; 'Olson' is a brief wistful fragment. Everything sounds as if it had been left in the sun on an old tape, the pitch wavering as the tape stretches.","The Scottish brothers Michael Sandison and Marcus Eoin had worked privately for years in the Pentland Hills near Edinburgh before Warp and the smaller Skam label released this in 1998. Their name comes from the National Film Board of Canada, whose educational films they watched as children during time spent in Canada. The album arrived just as sampling technology made it easy to rebuild the sound of old recordings.","The wavering pitch is deliberate: the brothers ran material through tape and old equipment to mimic the wow and flutter of worn cassettes and film soundtracks, so the music sounds like memory. Several tracks contain voices from children's television and documentary sources, and the sleeve's faded family photograph has the faces bleached out. The unease beneath the nostalgia is easy to miss on first hearing.","Critics praised it immediately, and it has grown into one of the most influential electronic records of its era, a source for chillwave, hauntology and much lo-fi hip-hop. Some listeners hear pure comfort; others find it quietly disturbing, a childhood recalled slightly wrong. That mixture of innocence and menace remains the thing people argue about.","Start with 'Roygbiv', then 'Turquoise Hexagon Sun'. Listen for the pitch drift in held notes, the signature of the whole record. Then hear Delia Derbyshire's The Dreams and her Doctor Who theme: the BBC Radiophonic Workshop's sound of 1960s and 70s British childhood is the ghost this album is chasing."],"boards-of-canada-geogaddi":["Twenty-three tracks, many under two minutes, that take the warm sound of the first album into darker territory. 'Music Is Math' sets a slow, heavy beat under a queasy, detuned melody and a child's voice counting; 'Dawn Chorus' is sunlit and hazy; 'Gyroscope' is claustrophobic. Short interludes of drones and voices connect the longer pieces, so the whole plays like a single, unsettled dream.","The brothers spent about four years making it in their rural studio, releasing it on Warp in early 2002 after a long near-silence. By now their first record had a devoted following that pored over every detail, and they responded with an album full of hints, numbers and references to nature, mathematics and the occult, seemingly designed to be decoded.","The album runs sixty-six minutes and six seconds, and that is no accident. It is full of patterns drawn from spirals, the golden ratio and the Fibonacci sequence, and listeners have found fragments that carry reversed voices. Titles such as 'A Is to B as B Is to C' point to proportion. The childlike surface hides a record about order, belief and fear.","Received warmly, though some reviewers found it too long and too uniform. Its reputation has risen as listeners have taken its darkness seriously, and it is often named alongside the debut as their best work. The decoding culture it inspired online, with fans mapping every number, is now part of how the record is known.","Listen straight through with no shuffle; the interludes matter. Start by noticing the running time, then listen to 'Music Is Math' for the way a simple beat becomes ominous. Pair it with Laurie Spiegel's Kepler's Harmony of the Worlds for older music built from mathematical ideas about nature."],"boards-of-canada-tomorrows-harvest":["Seventeen tracks that sound like the soundtrack to a lost science-fiction film about the end of the world. The warm nostalgia of earlier records gives way to bleak, pulsing synthesizer lines, cold arpeggios and long, desolate drones. 'Reach for the Dead' builds from a slow, sad theme to a heavy, rolling beat; 'Cold Earth' and 'Jacquard Causeway' are grim and hypnotic. The mood is of a deserted landscape after something terrible has happened.","Released on Warp in 2013 after eight years without an album, it was announced through an elaborate puzzle: a secret record slipped into a shop on Record Store Day, then numerical codes broadcast on radio, television and online that fans had to combine to unlock the release date. The hunt became a news story in its own right, a sign of how much mystique the brothers had built by saying almost nothing.","The palette is deliberately borrowed from late-1970s and early-1980s film scores, the synthesizer music of John Carpenter and Vangelis, a new source for the brothers. Titles hint at a story of famine, surveillance and collapse. The sleeve shows a city skyline bleached almost white, as if seen through heat haze or something worse, and the tracks move like scenes cut from a film you never see.","Critics welcomed it as a strong return, if less surprising than their early records. Some found it too consistent in mood; others heard their most coherent album, a single sustained vision. It is often described as a landscape record for an age of ecological anxiety, and it drew many younger listeners back to the brothers' earlier work.","Play it in one sitting, preferably while travelling or at dusk, and begin with 'Reach for the Dead'. Notice how rarely the beat arrives, and how much weight it carries when it does. Then hear Vangelis's Blade Runner score, the earlier synthesizer language this album echoes in spirit, and Tangerine Dream's Rubycon."],"squarepusher-feed-me-weird-things":["The debut: long tracks in which frantic, hyper-edited jungle breakbeats run under fluid, jazzy electric bass lines and glowing synthesizer chords. 'Squarepusher Theme' sets a funky, walking bass line over drums that race and stutter; 'Tundra' is spacious and melancholy, all cold pads and distant beats. The combination of a real bassist's phrasing and a programmer's impossibly fast drumming is what makes it unlike anything else of its year.","Tom Jenkinson, a young bass player from Essex who had grown up on jazz fusion and rave, was signed to Rephlex, the label co-founded by Aphex Twin. The album came out in 1996, when drum and bass was at the height of its fame and jazz was seeping into it from several directions. Warp signed Jenkinson soon afterwards, and the record has since been reissued there.","The bass is played, not programmed; listen for slides, ghost notes and small timing variations that no machine would produce, set against drums placed with inhuman precision. Jenkinson's debt to Jaco Pastorius, the fusion bassist of Weather Report, is audible in the singing, melodic lines high on the neck. The whole record is a duet between a human and a sequencer played by the same person.","It was received as a striking debut and an unusual meeting of jazz musicianship and electronic production. It is often ranked just behind his next record, though some fans consider its rawness more exciting. The question of whether its jazz is homage or parody has never quite been settled, and jazz critics have mostly left it alone.","Start with 'Squarepusher Theme' and follow only the bass, then go back and follow only the drums. Then hear Jaco Pastorius's solo debut and Weather Report's Heavy Weather to find the source of that fluid, singing bass tone, and Aphex Twin's Richard D. James Album for the drum side of the family."],"squarepusher-hard-normal-daddy":["Jazz fusion played through a rave sound system: glossy electric-piano chords, virtuoso bass runs and drum programming so fast and detailed it seems to be improvising. 'Beep Street' opens with a bright, looping groove before the breaks begin to fracture. Other tracks slow into dreamy, 1970s-flavoured passages of keyboard harmony. It is the most playful and melodic of his early records.","His first album for Warp, released in 1997, it arrived as drill 'n' bass was briefly fashionable in British electronic music. Jenkinson was still in his early twenties, working alone at home, programming every drum hit and playing the bass parts himself. The record drew heavily on the fusion of the 1970s: Miles Davis's electric bands, Weather Report and Herbie Hancock.","The keyboard chords are voiced like a 1970s fusion record, rich and extended, while the drums are chopped from breakbeats at a speed no drummer could play. That collision between two decades is the whole joke and the whole beauty. Listen for moments when bass and drums lock into unison, a flash of perfect coordination between a human and a machine.","It is often cited as Squarepusher's best record and one of the high points of drill 'n' bass. Critics in 1997 enjoyed its humour; later listeners have taken its musicianship more seriously. Jazz critics have been more sceptical, hearing pastiche rather than real improvisation, and that argument sits at the heart of how he is judged.","Start with 'Beep Street'. Listen for the bass first, then the drums, then how they fit together. Compare Miles Davis's Bitches Brew, where the same fusion textures were improvised by a band in a room, and the Mahavishnu Orchestra for the speed and sheen."],"plaid-not-for-threes":["Bright, intricate, cheerful electronic music: chiming synthesizer melodies, clicking and popping rhythms, interlocking patterns that sound like mechanical toys. Björk sings on 'Lilith', a slow, dark and breathy song, and other guest voices appear, but most tracks are instrumental. Compared with Autechre, Plaid are melodic and warm; compared with Aphex Twin, they are tidy and precise, like a small chamber group of synthesizers.","Ed Handley and Andy Turner had been two-thirds of The Black Dog, a pioneering British home-listening techno group, before concentrating on Plaid. Not for Threes, released in 1997, was their first album for Warp and arrived in the label's richest period. They had already worked with Björk, which is how she came to sing on this record, a sign of how closely IDM and adventurous pop were then connected.","Many of the rhythms are built from overlapping patterns of different lengths, so the accent shifts from bar to bar, a technique shared with Steve Reich and with West African drumming. Listen for how the melodies are handed between synthesizer voices like instruments in a chamber group, each taking a phrase and passing it on.","It was well received, though Plaid have always stood somewhat in the shadow of their Warp colleagues. Many listeners now regard them as one of the most consistently melodic groups in IDM, and this album as their breakthrough. Critics sometimes find them too polite; admirers answer that craft and charm are rare in the genre.","Start with 'Lilith' for Björk, then move to the instrumental tracks and follow a single melody as it passes around. Compare Björk's Homogenic, released the same year, and Reich's Drumming, where the interlocking-pattern idea is played by live percussionists on bongos and marimbas."],"mu-ziq-lunatic-harness":["Big, lush, hummable synthesizer melodies over furiously edited jungle drums. 'Hasty Boom Alert' sets a soaring, almost orchestral tune against breakbeats chopped to shrapnel; other tracks are slower and more wistful, with long string-like pads. Where much IDM is cool and cerebral, this is emotional and even bombastic, closer to a film score played over a drum-and-bass rhythm section.","Mike Paradinas, a London producer, had been releasing records since the early 1990s and was a friend and sometime collaborator of Aphex Twin. Lunatic Harness appeared in 1997, when drill 'n' bass was briefly in fashion. Paradinas had founded the label Planet Mu in 1995, which he later ran independently; it became one of the most important homes for electronic music, from IDM to Chicago footwork.","Paradinas often builds melodies from a few very simple notes, repeated and harmonised in thick chords, which is why they are so memorable. Listen for how the melody stays steady and slow while the drums race around it. The drums themselves are edited with enormous care: every hit is placed by hand.","Reviewers in 1997 saw it as a highlight of the drill 'n' bass moment, and it has kept a strong reputation. Later listeners sometimes find it overripe; fans find it more emotionally direct than anything else in the genre. Paradinas's influence as a label owner has sometimes overshadowed his own music.","Start with 'Hasty Boom Alert'. Listen to the melody first and treat the drums as a storm around it. Then hear Aphex Twin's Richard D. James Album, from the previous year, to compare two very different temperaments working in the same style."],"four-tet-rounds":["Warm, intricate music built from acoustic samples: plucked harps, bells, acoustic guitar, hand percussion, snatches of jazz drumming, all chopped and looped on a laptop. 'She Moves She' and 'My Angel Rocks Back and Forth' are gentle and intimate; 'Spirit Fingers' is jittery and bright; 'As Serious as Your Life' is propulsive. The record feels handmade, like electronic folk music played by one person at a kitchen table.","Kieran Hebden, a London musician who also played guitar in the post-rock band Fridge, made Rounds on a laptop at home and released it on Domino in 2003. The word 'folktronica' was coined partly to describe his sound, a label he disliked. Laptop production had just made it possible for one person to assemble a whole record from fragments of acoustic instruments.","Many of the drum patterns are sampled from jazz records and re-edited so they stumble and swing unpredictably; Hebden is a devoted fan of free and spiritual jazz. Listen for how acoustic sounds are looped with slight irregularities so they never quite repeat. The title 'As Serious as Your Life' borrows the title of Valerie Wilmer's classic book on free jazz.","Rounds was widely praised and has come to be seen as one of the defining electronic albums of the 2000s. Some critics found it too pretty; many musicians cite it as a model for the warm, sample-based electronic music of the following decade. Its reputation has only grown as the folktronica tag has faded.","Start with 'She Moves She' and listen for the harp and the loose jazz drums underneath. Then hear Alice Coltrane's Journey in Satchidananda for the harp and spiritual-jazz mood Hebden loves, and Reich's Music for 18 Musicians for the shimmer of many small patterns at once."],"four-tet-there-is-love-in-you":["A gentler, more dance-oriented record than Rounds. 'Angel Echoes' opens with a wordless female vocal fragment looped and layered into a shimmering chorus; 'Love Cry' is a nine-minute build over a steady, patient beat; 'Sing' and 'Plastic People' drift and pulse. The acoustic samples are still there, but now set to a house tempo, with long, gradual arcs of change.","By 2010 Hebden had become a club DJ with a residency at the London venue Plastic People, and his music had moved closer to the dancefloor. He had also begun collaborating with Burial, and the ghostly treatment of voices on this record shows that exchange. It was released on Domino in January 2010, just as British club music was dissolving the line between home listening and dancing.","'Love Cry' takes its title from an Albert Ayler album, a signal of the free-jazz devotion behind the calm surface. Listen to 'Angel Echoes' for how a single short vocal fragment is turned into a whole choir by layering and pitch-shifting. 'Plastic People' is named after the club where much of this music was first tested on a crowd.","It was widely praised as his most accomplished record, a bridge between his earlier home-listening music and the dancefloor. Some fans missed the rougher edges of Rounds; others heard a new maturity and patience. It is often credited with helping bring gentle, emotional house music back into critical favour after a decade of harder club sounds.","Start with 'Angel Echoes', then 'Love Cry'. Let 'Love Cry' play through without skipping: its change is slow, and the reward is in the last minutes. Compare Burial's Untrue for the same ghostly vocal technique used in a darker way, and Caribou's Swim from the same year."],"burial-burial":["Dark, rainy, sub-bass-heavy music at a slow dubstep tempo, full of crackle, hiss and fragments of distant voices. 'Distant Lights', 'Southern Comfort' and 'Night Bus' sound like south London at night: rain, empty streets, a half-heard song from a passing car. The beats are loose and shuffling rather than tight, and the bass is felt as much as heard.","William Bevan made the album in south London and released it in 2006 on Hyperdub, the label founded by the producer Kode9. He stayed anonymous for the first years of his career, refusing interviews and photographs until a tabloid threatened to unmask him in 2008, when he posted a picture of himself online. Dubstep was emerging from London's underground garage scene, and Burial was its most mournful voice.","Bevan built the tracks in a simple sound-editing program rather than a sequencer, placing sounds by eye without a grid, which is why his beats feel loose and human. The vinyl crackle and rain are part of the composition, not decoration. He has said that some of the clicks and textures came from video games he played.","The album was widely praised and placed high on many critics' lists for 2006. It was quickly seen as a defining document of dubstep, though Burial has always stood at an angle to the genre. Some critics hear pure melancholy; others hear a lament for the rave culture of the 1990s that Bevan was too young to have fully known.","Listen at night, ideally while travelling through a city, on headphones with decent bass. Start with 'Distant Lights', then 'Night Bus'. Compare Eno's Ambient 4: On Land, an earlier record that also turns a remembered place into sound, and Radiohead's Kid A for another dark turn of the same period's mood."],"burial-untrue":["Burial's second album brings voices to the front: fragments of R&B singing pitched up and down until they sound genderless and ghostly, floating over shuffling two-step garage rhythms and deep bass. 'Archangel' is the best-known track, a lovelorn vocal loop over a limping beat; 'Near Dark' and 'Etched Headplate' are darker and more fragmentary. The whole album sounds like someone walking home alone after a night out.","Released on Hyperdub in November 2007, it was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize in 2008, and the attention sent tabloids after Bevan's identity. Dubstep was by now a recognised genre, and Untrue took its bass and space but mixed them with the swing of UK garage, a sound Bevan had loved as a teenager through an older brother's records and pirate radio.","The vocal samples come from R&B and pop recordings, but they are so transformed that most are nearly impossible to identify; the pitch-shifting makes men sound like women and women like children. The beats are placed by hand, without a grid, so the rhythm has a subtle, human drag. Listen for the sound of lighters, rain and footsteps between the drums.","Untrue was widely acclaimed and is now often named among the most important electronic albums of the century. Its influence runs through British electronic music, pop production and even film scoring. Critics still debate whether it is a dance record, an ambient record or something else entirely, and whether its nostalgia is for a real scene or an imagined one.","Start with 'Archangel' and listen for how the vocal loop bends and returns. Then play the album straight through on headphones late at night. Arvo Pärt's Cantus in Memoriam Benjamin Britten makes a surprising companion: another piece that turns grief into a descending line repeated until it becomes weather."],"jon-hopkins-immunity":["An hour-long arc from dense, pounding techno to quiet piano. 'Open Eye Signal' and 'Collider' are long, relentless, distorted tracks with a physical, almost crushing weight; the second half turns inward with 'Abandon Window' and 'Form by Firelight', where slow piano chords emerge; the title track closes with a hushed song sung by the Scottish songwriter King Creosote. The shape is like a night out and the long walk home.","Jon Hopkins, a classically trained pianist from south-west London, had played keyboards for Imogen Heap and worked alongside Brian Eno before making Immunity, released on Domino in 2013. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize that year. Home studios now allowed a single producer to make music with the weight and detail of a full band, and Hopkins used that freedom to shape a whole album as one piece.","Many of the percussion sounds began as everyday noises recorded in and around Hopkins's studio, struck and handled objects processed until they hit like drums. The album was conceived as a single journey, and tempo and intensity are carefully mapped across its hour. The piano in the second half is played softly with the pedal held down, so the notes blur into each other.","Immunity was one of the most acclaimed electronic records of its year, and many listeners came to it from outside dance music. Some critics found its dramatic arc too polished; others heard a model of how to make an electronic album that works as a whole. It is often credited with bringing techno to audiences who otherwise heard ambient and modern classical music.","Play it through in one go, ideally loud, and notice the shift in the middle from heavy to gentle. Start with 'Open Eye Signal' if you can only hear one track. Then compare Arvo Pärt's Spiegel im Spiegel for the stillness of the closing piano, and Eno's Apollo for the ambient side of Hopkins's education."],"jon-hopkins-singularity":["A sequel in shape to Immunity: a first half of massive, surging techno and a second half that dissolves into ambient drift and piano. 'Emerald Rush' rides a bright, rising synthesizer figure over a thudding beat; 'Everything Connected' is long, dense and euphoric. Then 'Feel First Life', sung by a small choir, wordless and hymn-like, opens the quiet half, and the closing piano pieces are slow and luminous.","Hopkins wrote the album after years of interest in meditation and altered states of consciousness, and presented it as music about connection between mind and world. It was released on Domino in 2018, five years after Immunity had made him one of the best-known British electronic musicians. By then the border between electronic music and contemporary classical composition had become very porous, and he moved freely across it.","Hopkins has described the album as beginning and ending on the same note, so the record forms a closed loop and can be played round and round. Listen for how the choir in 'Feel First Life' is treated almost like a synthesizer, the voices held and blended until the words no longer matter. The two halves mirror each other in length and shape.","It was widely praised, though some critics found it too close in design to Immunity. Many listeners regard its first half as among the most powerful electronic music of the decade, while the choral and piano pieces divided opinion between those who heard depth and those who heard spa music.","Play 'Emerald Rush' loud, then 'Feel First Life' quietly, and notice how the album earns its calm. Compare Ligeti's Lux Aeterna, a choral piece of pure, suspended sound, and Pärt's Summa for the plainness of the closing mood. Then go back to Immunity and hear how the same two-part plan has grown bigger."],"oneohtrix-point-never-replica":["Short loops cut from television adverts of the 1980s and 1990s, slowed, repeated and set against drifting synthesizer chords and a few piano notes. The title track is a slow, melancholy piano loop that circles like a memory you cannot quite place; 'Andro' is dense and hazy; 'Sleep Dealer' is a stuttering collage of breaths and syllables. Old commercial music, made to sell, becomes strangely sad and uncanny.","Daniel Lopatin, working in Brooklyn, released the album in 2011 on Software, the label he had co-founded. He built much of it from compilations of old TV adverts, hunting through them for fragments of music and speech. Internet culture was then preoccupied with nostalgia for late-twentieth-century commercial media, and Replica gave that preoccupation a serious artistic form, halfway between ambient music and collage.","The adverts are chopped so finely that their products are almost never named; you hear a breath, a vowel, a scrap of jingle. Listen for how a loop repeats just long enough to become hypnotic, then changes. The name Oneohtrix Point Never is itself a found sound: a pun on the frequency of a Boston radio station Lopatin grew up hearing.","Replica was widely praised and is now often seen as an important work in the history of sample-based ambient music, and a key record of what critics called hypnagogic music. Some found it too clever by half; others heard a genuine meditation on memory and consumer culture, the way advertising lodges in us like folk song.","Start with the title track and follow the repeated piano fragment as it shifts. Then hear Steve Reich's Different Trains, where recorded speech becomes melody, and Boards of Canada's Music Has the Right to Children for an earlier record about media memory."],"oneohtrix-point-never-r-plus-seven":["Glassy, fragmented music built from the stock sounds of digital instruments: synthetic choirs, bell tones, bright plucked presets. Tracks such as 'Boring Angel', 'Zebra' and 'Still Life' move abruptly between ideas, cutting from a sustained chord to a burst of arpeggios to sudden silence. The result feels like a gallery of digital surfaces, beautiful and slightly hollow, with no beat to hold on to.","Lopatin's first album for Warp, released in 2013, it moved away from the sample collages of Replica towards a deliberately artificial palette. He was increasingly working with visual artists and on film, and the album shares their interest in the look and feel of digital technology: screensavers, stock imagery, the default settings of software. It marked his move from the underground into wider view.","Many sounds are the factory presets that come with software and keyboards, the very sounds most musicians avoid as clichés. Lopatin uses them precisely because they are cheap and familiar, and frames them so they sound strange and even holy. Listen for the abrupt cuts that break any sense of flow; the silences are part of the composition.","It was widely praised, though some listeners found it cold and arch. It is now seen as a key record in experimental electronic music's turn towards digital surfaces, and as the bridge to Lopatin's later work in film and pop. Whether its detachment is critique or pleasure is still argued.","Start with 'Zebra' and listen for the cuts, then 'Boring Angel' for the synthetic choir at its most hymn-like. Treat the silences as part of the music. Compare Yellow Magic Orchestra's BGM, another record that treated digital sound as its subject, and Laurie Spiegel's The Expanding Universe for an earlier, gentler computer music."],"fennesz-endless-summer":["Warm guitar melodies and chords, some almost like pop songs, dissolving into digital static, crackle and glitch. 'Made in Hongkong' is all shimmering digital fizz; the title track sets a sunny, sad guitar figure against gathering distortion; 'Caecilia' is slow and glowing. The record sounds like a summer memory being slowly eaten by the machine that stores it, the tune always just visible through the noise.","Christian Fennesz, a Viennese guitarist, made the album on a laptop with software such as Max, processing his guitar as he played. It was released in 2001 on Mego, the Vienna label at the centre of glitch music, where most artists worked with pure digital noise. The title borrows from a Beach Boys compilation, and the record is full of affection for 1960s Californian pop.","Beneath the noise, many tracks rest on simple, pretty guitar chord sequences that could come from a Beach Boys song, and the melodies are allowed to surface for only a few seconds at a time. Listen for the moments when the static clears and the guitar is heard clean. The digital errors are deliberate and carefully shaped, like a painter's scumble.","Endless Summer was widely praised and is now seen as a landmark of glitch and laptop music, the record that showed noise could be tender. It persuaded many rock listeners that computer music could be warm. Some hear nostalgia; others hear loss, and the album seems designed to hold both at once.","Start with the title track and listen for the guitar under the noise. Then hear the Beach Boys' Good Vibrations for the harmonic world Fennesz is remembering, and My Bloody Valentine's Loveless for the idea that melody can live inside distortion."],"fennesz-venice":["Denser and more mournful than Endless Summer: slow washes of guitar and noise that swell and recede like water against stone. Most tracks are instrumental, ranging from quiet, glowing drones to storms of distortion that break and ebb. David Sylvian sings on 'Transit', the album's one song, his low voice floating over a shimmering, unstable backdrop. The effect is of a city seen through mist.","Released in 2004 on the British label Touch, known for its sound-art and field-recording catalogue, the album took its name from the city where some of the material was developed. Fennesz was by now collaborating widely, with Sylvian and with Ryuichi Sakamoto among others, and his processed guitar had become one of the most recognisable sounds in experimental music.","Listen for how single guitar chords are stretched and smeared so that they last for minutes, the attack of each note removed so it seems to fade in from nowhere. The city of the title is present less as a picture than as a condition: water, reflection and slow decay. Venice has a long history of music written for its spaces, from the Gabrielis to Luigi Nono.","It was well received, though inevitably measured against Endless Summer. Many listeners prefer its darker, more enveloping tone, and 'Transit' is often singled out as one of the best meetings of song and glitch. Some critics found the instrumentals too similar to one another; admirers answer that the sameness is the point, a single weather system heard from different angles.","Start with 'Transit', then play the instrumentals in the dark at low volume. Compare Luigi Nono's La lontananza nostalgica utopica futura, another Venetian meditation in which a live instrument meets tape, and Eno's On Land for an earlier landscape of blurred sound."],"tim-hecker-harmony-in-ultraviolet":["Walls of distorted, luminous sound: chords that swell to overwhelming volume, then recede to fragile drones. The four-part 'Harmony in Blue' is a set of slow, glowing variations on a single mood; other tracks pile distortion onto soft, organ-like tones until they crack and fizz. The music is at once violent and tender, like looking at sunlight through a stained-glass window that is slowly shattering.","Tim Hecker, a Vancouver-born musician working in Montreal, released the album in 2006 on Kranky, the Chicago label that was home to much of North America's drone and ambient music. He was part of a wave of musicians who brought the textures of noise and shoegaze rock into ambient music, playing it at volumes Eno's ambient never intended.","Listen for the melodies buried under the distortion: often a simple, almost hymn-like chord progression is still audible beneath the roar. Hecker shapes the noise as carefully as a composer shapes an orchestra, so the swells have a clear architecture of build, peak and release. Some of the source sounds are organs and guitars, disguised almost completely.","It was widely praised and is often seen as the turning point in his career, the record where his style came fully into focus. Some listeners find it overwhelming or abrasive; others find it deeply moving. It helped establish loud, distorted ambient music as a genre in its own right.","Play it loud on speakers, not quietly in the background. Start with the 'Harmony in Blue' sequence. Compare Ligeti's Atmosphères, another music of clustered, shimmering sound in which there is no melody, only colour and mass, and My Bloody Valentine's Loveless for the rock side."],"tim-hecker-ravedeath-1972":["Built from recordings of a church pipe organ, processed into roaring, distorted drones and fragile, decaying chords. 'The Piano Drop' opens with an eruption of sound; the three-part 'In the Fog' is a long, slow storm; the 'Hatred of Music' pieces are bleak and hymn-like. The organ is recognisable throughout but transformed, like a church service heard through a broken radio.","Hecker recorded the organ in a single day in a church in Reykjavík, working with the producer Ben Frost, then processed the material at home in Montreal. The album was released on Kranky in 2011. Its cover shows students dropping a piano from a university roof in 1972, a picture of music as a physical object being destroyed, at a moment when music was dissolving into digital files.","Listen for moments when the organ's own sound breaks through the processing, a reminder of the stone room where it was captured. The title and cover point to a meditation on the end of music as a thing you could hold. The organ, the oldest machine in Western music, is here fed through the newest.","It was widely acclaimed and is often named Hecker's finest album and one of the key ambient records of its decade. Listeners from contemporary classical music have heard in it the massed organ sonorities of Ligeti and Messiaen; others hear the distortion of heavy rock. The argument over whether it is ambient at all is part of its fame.","Play it loud in a dark room and start with 'In the Fog I'. Compare Ligeti's Volumina, an organ piece of pure clustered sound, and Messiaen's L'Ascension for the organ as a vehicle of overwhelming awe. Aphex Twin's Selected Ambient Works Volume II is the gentler cousin."],"the-field-from-here-we-go-sublime":["Steady four-to-the-floor techno made from tiny fragments of pop songs, looped and layered into shimmering, blurred clouds. 'Over the Ice' and 'Sun & Ice' pulse with a gentle, hypnotic lift; tracks change slowly, adding and removing layers over six, eight, ten minutes. The kick drum never stops, but above it everything is soft focus, so the music is euphoric and dreamy at once.","Axel Willner, working in Stockholm, released the album on the Cologne label Kompakt in 2007. Kompakt was the centre of a soft, melodic kind of minimal techno, and Willner brought to it the washed-out textures of shoegaze rock and the looping methods of hip-hop producers. It was his debut album, and it made him one of the label's best-known artists almost overnight.","The loops come from pop and soul records but are so short, a fraction of a syllable, that the sources are almost unrecognisable; the title track lets its closing fragment, from a 1950s doo-wop hit, come briefly into focus. Listen for how the loops are set slightly out of step with one another, so they shimmer like light on water.","It was widely praised as one of the best electronic albums of its year and has remained a touchstone for melodic techno. Some listeners find it too repetitive; others hear a demonstration that repetition itself can be euphoric. Its influence on later ambient techno, and on producers such as Jon Hopkins, is widely acknowledged.","Start with 'Over the Ice' and listen for one loop at a time as it enters and leaves. Compare Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians for the same idea of pulsing, shimmering repetition made by players, and Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express for the machine pulse underneath."],"caribou-swim":["Liquid, off-kilter dance music with a soft human voice at its centre. 'Odessa' opens with a quirky, bouncing bass line and Dan Snaith's gentle falsetto; 'Sun' repeats a single word over a steadily building groove; 'Kaili' and 'Bowls' add horns, chimes and rattling percussion. The sounds seem to bend and melt as they play, as if heard underwater, while the beat stays firmly on the ground.","Dan Snaith, a Canadian with a doctorate in mathematics, had made psychedelic pop as Caribou and won Canada's Polaris Prize for Andorra. With Swim, released in 2010, he turned to the dance floor, influenced by nights in London clubs. He said he wanted dance music that sounded fluid, like water, rather than hard and mechanical, and he recorded much of it in his London home studio.","Many sounds are bent in pitch as they play, so notes slide rather than land, which is where the underwater feeling comes from. Listen for the small acoustic details that ride on top of the synthesizers: chimes, bells, breaths and struck metal. Behind the dance-floor surface the songs are about the end of relationships, sung without drama.","It was widely praised as his best record to that point and brought him a new audience in dance music. Some listeners missed his earlier psychedelia; others heard a songwriter finding the right form. It is often paired with Four Tet's album of the same year as a sign of electronica's warm turn.","Start with 'Odessa' and listen for the sounds that bend. Then play 'Sun' and notice how much comes from one repeated word. Compare Four Tet's There Is Love in You, from the same year, and Talking Heads' Remain in Light for an earlier band that also made dance music out of looping, layered parts."],"caribou-our-love":["Warm, soulful house music about love, family and commitment. 'Can't Do Without You' builds a single repeated vocal phrase, pitched and filtered, into a surging, joyous climax; 'Silver' and 'All I Ever Need' are tender and slow; 'Second Chance' features the Canadian singer Jessy Lanza. The textures are warmer and rounder than on Swim, with R&B and garage in the rhythms.","Snaith made the album after becoming a father and after years of touring Swim, and he described it as a record about the people close to him. It was released in 2014, when his club side project Daphni had also taken him deeper into DJ culture. The album brought Caribou to festival headline slots and a much wider audience.","Listen for how 'Can't Do Without You' changes over four minutes with only a handful of elements: the vocal loop, a bass, a kick drum, a rising filter. The repetition is the meaning, a phrase said so often it becomes both obsession and devotion. Several tracks borrow the pitched-up vocal technique of UK garage.","It was widely praised and became his most popular record. Some critics found it softer and less adventurous than Swim; others heard the ideal balance of songcraft and club music. 'Can't Do Without You' became one of the best-loved dance tracks of its year, played equally in clubs and at weddings.","Start with 'Can't Do Without You' and listen to how the build is made from almost nothing. Then compare Donna Summer's I Feel Love, the ancestor of every euphoric electronic love song, and Burial's Untrue for a darker use of the same pitched vocal technique."],"the-winstons-amen-brother":["A brisk instrumental B-side built on a gospel tune, 'Amen', given a soul-band workout: organ and horns trade the melody over a busy, bright groove. It is pleasant, short and unremarkable until about halfway through, when everyone else drops out and the drummer, Gregory Coleman, plays four bars alone. Those six or so seconds of snare, kick and ride cymbal, with a skipping syncopation in the third and fourth bars, are the reason the record is known to millions who have never heard the rest of it.","The Winstons were a Washington, D.C. soul group led by Richard Lewis Spencer, and 'Amen, Brother' was the flip side of their gospel-tinged hit 'Color Him Father', released on Metromedia in 1969. Drum breaks like this were a normal bit of arrangement, a chance for the band to breathe before the last chorus. No one in the studio could have imagined that the break would be lifted off the vinyl, looped and chopped by machines that did not yet exist.","The break reached hip-hop producers in the mid-1980s through bootleg compilations of drum breaks, and by the early 1990s British producers were slicing it into single hits and rearranging them on samplers, then speeding it up far beyond anything a human drummer could play. Listen for the ghost notes on the snare and the loose, open ride cymbal: those small human irregularities are exactly what made it sound alive when chopped and re-sequenced.","For decades the record was a collector's footnote while its four bars became probably the most sampled drum passage in recorded music, the backbone of jungle and drum and bass. Neither Coleman, who died in 2006, nor Spencer received royalties for that use. In 2015 two British DJs raised a public fund of about twenty-four thousand pounds for Spencer, a gesture that made the break a standing case in arguments over sampling and credit.","Play it straight through once as a soul record, then wait for the break and count the four bars. Then put on Goldie's 'Terminator' or Shy FX's 'Original Nuttah' and try to find Coleman in there: sped up, reversed, stretched, reassembled. Compare James Brown's 'Funky Drummer', the other great donor of the sampling era."],"lee-perry-blackboard-jungle-dub":["Credited to Perry's house band, the Upsetters, this is a set of reggae rhythms stripped almost to the bone: bass and drums carry everything, while organ stabs, guitar chops, horn phrases and scraps of voice drop in and out as if someone were switching the lights on and off in a house. Echo trails hang in the air after each fragment. There are no songs here in the usual sense, only grooves being taken apart and put back together in real time.","By 1973 Kingston producers had been issuing instrumental 'versions' on the B-sides of singles for a few years, so sound-system selectors and DJs could toast over them. Perry, a veteran of Studio One and producer of the Wailers' early records, went further and made a whole album out of versions, working with King Tubby on the mixing. It appeared in a tiny Jamaican pressing, and is usually counted among the very first dub albums.","Early pressings reportedly carried a stark stereo mix, with instruments split hard between the two speakers, so on headphones the band seems to be playing in two different rooms. Notice how often the bass is left entirely alone: the mixing desk is being used as an instrument, and silence is one of its sounds. Several rhythms come from Perry's earlier productions, recycled into something unrecognisable.","Rarely heard outside Jamaica on release, the album was rediscovered through reissues and is now treated as a founding document of dub, alongside King Tubby's early work. Arguments continue about exactly who did what at the desk, Perry or Tubby, and which pressing is the true one; scholars of dub treat the question as partly unanswerable, since the music was made live in the mix.","Use speakers with real bass or good headphones. Pick one instrument, perhaps the organ, and follow when it is allowed in and when it is taken away; the drama is in the removals. Then hear Public Image Ltd's Metal Box or The Slits' Cut to see how quickly London's post-punks learned the lesson."],"king-tubby-dub-from-the-roots":["A set of dub mixes in which King Tubby takes finished reggae rhythms and rebuilds them at the mixing desk. Drums and bass form a steady floor; everything else, the offbeat guitar, a horn line, a piano figure, appears for a bar, is thrown into a long spring-reverb tail, and vanishes. The signature sound is a sudden sweep that thins the whole band to a tinny rattle before the bass drops back in with physical force.","Osbourne Ruddock ran a small studio in his home in Waterhouse, a poor district of Kingston, where he cut dub plates for his sound system and voiced singers over rhythms recorded elsewhere. He was an electronics repairman first, and he modified his equipment himself, including a mixing console bought from the Dynamic Sounds studio. Albums like this one were made quickly, for local sound systems and a small record market, not for posterity.","The sweeping effect that makes the band sound as if it were being sucked down a drain is a filter on Tubby's console: he could cut away everything below a chosen pitch and slide the cutoff by hand. He was reportedly also fond of slamming the spring reverb unit so it crashed like thunder. Listen for these two gestures; they are his fingerprints on almost every record he touched.","In the 1970s dub albums were cheap by-products and rarely reviewed. Since the 1990s writers on electronic music have treated Tubby as a figure comparable to the tape composers of Paris and Cologne, a claim sometimes pushed too far, since he worked inside a commercial, collaborative culture. Credits on his albums are notoriously loose, and the exact provenance of many rhythms is still argued over.","Start with any track and listen for the moments when the whole band thins out and then crashes back. Then play Stockhausen's Kontakte or Varèse's Poème électronique to hear, from a very different direction, the same idea: that the studio itself can compose. A good reissue with the original mono or early stereo mix is worth seeking."],"lee-perry-super-ape":["Dub at its thickest and most humid. Where earlier dub stripped rhythms bare, Super Ape piles them up: murmuring backing vocals, hand percussion, a thick swirl of phasing over the cymbals, horns that arrive muffled as if from another room. Voices chant and exhort in fragments. The record moves slowly and heavily, and its sound, a blurred, swampy haze with a bass line cutting through, is instantly recognisable as Perry's Black Ark studio.","Perry built the Black Ark behind his home in Washington Gardens, Kingston, around 1973–74, and by 1976 it was producing a stream of records on modest equipment: a four-track tape machine, a tape echo unit, a phaser. Island Records, flush with the international success of Bob Marley, released this album abroad, and its gorilla cover became an emblem of Perry's mythology. It was issued in Jamaica with a different title.","Much of the density comes from 'bouncing': Perry recorded several parts, mixed them down onto one track, and then recorded more on top, so each generation of tape adds a layer of hiss and blur. What sounds like an effect is partly the accumulated grain of his tiny studio. Listen also for the phaser on the hi-hats, a sweeping shimmer that became one of the Ark's trademarks.","Received abroad as a curiosity alongside Island's roots reggae releases, the album is now considered one of the summits of dub and a key text for the British musicians who were listening closely: punk and post-punk bands took both its sound and its attitude. Some reggae purists prefer Perry's leaner early work, but Super Ape is usually the record cited when producers talk about his studio as an instrument.","This is an album to play loud, at night, on a system with real low end. Listen for the way voices drift past like people in a crowd. Then hear The Clash's Complete Control, which Perry produced the following year, to hear the Ark's reach across the Atlantic."],"augustus-pablo-king-tubby-meets-rockers-uptown":["Augustus Pablo's melodica, a humble school instrument that sounds like a wheezing harmonium, floats over slow, heavy rhythms from his Rockers label, while King Tubby mixes them into dub. The mood is sombre and eastern-tinged, built on minor modes. The title track, a dub of Jacob Miller's song 'Baby I Love You So', is the best-known dub in the world: a stately drum pattern, a bass that seems to walk on the floor, and Miller's voice surfacing and sinking.","Horace Swaby took the name Augustus Pablo and a melodica sound he called 'far east' in the early 1970s. He ran his own small label, Rockers, and brought his rhythms uptown to Tubby's studio for mixing. The album, compiled from dubs made over the preceding years, was issued in 1976, when roots reggae and Rastafarian devotion were at their height in Kingston and reggae was beginning to reach London and New York.","The drums on the title track are among the most imitated in reggae, but listen to the space: Tubby repeatedly lets the reverb swallow the snare, so each backbeat is followed by a long, fading room. Miller's vocal is never allowed a whole line; it is a ghost. And the melodica is often doubled with organ, which is why it sounds larger than a small reed instrument should.","Initially a specialist release, the album has become the dub record most often recommended to newcomers, and appears on nearly every list of essential reggae. Some critics find its reputation tilts attention toward the title track at the expense of the moodier pieces around it. Its sound fed directly into British sound-system culture and, through it, the bass-heavy music of the 1990s and 2000s.","Start with the title track, then listen to the whole album in one go as a single mood. Follow the bass rather than the melody. Then play Mala in Cuba or Digital Mystikz's 'Anti-War Dub' to hear how London's dubstep producers kept this weight and space thirty years on."],"scientist-rids-the-world-of-the-evil-curse-of-the-vampires":["Ten dubs of hard, spare dancehall rhythms played by the Roots Radics band, mixed by the young engineer Scientist with a comic-horror theme. Creaking doors, howls and cackles are scattered through the tracks, but underneath is serious mixing: rimshot snares ricochet in echo, the bass is round and enormous, and instruments are cut in and out with a drummer's timing. Each track is named after a horror episode, such as a plot of zombies or a mummy.","Scientist, Overton Brown, trained at King Tubby's studio as a teenager and became its leading engineer at the turn of the 1980s, when the slower, rootsy style of the 1970s was giving way to dancehall. Producer Henry 'Junjo' Lawes supplied the rhythms. The London label Greensleeves issued a series of themed Scientist albums, with lurid comic-book covers, aimed at a British market that bought dub avidly.","The horror sound effects are a joke, but the rhythms are not: they are the same Roots Radics tracks that carried hit vocal records by singers of the day, so Jamaican listeners would have recognised the songs behind the dubs. Listen for how Scientist uses the echo on the snare to create a second, off-kilter rhythm that answers the real one.","Long dismissed as a novelty, partly because of its cover and title, the album is now respected as a showcase of early-dancehall dub mixing and as Scientist's best-known work. Debate over who deserves credit for the golden age of Tubby's studio, the master or his gifted engineers, often centres on records like this.","Enjoy the silliness, then listen past it to the drums and bass. Compare it with King Tubby Meets Rockers Uptown: here the rhythms are tighter, drier, faster, and the echo is sharper. The comic-book cover is worth seeing on vinyl."],"4hero-mr-kirks-nightmare":["An early breakbeat hardcore track from North London: fast, jittering sampled breakbeats, a rubbery bass line, and a stark spoken-word sample in which a police officer tells a father that his son has died of an overdose. The effect is unsettling, a dance track that repeatedly stops to deliver bad news. Synthesizer stabs and rave sirens keep it moving.","4hero, Mark Clair and Dego McFarlane, co-founded Reinforced Records in Dollis Hill around 1989–90, when the acid-house rave boom was mutating in Britain into something faster and more breakbeat-driven. Pirate radio stations and warehouse raves were the channels; tracks circulated on white labels. This record was a hit in that underground and a template for hardcore's darker turn.","The drug narrative made the record controversial as a pro- or anti-drug statement; in fact it is ambiguous, and its bleakness points ahead to the 'darkside' hardcore of 1992–93 from which jungle grew. Listen for the way the breakbeat is chopped and rearranged rather than simply looped, an early sign of the techniques Reinforced's artists, including Goldie, would push much further.","Treated at the time as a rave anthem, it is now cited in histories of hardcore and jungle as one of the first records to put darkness and narrative into the music. 4hero themselves moved on to the jazz-inflected Parallel Universe and then broken beat, and their early hardcore is sometimes overshadowed by that later work.","Play it loud and notice how often the music is interrupted: the groove builds, the spoken scene cuts in, and the dancefloor has to wait. Listen for the break being re-sequenced rather than looped. Then hear Goldie's 'Terminator', also on Reinforced, to hear the breakbeat pulled further out of shape two years later, and Afrika Bambaataa's 'Planet Rock' for the electro ancestor."],"goldie-terminator":["Released under the name Metalheads (a Rufige Kru production), 'Terminator' is a track in which the drums seem to melt. Sampled breakbeats are sped up, slowed down and phased against each other while staying in time, so a snare roll suddenly stretches and warps like tape pulled through a machine, then snaps back. Dark synthesizer pads and film-derived vocal fragments set a sci-fi mood. It sounds like a drummer being bent by the forces of a black hole.","Goldie, a graffiti artist from the Midlands who had spent time in New York's hip-hop scene, fell in with the Reinforced Records crowd in London in the early 1990s. New samplers and computer software allowed time-stretching: changing a sample's speed without changing its pitch, or vice versa. Goldie and his engineers used the technique not to hide the process but to make it the point, in the thick of London's hardcore rave scene.","The warping sound is a side effect: early time-stretching algorithms produced a grainy, metallic smear when pushed hard, and Goldie used that artefact deliberately. Listen closely to the snare in the passages where it shifts: the timbre changes too, becoming more metallic and shimmering. That digital flaw became one of drum and bass's signature textures.","A landmark for producers rather than the public, it is widely credited as the first record to make time-stretching an expressive device, and it moved hardcore toward the darker, more intricate sound of jungle. Some historians caution that several producers were experimenting with the technique simultaneously; Goldie's record is the one that made it famous.","Ignore the pads at first and focus on the drums alone: wait for the moments when a snare roll stretches, goes metallic and snaps back into time. Those bends are the whole point. Then play the Winstons' 'Amen, Brother' to hear the kind of raw material being bent, and Goldie's Timeless to hear where the technique went three years later."],"origin-unknown-valley-of-the-shadows":["A jungle track that feels like drifting through a dark corridor. A spoken sample, a woman describing a near-death experience of travelling through a long dark tunnel, repeats over a sparse, rolling breakbeat, a deep sub-bass and wide, ghostly synthesizer pads. The track builds and releases without drama; it is patience and atmosphere, not aggression, that carry it.","Origin Unknown was the partnership of Andy C and Ant Miles, from Essex; Andy C was a teenager when they started Ram Records in 1992. The track came out in the transitional year when hardcore rave was splitting into jungle and the more atmospheric strain soon called drum and bass. Pirate radio and club DJs built its reputation long before most listeners knew who had made it.","The spoken fragment is looped and repeated until its meaning slips into sound, a technique familiar from Steve Reich's early tape pieces. Listen to how the breakbeat underneath is kept surprisingly low in the mix: the vocal and pads carry the foreground, which was unusual in a scene obsessed with drums.","Still one of the most requested tracks in drum and bass, often cited as the scene's first atmospheric classic and reissued many times. Andy C became one of the genre's biggest DJs, and critics sometimes contrast the track's restraint with the louder, harder sound he later championed.","Listen on headphones in the dark and follow the vocal as it turns from speech into texture; by the fifth repetition you hear its rhythm more than its story. Then notice the sub-bass entering underneath. Compare Steve Reich's Different Trains for another looped voice, and LTJ Bukem's 'Horizons', which took this atmospheric direction and made it lush."],"shy-fx-original-nuttah":["Ragga jungle at full tilt. A dancehall-style MC, UK Apachi, chants and boasts over hyperkinetic chopped breakbeats at about 160 beats per minute, while a sub-bass line moves at half that speed, like a reggae bass line trapped inside a drum and bass track. Sirens, gunshot samples and rewind cues from the sound-system world punctuate the chaos. It is loud, joyful and aggressive at once.","Andre Williams, Shy FX, was a teenager from North London when he made this. In 1994 jungle was at the peak of its first wave, Jamaican sound-system culture fused with rave: MCs, dubplates and bass weight from reggae, breakbeats from hardcore. The track was a club and pirate radio phenomenon, and it became one of the records that brought jungle into the national press.","The layering is the key: the fast breakbeats and the slow bass line are really two tempos, one double the other, which is how jungle keeps a reggae body under a rave pulse. Listen for how the MC phrases his lines against the bass, not the drums, just as a Kingston toaster would over a version on a sound system.","Celebrated as a ragga jungle anthem and sometimes criticised at the time for its violent imagery, which Shy FX later described as part of the sound-system language rather than a literal message. The ragga strand fell out of favour with drum and bass tastemakers in 1995–96, but the track's reputation has only grown.","Turn it up and listen for the two tempos at once: dance to the bass or to the drums. Then compare Scientist's dubs to hear where the bass weight comes from, and Dizzee Rascal's Boy in da Corner to hear where the MCing went next."],"basic-channel-quadrant-dub":["A long, hypnotic piece of dub techno: a muffled kick drum, a single chord stab that echoes into infinity, a haze of hiss, and slow filter sweeps that open and close the sound like breathing. Over many minutes almost nothing changes, and yet the chord's echoes pile up and shift so that the texture is never static. It feels like listening to a techno track through a wall, or underwater.","Basic Channel was the duo of Moritz von Oswald and Mark Ernestus, who ran the Hard Wax record shop in Berlin and a small group of labels. In the early 1990s, after reunification, Berlin techno was raw and industrial; Basic Channel combined it with the techniques of Jamaican dub, which both men loved, releasing plain-sleeved records that set a quiet, severe template.","The chords are processed with the same tools King Tubby used, delay and filter, but applied to a single repeated gesture rather than a band. Listen for the tiny pitch and timbre shifts in the echoes: most of the music happens in the decaying tails, not the notes themselves. Their mastering studio, Dubplates & Mastering, was also where the pair cut their vinyl.","Influential far beyond its sales, the track and its siblings founded dub techno as a genre and shaped minimal techno for decades. Critics sometimes debate whether the style's austerity became a formula in lesser hands; the originals are widely treated as untouchable.","Put it on while doing something else and notice the moment you start hearing the echoes rather than the beat; that shift of attention is how the piece works. Play it at moderate volume on speakers, not earbuds, so the hiss fills the room. Then play King Tubby's Dub from the Roots and Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express to hear the two parents of this sound."],"goldie-timeless":["A double album that treats drum and bass as a medium for grand statements. It opens with the twenty-one-minute 'Timeless' suite: Diane Charlemagne's soaring soul vocal on 'Inner City Life' floats over lush strings and a frantic, intricately edited breakbeat, which then plunges into darker, heavier movements before returning. The rest of the album moves between jazz-inflected softness and aggressive, metallic drum programming. Everything is dense, cinematic and deliberately ambitious.","By 1995 jungle was becoming a national story in Britain, and Goldie, with his gold teeth and graffiti background, was its public face. He signed to London Records' FFRR imprint and worked with engineer Rob Playford for months in the studio. The album appeared when major labels were competing to find the jungle album that would cross over, as trip-hop and Britpop were doing.","The orchestral sounds are mostly samples and synthesizers, arranged with a film composer's attention; listen for how the strings in 'Timeless' move in slow, sustained chords while the drums race, a double tempo borrowed from dub and ragga jungle but turned toward the symphonic. Much of the drum editing was done hit by hit, not loop by loop.","Hailed as the album that proved jungle could be art, it reached the British top ten and is often called the genre's first great long-form work. Some in the scene felt it was overproduced and too eager for respectability; that argument over ambition versus dancefloor function has followed drum and bass ever since.","Begin with the 'Timeless' suite, all twenty-one minutes, and let it play through. Then sample the rest. Compare Roni Size's New Forms, the next attempt at a big statement, and Brian Eno's Music for Airports for the ambient side of Goldie's ambition."],"ltj-bukem-horizons":["Drum and bass at its most serene. Soft, rolling breakbeats move quickly but lightly, like rain on a window, beneath warm synthesizer pads, gentle electric piano chords and a deep, smooth bass line. Nothing is harsh; the track seems to hover. It is dance music whose main aim is to be beautiful and spacious, with a mood closer to 1970s jazz fusion than to rave.","LTJ Bukem, Danny Williamson, founded Good Looking Records around 1991 and built a following as a DJ with a smooth, jazz-influenced style. At his club night Speed in London, with Fabio, he championed the 'intelligent' or atmospheric strand of drum and bass, a reaction against the ragga and darkside sounds dominating jungle in 1994–95.","Listen for the chords: they use the extended, jazz-derived harmony of fusion keyboards, with added notes that give a sense of openness rather than resolution. The drums are programmed with lots of ghost notes and soft hits, giving a more human, brushed feel than most jungle breaks.","Admired by many as drum and bass's most refined voice, and criticised by others, especially at the time, as coffee-table music that sanded off the scene's energy. The labels 'intelligent' and 'atmospheric' were themselves resented by some who saw in them a class-coded snobbery toward ragga jungle.","Play it in the evening at moderate volume and follow the chords rather than the drums. Compare Miles Davis's In a Silent Way for the fusion mood it echoes, and Origin Unknown's 'Valley of the Shadows' for its darker cousin."],"roni-size-new-forms":["A double album of drum and bass with live instruments at its heart. On 'Brown Paper Bag', a jazz upright bass walks at half-time while the drums skitter above it, and a soul singer, Onallee, and an MC, Dynamite, add voices. Elsewhere the sound is by turns funky, dark, jazzy and minimal, always rhythmically busy, with a warmth that comes from acoustic textures rather than synthesizers.","Roni Size, Ryan Williams, came from Bristol, where he worked with Krust, DJ Die and Suv under the Full Cycle label. Bristol's scene, home also to Massive Attack and Portishead, mixed sound-system culture with hip-hop and soul. Signed to Gilles Peterson's Talkin' Loud label, the collective called itself Reprazent and performed live, which few drum and bass acts attempted.","The upright bass player, Si John, often plays a straightforward jazz line, but the programmers edit and loop it to sit against the breakbeat, so what sounds like a live take is partly assembled. Listen for this in 'Brown Paper Bag': the bass is both human and machine-cut.","It won the 1997 Mercury Prize, beating Radiohead's OK Computer, a result that became a symbol of drum and bass's moment in British culture. Some scene insiders felt the album's success came at the expense of its dancefloor edge; others saw it as the genre's high point of musicianship.","Start with 'Brown Paper Bag', then 'Heroes'. Follow the double bass line alone for a minute, then switch to the drums, and hear how the two run at different speeds. The album is long, so take one disc at a time. Compare A Tribe Called Quest's Low End Theory and Miles Davis's Bitches Brew for other meetings of jazz players and the studio."],"photek-ni-ten-ichi-ryu":["Drum and bass as precise as a sword stroke. Sharp, clipped drum hits and metallic percussion are placed with obsessive care over a deep, slow bass; there is little melody and a great deal of silence. Each snare and cymbal seems to have been individually sculpted. The effect is tense, minimal and martial, as the title, taken from a Japanese school of swordsmanship, suggests.","Photek, Rupert Parkes, from Ipswich, emerged in the mid-1990s as the most technically obsessive producer in drum and bass. The track appeared as a single and on his debut album Modus Operandi (1997), released by Virgin's Science imprint in a year when major labels were signing drum and bass artists in the hope of crossover.","Most of the drums are not a looped break at all but programmed hit by hit from tiny fragments, which is why they sound so clean and dry. Listen to the gaps between hits: Photek treats silence as a percussion instrument, closer to Japanese court music or Feldman than to rave.","Celebrated as a peak of drum and bass programming and later used in films and advertising, the track is still a reference point for producers. Some critics found Photek's precision cold; his admirers call it exactitude, and the argument sums up the 'intelligent' drum and bass debate of the period.","Listen with headphones and focus on individual hits: each snare has its own shape and decay. Count the silences between them, and notice how the tension comes from waiting. Compare Goldie's 'Terminator', its opposite in texture, Feldman's Triadic Memories for another music of carefully placed sounds, and Stockhausen's Kontakte, another work of sculpted, isolated events."],"mj-cole-sincere":["UK garage at its most elegant: a skipping two-step beat in which the kick drum seems to hop over the second beat, a warm bass line, jazzy piano chords and chopped-up snatches of a female soul vocal. The whole thing swings with a light, sophisticated feel, sitting halfway between London club music and American R&B. It is music for dressing up to go out.","MJ Cole, Matt Coleman, trained as a classical musician before working in a London studio in the mid-1990s. At that time a scene was growing around 'speed garage', a London take on New York house with heavier basslines, which by 1998 was shifting to the two-step rhythm heard here. Pirate radio and Sunday-afternoon clubs were its heartland.","The vocal is cut into syllables and re-sequenced so it becomes part of the rhythm, a technique shared with jungle's breakbeat chopping; listen for how the fragments land on offbeats. The piano chords owe as much to his classical training as to house.","A garage anthem when released, it gave its name to his album (2000), which was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize. Critics now see it as a high-water mark for garage's smooth, musical side before the scene darkened and fed into grime and dubstep.","Listen for the skip in the drums: count one-two-three-four and hear which beats the kick avoids, then notice how the vocal fragments fill the gaps it leaves. The piano chords reward a second listen on their own. Then play Artful Dodger's 'Re-Rewind' for garage in party mode and Burial's Untrue, which takes the same vocal chops into a lonelier place."],"artful-dodger-re-rewind":["The record that took UK garage to the top of the pop charts. Over a jaunty two-step beat, rubbery bass and bright keyboard stabs, a young Craig David sings a slick, fast-moving R&B vocal about a DJ at the peak of the night, when the crowd demands the track be pulled back and played again. It is short, catchy and relentlessly upbeat.","Artful Dodger, Mark Hill and Pete Devereux, were producers from Southampton who ran a record shop and nightclub scene there. Garage in 1999 was at its commercial peak in Britain, with pirate radio feeding directly into chart hits. Craig David, also from Southampton, was an unknown teenager when he recorded the vocal.","The 'rewind' in the title is a sound-system ritual borrowed from Jamaican dancehall: when a crowd loves a track the DJ spins it back to the start. Garage inherited that ritual directly from reggae and jungle, and the song describes it. Listen for the MC-style exclamations, another sound-system habit.","A big hit, it launched Craig David's career and remains a symbol of the garage boom. Critics once dismissed it as pop; nostalgia and garage revivals have made it a beloved anthem, and its title phrase became a British catchphrase.","Enjoy it first as pure pop, then listen again to the drums alone: they are the two-step pattern of MJ Cole's 'Sincere' in a party mood, with the kick dodging the second beat. Notice how quickly Craig David's syllables move against it. Compare Chic's 'Le Freak' for an earlier dancefloor hit, and The Streets' Original Pirate Material for the same beat turned toward storytelling."],"the-streets-original-pirate-material":["Mike Skinner half-raps, half-talks in a flat Birmingham-inflected voice over garage beats, cheap-sounding strings and piano loops. The songs are short stories of ordinary British life: a night in the pub, a fry-up, a phone running out of credit, a weekend of clubbing and its comedown. The beats are skeletal and home-made, and the tone swings from comic to tender to bleak.","Skinner made most of the album in his bedroom, partly in Brixton, reportedly using a wardrobe stuffed with mattresses as a vocal booth. It came out on the garage label Locked On in 2002, when UK garage was beginning to fall out of fashion and British rap was still seeking a voice distinct from American hip-hop.","The 'garage' beats are often not club tracks at all but stripped, downbeat sketches borrowed from garage's rhythms; listen to how often the kick drum is missing, leaving Skinner's voice nearly alone. The strings are unashamedly cheap preset sounds, deliberately evoking pirate radio's scrappy production. And the album is sequenced like a night out, from anticipation through the club to the comedown and the walk home.","It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize and hailed by critics as one of the defining British albums of the decade, though garage purists scoffed at its sound and some rap fans at its flow. It is now seen as a bridge between garage, British rap and indie-style songwriting.","Listen straight through as a narrative, the way you would read a book of short stories. Start with 'Has It Come to This?' for the manifesto, then let the album's night unfold. Notice the humour, then the sadness underneath. Compare Dizzee Rascal's Boy in da Corner, released a year later, for a harsher view of the same Britain, and The Clash's London Calling for an earlier portrait of the city."],"horsepower-productions-in-fine-style":["An album of dark, spacious two-step: garage's skipping rhythms slowed, emptied out and filled with dub echoes, reggae samples, Latin percussion and film-noir atmosphere. The bass is deep and heavy, and melody is kept to a minimum, a few keyboard stabs or a snatch of horn at most. Tracks run long and change slowly, built for DJs to mix. It sounds like garage wandering at night through a reggae record shop, stopping to listen at every door.","Horsepower Productions, a south London collective led by Benny Ill, made records for the Tempa label as garage split in two directions: toward MC-driven grime and toward the darker, instrumental 'dark garage' that soon became dubstep. Croydon's record shops, especially Big Apple Records, and the club night FWD>> in central London were the hub. The scene was tiny, a few hundred people, and its records were pressed in small runs for DJs rather than the shops.","Many of the samples come from old reggae, soul and jazz records, recontextualised in the half-empty space of the beats. Listen for the half-time feel: the drums are at garage speed, but the snare often lands at half that rate, which is the key rhythmic signature of dubstep.","Often called the first dubstep album, though that label came only later and the members themselves did not use it. It was little noticed outside the scene at the time, and its reggae sampling seemed old-fashioned beside grime's raw synthesizers. Retrospectives now credit it as the missing link between garage and dubstep, and producers such as Skream have spoken of its influence on their early tracks.","Listen for how much space there is: count how long the bass is left alone between drum hits. Try to feel the snare at half the speed of the hi-hats. Compare MJ Cole's 'Sincere' to hear how far garage has been stripped, King Tubby for the dub method, then Skream's 'Midnight Request Line' and Burial's first album for where it went."],"wiley-eskimo":["An instrumental built from raw, cold-sounding square-wave synthesizer notes that bounce and slide over a sparse, stuttering beat at about 140 beats per minute. There is no singing, only a chilly, video-game-like melody and a heavy, buzzing bass. The sound is bare and a little eerie, and it left huge space for MCs.","Wiley, Richard Cowie, from Bow in east London, was an MC and producer in the garage crew Pay As U Go and then led Roll Deep. Around 2002 he began making instrumentals he called 'eskibeat', with cold titles and cheap synthesizer tones, for pirate radio sets where teenagers from Bow and nearby estates rapped in turns. Grime was taking shape.","The sounds are default or near-default presets from affordable software and keyboards, deliberately left raw. Listen for the gaps in the drum pattern: the rhythm is garage's skeleton with the swing removed, making it jerky and angular, which suits MCs who want to rap in fast bursts.","Initially a white label for the scene, 'Eskimo' is now regarded as one of the founding grime instrumentals, and Wiley as the 'godfather of grime'. Critics have noted how its chilly sound reflected east London's estates, though Wiley's own explanations of the icy theme varied.","Imagine it on pirate radio, with a crowd of MCs taking turns. Then hear Dizzee Rascal's Boy in da Corner to hear what an MC could do over this kind of beat, and Missy Elliott's 'Get Ur Freak On' for the Timbaland production style that influenced grime's jagged rhythms."],"dizzee-rascal-boy-in-da-corner":["A teenager's debut, made largely on a home computer, full of abrasive synthesizer tones, clattering off-kilter beats and dense rapping in an east London accent. Dizzee raps at great speed, with a high, strained voice, about the pressures of estate life, teenage relationships, violence and ambition. Tracks such as 'I Luv U' and 'Fix Up, Look Sharp' are jagged and minimalist, the beats often unsettling rather than pleasant.","Dylan Mills, Dizzee Rascal, grew up in Bow and was a member of Wiley's Roll Deep crew, sharpening his style in turns on pirate radio sets. He had started making beats in a school music class. Grime was an underground scene of pirate radio, MC clashes and white labels when XL Recordings released the album in 2003. That year it won the Mercury Prize, making Dizzee a national figure at nineteen and the scene's first mainstream star.","'Fix Up, Look Sharp' is built on a huge drum break from Billy Squier's rock song 'The Big Beat', a staple of hip-hop DJs; much of the rest of the album leans on stark, cheap synthesizer sounds. Listen for how the beats seem to stumble: they are designed to throw a listener off balance.","The Mercury win announced grime to the mainstream, and critics hailed it as one of the defining British albums of the century. Some worried about its violence and bleakness; most now see it as a vivid portrait of a generation. It is often compared with Illmatic as a debut that defined a scene.","Listen for Dizzee's rhythm rather than his words at first: how he crams syllables into bursts, then leaves a gap, like a drummer. Try 'I Luv U' and 'Fix Up, Look Sharp' first, then the quieter, stranger tracks. Compare Nas's Illmatic for a different city's debut portrait, Wiley's 'Eskimo' for the beat style he grew from, and The Streets for a lighter view of Britain."],"skream-midnight-request-line":["A dubstep track built on a woozy, circling synthesizer melody that sounds like a music box played underwater, over a half-time beat and a sub-bass that is felt more than heard. The melody repeats with small variations, and the drums stay sparse, leaving huge space. It is catchy and eerie at once.","Skream, Oliver Jones, was a teenager in Croydon, where he worked at Big Apple Records, a shop where garage and dubstep producers traded dubplates and ideas; his older brother was part of that circle. He made tracks at speed on a home computer. The track, released by Tempa in 2005, became the scene's first crossover anthem, played by DJs well beyond the small circle of FWD>> club nights and championed on national radio.","The melody is in an unusual, slightly off-kilter mode that gives it its dreamlike feel. Listen for how the bass follows the melody an octave or two lower, a technique that makes the track feel huge on a big sound system and almost empty on small speakers.","Hailed as a dubstep classic and widely credited with taking the genre beyond its south London base, it showed that the style could carry a melody as well as bass pressure. Some purists felt its hook was too accessible, a warning of the brash, aggressive dubstep that later conquered America; most now see it as the scene's calling card, and its gentleness as the road not taken.","Play it on a system with real bass or good headphones; on a phone speaker half the track disappears. Hum the melody, then try to feel the bass shadowing it far below. Notice how few drum hits there are. Compare Horsepower Productions and Digital Mystikz to hear the scene's darker, more minimal side, and Overmono for its descendants."],"digital-mystikz-anti-war-dub":["A heavy, slow dubstep track in which a mournful vocal, half-sung and half-spoken, repeats an anti-war plea over a deep, rolling bass and sparse, echoing drums. The atmosphere is meditative and grave, closer to 1970s roots reggae than to club music. The bass moves slowly enough that each note seems to fill a room.","Digital Mystikz, Mala and Coki from south London, started the DMZ label and a club night in Brixton in 2004–05, which became the spiritual home of dubstep: a dark room, a huge sound system and a crowd there to feel the bass rather than dance showily. The track appeared during the Iraq War, when anti-war feeling in Britain was strong, and its message reflects the protest traditions of roots reggae as much as the club scene.","The drums are placed on a half-time grid so the snare lands only once a bar, leaving the bass to fill the space; this is the same idea as a dub mix where the drummer stops and the bass holds the floor. Listen for the echo on the vocal, a direct inheritance from King Tubby's studio.","Celebrated as a dubstep classic and a statement of the scene's roots-reggae conscience, it circulated first as a dubplate played at DMZ before its release. Critics have praised it as proof that dubstep could carry message and spirituality, not just bass pressure, and it is often set against the louder, more aggressive dubstep that followed as a reminder of the style's meditative beginnings.","Listen on a system with real bass, loud enough to feel it in your chest; this music was built for a room, not earbuds. Follow the vocal and its echoes, then the long notes of the bass. Compare Augustus Pablo's King Tubby Meets Rockers Uptown to hear the mood it comes from, and Public Enemy's 'Fight the Power' for protest in a louder key."],"kode9-memories-of-the-future":["A collaboration between Kode9 and the poet-vocalist The Spaceape, whose deep, slow, spoken voice delivers dark, apocalyptic poems over minimal, heavy dubstep beats. The mood is paranoid and dystopian, with sparse drums, deep bass, sirens and sci-fi textures; a few instrumentals give the voice a rest. Tempos stay slow and the tracks rarely build. It sounds like a radio broadcast from a ruined city, heard late at night.","Kode9, Steve Goodman, was both a producer and an academic who studied sound and culture, and he founded Hyperdub in 2004. The Spaceape, Stephen Gordon, was a poet from south London. The album appeared in 2006 as dubstep emerged, and it draws on the traditions of dub poetry as well as science fiction.","The Spaceape's delivery owes much to Jamaican dub poetry, which combined spoken verse with reggae rhythms; listen for how he stretches words over the beat. Kode9's sub-bass often sits just below where small speakers can reproduce it, so on a big system the poems seem to float on pressure rather than notes.","Admired for its darkness and intellect, it is considered one of dubstep's key early albums, though it has been far less widely heard than Burial's debut from the same label and year. Some found it too bleak and too slow for the dancefloor; others praised it as the genre's most conceptual record. The Spaceape's death in 2014 gave its themes of illness and endurance a new weight.","Listen with headphones and focus on the voice: its slowness, its accent, the way words are stretched across bars. Then shift attention to what happens in the silences between lines. Compare King Tubby's dubs for the space, Public Enemy's Fear of a Black Planet for a louder dystopia, and Burial's album, also on Hyperdub, for a different kind of darkness."],"actress-splazsh":["An album of warped, murky electronic music that draws on house, techno, dubstep and hip-hop but sounds like none of them. Beats are wonky or buried in hiss; synthesizer lines are fragmented and smeared; some tracks are almost ambient. The mood is strange and dreamlike, as if dance music were being remembered badly.","Actress, Darren Cunningham, from Wolverhampton, had been a promising young footballer before turning to music, and ran the Werkdiscs label. The album, released by Honest Jon's in 2010, appeared as the London dubstep scene was diversifying into new, less defined forms, often called post-dubstep or 'outsider house', and as Detroit techno and Chicago house were being rediscovered by British producers who wanted something stranger than the club mainstream.","Many sounds are deliberately degraded, run through low-quality effects and filtered until they lose their edges, which gives the record its blurry character. Listen for how the rhythms drift slightly out of time, and for drum-machine patterns that recall Detroit techno but seem to have been left out in the rain. The imperfection is intentional: familiar club forms are being made unfamiliar.","Critics praised it for its originality and it appeared on many year-end lists, though some listeners found it too obscure and unfinished-sounding. Its reputation has grown with Actress's later albums, and it is now seen as a key text of the post-dubstep era, the point where British bass music turned inward and began to sound like an art music of memory and texture.","Listen once as you would to ambient music, in the background, then again as dance music, at volume, paying attention to the beats. The two listens give different albums. Do not expect hooks; expect moods. Compare Burial's albums, Brian Eno's On Land and David Bowie's Low for other ways of making electronic music feel like a place."],"mala-mala-in-cuba":["Mala fuses dubstep's deep bass and half-time beats with Cuban piano, percussion and voices, recorded with musicians in Havana. The result is a warm, spacious album where congas and batá drums move against heavy sub-bass, and repeating piano figures in the Cuban style weave through dub echo. Some tracks are almost pure dubstep, others close to Cuban dance music, and a few are slow and devotional. The album unfolds over more than an hour.","Mala, of Digital Mystikz and the DMZ label, was invited by the DJ and broadcaster Gilles Peterson to Cuba as part of a project connecting London producers with Havana musicians. He recorded sessions with local players, then spent a long time in London editing and building tracks around them. The album appeared in 2012 on Peterson's Brownswood label, as dubstep was fragmenting and its loudest American form was dominating the charts.","Listen for how the Cuban rhythms, built on the interlocking patterns of the clave, sit against the steady half-time of dubstep: the two systems never quite merge, and that tension is part of the appeal. Many percussion and piano parts were edited and rearranged after recording, so a phrase that sounds like a live take may be a collage. Jamaica and Cuba, neighbours across a narrow sea, meet here via London.","Praised for its originality and its respect for the Cuban players, who are heard as partners rather than exotic samples, it was taken as a sign of dubstep's maturity. Some purists missed the harder edge of Mala's DMZ work, and some world-music critics questioned how much of Cuba survived the editing. It remains the most cited example of dubstep reaching beyond London.","Listen for the conversation between percussion and bass: the drums chatter quickly while the bass moves slowly underneath. Pick one piano phrase and follow it through its echoes. Play it loud on speakers with real low end. Compare Augustus Pablo's King Tubby Meets Rockers Uptown for the dub roots, and Miles Davis's Sketches of Spain for another cross-cultural album."],"floating-points-promises":["A single continuous work in nine movements. A short, gentle keyboard figure repeats throughout, while Pharoah Sanders's tenor saxophone improvises around it, sometimes tender, sometimes raw. Strings of the London Symphony Orchestra gradually swell, and synthesizers add depth. The music builds slowly to a climax, then falls away into silence.","Floating Points, Sam Shepherd, a producer with a doctorate in neuroscience and a career in club music, collaborated with the veteran spiritual-jazz saxophonist Pharoah Sanders, John Coltrane's former partner, then in his late seventies. The two had met through Sanders's admiration for Shepherd's earlier album. The sessions stretched over several years, with the London Symphony Orchestra's strings added later. The album appeared in 2021 during the pandemic, and was Sanders's last major recording before his death in 2022.","The repeating seven-note figure is played on different instruments at different moments, including harpsichord, piano and celesta, so it seems to change colour while staying the same. Listen for how Sanders often plays around the figure rather than with it, creating a gentle tension. In one passage he sets the saxophone down and simply vocalises; it is easy to miss, and one of the most human moments on the record.","Widely acclaimed and named on many lists of the best albums of the year, it was praised for its patience and beauty and brought Sanders to a young audience who had never heard Karma. Some jazz critics wished he had been given more room to burn as he did in the 1960s; others saw the restraint as the point, an elder's late style. Whether it is jazz, ambient or classical is still argued.","Listen from start to finish in one sitting, with no distractions and the volume fairly high, since much of it is quiet. Hold on to the repeating keyboard figure as a landmark, and notice when the strings begin to swell beneath it. Compare Pharoah Sanders's Karma for his fiery youth, and Arvo Pärt's Spiegel im Spiegel for another music built from stillness."],"overmono-good-lies":["An album of emotional, euphoric UK bass music. Chopped and pitched vocal samples, rushing breakbeats, deep bass and bright synthesizers combine into tracks that feel both nostalgic and new. The music draws on rave, garage and dubstep, with an emphasis on melody and feeling rather than hardness. Most tracks are compact, four or five minutes, built around a single vocal hook that is repeated, pitched and cut until it becomes the melody.","Overmono are the Welsh brothers Tom and Ed Russell, who had separately produced hard techno and breakbeat music as Truss and Tessela before joining forces. After several years of club EPs and a reputation for their live shows, their debut album appeared in 2023 on XL. It arrived at a time when British club music, after the pandemic's closed venues, was eagerly revisiting its own rave and garage past.","Listen for the vocal chops, which owe much to Burial and to garage producers like MJ Cole: fragments of words are pitched and re-sequenced until they become hooks with no clear meaning. The breakbeats often recall the classic breaks of jungle, but slowed to house tempo, so a sound once built for frenzy now carries longing. The brothers' techno past shows in the tight, punchy kick drums.","Widely praised as one of the best electronic albums of its year, it also found listeners well beyond the clubs. Some critics found it too nostalgic, a greatest-hits tour of British dance music's past rather than a step forward; others praised it as a joyous celebration of that heritage, made with rare craft. The debate mirrors wider arguments about revivalism in 2020s club music.","Listen loud, ideally on a good system, and let the vocal hooks lodge themselves before you try to hear the words. Notice how the drums push forward while the voices sound wistful. Compare Burial's Untrue for the emotional source, Skream's 'Midnight Request Line' for the dubstep roots, and New Order's 'Blue Monday' for older British dance music with a sad heart."],"raga-megh-malhar":["The rain raga. Five notes going up, more coming down, with two forms of the seventh degree rubbing against each other — a flattened one on the way down that gives the raga its heaviness. Performances tend to be broad and slow-swelling rather than ornate, with long held notes that the singer lets thicken and then break, and a characteristic swing between the second and fourth degrees that imitates, by common agreement, the roll of approaching thunder.","Ragas in the North Indian system are tied to hours and seasons, and Malhar belongs to the monsoon, the weeks in July and August when the whole subcontinent waits for water. The family is old: Megh appears in medieval treatises, and the Malhar group grew through the Mughal centuries into a dozen related ragas. The legend that Tansen's Megh Malhar brought rain to Akbar's drought-struck court is the best-known story in Indian music.","Listen for the two different sevenths. Most ragas settle on one form of a note; here the flattened seventh appears in descending phrases while the natural one is reserved for the climb, so the same scale degree keeps changing its colour. That instability is the raga's weather. Notice also how rarely the performer ornaments the tonic itself — it is left plain, a horizon line under the moving clouds.","Whether Tansen or anyone else can cause rain is not a question musicians spend much time on, but the seasonal system itself is argued over: concert life, radio schedules and air conditioning have loosened the old rules, and many performers now play a monsoon raga in February without apology. Scholars also disagree about how much of the modern Malhar family descends from a single older raga and how much was assembled later.","Hear it in the rainy season if you can — the association is half the effect. Start with a vocal performance rather than an instrumental one, since the words of the traditional compositions are almost all about clouds, peacocks and absent lovers. Then compare a Miyan ki Malhar, the grander cousin, and listen for how much darker the same weather can be made to sound."],"raga-malkauns":["Five notes only: the home note, a flattened third, the fourth, a flattened sixth and a flattened seventh. No second degree and — the crucial absence — no fifth. Performances are slow, low and dark, usually beginning far below the tonic and taking twenty minutes to climb an octave. The gesture you will recognise is the long glide down from the flattened sixth onto the fourth, a heavy, sagging fall that the raga keeps returning to.","Malkauns is a midnight raga, and the tradition surrounds it with warnings: it belongs to ascetics, to Shiva in his destroying aspect, to cremation grounds, and older musicians would say it should not be sung carelessly or in daylight. Something similar exists in the Carnatic south as Hindolam. The name is usually read as a compound of Mala and Kaushik, though the etymology, like most raga etymologies, is contested.","The missing fifth is the whole point. In almost every other system the fifth above the home note is the anchor that tells the ear where it stands; without it, and with the tanpura drone sounding the tonic and fourth instead, the music floats. That is why Malkauns sounds unmoored even to listeners who cannot name a single note in it.","Because so few notes are permitted, Malkauns is treated as a test: there is nowhere to hide, and a performance stands or falls on the shaping of individual notes rather than on display. Some critics complain that its solemnity has become a mannerism and that every performer now sounds like a priest. Its scale has been borrowed freely by film composers, which purists have resented for eighty years.","Give it time — the first fifteen minutes will seem to contain almost no events, and then you will notice you have learned the raga's gravity. Sarod suits it particularly well, since the fretless fingerboard makes the descending glide continuous. Afterwards, play any Western piece built on five notes and hear how differently a pentatonic scale can be inhabited."],"raga-darbari-kanada":["The grandest of the late-night ragas, and the slowest. It lives in the lower octave, where a singer's voice loses brightness and an instrument's strings sound thickest. Two notes — the flattened third and the flattened sixth — are never played straight: they are given a wide, slow oscillation, a shake so unhurried you can hear each swing separately. A full performance can spend forty minutes below the home note before rising above it.","Darbar means court, and tradition assigns this raga to Tansen, the singer at the Mughal emperor Akbar's court in the later sixteenth century, who is said to have shaped it for performance late at night before a tired ruler. The Kanada family it belongs to is older and large. Whatever Tansen actually sang, the raga as performed today was fixed in the nineteenth century by the gharanas of Gwalior, Jaipur and Kirana.","The oscillation on the flattened third is the raga's signature and its trap. It must be slow and must land neither on the note above nor on the note below; speed it up and you are in a different raga altogether, as teachers demonstrate by doing exactly that. Listen for how the singer enters the shake from underneath, so the note seems to arrive out of tune and then settle.","The Tansen attribution is legend rather than documentation, and scholars treat the four-century pedigree with caution. Musicians argue instead about tempo: the very slow tempos favoured since the 1950s, especially by Amir Khan, are heard by some as the raga's fullest form and by others as an indulgence recordings made possible. Its use in Hindi film scores has made its opening phrase instantly recognisable across South Asia.","Start with a vocal performance rather than an instrumental one, because the oscillation is easiest to hear in a human voice. Count how long it takes the singer to reach the octave above the home note. Then hear the same raga on sarod and notice how the fretless instrument turns the shake into a continuous bend rather than a series of separate pitches."],"raga-miyan-ki-todi":["A morning raga, and one of the strangest-sounding to an ear raised on European scales: flattened second, flattened third, flattened sixth and a sharpened fourth, all at once. The result is a scale in which almost nothing sits where a listener expects. Performances are plaintive rather than heavy, full of sliding approaches from below, and they lean hard on the sharpened fourth, which is bent and held until it aches against the drone.","The prefix Miyan marks it as belonging to Tansen, who was given that honorific at Akbar's court, and it distinguishes this Todi from a crowd of related ragas — Gujari Todi, Bilaskhani Todi, Multani — that share most of the same notes and differ in their permitted phrases. Todi gave its name to one of the ten thaats, the scale-families into which Vishnu Narayan Bhatkhande sorted North Indian music in the early twentieth century.","Listen for what the performer does with the flattened second. It is approached from above, pulled down onto, and almost never struck cleanly; teachers say the note should sound as though it is being wept rather than sung. Notice too that the raga's morning identity survives in the compositions, whose texts are full of waking, sunlight and the business of the household starting up.","The Todi group is the standard argument about how finely a raga can be split: several of its members use identical notes and are distinguished only by which phrases are allowed and which note is emphasised, and musicians disagree about whether those distinctions are real or inherited etiquette. Bhatkhande's thaat system, which made Todi a category as well as a raga, is itself still debated as a tidy scheme imposed on an untidy practice.","Play it in the morning, then in the evening, and see whether the tradition is on to something. A khayal performance in slow tempo shows the raga best. Afterwards listen to Multani, an afternoon raga built from the same notes, and try to hear what makes them different — that comparison teaches more about ragas than any definition."],"raga-yaman":["Yaman uses the seven notes of the major scale with the fourth raised a semitone. A full recital moves through three stages: an unmeasured alap in which the soloist unfolds the raga note by note over a tanpura drone, a pulsed jor, and then a fixed composition set in a rhythmic cycle and driven from slow to fast. The sound to recognise is the long descent that leans on the raised fourth and rests a semitone below the home note before arriving.","Yaman belongs to the Kalyan family and is performed in the first hours after sunset. Its double name carries its history: Kalyan is the older Sanskrit term, Yaman the Persianised one that spread through the courts of northern India, where musicians, Sufi poets and instrument makers from further west worked alongside temple-trained singers. By the seventeenth century it appears in treatises and in the ragamala paintings that gave each raga a face. It is still the first raga most students are given.","Listen to the note a semitone under the home note. In European music that is a leading tone, obliged to resolve upward at once; in Yaman it is a resting place, and phrases will sit on it, circle it, and only then fall home. Notice too how rarely the soloist approaches the tonic head-on. The raga is defined as much by the paths it forbids as by the notes it permits.","Musicians argue about where Yaman ends and Yaman Kalyan begins — the latter admits the natural fourth, and opinions differ on whether that makes a separate raga or a licence inside the same one. Gharanas disagree about how much time the alap deserves and whether the character lives in the composition or the improvisation. Recordings have quietly shortened performances that once ran two hours into something that fits an album.","Start with an unaccompanied alap, where nothing distracts from the notes themselves; a sitar or a khayal voice will do. Give it ten minutes before expecting a rhythm. Then hear its southern cousin Kalyani and listen to what the two traditions make of the same scale: one unfolds it in silence, the other sets it to words and a drum cycle."],"raga-bhairavi":["All four alterable notes are flattened — second, third, sixth and seventh — which gives Bhairavi a soft, minor-sounding base. But the raga is famous for what it allows on top of that: in practice performers borrow any of the remaining notes as passing colours, so a Bhairavi can touch all twelve pitches without leaving itself. It is usually short, warm and song-like, with thumri and bhajan texts rather than grand abstract compositions.","By convention Bhairavi ends a concert, whatever the hour, and the audience knows the evening is over the moment it begins. Its original place was early morning, and temple singers used it at dawn; the closing role grew out of that association with completion and blessing. Its openness made it the natural raga for lighter forms — the thumri, the ghazal, the devotional song — and for film composers, who have used it more than any other.","The name travels badly. In the Carnatic south, Bhairavi is a different raga entirely, while the northern Bhairavi's scale corresponds to the southern Hanumatodi — so two musicians from either side of the country can agree on a raga's name and play nothing alike. Listen also for the moment a performer slips in a natural note that the strict scale forbids: that licence is the raga's identity, not a mistake.","Because so much is permitted, teachers warn that Bhairavi is the easiest raga to play badly and the hardest to play well; the standard complaint is that it has been loosened into a mood rather than a raga. Its use as a closing number has also been criticised for turning a morning raga into a curtain call. Nobody proposes changing the custom.","Listen to the last ten minutes of any long Hindustani concert recording and you will probably be hearing it. A thumri in Bhairavi, with its half-sung, half-spoken text, is the fastest way in. Then find a Carnatic Bhairavi and hear the confusion for yourself — the same word, an entirely different piece of music."],"purandara-dasa-jagadoddharana":["A devaranama — a devotional song in Kannada — of four short verses over a refrain. Yashoda rocks the infant Krishna on her lap and the poet points out the absurdity: she is dandling the one who upholds the world. The melody is simple enough for a child, moves in a narrow range, and is usually sung slowly with a violin shadowing the voice a beat behind and a mridangam keeping a light eight-beat cycle.","Purandara Dasa was a wealthy merchant in the Vijayanagara empire who, by tradition, gave away everything and became a wandering singer. He wrote in Kannada rather than Sanskrit, for ordinary listeners rather than temple specialists, and he devised the graded exercises with which Carnatic students still begin — the same finger patterns and syllables, five centuries on. Later musicians named him the grandfather of the tradition.","The tune most singers use is not his. What survived was text and a raga name; the melodies heard today are largely nineteenth- and twentieth-century settings, and the familiar version in raga Kapi owes much to performers of the recording era. Listen for the refrain returning after each verse with a different weight of ornament — that variation is the performer's contribution, not the composer's.","The corpus attributed to Purandara Dasa runs into the hundreds of thousands of songs, a figure no scholar accepts literally; sorting his work from four centuries of accretion is unfinished. What is not in doubt is the pedagogy, which is documented, continuous and still in daily use. M. S. Subbulakshmi's recordings made this particular song known far beyond Karnataka.","Hear it sung by a great voice with almost no accompaniment first, then in a full concert setting; the song survives both. Follow the refrain and count how many times it returns. If you want to understand what the Carnatic tradition owes him, listen to a beginner's first lesson — the exercises are his, and they have not changed."],"raga-kalyani":["The southern cousin of Yaman: the major scale with a raised fourth, and in the Carnatic reckoning the sixty-fifth of the seventy-two parent scales. What differs is the treatment. Where the north unfolds the raga in free time, a Carnatic performance takes a shorter improvised alapana and then settles into composed songs, with the raised fourth given a shaking, sliding ornament that never lets it sit still. A long Kalyani may end in a rapid exchange between singer and drummer.","Venkatamakhin's seventeenth-century treatise sorted South Indian ragas into seventy-two parent scales by counting every possible combination of altered notes — a piece of systematic thinking that has no exact parallel in the north, and that gave later composers a map of scales nobody had yet used. Kalyani sits in the middle of that scheme and became one of the most performed ragas of all, carrying major works by every composer of the Trinity.","Listen to the raised fourth. In the north it is bent and leaned on; here it is shaken, with an oscillation applied to the note as a property of the raga rather than a choice of the performer. Carnatic music notates such ornaments as part of the raga's identity, which is why a plain, unshaken performance sounds not austere but wrong.","The seventy-two-scale system has been criticised since it was published for generating scales that no one sings and for implying that ragas are scales, which they are not. Its defenders point out that it gave composers a usable grammar and that dozens of once-theoretical scales have since become living ragas. The overlap with Hindustani Yaman is a standard exhibit in arguments about how far the two systems were ever one.","Listen to a Carnatic Kalyani and a Hindustani Yaman back to back — the same notes, two completely different manners. Notice how much faster the southern performance gets to a fixed composition, and how the drum joins in. A violin performance shows the ornaments most clearly, since the player can slide where a singer must breathe."],"dikshitar-vatapi-ganapatim":["The piece that opens more Carnatic concerts than any other. A Sanskrit invocation to Ganesha, set in a bright five-note raga, Hamsadhwani, that leaves out the fourth and sixth degrees and so climbs in clean leaps. The form is the standard kriti — an opening refrain, a second section, a longer verse — with room for the singer to improvise sequences of note-names before returning to the words. Ten minutes, brisk, and built to clear the air.","Muthuswami Dikshitar was one of the three composers who defined Carnatic music in the decades around 1800, all three living within a few miles of one another near Thanjavur. He had spent years in Varanasi, absorbed North Indian ragas, wrote almost entirely in Sanskrit, and composed pilgrimages: song after song addressed to a specific deity in a specific temple. Vatapi is Badami, in the Deccan, from which the image of Ganesha was said to have been carried south as war booty.","The raga is younger than the song. Hamsadhwani is generally credited to Ramaswami Dikshitar, the composer's father, which makes this one of the first pieces written in a scale invented within the family. Listen also for the word guruguha buried in the final verse — Dikshitar signed every composition with it, a name for the god Murugan and for himself.","Hamsadhwani travelled back north in the twentieth century and is now sung in Hindustani concerts too, one of the few clear cases of traffic running that way. Performance practice is argued over: how much improvisation an opening item should carry, whether the tempo has crept up over the past century, and how far singers may stray from the version transmitted by Dikshitar's disciples.","Notice the first phrase — it lands on a high note almost immediately, which is why the song works as a concert opener. Then listen for the passage where the singer abandons words for note-names and the violin answers each phrase. Compare two recordings a generation apart and you will hear the tempo question for yourself."],"tyagaraja-endaro-mahanubhavulu":["The last of the five Pancharatna, the Five Gems, and the longest. A Telugu text saluting the many great souls who have praised the divine, set in raga Sri and an eight-beat cycle. After the opening refrain the song runs through a chain of verses, each beginning with a passage of sung note-names before the words return — a structure that turns the piece into a tour of the raga. It is normally performed by everyone present at once, in unison, rather than by a soloist.","Tyagaraja lived in the village of Tiruvaiyaru on the Kaveri, sang to Rama every day, and by all accounts refused the patronage of the Thanjavur court when it was offered — the standard telling has him rejecting wealth in a song composed on the spot. He wrote in Telugu, the language of the region's elite, and left several hundred kritis. Each January musicians gather at his samadhi and sing the Five Gems together.","The unison performance is a modern invention, not a composer's instruction: the mass singing at the annual festival dates from the twentieth century, when rival factions of musicians were brought together and given something they could all perform side by side. Listen for the sections of pure note-names — they are written out, not improvised, which is unusual, and they are the reason the piece doubles as a lesson in raga Sri.","Nothing survives in Tyagaraja's hand. His songs passed through disciples and their disciples, and different schools transmit different versions of the same kriti, so editions argue over notes and even words. Scholars have also questioned how much of the biography — the rejected patronage, the brother throwing the Rama idols in the river — is hagiography. The music's authority has never depended on settling either question.","Find a recording of the January festival, with hundreds of musicians singing together and the violins doubling them, then hear a single voice perform the same piece: the difference tells you what the tradition does with its founder. Listen for the moment each verse switches from words to note-names, and follow how the melody climbs a little higher each time."],"tyagaraja-nagumomu-galaleni":["A reproach disguised as a love song: the poet asks Rama why he withholds his smiling face, and whether the god has forgotten who carried the earth on his back. The raga, Abheri, uses a flattened third and seventh with a plain second and sixth, which gives it a wistfulness close to the blues. The melody falls more than it rises, and the refrain returns each time from a little further above, so the song seems to keep sinking back.","This is Tyagaraja in his most personal mode — not doctrine but complaint, addressed to a deity he treated as a household presence. The kriti form he worked in had been a devotional song; he made it a vehicle capable of argument, doubt and irritation, with the improvised passages placed where the emotion needed room. Its popularity outside the temple circuit came through the concert stage and then the gramophone.","Which raga this actually is has been argued for a century. Sung with one form of the sixth degree it is Abheri; with another it is closer to the raga now called Karnataka Devagandhari, and performers have quietly changed sides over the decades. Listen to two recordings from different generations and you may hear two different ragas under the same title — a rare case of the tradition disagreeing in public.","The song's fame has made it a test piece and, some critics say, a crowd-pleaser performed too often and too sweetly. Its scale is close enough to a minor pentatonic with added notes that it has been picked up by film composers and by jazz musicians, which has both spread it and blurred it. Scholars continue to work on establishing texts from the disciple lineages.","Listen for the falling refrain and how each return is approached from higher up. Then hear the passage where the singer improvises on a single line of text, repeating it with a new shape each time — Carnatic music calls this neraval, and this song is the standard place to learn what it sounds like."],"bismillah-khan-red-fort-recital":["The shehnai is a conical double-reed pipe with a flared metal bell, loud enough to be heard across a temple courtyard and traditionally played in groups, with one player holding a single unchanging note as a drone beneath the soloist. Bismillah Khan's playing bends between pitches rather than stepping, using the reed and the half-covered finger holes, and his closing Bhairavi — short, sweet, endlessly ornamented — became the sound the instrument is remembered by.","He played at the Red Fort in Delhi as India became independent in August 1947, and for decades afterwards his shehnai opened the Independence Day broadcast on All India Radio, so that the instrument became a national sound before most listeners had ever seen one. Until then the shehnai had belonged to weddings and temple processions; Bismillah Khan, who played daily at the Balaji temple in Varanasi, carried it onto the concert platform.","Listen for the breath that never stops. Reed players in this tradition inhale through the nose while keeping air pressure in the cheeks, so a phrase can run for a minute without a seam. Listen also for the second shehnai droning underneath: it takes the place of the tanpura, and because it is a wind instrument, the drone itself breathes and wavers rather than sitting still.","A Shia Muslim whose life's work was performed in and for a Hindu temple, he became the standard example in arguments about India's shared culture, and was invoked by every side. He received the Bharat Ratna in 2001 and died in Varanasi in 2006, having refused to move abroad. Critics who wanted him to record more concert-length ragas got fewer than they hoped; his fame rested on short pieces.","Start with a Bhairavi rather than a long raga — his gift was for the closing miniature. Listen for the drone shehnai underneath and notice it moving. Then hear the same raga on a stringed instrument and you will understand why a wind instrument's inability to sustain forever changes the shape of every phrase."],"kesarbai-kerkar-jaat-kahan-ho":["Three and a half minutes of khayal in Bhairavi, cut to fit one side of a shellac disc. The text asks a young woman where she is going alone. Kesarbai Kerkar sings in a voice of unusual weight and squareness, attacking notes straight rather than sliding into them, and the compression is audible: phrases that would take five minutes to unfold in concert are stated once, complete, and left.","She was trained in the Jaipur-Atrauli style by Alladiya Khan, whose method built long, complex melodic sentences rather than displays of speed, and she guarded her art fiercely — she recorded rarely, controlled what was issued, and reportedly forbade broadcasts she had not approved. The 78rpm disc, with its four-minute limit, forced an entire tradition of hour-long performances into a form it was never designed for.","This recording is in interstellar space. The committee assembling the Voyager Golden Record in 1977 chose it to represent India, and copies are aboard both probes, now beyond the edge of the solar system. Listen to how she ends phrases — cleanly, on the beat, with none of the trailing ornament most singers use — and you can hear a performer who had learned to make a shellac side sound finished.","Her reputation among musicians was always higher than her public profile, precisely because she recorded so little; the handful of surviving sides are treated as documents. The Voyager selection has been discussed ever since as a piece of cultural politics — why this singer, this raga, this fragment — and the fact that the Golden Record's most-played Indian track is four minutes of a two-hour art form is part of the argument.","Play it twice: once as a piece of music, once as a message to no one in particular. Listen for the squareness of her attack, which sounds almost blunt beside later singers. Then find a full-length Jaipur-Atrauli khayal and hear what the disc had to leave out."],"ali-akbar-khan-morning-and-evening-ragas":["One raga to a side, played on sarod — a fretless instrument with a polished metal fingerboard, a skin belly and a bank of sympathetic strings that ring untouched. The absence of frets means every note can be slid into, and Ali Akbar Khan's sound is built on that: a plucked note that keeps moving after it is struck. Chatur Lal's tabla enters only after the long unmeasured opening, and the drone runs underneath throughout.","Yehudi Menuhin heard him play in India, arranged an American visit, and in 1955 Ali Akbar Khan performed on New York television and recorded this disc, with a spoken introduction by Menuhin to explain what listeners were about to hear. It was the first long-playing record devoted to Indian classical music issued in the West. The LP itself was the enabling technology: nothing shorter could have held a raga in recognisable form.","The sarod descends from the Afghan rabab, and the metal plate that replaced the fretted wooden neck was a nineteenth-century modification made for exactly this sliding style. Listen for the sympathetic strings: they are never plucked, but after a loud phrase you can hear the instrument continuing to sound by itself. His father Allauddin Khan of Maihar, who taught him, also taught Ravi Shankar.","The record established the terms on which Indian classical music would be received in the West for a generation — as spiritual, ancient and wordless — which musicians have been arguing against ever since. It also created the audience that made Shankar's career possible and that Coltrane, among others, listened into. Ali Akbar Khan later founded a college in California and spent forty years teaching Americans the system rather than the mystique.","Listen first for the difference between sarod and sitar: the sitar rings and buzzes, the sarod is drier and slides further. Track the moment the tabla enters and the music acquires a pulse. Then go to a live concert recording of the same raga, three times the length, and hear how much the LP compressed."],"shankar-three-ragas":["Three ragas, each compressed into the span an LP side allows, and each following the full arc: an unmeasured alap in which the sitar states the raga alone, a pulsed jor, a composition in a rhythmic cycle with the tabla, and a closing jhala where the drone strings are strummed between melody notes at increasing speed. The sitar sound — metallic, buzzing at the bridge, ringing on afterwards — is the thing most listeners had never heard before.","Ravi Shankar recorded it in London on his first Western tour, having spent his teens touring Europe with his brother Uday's dance company and then seven years in the village of Maihar learning from Allauddin Khan. He was thirty-six, already the director of All India Radio's orchestra, and he arrived with a plan: explain the form from the stage, then play. The record reached musicians in New York and Los Angeles within a few years.","Listen for what the sitar does after the note stops. Thirteen sympathetic strings run under the frets, tuned to the raga, and they keep sounding, so the instrument accompanies itself with a haze of the same notes. The curved bridge that produces the buzz is shaped by hand and adjusted with a strip of thread; players call the resulting shimmer jawari, and the whole sound of the record depends on it.","Shankar spent the rest of his life managing what this music became in the West — the applause for his tuning at Monterey, the audiences who came for the associations rather than the ragas, his own discomfort with the drug culture that adopted him. Critics in India sometimes accused him of simplifying for foreigners. The counter-argument is the record itself, which concedes nothing structurally and merely shortens.","Do what he asked audiences to do: learn the four stages before you listen, then follow them. The entry of the tabla is the easiest landmark. Notice how the jhala at the end works — the melody is the same speed, but the drone strums between the notes multiply. Then hear a two-hour concert version and the shape will be familiar."],"vilayat-khan-jalsaghar":["A score for Satyajit Ray's film about a landowner who spends the last of his money on concerts in his crumbling mansion. Vilayat Khan wrote and played the sitar music that runs between scenes, and the film's long performance sequences are shot almost unbroken: a thumri singer, a kathak dancer with ankle bells answering the drummer, a room of guests going quiet. The sitar outside the room is thin, wandering and unaccompanied; the music inside is full and social.","Ray had used Ravi Shankar for his first films; here he wanted a musician from inside the courtly world the film was mourning, and Vilayat Khan came from seven generations of players. The subject was the collapse of the zamindar class in Bengal after the 1793 land settlement and its final ruin at independence — patrons whose vanity had paid for this music for two centuries, filmed at the moment the money ran out. Begum Akhtar appears as a singer.","Vilayat Khan's style is called gayaki ang, the singing manner: he pulled the string sideways across the fret to bend up to five notes from one stopped position, so the sitar could imitate a vocalist's slides instead of striking separate pitches. He also altered the instrument's stringing to suit it. Listen to the solo passages between scenes and you are hearing a sitar deliberately trying not to sound like a sitar.","The film was slow to travel and is now among Ray's most admired; the music is one reason. Vilayat Khan's relationship with the Indian establishment was thorny — he declined state honours more than once — and the long-running comparison with Ravi Shankar, pressed by partisans of both, irritated him for fifty years. Critics still debate whether his cinema work diluted his concert art or simply preserved it.","Watch the film if you can; the music is inseparable from the rooms it echoes in. If you only listen, start with the unaccompanied sitar passages and notice how far a single note is bent. Then hear one of his concert ragas and listen for the same vocal shapes stretched over forty minutes instead of forty seconds."],"amir-khan-raga-marwa":["A sunset raga sung at a tempo so slow the beat almost disappears: a single cycle can take a minute, and the words of the composition arrive in fragments minutes apart. Marwa has a flattened second and a raised fourth, and it starves the listener of the home note, hovering instead around the sixth. Amir Khan's voice is unforced, almost conversational, and he builds by permutation — the same few notes reordered, again and again, each time revealing another path.","He came from Indore, learned from his father, a sarangi player, and took from the Kirana, Bhendibazaar and Gwalior traditions rather than belonging cleanly to any. His slow tempos were unusual enough to be considered a school of their own by the 1950s, and younger singers everywhere adopted them. He also sang on the soundtrack of Baiju Bawra in 1952, which put a strict classical voice in front of a mass film audience.","Notice how seldom the home note is sounded. Marwa leans on the note a major sixth above it, so the drone keeps insisting on a tonic the melody keeps avoiding, and the ear is left permanently off balance — the tension is the raga. Listen also for the way he arrives at a phrase: not by ornament but by reordering the notes he has already used, a permutational method borrowed from the old merukhand discipline.","The slowness divided opinion at the time and still does: admirers hear concentration, detractors hear a performance that refuses to begin. His influence, though, is not contested — most Hindustani vocalists after 1960 sing slower openings because of him. He died in a road accident in Calcutta in 1974, and the recorded legacy is smaller than his stature, which has kept the surviving concert tapes in constant circulation.","Do not start at the beginning if you are impatient; drop in twenty minutes and then go back. Listen for the drone and try to sing the home note yourself — you will feel how rarely the singer confirms it. Then hear a fast composition in the same raga and notice how the tension survives the tempo."],"subbulakshmi-venkatesa-suprabhatam":["A morning hymn to wake the deity at Tirumala, sung in Sanskrit over some thirty verses. It is closer to chant than to song: most of it moves within a few notes, in a measured recitation that opens out into melody only at the sections that close the sequence. M. S. Subbulakshmi's voice is bright and perfectly even, the accompaniment is thin, and the whole thing proceeds at walking pace, as though the listener is genuinely being woken.","Recorded for HMV in 1963, it became the most widely heard piece of devotional music in South India: temples, radio stations and households play it at dawn, and for millions it is simply the sound of five in the morning. Subbulakshmi had come to the concert stage from Madurai by way of the cinema, starring as the poet-saint Meera in 1945, and she carried a film star's reach into a repertoire of temple texts.","The piece works because it is not fully sung. Recitation on two or three notes gives the words room and keeps the pitch low enough for anyone to join, which is why it is one of the few classical recordings people sing along with. Listen for the shift near the end, where the flat recitation finally blooms into a shaped melody — the moment the deity is assumed to be awake.","Her career has been reassessed more than once. Admirers point to the discipline and the extraordinary evenness of tone; critics have examined how far her repertoire and public image were shaped by her husband and by a Brahminical cultural establishment into which she, from a devadasi family, was carefully assimilated. She was the first musician to receive the Bharat Ratna, in 1998, and the arguments have not dented the recording's reach.","Play it at dawn, which is what it is for. Listen for how few notes the first ten minutes use, then for the moment the melody widens. Compare it with a Latin office hymn sung at the same hour and you will find two traditions solving the same problem — how to sing before anyone is properly awake — in almost the same way."],"ali-akbar-khan-chandranandan":["A raga Ali Akbar Khan made himself, by folding together four of the Kaunsi ragas — Malkauns among them — so that the ear keeps recognising familiar phrases and finding them in the wrong company. The result is dark and pentatonic-feeling, like Malkauns with extra doors. On sarod it is played low, with the long sliding descents the fretless board allows, and the composition that follows the opening is unusually song-like for something invented in a studio.","By the mid-1960s he was recording regularly for Western labels and dividing his year between India and California, where he founded a school of music in 1967. The commercial demand for new material collided with a tradition in which the repertoire is inherited, not composed; the standard account has him producing this raga when asked, more or less on the spot, for something that had not been recorded before.","New ragas are proposed constantly and almost never take. This one did: other musicians learned it, taught it, and it now appears on concert programmes without explanation, which within a living oral tradition is the strongest possible verdict. The name means something like delight of the moon. Listen for the moments where a Malkauns phrase begins and then turns aside into a note Malkauns does not allow.","Purists object in principle to invented ragas, arguing that a raga is not a scale a person can design but a body of practice that accumulates; others point out that every raga was new once and that the Kaunsi family in particular has always bred hybrids. The debate has largely been settled by use. Ali Akbar Khan's own view, expressed in teaching, was that the raga found him rather than the reverse.","Listen to Malkauns first, then this, and the pleasure is in catching the differences. Notice how the sarod's slides let one raga slip into another without a seam. Then hear a recording by a musician of the next generation and consider that you are listening to a tradition absorbing something made within living memory."],"bhimsen-joshi-raga-multani":["An afternoon raga built from the same notes as Miyan ki Todi and handled entirely differently — the flattened second is skipped on the way up, the sharpened fourth carries the weight, and the mood is drier and less plaintive. Bhimsen Joshi sings it with enormous breath: single phrases arch across a whole rhythmic cycle without a pause, and the fast passages that close the performance are delivered in unbroken runs at a volume most singers cannot sustain.","He left home in Karnataka at eleven to find a teacher, travelling across northern India for years before ending up with Sawai Gandharva of the Kirana tradition, whose method concentrated on the precise placing of each note rather than on ornament or speed. He later founded the festival in Pune named after that teacher, which became the largest annual gathering in Hindustani music, and he sang there almost every year for half a century.","Kirana singing approaches a note from below and settles on it visibly, so you can hear the pitch being found and fixed; other schools prefer to arrive already there. Listen to the first minute and count how long each phrase lasts before he breathes. The famous power is a by-product of that breath control, not the aim: the school's concern is intonation, and the volume is what a well-placed note happens to sound like.","His devotional recordings in Marathi and Kannada sold in enormous numbers and brought him an audience far outside the classical circuit, which some critics treated as a lowering of standards and others as the natural continuation of a tradition that was always devotional. He received the Bharat Ratna in 2008. Recordings of his very late concerts, when the breath had gone, are argued over by admirers who would rather not hear them.","Listen for the breath, then for the moment the tempo doubles and the runs begin. Compare his Multani with a Miyan ki Todi by anyone — the notes are the same and the two performances share almost nothing. If you want the popular side, his Marathi devotional songs are the other half of the career."],"subbulakshmi-un-concert":["A Carnatic recital given in the General Assembly hall on United Nations Day, 1966: kritis by the great Tamil and Telugu composers, a violin answering each vocal phrase, a mridangam holding the cycle, and a tambura drone behind everything. The programme closes with Maitreem Bhajata, a Sanskrit benediction written for the occasion, asking the nations to cultivate friendship — a text composed for a hall full of delegates and sung in a language almost none of them knew.","India was two decades independent, at war with Pakistan the previous year, and looking for a face to present abroad; Subbulakshmi, already the best-known singer in the country, was sent as a cultural ambassador in the most literal sense. The concert was arranged around the UN's anniversary, broadcast, and issued on record. She sang in a hall designed for speeches, with no temple acoustic and an audience of diplomats.","The closing benediction was written for that room. Its text asks for peace between nations rather than for any deity's favour, which is unusual in a repertoire almost entirely devotional, and the Sanskrit is deliberately plain. Listen for the violin: in Carnatic concerts it does not accompany but shadows, repeating each improvised phrase a beat later, so you hear every invention twice, once sung and once bowed.","Whether this was a concert or a diplomatic event has been asked since the day it happened, and the answer is plainly both. Critics have noted that the programme was shortened and smoothed for an audience with no grounding in the form. Others point out that the same compression happens on every record. For Indian listeners the occasion mattered more than the performance, which is itself a kind of criticism.","Listen to the violin, not the voice, for one whole item: following the shadowing is the fastest way to understand what improvisation means here. Then hear the closing benediction on its own. Compare the concert with her Suprabhatam recording of three years before to hear the same voice doing two entirely different jobs."],"chaurasia-call-of-the-valley":["A sequence of short pieces tracing a day in the life of a Kashmiri shepherd, played by three instruments that had barely appeared in classical music before: Hariprasad Chaurasia's bamboo flute, Shivkumar Sharma's santoor — a hundred-string box struck with light wooden hammers — and Brijbhushan Kabra's guitar, laid flat and played with a steel slide. Each track takes a raga appropriate to its hour, and none outstays the format. The santoor's rapid tremolo is the sound that lingers.","The santoor was a Kashmiri folk instrument used for Sufi song; Sharma's father set him to making it a classical instrument, and elders objected that its struck strings could not sustain a note and therefore could not carry a raga. Kabra had adapted an imported guitar to do what the sarod does. The record came out in India in 1967 and became one of the best-selling Indian classical albums ever made, then a fixture in Western shops in the 1970s.","The concept — a programme of short tracks describing a day, rather than one long raga — comes from the LP, not from the tradition, and that is why the record travelled. Listen to how the santoor handles a sustained note: it cannot, so Sharma trills it, and the shimmer that results became the instrument's signature rather than its apology. The slide guitar solves the same problem by sliding.","Purists called it light music and said the raga sequence was decorative. The counter-case is that two instruments entered the classical canon because of it, and that millions of listeners met Hindustani music here first. Chaurasia and Sharma later composed Hindi film scores together as Shiv-Hari, which confirmed the critics' suspicions and did not trouble either of them.","Take the tracks as miniatures, not as a compromised raga recital, and listen for the three instruments passing phrases between them. Notice the santoor's tremolo, then the flute's breathiness where the player overblows. Afterwards hear Chaurasia play a full-length raga alone and you will hear how much the album left in reserve."],"shankar-west-meets-east":["Yehudi Menuhin and Ravi Shankar, with Alla Rakha on tabla, in pieces Shankar wrote so that a classical violinist could take part in music built on improvisation he had no training in. The violin plays written lines; the sitar plays around them; the tabla holds cycles that do not divide the way a violinist's bar does. The most affecting moments are the slow ones, where Menuhin abandons vibrato and tries instead to bend between notes.","Menuhin had been visiting India since the 1950s and had already introduced Ali Akbar Khan to American audiences. By 1967 Shankar was famous beyond music — Monterey was that June, Harrison was studying with him — and the album arrived in the middle of a Western enthusiasm for India that was equal parts curiosity and fashion. It won a Grammy the following year in a chamber-music category, which tells you how the industry filed it.","The compromise is audible in the notation. Shankar had to write out the violin part because the idiom cannot be sight-read, and what Menuhin plays is therefore fixed while everything around him is invented — the opposite of the usual relationship between a soloist and an accompanist. Listen for the moments Menuhin drops vibrato entirely; that continuous shimmer, standard in European playing, is exactly what a raga cannot use.","It has been dismissed as a diplomatic exercise and defended as the first honest attempt at the problem, and both readings survive. Musicians on the Indian side noted that the traffic ran one way: the violinist adapted, the ragas did not. What is beyond argument is the influence — the record made collaboration between Indian and Western classical musicians a normal ambition rather than a novelty.","Start with the slow movement and listen only to the violin, then only to the sitar; the two are playing under different rules. Notice where Menuhin's intonation sits against the drone. Then hear Shakti, nine years later, where the Western player improvises and the compromise runs the other way."],"kumar-gandharva-nirguni-bhajans":["Settings of Kabir and other poets of the formless divine, sung in short breaths and plain tunes that owe as much to the folk singers of central India as to any classical school. The best known, about a swan that will one day fly off alone, is barely five minutes and uses perhaps six notes. The voice is reedy and a little cracked, the phrases stop where a lesser singer would push on, and the effect is of speech that has become song without meaning to.","A prodigy who was performing at ten, Kumar Gandharva contracted tuberculosis in his late twenties, lost the use of a lung, and spent years in bed in Dewas, forbidden to sing. What he did instead was listen — to the folk singers, pedlars and mendicants passing the house — and when he returned he had a shorter breath, a smaller voice and a repertoire nobody else was singing. Nirgun poetry, which rejects image and ritual, suited all three.","The style is built from the illness. Short phrases, abrupt silences, a refusal to sustain — all of it began as physical necessity and hardened into an aesthetic that younger singers now imitate deliberately, breathing where they do not need to. Listen for the silences: they fall in the middle of lines, where the sense is still hanging, and the poems are about impermanence, so the gaps do half the work.","Traditionalists accused him of abandoning gharana discipline, of singing folk tunes and calling them classical, and of building a career on eccentricity. He replied, in effect, that a tradition that cannot absorb what is sung in the street outside is already dead. His standing rose steadily after his death in 1992, and the nirguni recordings are now the part of his work most widely heard.","Start with the song about the swan and read a translation of the words first, because here the text is the point. Listen for where he stops. Then hear a long, grand khayal by any other singer of his generation and notice how much air this music has in it by comparison."],"kishori-amonkar-sahela-re":["A short composition in raga Bhoop whose text invites a companion to come and sing — the music is about its own making. Bhoop uses five notes, the same five as the major pentatonic that Western ears know from folk tunes, with no fourth and no seventh. Kishori Amonkar sings it slowly, with long swelling notes that she lets bloom and then thin out, and a way of leaving the last note of a phrase suspended rather than resolved.","She was trained by her mother, Mogubai Kurdikar, in the Jaipur-Atrauli manner of Alladiya Khan, which builds long, intricate melodic sentences across the rhythmic cycle rather than decorating single notes. She spent years resisting and then reworking that inheritance, and her mature style — more emotional, slower, less concerned with structural display — was her own argument with the school that produced her.","The scale is the most ordinary in the world, and that is the lesson. Play a pentatonic folk tune, then this, and you will hear that a raga is not a set of notes but a set of permitted moves — which note you may linger on, which you must pass through, where you may begin a phrase. Listen for how she approaches the fifth degree from above each time, never from below.","She lost her voice for a period in mid-career and returned with the sound she is now known for, a story admirers treat as a transformation and sceptics as a convenient legend. She was famously exacting with audiences and would stop for a ringing phone or a whisper. Critics divided over whether her late style deepened the Jaipur method or diluted its architecture; the recording has outlasted the argument.","Sing the five notes first, so you know exactly what material she has. Then listen for what she refuses to do with them. The piece is short enough to hear three times in half an hour, which is the right way to take it. Afterwards, any Western pentatonic melody will sound curiously unexamined."],"lalgudi-jayaraman-thillanas":["The thillana is the fast closing piece of a Carnatic concert or a dance recital, built mostly out of drum syllables rather than words, with a short passage of text near the end. Lalgudi Jayaraman wrote a set of them that entered the standard repertoire in his lifetime. Played on his own violin they are bright, springy and full of cross-rhythm: the melody lands off the beat and the mridangam catches it, over and over, until the final cadence arrives three times.","The violin reached South India around 1800 and was absorbed completely — retuned, played seated on the floor with the scroll braced against the ankle, and given the job of shadowing the singer. Lalgudi came from a family in Tyagaraja's disciple line and was among the first violinists to be billed as a soloist rather than an accompanist. His compositions, written for dancers as well as concert players, extended the tradition from inside it.","The posture is the technique. With the instrument's scroll resting on the player's ankle, the left hand is free of any duty to support the neck, so it can slide continuously up and down a string instead of shifting between fixed positions — which is precisely what the oscillating ornaments of a raga require. The tuning is in alternating fourths and fifths rather than fifths throughout, so two strings sound the home note.","Carnatic concerts have long argued about the balance between soloist and accompanist, and his career is the case study: admirers say he raised the violin to equal standing, others that the shadowing role was the more interesting job and is now performed with less care. His thillanas are sometimes called too charming, a criticism that has never stopped anyone from programming them.","Listen to the last three minutes of any Carnatic concert recording and you will find this form. Follow the drum syllables — they are nonsense words that describe strokes, and the melody is shaped to them. Then watch a Bharatanatyam dancer perform one, and the reason for all that cross-rhythm becomes visible."],"shakti-with-john-mclaughlin":["Recorded in concert, and the first record on which North Indian and South Indian musicians and a jazz guitarist improvise together as equals. No drum kit, no chords, no amplification to speak of: L. Shankar's violin and McLaughlin's guitar trade lines at impossible speed, Zakir Hussain's tabla answers them phrase for phrase, and Vikku Vinayakram's clay pot supplies a bass drum sound made by pressing the opening against his stomach.","McLaughlin had just come from the Mahavishnu Orchestra, the loudest band in jazz, and turned to an acoustic guitar with a scalloped fingerboard and added drone strings. The Indian musicians were young professionals from two traditions that rarely shared a stage with each other, let alone with a jazz player. The mid-1970s appetite for fusion gave the group a platform; what it did with it was harder and less decorative than most of what shared the shelf.","The rhythm is worked out in words before it is played. South Indian musicians recite drum patterns as spoken syllables, and the long unison passages here were learned that way, counted in cycles of odd lengths that resolve exactly on the downbeat. Listen for the moment all four players land together after a passage that sounded like it was falling apart; nothing about it is accidental.","Jazz critics called it fusion and filed it with the electric records it was reacting against; Indian critics asked what a guitar was doing in a raga. Both objections have faded, and the group's reunion decades later as Remember Shakti was received as a homecoming. The lasting argument is about influence: whether this opened the door for genuinely two-way collaboration or simply set a standard nobody has matched.","Start with a fast track and just follow the tabla; then play it again following the violin. The pleasure is in the unisons. Compare it with West Meets East from nine years earlier — there the Western player read from a page, here he improvises, and the difference is the whole story."],"nikhil-banerjee-raga-manj-khamaj":["A light-classical raga that mixes two parents — the sweet, folk-leaning Khamaj and the graver Bageshri — and is usually placed at the end of a concert, when the strict repertoire is finished and the audience wants melody. Nikhil Banerjee plays it without display: the alap is long and quiet, the tabla enters late, and even the fast closing section is articulated rather than hurled. The tone is rounder and less percussive than most sitarists', with very little buzz.","He trained with Allauddin Khan at Maihar, in the same lineage as Ravi Shankar and Ali Akbar Khan, but stayed almost entirely in India and avoided the festival circuit and the crossover projects. He played long concerts to small halls, refused to shorten ragas for records, and died in 1986 at fifty-four. Most of what survives is concert tape made by listeners and organisers rather than studio sessions.","A mixed raga has to keep its parents apart. Phrases from Khamaj and phrases from Bageshri each have their own places in the cycle, and the skill lies in moving between them without letting either take over — which is why performers use this raga to show judgement rather than technique. Listen for the point where the melody turns grave for a few seconds and then brightens again.","His reputation has grown steadily since his death, partly because the tapes kept surfacing and partly because the qualities he was admired for — patience, tone, refusal of spectacle — became scarcer. Dating those recordings is often guesswork, and reissues carry years that should be treated as approximate. Some listeners rank him above his more famous contemporaries; the comparison would have embarrassed him.","Give the unaccompanied opening its full time; that is where he is unmistakable. Listen for the absence of the usual metallic rasp, then compare a Ravi Shankar recording of any raga and hear how differently two players from the same teacher set up the same instrument. Concert tapes rather than studio discs are the place to start."],"pran-nath-ragas-of-morning-and-night":["Two ragas, one for each end of the day, sung very slowly by a thin, unforced voice over two tanpuras tuned with unusual care. There is almost no ornament in the usual sense and no display at all; what there is instead is duration — single notes held until the ear stops hearing them as pitches and starts hearing them against the drone. The recording is close and dry, so the beating between voice and drone is audible.","Pandit Pran Nath trained in the Kirana style, spent years living as an ascetic, and in 1970 moved to New York at the invitation of La Monte Young and Marian Zazeela, who had heard his recordings and wanted to study with him. Terry Riley joined them. For the next quarter-century he taught a small circle of American composers in lofts and at the Dia Art Foundation, and sang far more often to them than to concert audiences.","The lesson his students took was about tuning. He insisted that each note of a raga has an exact place against the drone, audible as the point where the beating stops, and that most singers are approximate. That idea runs straight into the just-intonation obsessions of American minimalism — the tuned piano, the long-duration drone installations — which is one of the few clean cases of an Indian master reshaping Western composition directly.","Indian critics were lukewarm: the voice was slight, the repertoire narrow, the career spent abroad. His American reputation was the opposite, and the two assessments have never been reconciled. What is documented is the transmission — a generation of New York composers learned to sit with a drone and listen for beats because of these lessons, and said so repeatedly.","Use headphones and listen for the beating between the voice and the drone; when it slows to nothing, that is the note arriving. Give each raga twenty minutes before judging. Then play La Monte Young or Terry Riley immediately afterwards and the debt will be unmistakable."],"zakir-hussain-making-music":["Eight short pieces for tabla, Hariprasad Chaurasia's bamboo flute, John McLaughlin's guitar and Jan Garbarek's saxophone, recorded with the spacious, slightly cold sound the label is known for. Nothing here is a full raga; the tracks are scenes, some barely three minutes. The tabla is the lead voice as often as the accompanist, and the striking thing is how melodic it sounds — the lower drum bends its pitch under the heel of the hand, sliding between notes like a voice.","Zakir Hussain, son of the tabla master Alla Rakha, moved to the United States as a teenager and spent the 1970s and 1980s in projects that put Indian rhythm next to jazz, rock and West African drumming. This record came out of the European jazz world rather than the Indian concert circuit, produced in the label's house style, with a Norwegian saxophonist whose long tones sit on the drone like a second flute.","Listen to the pitch of the lower drum. Its black tuning patch is not decoration but mass, lowering and focusing the tone, and pressing the heel of the hand into the skin while striking bends the note upward — so a drum plays a melodic line. The smaller drum is tuned to the tonic with a hammer before every performance, which is why a tabla set belongs to one raga at a time.","The record sold well and was faulted, predictably, for prettiness; the label's aesthetic has always attracted that charge. Its defenders point out that it gave a mass audience the tabla as a melodic instrument rather than an exotic pulse. Hussain went on to win Grammys for cross-cultural percussion projects and became the most recognisable Indian musician of his generation after Shankar.","Listen to one track for the tabla alone and ignore everything else; the bends will emerge quickly. Then hear a straight tabla solo with only a drone for company, and finally an Indian classical duet, and you will have the three settings the instrument lives in. The original label mix is the one to find."],"fela-kuti-gentleman":["Three long tracks by Fela and his band Africa 70, the title piece filling most of the first side. It opens with Fela alone on tenor saxophone, a raw, searching solo, before the guitars lock into a tight, circling riff, the bass settles, Tony Allen's drums start their loose, rolling pulse and the horns punch in. Only after several minutes does Fela sing, in Pidgin English, answered by a chorus. The whole record moves like a slow tide rather than a song.","By 1973 Fela was running his own club, the Afrika Shrine, in Lagos, and had fully absorbed what he heard in America in 1969: James Brown's funk and the politics of Black Power. Nigeria was under military rule, flush with oil money, and its elite prized Western manners. Fela's answer was Afrobeat, sung in the Pidgin English that ordinary Nigerians across language groups could understand, and released on EMI Nigeria to a huge local audience.","The title song mocks Africans who wear a suit, tie and heavy socks in tropical heat to seem civilised, and Fela proudly declares himself no such gentleman. Listen to how the band withholds the downbeat: the bass and rhythm guitar play small, repeated figures that sit slightly off the beat, so the groove seems to float. The saxophone opening is deliberately rough; Fela was a limited but fearless player, and he knew it.","Gentleman was a local hit and is now counted among the first fully formed Afrobeat records, the point where the style's long form and its satire settled together. Critics outside Nigeria heard it only decades later through reissues from the late 1990s onward. Some jazz listeners find Fela's own solos crude; most now hear them as part of the record's candour rather than a flaw.","Give the title track its full length; the point is the build. Notice the moment each instrument enters over the first three minutes and how the horns stab rather than play melodies. Then listen to Tony Allen alone for a minute. Compare it with James Brown's Sex Machine to hear what Fela borrowed and what he slowed down."],"fela-kuti-expensive-shit":["Two long tracks, one per side. The title piece rides a bright, hurrying groove with stuttering guitars and blaring horn riffs, Fela's organ and saxophone breaking in before his vocal and the call-and-response chorus. The second side, Water No Get Enemy, is slower and warmer, a stately horn theme over a swaying beat that many listeners find the most beautiful thing Fela recorded. Both show Africa 70 at its tightest.","The title track comes from an incident in 1974: police raided Fela's commune, the Kalakuta Republic, and accused him of possessing cannabis; he is said to have swallowed the evidence, and when the authorities waited to retrieve it, he arranged for a clean sample to be handed over. He was freed and turned the humiliation of the police into a song. Water No Get Enemy uses a Yoruba proverb about the power of water, which no one can fight.","Water No Get Enemy is really an argument about power: water is gentle, needed by everyone and impossible to defeat, and Fela casts himself as water against the government. Listen for the tenor guitar, playing a short, choppy figure in the high register, and the rhythm guitar underneath it; the two interlock like the two hands of a drummer. The organ, Fela's own, often doubles the bass line in thick chords.","Nigerian audiences loved the cheek of the title track; outside Nigeria the crude title long kept the album out of polite discussion. Reissues since the late 1990s have made Water No Get Enemy one of Fela's most covered and sampled pieces. Historians note that the raid stories come mainly from Fela and his circle, and details vary between tellings.","Start with Water No Get Enemy and listen for the horn theme, then for the way the band drops almost to nothing and swells back. Then play the title track for the contrast in speed. Parliament's Mothership Connection, from the same year, is a good companion for hearing how two funk empires chose different paths."],"fela-kuti-zombie":["The title track is a twelve-minute march turned into a dance: a fast, driving beat, a curt, repeated horn riff that sounds like a bugle call, and Fela barking military commands that the chorus answers with the word zombie. Saxophone and trumpet solos stretch out the middle; then the commands return, faster and more absurd. The energy is relentless, the tone jeering. The companion side keeps the same band and the same attack on blind obedience.","Nigeria had been under military rule since 1966, and Fela's commune, the Kalakuta Republic, sat near a Lagos barracks. The song, which pictures soldiers as mindless zombies following orders, spread fast and was reportedly chanted at soldiers in the streets. In February 1977 around a thousand soldiers stormed Kalakuta, burned it, beat Fela and threw his elderly mother, the activist Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti, from an upstairs window. She died of her injuries the following year.","The horn riff is built like a parade-ground bugle signal, and the vocal is a parody of drill: Fela shouts orders such as attention, quick march and salute, and the chorus replies zombie each time. The satire is in the form itself. Listen to how the drums push slightly ahead of the band, making the march feel frantic rather than orderly, as if the parade is about to fall apart.","Zombie was a huge hit in Nigeria and the most costly record of Fela's life. Abroad it became his signature: Talking Heads, Brian Eno and later hip-hop producers cited it, and David Byrne said Remain in Light grew from listening to Fela. Critics now rank it among the defining protest records of the century, a case where a dance record brought down real state violence.","Put it on loud and let the first two minutes build before the vocal. Listen for each command and the chorus answer, then for the soldiers' march breaking into the band's much looser swing. Follow with James Brown's Cold Sweat, which it grows from, and Talking Heads' Remain in Light, which grew from it."],"fela-kuti-coffin-for-head-of-state":["A single piece in two parts across both sides of a record, played by Fela's new, larger band Egypt 80. The groove is slow, heavy and funereal, a mournful horn line over a dragging beat, and the whole first part is instrumental. When Fela finally sings, it is a long, narrated account: of Christianity and Islam as imported tools of control, and of the day he carried a coffin to the seat of government.","After the 1977 raid on Kalakuta, Fela's mother died in 1978. In 1979 he and his followers carried a coffin through Lagos and left it at Dodan Barracks, the residence of the military head of state Olusegun Obasanjo, a gesture both of mourning and accusation. Nigeria returned briefly to civilian rule that year. Fela had also founded his own political party and tried to run for president; the song is his record of a personal and national grief.","The song names names: Fela lists officials and lays blame on specific people, which almost no Nigerian musician would risk. It also refuses Christian and Muslim consolation, part of Fela's turn toward Yoruba religion and his own title, Anikulapo, meaning one who carries death in his pouch. Listen for the chorus gradually taking over the narration, as if the whole community were testifying.","In Nigeria the song was more reverenced than danced to; abroad it is less known than Zombie but many consider it Fela's most moving record. Some critics find its length and its religious polemic heavy going. It has become central to accounts of Fela as a political figure and to the Broadway musical Fela!, where the coffin procession is a climactic scene.","Treat it as a lament: let the long instrumental opening establish the mood before the words. Listen to how the horns keep returning to the same falling phrase like a dirge. Pair it with Billie Holiday's Strange Fruit and Marvin Gaye's What's Going On, other records that turned private grief into public accusation."],"ali-farka-toure-talking-timbuktu":["Ten songs in which Ali Farka Touré's guitar and quiet, rounded voice meet the American guitarist Ry Cooder's slide, bass and small studio band. The songs move in long, circling guitar figures over steady pulses, some with a light shuffle, some almost static. Touré also plays the njarka, a one-string fiddle. Cooder mostly listens and colours, his bottleneck guitar sliding into the gaps. The sound is spacious and unhurried, like a conversation on a porch.","Touré had become known outside Mali through albums for the London label World Circuit from 1987, and journalists kept comparing him to John Lee Hooker. Cooder, a scholar-guitarist with a gift for cross-cultural sessions, flew to meet him, and the two recorded quickly in Los Angeles with a few chosen players. The album won the Grammy for Best World Music Album and sold far beyond the usual audience for African records.","The American comparison was not flattery on either side: Touré said that when he heard Hooker he recognised the music of his own region, not the other way round. Listen to how many songs rest on one chord or a single bass note, the melody spiralling above: that is a Sahelian habit, not a blues one. Cooder also plays unusual instruments on some tracks; follow the textures rather than the solos.","The album was a critical and commercial success and fixed the phrase desert blues in Western minds. Some African music critics argued that the American framing made Touré seem an echo of the Delta rather than a source, and that his more rooted Malian albums show him better. Most now value both: the collaboration is gentle and the playing never strains for effect.","Play it at low volume in the evening. Pick one guitar line and follow it round; notice how small changes feel large after a while. Then play John Lee Hooker's Boogie Chillen' and Son House's Death Letter Blues and decide for yourself which way the river flows. Follow it with Niafunké for Touré at home."],"ali-farka-toure-niafunke":["Touré's homecoming record, made with his own musicians rather than foreign guests. His electric and acoustic guitars circle over calabash percussion and handclaps, answered by a small chorus; on some songs he takes up the njarka fiddle, whose thin, buzzing line sounds ancient beside the guitar. The pieces are sung in Songhai, Peul, Bambara and Tamashek, the languages of his region. The textures are dry and close, the grooves patient, sometimes almost trance-like.","After Talking Timbuktu, Touré had largely retreated from touring to farm rice and work his land in Niafunké, a town on the Niger in northern Mali, where he later became mayor. Rather than travel to a studio, he had the recording brought to him: the sessions were made with mobile equipment in a disused building on the edge of town in the heat, with the generator and the river never far away.","Several songs are about farming, work and community, and some are praise songs; Touré spoke of his music as rooted in the spirits of the river. Listen for small sounds of place: the room's ring, the dry click of the calabash, the loose timing between voices. The guitar sometimes imitates the njarka's buzzing drone. That texture is the link between his instruments and his region's older music.","Critics welcomed the album as a truer portrait of Touré than his American collaboration, less polished and more rooted, and it is often ranked among his finest. Some listeners find its long, repetitive songs monotonous without the Western frame; admirers answer that repetition is the point, as in the blues before it.","Listen right through, perhaps on headphones for the room sound. Notice how different the njarka tracks are from the guitar ones, and how the chorus answers rather than harmonises. Compare with Talking Timbuktu for the difference between a visit and a home, then with Tinariwen for the next generation of the Sahel."],"ali-farka-toure-in-the-heart-of-the-moon":["Largely instrumental duets between Touré's guitar and Toumani Diabaté's kora, the twenty-one-string harp-lute of the Mande griots. The guitar lays down steady, droning figures from the north; the kora answers with cascading runs and rippling patterns, often playing bass, harmony and melody at once. Voices appear only briefly. The music is intimate and flowing, two masters trading phrases like old friends in a quiet room.","The album was recorded in a few sessions at a hotel in Bamako, on the bank of the Niger, largely improvised with very little preparation. Touré was already ill with the cancer that killed him in 2006. The pairing had meaning in Mali: Touré came from the Songhai north, Diabaté from a long line of Mande griots in the south, two musical cultures of one country meeting as equals.","Many of the pieces start from Mande songs Diabaté knows from the griot repertoire, including praise songs for historical figures, while Touré's lines come from northern traditions; they meet on common ground in real time. Listen for moments where one player stops to let the other finish a phrase, and for the kora's thumbs plucking a low line that functions like a bass guitar.","It won the Grammy for Best Traditional World Music Album and has been widely admired as among the most beautiful records in African music. A few critics find it too gentle and easy on the ear; most hear it as a summation of two careers. A second volume of duets, Ali and Toumani, appeared posthumously in 2010.","Listen as you would to a string duo or two jazz players: follow who leads and who accompanies, and notice when they swap. Start with any track and simply try to separate guitar from kora. Pair it with Shankar's duets with Yehudi Menuhin, another meeting of traditions on equal terms."],"youssou-ndour-immigres":["Four long songs by Youssou N'Dour and Super Étoile de Dakar in full flight. The sound is mbalax: sabar drums and the tama, the small talking drum held under the arm, firing rapid cross-rhythms beneath chiming guitars, keyboards and a saxophone, with N'Dour's high, ringing voice riding on top, sometimes gliding in long melismas, sometimes almost shouting. The drums often break forward in hard, clattering bursts that invite dancers to answer.","By the early 1980s N'Dour was the biggest star in Senegal, playing weekly at his Dakar club. Many Senegalese had emigrated to France for work, and when he played for them in Paris he sang about their lives. Recorded in Dakar and released first on cassette at home, the album was licensed to European labels a few years later, just as world music was being marketed in London and Paris.","The title song speaks to Senegalese workers abroad, urging them to remember home and to return; its Wolof title means roughly the same thing, and the song avoids easy sentiment about exile. Listen for the tama: its pitch bends as the player squeezes the drum's cords, so it seems to talk, answering the voice. That speech-like drumming is the core of mbalax, not decoration.","European critics hearing it in the late 1980s treated it as a revelation, the purest example of mbalax available outside Senegal. Later N'Dour albums polished the sound for Western ears, and many critics still hold that Immigrés, rougher and closer to the Dakar club, is his best. Its relatively poor recording quality is part of its appeal for some and a barrier for others.","Turn it up and follow the drums rather than the voice at first: notice how often they seem to argue with the beat the guitars are keeping. Then follow N'Dour's voice as it floats free above. Compare with King Sunny Adé's Juju Music, another West African dance band recorded at the same moment."],"youssou-ndour-7-seconds":["A slow, hushed duet between N'Dour and the Swedish-born singer Neneh Cherry, over a soft, programmed hip-hop beat, keyboards and a spare guitar. N'Dour opens in Wolof, his voice light and floating; Cherry answers in English; both touch on French. The verses stay low and intimate, the chorus lifts only slightly. There is nothing of the clattering sabar drums of his Dakar records: this is late-night pop, built on mood and the contrast between two voices.","The song appeared on N'Dour's album The Guide (Wommat), made for an international audience after years of touring the world with Peter Gabriel and Amnesty International. Neneh Cherry, stepdaughter of the jazz trumpeter Don Cherry, was a star of British pop and hip-hop. The single became one of the best-selling records in France and across Europe that year, and the most widely heard song by any African singer of the decade.","The title refers to the first seconds of a newborn child's life, before it has learned anything of racism or division, and the song's three languages are the message itself: a plea that difference need not mean conflict. Many European listeners had no idea what the Wolof verses said, which suits the song's theme of hearing across languages.","It was an enormous hit in Europe, much less so in America. Some admirers of N'Dour's mbalax regretted the smoothness and thought him diluted for export; others defend it as a fine piece of pop craft that opened the door to his later work. It has been covered and sampled many times and remains a staple of European radio.","Listen for the moment each voice enters, and notice how neither singer pushes; the song works by restraint. Then play the title track of Immigrés straight after it to hear the same voice in its home setting. Sade's Love Deluxe, from the same years, is a good neighbour in mood."],"youssou-ndour-egypt":["Eight devotional songs in which N'Dour sings in Wolof over an Egyptian orchestra led by Fathy Salama, with Senegalese players joining in. The sound is lush and slow: massed strings in the style of Cairo film and concert orchestras, the plucked qanun zither and oud, the breathy nay flute, and frame drums. N'Dour's voice is gentler than in his dance music, bending around the orchestra's modal melodies rather than cutting through a band.","The songs praise the Sufi saints of Senegal, above all Cheikh Amadou Bamba, founder of the Mouride brotherhood, to which N'Dour belongs. He had grown up hearing Egyptian singers such as Umm Kulthum on Senegalese radio and wanted to join the two worlds of Islam he knew. Recorded in Cairo and Dakar, the album was held back for some time; he felt the moment after September 2001 was wrong for it.","The songs were controversial at home: some Senegalese objected to devotional texts being set as popular music and sold commercially, especially during Ramadan, and N'Dour defended the record as an act of faith. Listen for places where the Senegalese percussion enters beneath the Egyptian strings: two rhythmic habits meeting within one song, not always comfortably, which is part of the record's honesty.","Western critics praised it widely and it won the Grammy for Best Contemporary World Music Album. It gave many listeners a view of African Islam as tolerant and mystical at a time of hostile headlines. In Senegal it took longer to be accepted. Some fans of mbalax found it too far from his core style; many now count it among his best work.","Listen as you would to sacred music: in one sitting, without shuffle. Notice the orchestral introductions, then how N'Dour's voice enters softly and grows. Compare with Aretha Franklin's Amazing Grace or Coltrane's A Love Supreme, other records where a popular musician gave a whole album to devotion."],"salif-keita-mandjou":["A long praise song by Salif Keita with Les Ambassadeurs Internationaux. It opens with a slow, elaborate introduction, guitars and horns tracing a Mande melody, before a medium dance groove settles. Keita's voice enters high and keening, stretching words into long, ornamented lines with the declamatory force of a griot. Horn riffs and a thin, wiry organ answer him; the band keeps an even, rocking pulse beneath the soaring voice. The effect is both courtly and danceable.","Les Ambassadeurs, a rival of Bamako's Rail Band, had moved to Abidjan in Côte d'Ivoire amid Mali's political tensions. Mandjou praises Ahmed Sékou Touré, the president of Guinea, and his lineage, in the way griots traditionally praised rulers and patrons. Touré, who promoted Guinean popular music as national culture, honoured Keita in return. Recorded in Abidjan, the song became one of the best-known in West Africa.","Keita, a descendant of Mali's royal Keita line, was by birth someone whom griots praised, not a griot himself; that he took on the praise-singer's role scandalised his family. Mandjou therefore inverts an old order. Listen for the long unaccompanied-seeming vocal phrases where the band nearly stops: that freedom of timing is the griot style carried into a dance band.","It was a huge hit across West Africa and remains among the most loved songs of the period. Its praise for Sékou Touré, whose regime imprisoned and killed many opponents, sits uneasily today, and admirers usually separate the song's musical force from its dedication. Reissues have made it a classic for Western listeners discovering 1970s West African bands.","Wait through the introduction: it is part of the ceremony. When the voice enters, follow one long phrase to its end and notice how it curves. Compare with Aretha Franklin's Amazing Grace for another voice trained in praise and ornament, and with Soro to hear what Keita did with the same gifts in Paris a decade later."],"salif-keita-soro":["Six long songs in which Keita's voice meets a full Paris studio production: layered synthesisers, crisp drum machines and programmed rhythms, bright horn charts, electric guitars, kora and a female chorus. Each song builds from a spacious, atmospheric opening into a powerful groove. Keita's singing is at its most commanding, soaring above the arrangements in long, keening phrases. The sound is huge and glossy, the most expensive-sounding African record of its day.","Keita moved to Paris in 1984, where a large West African community and several labels were turning African music into a European market. Soro was produced by the Senegalese producer Ibrahima Sylla and arranged by the French keyboardists François Bréant and Jean-Philippe Rykiel. Released in 1987, the year the term world music was adopted by British record companies, it became a model for how African pop might sound on international radio.","Beneath the technology the songs rest on Mande tradition: melodies and praise formulas a griot would recognise, and rhythms from Mali's hunters' and dance music. Listen for the kora, often buried, playing interlocking patterns that the synthesisers imitate. Keita's lyrics address Mande history, social duty and his own experience as an outsider born with albinism; the production does not change what he sings about.","It was a critical success in Europe and made Keita an international star; some reviewers called it the best African album yet produced. Others, then and since, find the 1980s production dated and prefer his acoustic albums, such as Moffou (2002). It remains a key document of Paris as the capital of African pop in the 1980s.","Start with the opening track and wait for the voice to arrive over the synthesiser haze. Listen for the kora and the horn stabs. Compare it with Mandjou to hear how the same voice fares in a Bamako dance band and a Paris studio, and with Youssou N'Dour's Immigrés for a rougher contemporary."],"miriam-makeba-qongqothwane":["A short, bright song in Xhosa, sung by Makeba over a light accompaniment of guitar, bass and percussion. Its hook is the language itself: the Xhosa click consonants, which she snaps out with rhythmic precision so they work almost as percussion within the melody. The tune is simple and repetitive, a gentle, lilting verse sung over and over, and the whole song lasts barely two minutes. Makeba's voice is clear, relaxed and smiling.","Qongqothwane is a traditional Xhosa wedding song, sung to bless a bride as she leaves for her new home. Makeba recorded it early in her American career, on her first album for RCA, when Harry Belafonte was introducing her to American audiences. She was soon to be barred from returning to South Africa, and songs like this became, for foreign listeners, the first sound of Black South African culture.","The title names a beetle, the knocking beetle, whose tapping was traditionally taken as a good omen, and the song asks it to bless the journey. English-speaking audiences called it The Click Song because they could not say the title, which Makeba often joked about on stage before teaching them the sounds. Listen for the three kinds of click; each is a different consonant, not a sound effect.","It became Makeba's signature in concert, and for millions of Western listeners the first African language they ever heard sung. Some critics later worried that it was received as novelty, the clicks treated as exotic tricks; Makeba herself used it to insist on the dignity of her language. It remains one of the most performed South African songs in the world.","Listen once for the melody, then again just for the clicks, and notice how they fall in rhythm. Then find a live recording where Makeba explains the song, because her introduction is part of the performance. Pair it with the spirituals Steal Away and Wade in the Water, other traditional songs that crossed into concert life."],"miriam-makeba-evening-with-belafonte-makeba":["An album of South African songs sung by Makeba and Harry Belafonte, in solo and duet, with small acoustic accompaniment of guitars, bass and percussion and occasional chorus. The songs are in Zulu, Xhosa, Sotho and Swahili: lullabies, courtship songs, work songs and pointed political songs. The two voices contrast well, Belafonte's husky and conversational, Makeba's clear and agile. The recording is intimate and warm, closer to a folk session than a show.","Makeba had been in exile since 1960; in 1963 she testified against apartheid at the United Nations, and South Africa banned her records. Belafonte, at the height of his fame and deeply involved in the American civil rights movement, had sponsored her move to the United States. The album was released in 1965, as the civil rights struggle peaked in America, linking the two causes for American listeners.","Among the gentle songs sit direct protests: one warns the architect of apartheid, Hendrik Verwoerd, that Black South Africans will not be held down, sung in a language most American buyers could not understand. The album's sweetness is partly camouflage. Listen for the change of tone when a political song arrives; the rhythm hardens and Makeba's voice darkens.","It won the Grammy for Best Folk Recording, the first Grammy for an African artist, and was a best-seller. Some critics felt Belafonte's star power overshadowed Makeba; others hear a genuine partnership. Its standing has grown as histories of the anti-apartheid movement recognise music's role in it, and it is now a key document of African music's arrival in America.","Listen through in order, and notice how lullabies and protest songs sit side by side. Follow Makeba's voice alone on her solo tracks. Pair it with Pete Seeger's We Shall Overcome and Bob Dylan's Blowin' in the Wind, the American protest songs of the same moment, to hear two freedom movements sharing a folk language."],"miriam-makeba-pata-pata":["A joyous, bouncing dance song. A loping, circular bass line and chugging guitar give it the lilt of South African township jive; a small group of backing singers answers Makeba; organ and horns fill out the sound. Makeba sings partly in Xhosa and partly in English, including a spoken introduction explaining the dance. The whole thing is built on a short, endlessly repeated chord cycle, so it seems as though it could go on for ever.","Pata Pata was a dance in the Johannesburg townships of the 1950s, and Makeba had recorded a song about it with her group the Skylarks before exile. In 1967, now living in New York, she recorded a new version for Reprise with the American producer Jerry Ragovoy. It became a hit in the United States and around the world, the first by a South African artist, shortly before her marriage to the Black Power activist Stokely Carmichael, a match that cost her American bookings.","The name means touch touch, describing a dance in which partners pat each other. Makeba privately thought it one of her most trivial songs and was bemused that it was her biggest hit. Listen to the repeating chord cycle; it is the same circular pattern that runs through South African township music from marabi to mbaqanga, and through much later Afropop.","It was an international hit and has been rerecorded countless times, including by Makeba herself in later decades. Some critics dismissed it as lightweight next to her political songs; others argue its pleasure was itself a statement from an exile. The authorship of the original 1950s song has been debated, with other South African musicians credited in some accounts.","Just move to it. Listen to the bass: it never stops circling, and everything else is decoration. Then play Qongqothwane and the Belafonte album to hear the range of her voice. For the later life of this groove, move on to Ladysmith Black Mambazo and the South African side of Paul Simon's Graceland."],"ladysmith-black-mambazo-amabutho":["Twelve short a cappella songs in Zulu. Joseph Shabalala's high tenor leads, calling out a line; the choir answers in a deep, soft, rolling chord, basses underneath, tenors above, in close harmony that swells and recedes like breathing. There are no instruments. Some songs are gentle hymns, others brisk and playful, with whistles, ululations and clicked consonants. The texture is velvety rather than loud, a quality the style's name, isicathamiya, from a word meaning to walk softly, suggests.","Isicathamiya grew among Zulu men working far from home in the mines and factories of Johannesburg and Durban, who held all-night singing competitions in their hostels. Shabalala had rebuilt his group's style after dreams in which he said he heard new harmonies. After their success on Radio Zulu, they recorded Amabutho for Gallo in 1973. It became the first album by Black South African musicians to sell enough to be certified gold.","The soft singing and tiptoeing steps are a legacy of the hostels, where men had to rehearse without disturbing sleeping workers or attracting the guards. The title means warriors, referring to the old Zulu regiments. Listen to how the basses sustain a long, low chord while the leader sings free phrases above: the church harmony the missionaries brought is carrying an older Zulu call-and-response.","It made the group stars across South Africa, where they recorded prolifically through the 1970s. Outside the country it was almost unknown until the Graceland period, when earlier albums were reissued. Scholars of South African music now value these early records as the purest form of the group's style, before the international years polished it.","Listen with the lights low. Follow the leader and the choir as a conversation, and notice how the bass voices move very little. Compare with the American gospel quartets, such as the Golden Gate Quartet, whose records reached South Africa and influenced the hostel choirs, then with Shaka Zulu fourteen years later."],"ladysmith-black-mambazo-shaka-zulu":["Ten a cappella songs, some in Zulu, a few mixing in English. The group sings with its trademark cushioned bass sound and sweet leader's voice, but the production is spacious and clean, each voice audible, the tempos slightly more relaxed. The songs range from hymns of consolation to celebrations of Zulu history, including the title song about the nineteenth-century Zulu king. The effect is serene, warm and devotional.","Paul Simon heard the group on a cassette, went to South Africa in 1985 and recorded with them for Graceland (1986), which made them world famous. He then produced this album for Warner Bros. The period was the height of the cultural boycott of apartheid South Africa; Simon was widely criticised for breaking it, while the group's members pointed out that the collaboration brought their music and its context to a global audience.","Several songs look back to Zulu kingship and pride at a time when the apartheid state promoted ethnic division; listening now, the choice of Shaka as subject is not merely picturesque. Listen also for the vocal effects between lines, the clicks, whistles and small percussive grunts; those are the sounds of the dancing, the soft stomps and kicks that accompany the songs on stage.","It won the Grammy for Best Traditional Folk Recording and sold widely. Critics praised the singing; some felt the production made the style more polite than the earlier Gallo records. The wider Graceland debate, about cultural boycott, credit and payment, continues, though the group's members have consistently defended Simon and their collaboration.","Begin with the title song. Listen for the bass voices underneath and how they move together like one instrument. Then return to the early album Amabutho to hear the rougher original, and look for Graceland's Homeless, sung with Simon, to hear how the collaboration sounded."],"thomas-mapfumo-chimurenga-singles":["A gathering of singles recorded by Mapfumo in the last years of Rhodesia. Two or three electric guitars play short, interlocking, repeating patterns copied from the mbira, the thumb piano of Shona ceremony, over a rolling bass, drums and the steady hiss of hosho gourd shakers. Mapfumo's voice, low and husky, sings in Shona in a declamatory style, often answered by a chorus. Each song cycles like a ceremony rather than following a verse-chorus plan.","In the 1970s a guerrilla war was fought against Ian Smith's white-minority government of Rhodesia. Mapfumo's songs, broadcast and sold as singles, used Shona proverbs and imagery to express support for the fighters and to urge people to resist, in ways the authorities could not always prove. His records were restricted and he was detained without charge in 1979. Zimbabwe became independent in 1980.","The guitar patterns are not decorative: they reproduce the mbira's two interlocking parts, each hand playing a different line so that a third melody seems to emerge between them. Even the heavy guitar tone imitates the mbira's buzzing bottle-caps. Ancestral songs sung to call spirits at ceremonies are here aimed at political liberation; the music's function, not just its sound, carries across.","In Zimbabwe these songs are part of the story of the war, and Mapfumo was celebrated as a national hero at independence. International listeners came to them later through reissues, and critics often rank them as among the most original fusions of traditional and electric music in Africa. His later criticism of the Mugabe government complicated his standing at home.","Pick one guitar and follow it; then shift to another and notice how together they make a pattern neither plays alone. Compare with Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians or Ligeti's piano études, which use similar interlocking ideas, and then look for a recording of traditional mbira to hear the source."],"thomas-mapfumo-corruption":["A later album by Mapfumo and the Blacks Unlimited, fuller and more polished than the wartime singles. Real mbiras now sit alongside the electric guitars, their bright, buzzing tones interlocking with the guitars' imitation of them; horns and keyboards add colour; the rhythm section is tight and propulsive. Mapfumo's voice is as husky and declamatory as ever, the songs long and cyclical, built for dancing and listening at once.","By the late 1980s the hopes of independence were souring: Robert Mugabe's government was accused of corruption and of repression, notably in Matabeleland. Mapfumo, once a hero of the liberation war, turned his songs against the new elite. Released internationally on Island's Mango label, the album brought his music to Western audiences just as world music was being marketed, while making him enemies at home.","The title song, sung partly in English, is aimed at officials who had become the new oppressors, which in Zimbabwe of 1989 was a risky thing to say. Listen for the mbira and guitars playing the same kind of interlocking pattern side by side: the album makes audible how Mapfumo's electric style began as a translation of the mbira, and now brings the original back in.","It was well received abroad, less welcome among Zimbabwe's rulers. Critics generally prefer the rawer wartime recordings, but this album is valued as the moment the liberation singer turned critic. After years of harassment, Mapfumo left for the United States in 2000 and has lived there since, which gives the album a prophetic weight.","Listen for the mbira first, then for the guitars copying it. Compare with the wartime singles to hear how much has been added. Pair it with Fela's Coffin for Head of State for another African musician turning his art on a post-independence government."],"franco-tpok-jazz-mario":["A long song in two halves, as is usual in Congolese rumba. The first part is a relaxed, swaying story sung in Lingala, Franco's gruff voice alternating with the band's sweeter vocalists over gently interlocking guitars and saxophones. Then comes the sebene, the fast instrumental section: bright lead guitars ring in quick, cycling patterns, the saxophones repeat short riffs, the drums lift, and the song becomes pure dance music, driven by Franco's thick, chopping rhythm guitar.","By 1985 Franco's TPOK Jazz was the most popular band in Zaire and across much of Africa, with dozens of musicians and a vast catalogue, working in Kinshasa and Brussels. Franco was famous for songs of social comment, often thinly veiled, which had landed him in prison under Mobutu. Mario was one of his biggest hits, selling widely across Central and East Africa.","The song tells the story of Mario, a young man with diplomas who lives off an older, wealthy woman, spends her money and treats her badly, and the narrator scolds him at length; listeners heard in it a satire of a whole class of idle, educated young men in Zaire. Listen for how the story's tone shifts from amused to exasperated, then is swept away by the sebene.","Mario was an enormous success and remains one of the best-known Congolese songs. Critics see it as a peak of Franco's late style, combining storytelling with the band's power. Some modern listeners find its sexual politics, in which the older woman is both victim and joke, uncomfortable; others hear sharp social observation of Mobutu-era Kinshasa.","Listen to the whole song. Enjoy the story's easy swing, then notice the moment the sebene arrives and the guitars take over. Follow one guitar pattern round, then the saxophones. Compare with Dizzy Gillespie's Manteca for the Cuban sound that Congolese rumba grew from, and Bob Dylan's Like a Rolling Stone for another long song scolding a fallen character."],"franco-tpok-jazz-attention-na-sida":["A long, slow, sombre song. Over a gentle rumba pulse, lightly picked guitars and soft saxophones, Franco speaks and sings in French and Lingala, more narrator than singer, while a chorus answers him. The music is warm but restrained; the emphasis falls on the words. There is no exuberant dance section of the usual kind; the band keeps a grave, steady sway, as if accompanying a public announcement delivered with care.","By the mid-1980s AIDS was spreading across Central Africa, including Zaire, but was surrounded by silence and stigma. Franco made the song as a public-health message, addressing the whole of society. He was himself seriously ill at the time; he died in Brussels in 1989, amid widespread speculation about the illness, which was never publicly confirmed. The song has often been heard in that light.","The song does not preach from outside. Franco addresses doctors, governments, the young and the old in turn, calls on people to be tested and to care for the sick rather than cast them out, and speaks of the disease without the moral blame then common. That care for the sick, not the warning itself, is what gives it lasting weight.","It was widely played and discussed across Africa and is often cited as one of the first major popular songs about AIDS anywhere. Critics outside Africa, hearing it later, were struck by its seriousness from a musician known for dance songs and satire. It is now studied as an example of popular music's role in public health.","Listen with a translation if you can find one, since the words matter most. Notice how the band's sound is pared back and how the chorus answers Franco like a congregation. Compare with Mario to hear the range of his band, and with Marvin Gaye's What's Going On for another message record delivered with grace."],"tinariwen-aman-iman":["Twelve songs in Tamashek. Two or three electric guitars play rolling, looping riffs, some crisp and dry, some fuzzed and droning, over a loping bass, calabash and hand drums, and handclaps. The lead singers' voices are low and weathered, answered by a chorus of women who ululate and sing short refrains. The songs are mostly in slow to medium tempo, hypnotic and repetitive, with brief, stinging guitar solos. The whole record sounds like heat and distance.","Tinariwen formed among Tuareg exiles in Libyan and Algerian camps; some members fought in the Tuareg rebellion in Mali in the early 1990s. After peace they became musicians full-time and found an international audience from the early 2000s. This third international album was recorded in Bamako with the British guitarist and producer Justin Adams and was their breakthrough to rock audiences in Europe and America.","The title means water is life, and the songs return again and again to thirst, exile, the loss of land and the scattering of a nomadic people: the music is political without slogans. Listen for the guitar parts that copy the phrasing of the tehardent lute and older Tuareg songs; the riffs that sound like blues come from their own tradition as much as from any record.","It was widely praised by rock critics and musicians; several major rock guitarists named them as favourites, and the band toured with big rock acts. Some critics warned against romanticising rebel musicians as desert exotics; the band itself emphasised that the songs were about real hardship. It remains the album most listeners start with.","Start with the opening track and let the guitars settle into your ears. Notice the women's voices and the handclaps, which carry as much rhythm as the drums. Compare with Ali Farka Touré's Niafunké for an older Malian guitar style, and with John Lee Hooker's Boom Boom and Jimi Hendrix for the Western cousins of the sound."],"tinariwen-tassili":["A largely acoustic album. Tinariwen's guitars ring out unplugged, their looping riffs lighter and more intimate than on the electric albums, over hand percussion, handclaps and soft chorus. The voices are close and quiet, some songs almost murmured. Guest musicians from American rock add subtle touches: a horn section on one song, a second voice or an extra guitar on others. The mood is reflective, spacious and open-air.","Growing insecurity in northern Mali made recording at home difficult, so the band recorded in the Tassili n'Ajjer, a region of sandstone plateaus in south-eastern Algeria, camping in the desert with a mobile studio. American guests, including members of TV on the Radio and the guitarist Nels Cline, came out to join them. The following year, 2012, war broke out in northern Mali, and the region became dangerous for the band.","Listen for the outdoor sound: no studio walls, voices and guitars dissolving into open air, and occasionally the sounds of the camp itself. The songs return to exile and to the fracture of Tuareg society, but in a quieter voice than before. The guests stay in the background; one test of the album is how little they change its character.","It won the Grammy for Best World Music Album and was widely praised as a new direction, showing the band's songs could stand without electric power. Some fans prefer the rougher electric records. Its release on the indie-rock label Anti- confirmed the band's place in Western alternative rock culture as much as in world music.","Listen outdoors if you can, or at least with windows open. Notice how the unplugged guitars bring out the melodies inside the riffs. Compare directly with Aman Iman to hear the same band electric and acoustic, and with Ali Farka Touré and Toumani Diabaté's In the Heart of the Moon for another quiet desert conversation."],"amadou-and-mariam-dimanche-a-bamako":["Fifteen bright, catchy songs sung in Bambara and French. Amadou's guitar lines are crisp and bluesy, Mariam's voice plain and warm, the rhythms often a quick, bouncing Malian pulse. The producer Manu Chao wraps them in a collage of street sounds: sirens, radio snatches, crowd noise, traffic, whistles and his own voice. Songs run into one another like a walk through a city on a Sunday, the day of weddings and parties in Bamako.","The couple had been recording since the 1980s, with modest success in Mali and later in France. Manu Chao, the French-Spanish singer whose collage style had been hugely successful, heard them and offered to produce. The album was made partly in Bamako and partly in Paris and became one of the best-selling records by African artists in France, winning major French music awards and a new audience across Europe.","The title song pictures a Bamako wedding, and much of the album describes ordinary life: markets, radios, neighbours, love. Listen for the Manu Chao signature sounds, some of which he reuses from his own records, and for the way Amadou's guitar keeps the traditional Malian patterns underneath the pop sheen. The couple's blindness is not a theme; the album is about seeing the city through sound.","It was a huge success in France and Europe; critics praised its warmth and wit. Some critics found Manu Chao's production too dominant, with the couple sounding like guests on his record; others argued the collaboration was genuinely equal and gave them a sound that suited them. It remains their best-known album.","Listen straight through, as the album is designed to flow. Notice the street sounds between songs and imagine the city. Then listen to Welcome to Mali's Sabali for their later, electronic sound. Compare with Talking Heads' Remain in Light for another Western studio's encounter with African pop."],"amadou-and-mariam-sabali":["A gentle, electronic love song. Mariam sings alone, her voice processed slightly, over pulsing analogue synthesisers, a soft, looping drum-machine beat and bubbling electronic arpeggios. There is little of the couple's usual guitar or Malian percussion. The melody is simple, repetitive and tender, and the synthesisers create a dreamy, floating sound. It is short, spare and quite different from anything else on the album around it.","The track appeared on the album Welcome to Mali, which followed the success of Dimanche à Bamako. Damon Albarn of Blur and Gorillaz, who had been visiting and recording in Mali since 2000, produced Sabali and played on it. Albarn's interest in West African music, and his use of vintage synthesisers, shaped its sound; the track became a favourite of Western indie and electronic audiences.","The title means patience in Bambara, and the song is a plea for patience and gentleness in love. Listen to how Mariam's melody follows the shape of Malian song even over the synthesisers; the electronic sound is a costume, not a replacement. Albarn's arrangement uses synthesisers like the old organ sounds of 1970s Malian bands, which he loved.","Critics singled it out as the highlight of Welcome to Mali, and it was widely played and remixed. Some Malian music purists thought it strayed too far from roots; many indie critics thought it the couple's best song. It has been used in films and advertisements and remains their most streamed track.","Listen on headphones for the synthesiser textures. Notice how simple the melody is and how much the sound does around it. Compare with Brian Eno's Another Green World for similar electronic gentleness, and with Dimanche à Bamako for the busier side of the couple."],"king-sunny-ade-juju-music":["Seven long, flowing songs by King Sunny Adé and his African Beats. Several electric guitars play small, overlapping figures; a pedal steel guitar slides and swoops; talking drums chatter in dialogue; percussion shakers, bells and congas fill the rest; a bass holds everything together. Adé's voice is smooth and relaxed, answered by a chorus. The sound is dense but light, like a cloud of tiny patterns moving together, with occasional dub-style echo effects and synthesiser.","Jùjú was Yoruba party music, played for patrons at weddings and celebrations in Lagos. After the death of Bob Marley in 1981, Island Records looked for a new international star from outside the West and signed Adé. The album was produced by the French producer Martin Meissonnier, who selected and trimmed songs, often much longer at home, for a Western audience.","The talking drums are not merely rhythmic: Yoruba is a tonal language, and the drums imitate speech, sending proverbs and praise phrases that Yoruba listeners can understand. The pedal steel, an instrument associated with American country music, was an unusual addition that Adé had adopted and made his own. Listen to how it floats over the dense guitar web.","The album was widely acclaimed in Europe and America and toured with great success, though it did not make Adé the star Island had hoped for. Critics now see it as a landmark of world music's first wave. Some Nigerian listeners saw it as a trimmed, exported version of what they heard at home; the home recordings remain more expansive.","Choose one sound, the pedal steel or a talking drum, and follow it. Then step back and hear the whole web. Compare with Steve Reich's Drumming for interlocking patterns and with Talking Heads' Remain in Light for American musicians trying to build a similar web."],"cesaria-evora-miss-perfumado":["Ten songs, mostly mornas, the slow, melancholy song form of Cape Verde, plus a few coladeiras, their quicker, lighter cousins. Évora's voice is low, unhurried and unforced, sung in Cape Verdean Creole over acoustic guitars, the small four-string cavaquinho, piano, violin, clarinet and light percussion. The music is intimate, the rhythms gently swaying, and many listeners hear kinship with Portuguese fado and Brazilian samba-canção.","Cape Verde, an archipelago off West Africa, was a Portuguese colony until 1975, and many of its people emigrated. Évora sang in the bars of Mindelo on São Vicente for decades, with little reward. Recorded in Paris for the small Lusafrica label founded by her producer José da Silva, the album became her international breakthrough when she was over fifty, and made her a star in France.","The album includes Sodade, a song about the longing of emigrants that has become a Cape Verdean anthem; the word means yearning. Listen to Évora's phrasing: she sings behind the beat with a slightly lazy ease, never pushing, which is the essence of morna. The cavaquinho's steady, brushed chords carry the rhythm while the guitar plays the bass line.","It was a surprise success in France and later across Europe and America, and made morna internationally known. Critics praised the purity and dignity of her voice and the simplicity of the arrangements. Some Cape Verdeans noted that the album presented a romantic view of their islands for foreign listeners; many others celebrated it as recognition of their culture.","Listen in the evening, perhaps with a glass of something. Notice how she lets phrases fall behind the beat. Start with Sodade, then listen through. Compare with Billie Holiday's God Bless the Child for another singer who works through understatement, and with Stan Getz and João Gilberto for Brazil's cousin tradition."],"cesaria-evora-voz-d-amor":["A later album in which Évora's mornas and coladeiras are set with fuller arrangements: string sections, piano, accordion and brass alongside the traditional guitars and cavaquinho. Her voice has deepened with age, still unhurried, now darker and more grained. The songs alternate slow, aching mornas and lightly swinging coladeiras. The production is polished but never busy; the voice remains the centre of every song.","By 2003 Évora was an international star, touring constantly and selling widely in France, Portugal, Brazil and beyond. She continued to record new songs by Cape Verdean writers alongside traditional material. Her Cape Verdean touring musicians formed the core, with arrangers adding strings and other colours around them, and the album, made for Lusafrica like her other international records, became among her most successful late releases, carrying morna to audiences who had first met it through Sodade.","Listen for the arrangements' borrowings: some strings recall Brazilian and Cuban orchestral traditions, reflecting the wide Lusophone and Atlantic world that Cape Verdean music always belonged to. The accordion has its own history in the islands' funaná style. Évora's voice is audibly older than on Miss Perfumado; its roughness adds weight to the songs of loss.","It won the Grammy for Best Contemporary World Music Album. Critics praised the arrangements; some felt the fuller production softened the intimacy of her earlier records. It stands as a summation of her late career, the record of a singer secure in her fame and free to widen her palette, and her death in 2011 gave these recordings added poignancy.","Compare a song here directly with one from Miss Perfumado: listen for the changes in the voice and the arrangements. Notice the strings and accordion. Follow it with Billie Holiday's late recordings, another great voice that deepened and roughened with age."],"mulatu-astatke-mulatu-of-ethiopia":["A short instrumental album. Mulatu's vibraphone and organ lead a band of horns, electric guitar, bass and drums through slow, brooding grooves and brighter funk pieces. The melodies use Ethiopian modes, five-note scales with unusual gaps that make them sound mysterious and minor, harmonised with jazz chords. The horns play in unison or close harmony; the rhythm section sits in a heavy, loping pulse. The atmosphere is dark, cool and hypnotic.","Mulatu, who had studied in London and at Berklee in Boston and played Latin jazz in New York, recorded this album in New York with local musicians. It shows the fusion he had been developing in Addis Ababa: Ethiopian modes set to jazz and funk. Released on a small label, it had little impact at the time and became rare, prized later by collectors and DJs.","The Ethiopian modes, called qenet, have names such as tezeta, a mode of nostalgia and longing; Mulatu writes melodies in these modes and harmonises them in ways that make them sound both jazz and not. Listen for the vibraphone, then unusual in Ethiopian music: Mulatu chose it partly because its sound suggested the resonance of traditional Ethiopian instruments.","Little noticed on release, the album was reissued in the 2000s and hailed as a lost classic of Afro-jazz. Critics now see it as an essential document of Ethio-jazz, and its tracks have been widely sampled. Its New York recording is sometimes contrasted with the rougher Addis recordings on the Éthiopiques series.","Listen in the dark. Follow the vibraphone melody and notice the gaps in the scale. Compare with Coltrane's Africa/Brass and Miles Davis's Kind of Blue for the modal jazz Mulatu drew on, and then with the Éthiopiques recordings made in Addis."],"mulatu-astatke-ethiopiques-4":["A compilation of instrumental recordings Mulatu made in Addis Ababa, collected decades later. Slow, minor-key grooves dominate: vibraphone and organ play long, snaking melodies in Ethiopian modes over bass and drums; horns enter in weighty unison; guitar and percussion add colour. Some tracks are brighter and funkier, others languid and eerie. The recording is raw and warm, with a live, slightly smoky room sound that has become the signature of the whole Éthiopiques series.","Addis Ababa in the late 1960s and early 1970s had a lively nightclub scene, with bands playing Ethiopian songs alongside soul, jazz and Latin music. Mulatu recorded many sessions for local labels, especially Amha Records. The 1974 revolution that overthrew Haile Selassie and brought a military regime ended the scene. The French producer Francis Falceto collected these recordings on Buda Musique's Éthiopiques series; this volume appeared in 1998.","Several of these tracks, including the brooding Yèkèrmo Sèw, were used in Jim Jarmusch's film Broken Flowers (2005), which brought Mulatu a new generation of fans. Listen for the tezeta mode, the Ethiopian scale of longing, in the slower pieces. The rough recording is not a flaw; Addis studios were basic, and the grit is part of the sound.","The compilation made Mulatu an international name and helped launch a global revival of interest in Ethiopian music. Critics praise it as essential listening; some have debated whether the series presents a romantic view of a vanished golden age. Mulatu has since collaborated with Western bands and toured widely, a late recognition that owes much to this album.","Start with Yèkèrmo Sèw. Listen to the vibraphone and horns in turn. Then play it after Coltrane's Africa/Brass or Ornette Coleman's Dancing in Your Head to hear different routes between Africa and jazz. Watch Broken Flowers for the music in its film setting."],"carlos-gardel-mi-noche-triste":["A man alone in the room his lover has left, talking to the things she left behind: that is the whole drama of Mi noche triste, sung by Carlos Gardel over a single guitar. The melody is an older instrumental tango, Lita, by Samuel Castriota; the words, by Pascual Contursi, turned it into a first-person lament. Gardel sings it almost conversationally, sliding into notes, leaning on the ends of phrases, a baritone that sounds like it is confiding rather than performing.","Before 1917 tango was mostly danced, not sung, and its lyrics, where they existed, were jaunty and often ribald. Gardel was then half of a popular folk duo with José Razzano, singing rural songs of the pampas. Recording Mi noche triste for the Odeon company moved him into the city's own music and changed the direction of both his career and the genre. Buenos Aires was swelling with immigrants, and its cafés and tenements wanted songs about their own heartbreak.","Listen to the vocabulary rather than the tune. Contursi wrote in lunfardo, the Buenos Aires street slang that mixed Italian, Spanish and underworld cant, and the lyric sets the abandoned lover among ordinary domestic objects, a bed, a mirror, a guitar hanging on the wall. That shift from pampas imagery to a rented room in the city is the birth of the tango-canción: the song as a small urban short story.","Historians of tango almost unanimously date the tango-canción, the sung, narrative tango, from this recording, which makes it one of those rare records that plainly starts something. The acoustic recording is thin to modern ears, and the guitar sits far back, so it is often admired more than played. Later Gardel recordings show a riper voice, but none has quite the same historical weight.","Hear it in a good transfer and forgive the surface noise. Follow the voice: notice how Gardel stretches a phrase and then catches up, never quite on the beat. Then play one of his mid-1930s film songs, such as Por una cabeza, to hear how far the singer and the genre travelled in under twenty years."],"carlos-gardel-por-una-cabeza":["Por una cabeza, by a head, is a tango about a man who loses at the racetrack and in love in exactly the same way: by a nose, every time, and he keeps betting. Gardel's melody opens with a rising, swaying phrase over a lilting orchestra, then turns darker and more insistent in the second section before swinging back. His voice, by now fuller and polished, sits on top of a small orchestra with violins, guitars and piano.","Gardel wrote the music, with lyrics by his regular partner Alfredo Le Pera, for the Paramount film Tango Bar, made in New York in 1935. By then he was an international film star across Latin America, recording and filming in Paris and New York. A few months later, in June 1935, he died in a plane collision at Medellín, Colombia, and the songs of his final films became the relics of a national grief.","The tune works as well without words as with them, and that is how most of the world now knows it. Notice how the first section keeps climbing by small steps, as if pushing its luck, while the second drops into a minor-key sway that sounds like the moment the horse is overtaken. The racing metaphor is carried by the music itself, not just the lyric.","In Argentina it was always a beloved standard; abroad it had a second life through film, above all the tango scene in Scent of a Woman (1992), and later in Schindler's List and True Lies. That fame has made it a violin encore and a dance-class staple, which some tango purists find reductive. Heard in Gardel's own voice, it recovers its wit and its ruefulness.","Start with Gardel's 1935 recording, then an instrumental version by a violinist, and notice what goes missing when the words go: the bitter joke about the gambler who never learns. Compare it with Mi noche triste to hear the young café singer become a movie star."],"piazzolla-tango-zero-hour":["Seven pieces for Piazzolla's Quinteto Tango Nuevo: bandoneón, violin, piano, electric guitar and double bass. The sound is lean and fierce, with rhythmic accents slammed across the beat, fugue-like passages in which the instruments chase each other, and slow sections where the bandoneón sighs over a single sustained chord. Tanguedia III opens in a blaze of accents; Milonga del ángel is its quietest heart; Contrabajissimo gives the bass a long, singing solo.","Produced in New York by Kip Hanrahan for his small American Clavé label, the record caught Piazzolla's second quintet at its peak, with Fernando Suárez Paz on violin, Pablo Ziegler on piano, Horacio Malvicino on guitar and Héctor Console on bass. By 1986 the battles of the 1950s and 60s, when traditionalists accused him of killing tango, had largely been won abroad, and he was touring Europe and North America as a concert artist.","Behind the street-fighting energy is a conservatory training. Piazzolla studied composition with Alberto Ginastera in Buenos Aires and with Nadia Boulanger in Paris, and the counterpoint here, the lines entering one after another in the manner of a fugue, is Bach learned through Boulanger. Listen for the percussive effects the violinist makes by scraping behind the bridge: a tango orchestra's rhythm section imitated by strings.","Piazzolla himself reportedly called it the best record he ever made, and critics have mostly agreed. It became the entry point to nuevo tango for listeners outside Argentina and helped fuel the boom in classical performers adopting his music. Some tango dancers still object that this is music for listening, not dancing, which is exactly Piazzolla's point.","Start with Milonga del ángel for the melody, then Tanguedia III for the attack. Listen for the violin's squeaks and slides as rhythmic colour, and for the moment in Concierto para quinteto when all five lock into one accent. Then hear his classical-concert pieces such as Adiós Nonino to judge where the border lies."],"elizeth-cardoso-cancao-do-amor-demais":["An LP of songs written entirely by Antonio Carlos Jobim and the poet Vinicius de Moraes, sung by Elizeth Cardoso, then one of Brazil's great samba-canção voices. Most of it is lush and melancholy, arranged by Jobim for strings and piano. But on two songs, Chega de saudade and Outra vez, a young guitarist from Bahia named João Gilberto plays accompaniment in a new, clipped, syncopated way that would soon have its own name.","Festa was a tiny label devoted largely to poetry recordings, and this album was a partnership project: Vinicius, a diplomat-poet, had recently teamed up with the young Jobim for the play Orfeu da Conceição. Rio de Janeiro in 1958 was optimistic, with a modernising president, Juscelino Kubitschek, and a new capital, Brasília, rising in the interior. The record appeared months before Gilberto's own single of Chega de saudade.","Listen past the singer to the guitar on Chega de saudade. Gilberto plucks the bass note with his thumb while his fingers snap out chords slightly off the beat, a compressed version of a whole samba percussion section. Cardoso's big, vibrato-rich voice sits on top of it in the older style, and the mismatch is the sound of one era handing over to the next.","Historians usually call it the first bossa nova record, or at least its prehistory, and it is cited more often than heard. Cardoso's operatic phrasing can sound old-fashioned beside Gilberto's whisper, which is precisely why the comparison is instructive. Its reputation rests on its songs and on those few minutes of guitar.","Play Cardoso's Chega de saudade, then João Gilberto's own single of the same song from a few months later. Same melody, same guitarist, but his voice drops to a murmur and everything else is stripped away. That before-and-after is the clearest lesson in what bossa nova changed."],"joao-gilberto-chega-de-saudade":["Twelve short songs, most under three minutes, in which João Gilberto sings almost at speaking volume while his nylon-string guitar ticks out a syncopated pattern. Jobim's arrangements add a flute here, a few strings there, a brushed snare, never more than the song needs. The title track, Desafinado, Brigas nunca mais and Bim bom set out the whole language: cool voice, warm harmony, rhythm felt rather than hammered.","Odeon released Gilberto's single of Chega de saudade in 1958, reluctantly; one executive was said to dislike it. It became a hit with young middle-class listeners in Rio's beach neighbourhoods, and the LP followed in 1959. Jobim served as arranger and producer-in-all-but-name. Brazil was booming, and this quiet, urbane music, made in apartments rather than samba schools, sounded like the confident new country.","The guitar is doing the work of a whole samba percussion section in miniature: thumb for the surdo bass drum, fingers for the tamborim's off-beat chatter. Meanwhile Gilberto's voice floats slightly ahead or behind the guitar, so the two never quite coincide. Desafinado, meaning out of tune, is a sly answer to critics who called the new style's rich chords and gentle voices unmusical.","Its impact is hard to overstate: within a few years a generation of Brazilian musicians, including Caetano Veloso and Gilberto Gil, said this record decided their lives. Older critics dismissed the whispered singing as weak. American jazz musicians, from Stan Getz to Charlie Byrd, soon borrowed the style. It is now treated as the founding document of bossa nova.","Listen with headphones and ignore the words at first: follow the guitar's thumb and fingers as two separate parts. Then notice how little the voice pushes. After it, hear Getz/Gilberto for the international version, and Miles Davis's Birth of the Cool for the American cool that Rio heard as a kindred spirit."],"joao-gilberto-joao-gilberto-1973":["Often called the white album for its plain cover, this is João Gilberto almost alone: voice, guitar and the faintest percussion. Ten songs, old sambas and new ones, including Jobim's Águas de março, Caetano Veloso's Avarandado and Gilberto Gil's Eu vim da Bahia. Undiú is a wordless drift of hummed syllables. The effect is of a man playing for himself late at night, every phrase weighed to the gram.","Gilberto had lived mainly in the United States and Mexico since the early 1960s, and recorded this in New York. Back in Brazil the military dictatorship was at its harshest, and the bossa nova he founded had been overtaken by Tropicália and MPB, whose leading figures, Caetano and Gil, he now recorded. The album is a sort of reply: the master acknowledging his heirs in his own spare terms.","Listen to how Gilberto slightly rewrites every song's rhythm and harmony, so that a tune you know from another singer arrives subtly re-cut. In Águas de março he sets the famous list of images with no drama at all, letting the descending bass line in the guitar carry the song's sense of things running down. The percussion is barely there, a brush of sound.","Many musicians rank it above his early records, precisely because it is so extreme in its restraint; some listeners find it too hushed to hold them. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is often named as a favourite by singer-songwriters and producers interested in intimacy and space. It is the late summation of the Gilberto style.","Play it quietly, in a quiet room. Start with Águas de março, then compare Elis Regina and Jobim's duet version from the next year to hear how much drama Gilberto chooses to leave out. Undiú is the track to test whether this music has caught you."],"jobim-wave":["Ten short, mostly instrumental pieces in which Jobim plays piano and guitar against Claus Ogerman's orchestration: soft strings, flutes, a warm trombone, bass and brushed drums. The title track rides a gently rocking melody over rich chords; Triste and Look to the Sky are yearning ballads; Mojave and Captain Bacardi add a little bite. It sounds like the music of a Rio apartment at dusk, arranged with the care of a film score.","Jobim was by then an international name thanks to The Girl from Ipanema. This record was produced in New Jersey by Creed Taylor, who was building the polished CTI sound, with Ron Carter among the players. Taylor and Ogerman made Jobim's harmonies the centre of the record rather than his singing, and it became one of the most played albums of 1960s easy listening and late-night jazz radio.","Jobim loved Debussy and Villa-Lobos, and you can hear both. Ogerman's sustained string chords and the parallel moving harmonies under the tunes come straight out of French impressionism, while the modal melodies owe something to Villa-Lobos's Bachianas. Jobim's own piano is almost shy, often a single-note line; the sophistication is in what moves underneath.","Jazz purists sometimes dismissed it as background music, and it was indeed played everywhere as exactly that. But musicians have always treated its songs, especially Wave and Triste, as serious repertoire, and the album is now valued as the purest statement of Jobim as a composer rather than a pop star.","Listen for the chords under the title track: they keep shifting colour while the melody barely moves. Then play Debussy's Prelude to the Afternoon of a Faun and Villa-Lobos's Bachianas Brasileiras No. 5 aria, and hear where the harmony came from. Finish with Getz/Gilberto for the same composer in song."],"jobim-aguas-de-marco":["The waters of March are the late-summer rains in Rio, and the song is a list: stick, stone, the end of the road, a sliver of glass, life, the sun, night, death, a snare, a fish, one image after another, each over the same short melodic figure. Underneath, the bass keeps stepping downward. The accumulation becomes hypnotic, and by the end the list has turned from scattered debris into a promise of renewal.","Jobim first released it in 1972 on a small disc given away with the satirical Rio weekly O Pasquim, then on his next album. It was written at his country house near Petrópolis during a rainy season, and came in the grimmest years of the military regime, when plain speech was risky. Jobim later wrote his own English version, Waters of March, in which the season shifts, for northern listeners, from the end of summer to the promise of spring.","The melody barely moves; almost all of the drama is in the harmony and in that falling bass line, a device older than Bach. Jobim also wrote the English lyric using mainly words of one syllable, rather than translating, so that the list keeps its tumbling rhythm. Some listeners hear, in the lists of odds and ends, a coded picture of a country under dictatorship.","It has topped at least one major Brazilian newspaper poll of musicians and critics as the greatest Brazilian song, though such polls vary. The best-known version is the 1974 duet with Elis Regina, whose laughter as they sing became part of its legend. Its reputation among songwriters worldwide rests on its structure as much as its tune.","Start with the Elis Regina and Jobim duet, then Jobim's own earlier recording, then João Gilberto's hushed 1973 version. Listen each time for the falling bass line, and notice how the tiny melodic figure becomes something you want never to stop."],"elis-regina-elis-and-tom":["Fourteen Jobim songs sung by Elis Regina, Brazil's most powerful popular singer, with Jobim himself on piano, guitar and occasional vocals. The arrangements, by César Camargo Mariano, add electric piano and a jazz rhythm section to the bossa nova palette. Águas de março, sung as a duet, opens with a playful exchange of lines; Triste, Corcovado, Só tinha de ser com você and Retrato em branco e preto show Elis moving from whisper to full cry.","The album was recorded in Los Angeles in early 1974, a prestige project backed by her label. Jobim, living partly in the United States, was wary of the younger musicians and their electric instruments, and the sessions were tense before they were joyful. Brazil was still under military rule; Elis had been pressured by the regime, and a project with Jobim was a return to a less dangerous, more classic repertoire.","Listen for the moments when Elis nearly laughs during Águas de março: the duet was kept loose, not polished. The tug of war between Jobim's acoustic sound and Mariano's Fender Rhodes is audible throughout, the older master's piano ringing out beside the younger arranger's electric shimmer. That friction gives the record its warmth.","It is widely regarded as one of the best Brazilian albums ever made, and the definitive vocal treatment of Jobim's songbook. Some Jobim devotees prefer the dry João Gilberto approach; Elis's admirers answer that she finds emotions in these songs that no one else does. Its standing has only grown since her death in 1982.","Start with Águas de março, then Retrato em branco e preto for the other side of Elis, dark and dramatic. Compare Getz/Gilberto's cool, and ask which approach you prefer. Then follow Elis into her later, harder repertoire with Como nossos pais."],"elis-regina-como-nossos-pais":["A slow-building song that begins almost spoken over piano and grows, verse by verse, into a full-band cry. Elis Regina sings a young person's bitter realisation that despite all the rebellion, the generation that promised to change everything is ending up living just like its parents. Her voice moves from confiding to furious and back, every syllable given dramatic weight, until the last line lands like an accusation.","The song was written by Belchior, a songwriter from Ceará in the northeast, and Elis made it the centrepiece of her stage show and album Falso brilhante in 1976. Brazil's military regime had begun a slow political opening, but censorship was still active. A song about a generation's disillusion was understood by everyone as being about the dictatorship and the failure of the 1960s dream, without naming either.","Notice how the song's accompaniment stays harmonically simple while Elis does all the work of escalation: the drama is in the singer, not the arrangement. Belchior's lyric also nods to the counterculture's heroes and slogans, but with the tone of someone who no longer believes them. The title's ordinariness, like our parents, is the whole blow.","It became one of Elis's signature performances and one of the anthems of her generation. Belchior's own recording, released the same year, is sparer and more ironic, and some listeners prefer it; most Brazilians know Elis's. Her early death in 1982 gave the song an extra layer of elegy, and it is still covered and quoted in Brazilian political debate.","Listen for the long crescendo: note the moment Elis stops sounding sad and starts sounding angry. Then hear Belchior's own version for the contrast. Compare Chico Buarque's Construção for another song that sneaks politics past the censors through structure. Any live recording from the Falso brilhante tour shows the song at full stretch."],"caetano-veloso-alegria-alegria":["A bright, marching pop song in which the singer walks through the city, against the wind, with no handkerchief and no documents, noticing newspaper headlines, film stars, bombs and Coca-Cola as they pass. Caetano Veloso sings over an electric rock band rather than the acoustic guitars expected of Brazilian song. The tune is simple and catchy; the lyric is a collage of the modern city in the present tense.","Caetano performed it with an Argentine rock group, the Beat Boys, at the 1967 festival of Brazilian popular music on TV Record in São Paulo, a televised contest watched by millions. Electric guitars were a political statement: the nationalist left saw them as American imperialism. At the same festival Gilberto Gil sang Domingo no parque with Os Mutantes. Together the two performances launched the movement soon called Tropicália.","Listen to the list of things the singer sees: images from magazines, cinema and the news appear side by side with equal weight, like a Pop Art canvas. Caetano borrowed the tone from Godard's films and from the Beatles as much as from Brazilian poetry. The song's cheerfulness is deceptive; it is about drifting unprotected through a world of signs, under a dictatorship.","The festival audience initially booed the electric guitars, then took to the song; it finished fourth but became the bigger hit. Nationalist critics called the new movement a sell-out; later historians see it as the moment Brazilian music absorbed rock on its own terms. The song is now a classic of Brazilian pop.","Hear it next to João Gilberto's Chega de saudade, which Caetano revered, to understand the leap: same country, nine years apart, whisper against electric band. Then go straight to the Tropicália ou Panis et Circencis album. Look for footage of the 1967 festival performance, where you can hear the audience turn from jeering to singing along."],"tropicalia-ou-panis-et-circencis":["A manifesto in the form of an LP: twelve songs by Caetano Veloso, Gilberto Gil, Gal Costa, Os Mutantes, Tom Zé and Nara Leão, bound together by Rogério Duprat's orchestral arrangements. The styles collide deliberately, northeastern baião, sentimental bolero, psychedelic rock, church hymn and radio jingle. Panis et circencis is sung by Os Mutantes and ends in a staged dinner party; Baby, sung by Gal Costa, is a soft ode to consumer culture; Geléia geral piles Brazilian clichés into a comic heap.","Recorded in São Paulo in 1968, the year the military regime issued Institutional Act No. 5, suspending civil liberties. By December, Caetano and Gil were arrested, and they were later pushed into exile in London. The album's idea, drawn from the 1920s poet Oswald de Andrade's Cannibalist Manifesto, was that Brazil should devour foreign culture, from the Beatles to the avant-garde, and digest it into something its own.","Listen for Duprat, a conservatory-trained composer who had studied the European avant-garde; he slips orchestral quotations and deliberate kitsch into the arrangements, including snatches of familiar classical tunes. The cover, a family-portrait parody with Caetano holding a photograph of Nara Leão and Gil holding a picture of Capinam, echoes Sgt. Pepper's group costume while mocking Brazilian bourgeois respectability.","It was divisive: the left accused it of Americanisation and frivolity, the right distrusted its irreverence. Since the late 1990s, with reissues and David Byrne's advocacy, it has been celebrated internationally as one of the great albums of the psychedelic era. In Brazil it is treated as a founding cultural document, as much studied as listened to.","Take it as a whole, like a film. Start with Panis et circencis to hear the joke land, then Baby for the melting sweetness. Pair it with Sgt. Pepper and with Os Mutantes' own debut; then read a little about Oswald de Andrade's cannibalism metaphor, which unlocks the record."],"os-mutantes-os-mutantes":["The debut of a São Paulo trio, the brothers Arnaldo Baptista and Sérgio Dias with singer Rita Lee, and a riot of ideas: fuzz guitars, harpsichord, tape tricks, orchestral swells, a bossa nova turned inside out. Panis et circencis appears again, this time in their own version; Bat macumba builds a chant syllable by syllable; A minha menina, a Jorge Ben song, is garage rock with a samba heart. The voices are young, playful and unafraid to sound silly.","Os Mutantes were the house band of Tropicália, backing Gilberto Gil and Caetano Veloso on television and on record in 1967 and 1968. Rogério Duprat arranged the orchestral parts. Sérgio Dias's brother Cláudio built their guitars and effects devices at home, because imported gear was scarce and expensive under the import rules of the day. They made records in Brazil's studios with a lab-experiment attitude.","Much of the strange sound comes from homemade equipment and studio accidents deliberately kept. Listen, too, for how often they send up both Brazilian and Anglo-American clichés at the same time: a sentimental bolero phrase sung with a straight face, then broken by a sound effect. Bat macumba mixes Afro-Brazilian religious language with the Batman TV theme.","In Brazil they were a TV sensation and a critical puzzle; abroad they were barely known until the 1990s, when musicians such as Kurt Cobain and Beck praised them and a 1999 compilation on David Byrne's Luaka Bop label revived their name. They are now cited as one of the most inventive psychedelic bands anywhere.","Start with Panis et circencis, then compare it with the version on the collective Tropicália album. Listen for the home-built guitar tones and the moments where tape is simply cut. Pair it with Revolver to hear what they took and what they added."],"caetano-veloso-transa":["Seven long, loose songs, mixing English and Portuguese, played by a small band that sounds as if it is in the room. You Don't Know Me opens in an easy groove; Nine Out of Ten drifts through London street life; Triste Bahia sets a seventeenth-century Brazilian poem over berimbau and chanting; It's a Long Way stretches out for six minutes, stitching fragments of other songs together. The mood is homesick, curious, alert.","After their arrest in December 1968, Caetano and Gilberto Gil were forced into exile, and Caetano spent 1969 to 1972 in London. Transa was made there, with guitarist Jards Macalé leading the band, and released after his return to Brazil. London then was full of rock, and also of new Jamaican music; Caetano absorbed both while writing about displacement and a Brazil he could not reach.","In Nine Out of Ten Caetano mentions hearing reggae on Portobello Road, often cited as the first reference to reggae in Brazilian music. Triste Bahia sets lines by the Baroque satirist Gregório de Matos, a 1600s poet who also mocked colonial Bahia; the band is quoting four centuries at once. It's a Long Way threads in snatches of Beatles and Brazilian folk material.","At the time it was a modest seller, but its reputation has grown to the point that many Brazilian critics call it Caetano's best album. Listeners abroad, discovering it later, hear it as a bridge between Tropicália's collage and a more intimate singer-songwriter style. The live, unpolished sound is now considered a virtue.","Listen straight through, preferably on a remaster that keeps the room sound. Start with Triste Bahia and follow the berimbau. Then play Joni Mitchell's Blue or Bob Dylan's Blonde on Blonde, and João Gilberto's white album from the next year, which records one of these songwriters' songs."],"gilberto-gil-expresso-2222":["Gilberto Gil's homecoming record, bright with acoustic guitar and northeastern rhythms. The album brings in the fifes and drums of the Banda de Pífanos de Caruaru, rural northeastern music heard rarely on a pop record, then moves into Back in Bahia, a joyful song of return, the jaunty title track about a train to the future, and Oriente, a long meditative guitar piece. Gil's guitar playing, percussive and supple, is everywhere.","Gil, like Caetano, was arrested in 1968 and spent roughly three years in London, where he played with rock musicians and heard everything from Hendrix to reggae. Expresso 2222 was recorded soon after he came home to Brazil in 1972, still under dictatorship. It is the sound of a musician rejoining his own traditions, northeastern forró, baião and samba, with new ears.","Listen to how Gil's acoustic guitar playing borrows from the zabumba drum and triangle of northeastern forró, strumming patterns that become a whole percussion band. Chiclete com banana, bubblegum with banana, is a slyly political song about mixing Brazilian and American culture, arguing that the exchange should go both ways.","It was well received in Brazil and is now counted among his classic records. Critics abroad came to it later, through world-music reissues. Gil went on to serve as Brazil's Minister of Culture from 2003 to 2008, and this album is often cited as the essential statement of his fusion of northeastern roots and global pop.","Start with Back in Bahia, and follow Gil's guitar like a drummer. Then compare Caetano's Transa, made in London at the same moment, to hear the two exiles' different moods: Caetano homesick, Gil coming home. Then hear the collective Tropicália album where they began."],"chico-buarque-construcao":["Ten songs of controlled anger and tenderness, framed by Rogério Duprat's orchestral writing. The title track tells the last day of a construction worker who falls from a building, in a single long, relentless sentence over a pounding samba rhythm and swelling brass and strings; the song then repeats its story with the words shuffled. Cotidiano is a gentle, trapped portrait of daily routine; Deus lhe pague offers bitter thanks.","Chico Buarque had left Brazil for Italy after 1968 and returned in 1970 to a country at its most repressive under President Médici. His songs were routinely censored, and he became a master of double meaning. Construção was a huge seller, and its title song, written about a labourer's anonymous death during the so-called Brazilian economic miracle, was understood as an indictment of the regime's development without justice.","In Construção nearly every line ends on a word stressed on its third-last syllable, a rare shape in Portuguese that gives the song its tumbling, mechanical gait. In the second telling, those end-words are swapped among the lines, so the worker's day is rebuilt from the same bricks in a new order, and the meaning shifts: the song itself is constructed like a building.","Critics immediately recognised it as one of Brazil's great albums, and it has kept that rank. The title track is studied in Brazilian schools for its form. Some listeners find Duprat's orchestra overwhelming; others hear it as part of the argument, the machine that crushes the man. The album secured Chico's place as the conscience of Brazilian song.","Listen to the title track with a translation, and then again without, noticing only the rhythm of the line-endings and the orchestra's build. Compare Jobim's Águas de março, another list song, and Elis Regina's Como nossos pais for politics carried by structure."],"milton-nascimento-clube-da-esquina":["A double album by Milton Nascimento and the young Lô Borges with a circle of musicians from Belo Horizonte. Milton's high, church-trained voice floats over songs that mix Minas Gerais folk and Catholic processional music, Beatles harmony, jazz chords and progressive rock. Tudo que você podia ser opens with rolling guitars; Cais is a slow, oceanic ballad; O trem azul is sunny pop; San Vicente and Nada será como antes are darker and more ambitious.","Clube da Esquina, the corner club, was the nickname for a group of friends who met on a street corner in Belo Horizonte, including Lô and Márcio Borges, Beto Guedes, Toninho Horta, Wagner Tiso and the lyricist Fernando Brant. Released by EMI-Odeon in Rio in 1972, it came from the interior state of Minas Gerais, far from the Rio-São Paulo centres of bossa nova and Tropicália, and brought a new regional sound into Brazilian pop.","Listen for the harmonic language: Milton and Toninho Horta use chords that move in unexpected, modal ways, closer to Jobim's impressionism and to church music than to rock. The famous cover photo of two boys sitting by a dirt road does not show the musicians; it was a snapshot of local children, chosen for its feeling of a rural childhood.","It sold modestly at first but is now regularly voted among the greatest Brazilian albums, sometimes at the very top. Jazz musicians, notably Wayne Shorter, who recorded Native Dancer with Milton in 1975, spread his name abroad. Critics discuss it alongside Pet Sounds and Abbey Road as a landmark of studio pop songwriting.","Take it in two sittings, one disc at a time. Start with Cais and Tudo que você podia ser, and listen for the choir-like voices. Then compare Abbey Road for the Beatles connection, and Villa-Lobos's Bachianas Brasileiras No. 5 for a classical Brazilian vocalise with the same soaring feeling."],"machito-tanga":["A big-band piece in which a Cuban rhythm section, congas, bongos, timbales and a repeating bass pattern, drives while trumpets and saxophones play jazz-harmonised riffs and open up for improvised solos. It has no song in the usual sense: a vamp, a set of layered horn figures, a building intensity. The feeling is of two separate musics, Cuban dance and swing-era jazz, locking into one groove for the first time.","Tanga was composed by Mario Bauzá, trumpeter and music director of Machito and his Afro-Cubans, the New York band led by the singer Frank Grillo, known as Machito, Bauzá's brother-in-law. It grew out of a jam at a club in 1943. Bauzá had played in Chick Webb's and Cab Calloway's bands and knew jazz from the inside; Machito's band played for dancers across Harlem and midtown.","Listen to how the horn riffs are written on top of the clave, the two-bar Cuban rhythmic key, rather than on top of swing: the jazz harmonies are there, but the rhythm is Cuban at root. This band's fusion is the origin of the Afro-Cuban jazz that Bauzá's friend Dizzy Gillespie would take to Carnegie Hall with Chano Pozo, whom Bauzá introduced to him, in 1947.","Tanga is widely described as the first Afro-Cuban jazz composition, though such firsts are always debatable. The band was a hit with dancers and admired by jazz musicians; Charlie Parker later recorded with it. Historians now credit Bauzá and Machito as architects of Latin jazz and, through the mambo era, of salsa.","Listen first to the rhythm section alone, then add the horns in your ear. Then play Gillespie's Manteca and hear the same idea in bebop clothes. Later, Tito Puente's Dance Mania shows where it went in the dance halls. Any compilation of the Afro-Cubans from the 1940s will do; the drums matter more than the fidelity."],"tito-puente-dance-mania":["Twelve dance numbers, mambos, cha-cha-chas and guarachas, played by Tito Puente's big band with the singer Santos Colón. Brass sections punch out riffs over a dense percussion engine led by Puente's own timbales, and the pianist hammers a repeating montuno pattern. El cayuco and Complicación are among the best known. Everything is tight, precise and built for dancers, yet full of jazz harmony and fierce solos.","Puente, born in Spanish Harlem to Puerto Rican parents, trained at the Juilliard School after wartime navy service and ruled the Palladium Ballroom in midtown Manhattan, the temple of the 1950s mambo craze, alongside Machito and Tito Rodríguez. RCA Victor issued Dance Mania at the height of that craze, and it became his best-selling album and a standard for Latin bandleaders.","Listen to the brass voicings: Puente arranged like a jazz composer, stacking trumpets in close, bright dissonances borrowed from Stan Kenton and Gillespie. And listen to the timbales: Puente's fills between sections are not decoration but cues to the band and the dancers, a drummer conducting from behind. His solos are short and structural.","It is frequently named the most important Latin album of the 1950s. Later salsa musicians studied its arrangements closely. Carlos Santana's 1970 cover of Puente's Oye como va, a later song, spread his name to rock audiences. Dance Mania's reputation rests on its sheer craft. Musicians still copy its charts note for note in rehearsal rooms from San Juan to Tokyo.","Put it on and move. Then listen for the three layers: percussion, piano montuno, brass riffs. Compare Machito's Tanga and Gillespie's Manteca for the jazz side, and Celia Cruz's work with the Fania musicians for where this sound went next."],"fania-all-stars-live-at-the-cheetah":["Two volumes recording one August night in 1971 at the Cheetah club in Manhattan, where Fania Records assembled its stars into a single band: Johnny Pacheco leading, Ray Barretto on congas, Willie Colón on trombone, Larry Harlow on piano, and singers including Héctor Lavoe, Cheo Feliciano, Ismael Miranda and Pete El Conde Rodríguez trading verses. Long jams, Anacaona, Quítate tú, build through improvised vocal calls and brass mambos to crowd-roaring peaks.","Fania, founded in 1964 by the Dominican flautist Johnny Pacheco and the lawyer Jerry Masucci, was selling Cuban-rooted dance music to New York's Puerto Rican and Latin communities under a new marketing word, salsa. The concert was filmed for Leon Gast's documentary Our Latin Thing, shown in 1972, which carried the scene to cinemas across Latin America. The club was packed, and the heat is audible.","Listen to the soneos, the improvised lines each singer throws in between the chorus's repeated refrain: they are competing, name-checking each other and the crowd, and the chorus answers every time. Quítate tú, meaning get out of the way so I can take over, is literally about this: a ritual of friendly rivalry that is the heart of salsa.","It is generally seen as the founding live document of the salsa era, and helped turn a New York label into an international movement. Some Cuban musicians and critics argued that salsa was simply Cuban son and mambo rebranded; salseros answered that the New York barrio had made it something new. The debate still runs.","Start with Quítate tú and count the singers. Listen for the moment when the brass cuts in with a new riff, the mambo section, and the crowd lifts. Then hear Tito Puente's Dance Mania for the big-band parent, and Siembra for the thinking-person's salsa that came next."],"celia-cruz-celia-and-johnny":["Celia Cruz, the great Cuban singer, joins Johnny Pacheco's conjunto, a band of trumpets, piano, bass and hand percussion rooted in Cuban son rather than big-band mambo. Her voice is enormous, contralto-dark and rhythmically exact, commanding the band. Quimbara opens with a torrent of rhythmic syllables; Toro mata draws on Afro-Peruvian music; Vieja luna slows the tempo for a bolero-flavoured lament.","Cruz had sung with the Sonora Matancera, Cuba's most famous band, through the 1950s, left Cuba in 1960 after the revolution and never returned. Her career in the United States had stalled until Fania's owners paired her with Pacheco on their Vaya label. This album brought her to the young salsa audience and made her its queen. She had been singing professionally for more than twenty years.","Listen to Quimbara: the lyric is largely drum-syllables, the voice imitating percussion, and Cruz treats her singing like another drum in the section. Her rhythmic placement, slightly ahead of the beat and then sharply on it, is what makes the band feel faster than it is. Her famous shout of azúcar, sugar, is said to have begun as a joke about how she took her coffee.","It was a hit and turned Cruz from nostalgic figure to present star; it is regularly listed among the most important salsa albums. Critics praise the pairing of her Cuban vocal authority with the leaner Dominican-led conjunto. Her later career, into her seventies, confirmed her as the defining voice of Latin dance music.","Start with Quimbara and follow her against the percussion. Then play a 1950s Sonora Matancera track to hear her younger voice, and her late La vida es un carnaval to hear the same power at seventy-three. Notice, too, how rarely the trumpets play at the same time as her voice: the arrangements clear space for her."],"celia-cruz-la-vida-es-un-carnaval":["A buoyant salsa anthem urging the listener not to weep over hardship because life is a carnival, and it is better to sing through it. Celia Cruz's voice, in her seventies, is still enormous. The song starts slowly, almost as a spoken exhortation over piano, then kicks into full band with brass and a chorus that answers her every line, building toward a jubilant finale.","The song was written by the Argentine songwriter Víctor Daniel and recorded for Cruz's album Mi vida es cantar, released in 1998 on the RMM label. Salsa by then had softened into romantic ballad-style salsa, but Cruz stayed with the harder, brass-driven Cuban tradition. She had been in exile nearly four decades, and her audience included three generations. It was a late triumph in a career of five decades.","The song's optimism is doubled by its singer's biography: a Cuban exile who could never go home, singing that there is no need to cry. Listen for the phrasing on the chorus's key words; Cruz leans on the vowels and bends the rhythm in a way that no younger salsa singer of the 1990s did. The slow introduction makes the first full band hit feel like sunrise.","It became her most famous late song, a fixture at parties, funerals and protests across Latin America, and one of the most played Spanish-language songs of its decade. After her death in 2003, it took on the role of an epitaph. Critics note that it is simpler than her 1970s work; audiences have not minded.","Wait for the full band to enter, and feel how the chorus lifts. Then go back to Quimbara from Celia & Johnny, twenty-four years earlier, to hear what age added and what it took away. Watch a live performance from her last years to see what her audiences saw: a party with a matriarch in charge."],"hector-lavoe-el-cantante":["A ten-minute salsa epic about a singer who makes everyone happy on stage while nobody asks about his own sorrow. It opens with a slow, string-laden introduction, almost a film overture, before the band surges into a hard-swinging groove. Héctor Lavoe's nasal, sweet, cutting voice carries the verses, then launches into improvised lines over a repeating chorus, and trombones blaze in the mambo sections.","The song was written by Rubén Blades and arranged by Willie Colón for Lavoe's 1978 Fania album Comedia. Lavoe, born in Ponce, Puerto Rico, had come to New York at seventeen and become the voice of Willie Colón's band before going solo. By 1978 he was salsa's most beloved singer and struggling with addiction; the song's picture of the performer's hidden pain was read as his portrait.","Blades wrote it and gave it away, and it fits Lavoe so well that the gift became part of the legend. Listen to the strings of the introduction: they set up a theatrical frame, a curtain opening, before the salsa band enters. In the improvised section Lavoe turns the song into a direct conversation with his audience, the singer about the singer.","It became Lavoe's signature and, after his death in 1993, his epitaph; the 2006 biopic starring Marc Anthony took its title. Salsa historians regard it as one of the high points of the Fania era. Some find the introduction overblown; most hear it as essential theatre. Live versions from his later concerts show the song taking on more weight each year.","Let the introduction play out; it is the point. Then listen for the change of gear when the band arrives, and count the minutes of improvisation. Follow with Siembra to hear Blades's own voice and the songwriting behind this song."],"ruben-blades-siembra":["Seven songs by the Panamanian singer-songwriter Rubén Blades with Willie Colón's trombone-heavy band. Plástico opens with a mock disco beat before snapping into salsa, satirising status-obsessed consumer life and calling for Latin American unity. Pedro Navaja tells in cinematic detail the story of a street hustler and a sex worker on a New York corner. Buscando guayaba, Juan Pachanga and the title track, sow, complete a record that thinks as it dances.","Credited to Willie Colón and Rubén Blades, Siembra was released by Fania in 1978 and became, by most accounts, the best-selling salsa album of its time. Blades, a law graduate from Panama who had worked in Fania's mailroom, brought the literate, political storytelling of Latin American nueva canción into New York dance music, then dominated by love songs and party tunes.","Pedro Navaja borrows its idea, and its opening, from Kurt Weill and Bertolt Brecht's Mack the Knife in The Threepenny Opera, transplanted to a Manhattan street. Listen for the sound effects, a car, a siren, at the end: salsa as radio drama. Plástico's disco-style introduction is a joke on the dance fashion that was then threatening salsa's audience.","It was a commercial and critical landmark, and is often cited as the album that gave salsa a political and literary conscience, sometimes called salsa consciente. Some dancers found the long narrative songs too wordy. It remains among the most acclaimed Latin albums, and Blades later ran for president of Panama.","Start with Plástico for the argument, then Pedro Navaja for the story; read a summary of the lyric first. Then play Weill's Threepenny Opera to hear the source. Héctor Lavoe's El cantante, written by Blades the same year, completes the picture."],"buena-vista-social-club-buena-vista-social-club":["Fourteen songs of Cuban son, bolero and danzón played by veteran Havana musicians, many then in their seventies and eighties: the singer and guitarist Compay Segundo, the crooner Ibrahim Ferrer, the pianist Rubén González, the singer Omara Portuondo and guitarist Eliades Ochoa. Chan chan, with its four-chord loop and laconic voices, opens it. The sound is warm and roomy, acoustic instruments in a big studio, with Ry Cooder's slide guitar threaded in.","The project began when a planned session pairing Malian and Cuban musicians fell through because the Malians could not travel. Producer Nick Gold of World Circuit, arranger Juan de Marcos González and Ry Cooder instead gathered older Cuban players at Havana's EGREM studios in March 1996. The United States embargo made Cooder's participation legally awkward. Wim Wenders' 1999 documentary made the musicians international stars.","Listen to the tres, the small Cuban guitar with doubled strings that gives son its metallic, chiming lead. The album's title comes from a members' social club in the Havana suburb of Marianao that had closed decades earlier; the name was a memory, not a venue. Many of the players had been unrecorded or forgotten for years.","It won a Grammy in 1998 and became one of the best-selling world-music albums ever made. Some Cuban critics complained that it froze Cuba in a pre-revolution nostalgia for foreign consumption, while contemporary Cuban musicians were making different music. Others see it as a genuine rescue of a generation's art.","Start with Chan chan, then Dos gardenias for Ferrer's crooning and a Rubén González piano solo. Then watch the Wenders film. For contrast, hear Celia Cruz's New York salsa, the same roots transplanted and electrified. Then seek out Ibrahim Ferrer's own solo album, made soon after, which continues the story."],"violeta-parra-las-ultimas-composiciones":["The last album Violeta Parra released, and the one containing Gracias a la vida, her hymn of thanks for the senses, for sound and sight, for the alphabet and the paths of the feet, all of which give her both laughter and tears. Elsewhere Volver a los diecisiete celebrates the renewal of love; Run run se fue pa'l norte tells of a lover's departure; Rin del angelito is a lament for a dead child in Chilean folk ritual. The sound is spare: voice, strings, a few friends.","Parra had spent two decades collecting rural Chilean folk song, painting, and making embroidered tapestries shown at the Louvre in 1964. By 1966 she had set up a performance tent in the La Reina district of Santiago, which struggled to draw audiences. She recorded this with her children Isabel and Ángel Parra and the Uruguayan musician Alberto Zapicán. She took her own life in February 1967.","Gracias a la vida is built on repetition: each verse begins with the same thanks, then names a different gift, so the song becomes a litany. Its gentle strummed pattern is played in a way that almost hides how unusual the harmony is. Knowing the singer's state of mind at the time turns the gratitude into something much more complicated.","Barely noticed commercially at first, Gracias a la vida became, especially through Mercedes Sosa's version, one of the most widely sung Latin American songs. Parra is now regarded as the mother of the nueva canción movement. Scholars debate how to read the late songs in light of her suicide; many resist reducing them to a farewell.","Listen to Gracias a la vida and notice the rough, unprettified voice; then hear Mercedes Sosa's version and compare the two singers' emotional temperatures. Continue with Víctor Jara, whom Parra inspired. Then return to the album's quieter songs, such as Rin del angelito, which show how deeply her writing is rooted in rural Chilean ritual and verse forms."],"mercedes-sosa-gracias-a-la-vida":["Mercedes Sosa sings Violeta Parra's song of thanks for life, for the senses, for sound, sight and love, with a deep, measured contralto over guitar and the soft thud of the Argentine bombo drum. Where Parra's original is rough and intimate, Sosa's is broad and dignified, a slow processional that grows with each verse. It sounds less like a confession than a prayer spoken on behalf of a crowd.","Sosa recorded it on her 1971 album Homenaje a Violeta Parra, four years after Parra's death. She had helped launch the Argentine Nuevo Cancionero movement in 1963, committed to folk music with a social conscience. In 1978, under Argentina's military junta, she was arrested on stage in La Plata and soon went into exile in Europe; she returned in 1982, and this song became a centrepiece of her homecoming concerts.","Listen to the bombo legüero, the large Argentine drum she often played herself: its low pulse gives the song the tread of a march or a procession. Sosa slows Parra's tempo and holds the long notes, so the list of gifts becomes monumental. Heard after her exile, the thanks became a political statement about surviving.","It is the version most of the world knows and one of the anthems of Latin American democracy movements. Some prefer Parra's frailer original as more personal; Sosa's admirers answer that she turned a private song into a public one. After her death in 2009, Argentina held three days of national mourning.","Listen twice, first to Sosa, then to Parra, and notice what each voice makes of the same words. Then hear Sosa's Alfonsina y el mar for her tender side, and Joan Baez's version of Gracias a la vida for the North American folk-revival connection."],"mercedes-sosa-alfonsina-y-el-mar":["A zamba, the slow Argentine folk dance in a lilting triple time, sung by Mercedes Sosa with piano. The song imagines the poet Alfonsina Storni walking into the sea, drawn by its voices and creatures, seen tenderly as an entry into a dream rather than a death. The melody rises and falls like a swell; Sosa sings it with a restraint that makes the image almost unbearably gentle.","The music is by the pianist-composer Ariel Ramírez, the words by the historian Félix Luna, for their 1969 concept album Mujeres argentinas, portraits of notable Argentine women sung by Sosa. Storni, a celebrated modernist poet, drowned herself at Mar del Plata in 1938 while ill with cancer. Ramírez was also the composer of the Misa Criolla, a folk-mass that had made him famous.","Listen to the piano's rocking figure: it is both the zamba's dance step and the motion of waves. The lyric never names suicide; it describes a walk, a sleep, a conversation with the sea, and the tact is the point. The rhythm, a triple metre that sometimes feels like two, gives the song its floating, unsettled quality.","It became one of Sosa's signature songs and one of the most covered Argentine songs, recorded by singers and classical guitarists alike. Some critics find later orchestral versions over-sweetened; Sosa's plain piano versions remain definitive. It has become a standard of Latin American repertoire, sung in concert halls as well as folk clubs, and Storni's own poetry has gained readers from it.","Hear it in Sosa's original with piano. Listen for the waves in the accompaniment. Then compare Gracias a la vida to hear her public voice, and Villa-Lobos's Bachianas Brasileiras No. 5 aria for another South American song of longing. Read one of Storni's poems before listening."],"victor-jara-te-recuerdo-amanda":["A short, quiet song for voice and fingerpicked guitar. Víctor Jara remembers a young woman, Amanda, running through the rain to meet Manuel at the factory gate for five minutes during his break; one day Manuel goes to the mountains and does not come back. The melody is simple and tender; Jara's voice is plain, warm and unhurried, and the tragedy is told without a single raised note.","Jara, a theatre director as well as a singer, was a central figure of Chilean nueva canción, following Violeta Parra. The song appeared on his 1969 album Pongo en tus manos abiertas, as Chile moved toward the 1970 election of Salvador Allende, whom Jara supported. Amanda and Manuel were the names of his parents. After the military coup of 11 September 1973, Jara was arrested, tortured and murdered in a Santiago stadium.","The song never explains why Manuel went to the mountains, where guerrillas fought, and the omission is its force. Listen to the guitar: a gentle, rocking pattern that barely changes, like the repetition of daily routine, which makes the missing return all the more final. The five minutes in the lyric are a working man's whole allotment of love.","It is one of the best-loved songs in Latin America and has been recorded in many languages. After Jara's murder, the stadium where he died was renamed after him in 2003, and in 2023 a Chilean court convicted former army officers of his killing. His songs are now sung as memorials to all victims of the dictatorship.","Listen once with a translation, then once without, following only the guitar. Then hear Violeta Parra's Gracias a la vida for the tradition he inherited, and Pete Seeger or Woody Guthrie for the North American folk singers he admired. Any of his original recordings is best."],"juan-luis-guerra-bachata-rosa":["Juan Luis Guerra and his band 4.40 mix two Dominican forms: the fast, accordion-and-drum merengue and the guitar-led bachata, a lovelorn song style from the countryside. Burbujas de amor is a slow bachata full of whimsical romantic images; Como abeja al panal and the title track glow with close vocal harmony. The merengue tracks, including La bilirrubina, a joking song about lovesickness as a medical condition, drive at full speed.","Guerra, from Santo Domingo, studied at Berklee College of Music in Boston, where he absorbed jazz harmony and vocal-group arranging. Bachata was then a looked-down-on music of the rural poor and urban migrants, played in bars and often banned from radio. Bachata rosa, polished and poetic, brought it into middle-class homes across the Spanish-speaking world. The timing was right: Latin pop was booming.","Listen to the vocal harmonies: Guerra borrowed the close-harmony style of the Manhattan Transfer and jazz vocal groups and grafted it onto rural Dominican forms. His lyrics, full of surreal metaphors, draw on Latin American poetry rather than bar-room plain speech. That combination of Berklee craft and bachata's heartache is the whole innovation.","The album sold in the millions and won a Grammy for best tropical Latin album. Traditional bachateros sometimes felt it gentrified their music; others credit it with finally legitimising the style, which in later decades became a global pop genre. Guerra is now regarded as the Dominican Republic's most important modern songwriter.","Start with Burbujas de amor and listen for the guitar's plucked, bell-like lead. Then La bilirrubina for merengue's speed. For the older, rougher bachata, look for recordings by rural guitarists of the 1970s and compare. Then play Siembra for a very different kind of literate Latin dance record."],"umm-kulthum-enta-omri":["A single love song that lasts about an hour. It opens with a long orchestral introduction, a small overture in itself, before Umm Kulthum enters with a line that she will then repeat, bend and stretch many times over. The shape is not verse and chorus but a chain of sections, each one returned to whenever the audience demands. The sound to recognise it by is the massed violin section playing in unison, a wall of strings moving as one voice.","Cairo, 1964. Umm Kulthum and the composer Mohammed Abdel Wahab were the two giants of Egyptian music and had never worked together; the pairing was widely credited to the encouragement of President Nasser, and the press called it the meeting of the summits. Ahmed Shafik Kamel wrote the words. It was premiered at one of her monthly Thursday concerts, broadcast live across the Arab world by radio, when streets from Casablanca to Baghdad reportedly went quiet.","Abdel Wahab was a modernist who loved Western dance bands and film music, and he brought that taste into her ensemble: listen for Western instruments and harmonised passages in the introduction, which purists heard as a provocation. Then notice how little of the song is fixed. Each recorded performance differs, because the singer lengthened whichever phrase made the hall cry out, and the orchestra had to follow her.","It became the most famous Arabic song of the century and remains the one people name first. Contemporary critics argued over whether Abdel Wahab had diluted her classical style with Western colour; the collaboration succeeded so well commercially that the argument was quickly buried. Today scholars read it as the high point of the golden age of Egyptian song, and as proof that tradition and modern orchestration could share one stage.","Do not start with a three-minute excerpt; the whole point is duration. Put on a full live recording, let the overture pass as you would a symphony's first movement, and listen for the audience. Their cries after a phrase are part of the music, the signal that she has reached tarab, the shared state of rapture. Then compare Al-Atlal to hear her with a more traditional composer."],"umm-kulthum-al-atlal":["The title means The Ruins, and the song looks back over a dead love as a traveller looks over an abandoned campsite. Riad Al Sunbati's setting is grave and spacious: long instrumental passages, a slow opening in which the voice seems to walk among the stones, then faster, more urgent sections as memory becomes accusation. The recognisable sound is the qanun, the plucked zither, answering every phrase of the voice with a shimmer of its own.","Premiered in 1966, the song set verses by the Egyptian poet Ibrahim Nagi, who had died in 1953, with some lines drawn from another of his poems. Sunbati was the composer most associated with Umm Kulthum's classical side, and the poem draws on the oldest image in Arabic verse, the lover weeping at the ruins of a beloved's camp. After the defeat of 1967 many listeners heard the song's loss as political as well as personal.","Listen for the way Sunbati moves between maqamat, the Arabic melodic modes, as the poem changes mood. Each shift brings different intervals, including notes that fall between the keys of a piano, so the ground under the melody literally changes colour. The celebrated closing section, where the lovers are asked to part, is set far more briskly than its sorrowful words suggest; the contrast is deliberate.","Many Arab critics and listeners rank it above Enta Omri as her supreme performance, precisely because it is less modern: classical poetry, a classical composer, maqam at full depth. Its line about freedom was chanted by protesters long after her death. Scholars debate how far the political reading was intended; the text predates 1967 by decades, but performances after the war gave it new weight.","Listen with the translation beside you, reading the poem's arc before you hear it. Then notice how the audience responds when a line is repeated: each repetition is a different ornament, a different emphasis, never a copy. If you know the lament tradition in Western song, from Dido to Schubert, you will recognise the stance of looking back, rendered here through a wholly different craft."],"abdel-halim-hafez-qariat-al-fingan":["The Fortune Teller of the Cup: a woman reads the coffee grounds in a young man's cup and tells him of a love he will never reach. Mohammed al-Mougy's setting unfolds as a long dramatic scene rather than a song, with an orchestral prelude, recitative-like passages and soaring refrains. Abdel Halim's voice is lighter and more intimate than Umm Kulthum's, closer to a crooner's, and that warmth against the vast orchestra is the sound to recognise.","The words are by the Syrian poet Nizar Qabbani, the most popular love poet of the Arab world. Abdel Halim, the Nightingale of the Nile, was by 1976 the idol of a generation, a film star and the voice of Nasser-era optimism, but he was also gravely ill from the schistosomiasis he had caught as a village child. This was among his last great concert pieces; he died in London in 1977.","Qabbani's poem is a lament disguised as a prophecy: the fortune teller's details about the beloved, locked away in a guarded palace, can be read as a portrait of love forbidden by class and custom. Listen too for moments where the tempo quickens toward a dance rhythm, then collapses back; the orchestra keeps pulling him toward hope and letting go.","The song became inseparable from the singer's death, and later listeners hear its fatalism as autobiography. A much-told concert episode, in which he rebuked part of the audience for whistling and heckling, remains one of the famous moments of Egyptian stage history, though accounts differ on details. Critics generally place it with his finest late work, the moment the romantic film idol became a tragic figure.","Begin with a live concert recording rather than a studio cut, since the audience's rising excitement is part of the form. Listen for the change in his voice when the fortune teller delivers the final verdict. Then play Enta Omri straight after: two styles of Cairene grandeur, one monumental and one confiding."],"fairuz-zahrat-al-madaen":["The Flower of Cities is a hymn to Jerusalem. Where Egyptian song stretches out for an hour, this is compact and dramatic: bells, a choir, then Fairuz's clear, cool voice, almost without vibrato, singing of the city's streets, churches and mosque. The middle rises to an anthem-like climax with the full chorus, then returns to prayer. The instant marker is that voice, pure and still, sitting above a large ensemble without ever straining.","Written by the Rahbani Brothers, Assi (Fairuz's husband) and Mansour, and released in 1967 in the aftermath of the Six-Day War, when Israel took East Jerusalem. The Rahbanis had built a new Lebanese song: short forms, European harmony and orchestration mixed with Levantine folk and Byzantine chant. Fairuz, a Christian singer beloved by Muslims and Christians alike, made the song a shared lament across the Arab world.","Listen for the church-bell sound and the choral writing, which borrow from the Eastern Christian liturgy Fairuz grew up singing, placed alongside references to the Al-Aqsa mosque; the song is deliberately built to belong to both faiths. And notice the brevity. The Rahbanis were cutting Arab song down to radio length at the very moment Umm Kulthum was stretching it further.","It became the standard musical expression of Arab grief over Jerusalem and is still played on anniversaries. Some critics find its anthem style too public for Fairuz's intimate gift; others regard it as the Rahbanis' most perfect synthesis. The Rahbani project itself divided opinion: traditionalists accused it of Westernising Arab music, while supporters credited it with making a modern Lebanese identity in sound.","Listen first for the voice alone, then for the texture behind it: chorus, bells, strings, each entering as the city is described. Compare it with Al-Atlal from the year before, where time and repetition do the work; here it is concentration. Then follow Fairuz forward to Li Beirut, her song for her own city in ruins."],"fairuz-li-beirut":["For Beirut: a love song to a city being destroyed. The melody will sound familiar to classical listeners, because it is the slow movement of Joaquín Rodrigo's Concierto de Aranjuez, with the cor anglais line given to Fairuz's voice. The arrangement keeps the guitar's strummed opening and the aching, rising phrase, but the tempo slows and the voice sits low and grave. It is the gesture people know it by across the Arab world.","Arranged by Ziad Rahbani, Fairuz's son, with words by the poet Joseph Harb, during the Lebanese civil war, which ran from 1975 to 1990 and tore Beirut apart. Fairuz stayed in the city through much of the war and refused to sing for any faction, which made her a rare shared figure. Ziad, a composer and satirist steeped in jazz, belonged to a harder, more ironic generation than his father's.","Rodrigo wrote his Adagio in 1939, just after the Spanish Civil War, and many have heard grief for a ravaged country in it. Whether or not Ziad Rahbani meant the echo, the borrowing carries one civil war's lament into another's. Listen also for the Andalusian flavour of the original melody, with its bending half-steps, which sits naturally in an Arab voice.","It became one of Fairuz's most loved songs and a civil-war anthem sung by all sides of a divided city. Some listeners in Europe first encountered it as a curiosity, a pop song on a classical tune; in Lebanon it is anything but. It has since been covered widely and is often played at memorials, most recently after the 2020 port explosion.","Play Rodrigo's Adagio first, then this. Notice what changes when an instrumental line acquires words and a single voice: the melody slows, the ornaments drop away, and the tune becomes speech. Listen to how the guitar remains as a memory of Spain beneath a Lebanese song."],"khaled-didi":["A dance song built on a rolling, heavily accented groove, bright synthesizer riffs and Khaled's voice: nasal, powerful, full of ornamental turns and long melismas that break through the pop production. The words, in Algerian Arabic, are a teasing plea to a girl. The track's instant marker is the chanted hook of the title, repeated like a playground call, over a beat that sits between North African rhythm and Western club music.","Khaled grew up in Oran, the port city where raï had developed out of rural Bedouin song in the bars and weddings of the early twentieth century. Raï was frowned upon by religious conservatives and by the Algerian state, and as the civil war of the 1990s approached, several raï figures were threatened or murdered. Khaled had moved to France in the late 1980s; this record, produced in Los Angeles by Don Was, was his international breakthrough.","Behind the modern production, the vocal line keeps the melodic habits of old raï and the older Bedouin song beneath it: short phrases circling a few notes, with sudden leaps and quavering ornaments. The dropped 'Cheb', meaning young man, from his name on this album was deliberate, a move away from the raï scene's youth branding toward international stardom.","It was a hit across France, the Arab world, and far more surprisingly India, and it remains the most famous raï song worldwide. Some raï fans regard it as a glossy compromise and prefer the rawer cassette recordings of the 1980s. It was banned in parts of the Arab world and criticised by Islamists, which in its time gave the playful song an unintended political edge.","Listen once for the beat and the hook, as a dance record. Then listen again only for the voice, and hear how much of an older, freer vocal art survives inside a four-minute pop song. For the roots, go to Cheikha Rimitti, the woman whose songs gave raï its outlaw reputation."],"cheikha-rimitti-sidi-mansour":["The grandmother of raï, recorded in her seventies with rock musicians. Rimitti's voice is rough, low and commanding, delivering short melodic cells over and over with small changes, as a village singer would at a wedding. Around her the band plays loose, driving grooves while the gasba, a reed flute, and the frame drum keep the older sound in view. The marker is her voice itself, hoarse and unhurried, owning every track.","Born in 1923 in western Algeria, Rimitti sang in the bars and festivals of the Oran region from the 1940s, and her frank songs about love, drinking and women's lives earned her notoriety and banning. She settled in France and was rediscovered in the 1990s as the source of the music that had made Khaled famous. This album paired her with the guitarist Robert Fripp and the bassist Flea, among others.","Her songs are not written in the Western sense; they are assembled from formulaic lines and refrains she had sung for decades, reshaped each time. Many younger raï singers recorded her songs as their own, and one of the pleasures here is hearing the original owner reclaim her material. Listen for how the rock rhythm section is made to follow her phrasing, not the other way round.","Critics welcomed it as overdue recognition; some purists found the Western rock backing awkward and preferred her acoustic recordings with gasba and drums alone. It helped establish her late international career, which continued until her death in Paris in 2006. Her place as the fountainhead of modern raï is no longer disputed.","Start with the title track and listen to how little the melody moves; the energy comes from repetition and from the grain of the voice. Then turn to Khaled's Didi, recorded two years earlier by a singer who grew up on her songs, to hear what the younger generation kept and what it polished away. Seek out her sparser early recordings too."],"rachid-taha-ya-rayah":["A cover of an Algerian standard about emigration, addressed to someone leaving for foreign lands who will return with nothing. The arrangement opens with a plucked mandole and a swaying string riff, then locks into an insistent rhythm under Taha's rasping, half-spoken voice. The recognisable sound is that circular string line, part Cairo film orchestra and part dance loop, which repeats like a tide.","The song was written by Dahmane El Harrachi, a master of chaabi, the urban popular music of Algiers, and he recorded it in the early 1970s. Taha, born in western Algeria and raised in France, had led the rock band Carte de Séjour in Lyon in the 1980s. This version appeared on his 1997 album Diwân, produced by the guitarist Steve Hillage, which reworked Algerian and Egyptian classics.","The subject is the life of North African immigrant workers in France, Taha's own family story, which makes the cover a kind of return address. Chaabi itself descends in part from the Andalusian music brought to Algiers by refugees from Spain, so the lament of leaving home rests on older layers of exile. Listen for that Andalusian turn in the melody's cadences.","It became a huge hit in France and the Arab world, and the song's revival carried El Harrachi's name to a new generation. Taha's style, which fused punk attitude with Arab tradition, was criticised by some traditionalists as disrespectful; he later won even more attention with a cover of The Clash's Rock the Casbah. He is now remembered as a key voice of the French-Algerian experience.","Hear El Harrachi's own recording first, a single voice with a small ensemble, then Taha's. The tune is identical; everything else, from the beat to the swagger, is new. Follow it with Khaled's Didi to hear two routes from Algeria into French pop."],"master-musicians-of-jajouka-pipes-of-pan":["Field recordings from a hill village in northern Morocco: massed rhaitas, double-reed pipes played with circular breathing, shrieking in close intervals over relentless drumming. There are no songs in the pop sense, only long blocks of sound that build and shift. Brian Jones added studio effects, phasing and echo, which make the sound swim. The marker is the rhaita chorus, loud and piercing enough to carry across valleys.","Jones, the Rolling Stones' founder, visited Jajouka in 1968, guided by the painter and writer Brion Gysin, who had long championed the musicians. The recordings were made during the rites associated with Boujeloud, a goat-skinned figure whom the Western visitors linked to the Greek god Pan. Jones died in 1969; the album came out in 1971 on the Stones' own label, with a sleeve full of psychedelic colour.","The Pan connection was largely the visitors' romantic theory; the villagers' own tradition ties the music to a local Sufi saint and to a pre-Islamic festival whose meaning is debated. Listen for the drums' interlocking patterns, which keep the pulse moving while the pipes seem to hover free of it. The studio effects mean this is as much Jones's psychedelic collage as a document.","It was one of the first albums of non-Western traditional music issued by a rock star, and it helped create the audience that would later be called world music. Ethnomusicologists have criticised the exotic framing and the treatment; the musicians' own group has since split into rival ensembles over the name. Still, the record introduced a whole generation to North African trance.","Play it loud and do not try to follow melodies; follow the pulse and the changes of density. Then hear Ornette Coleman's Dancing in Your Head, where he recorded with these musicians in 1973, and notice how much his harmolodic freedom has in common with the pipes' hovering."],"andalusi-nuba-moroccan-ala":["A nuba is a suite: many songs in a single melodic mode, arranged in movements that begin slowly and accelerate. A performance opens with unmeasured instrumental preludes, then the ensemble sings strophic poems in unison, with the violins played upright on the knee and the rabab, a bowed boat-shaped fiddle, giving a nasal edge. The sound to recognise is the choral unison, many men singing one line, and the steady quickening toward the end.","Tradition traces the form to Ziryab, a musician who came from Baghdad to Córdoba in the ninth century. As Muslims and Jews were driven out of Spain, the repertoire travelled to Fez, Tetouan, Tlemcen, Algiers and Tunis, each keeping its own branch. In Morocco the surviving texts were gathered in the late eighteenth century in a songbook associated with Muhammad al-Ha'ik; eleven nubas survive there, fragments of a legendary twenty-four.","Many of the poems are muwashshahat, a strophic form invented in al-Andalus, whose final lines were sometimes in the Romance vernacular of Spain rather than Arabic. So the same courts that made this music produced some of the earliest lyric fragments in a Spanish tongue. Listen for rhythmic cycles that stay constant within a movement while the melody drifts over them.","Scholars debate how much of today's nuba goes back to Andalusia and how much was rebuilt in North Africa over centuries; the Ziryab story is at least partly legend. What is undisputed is the continuity of transmission, teacher to pupil, across five hundred years. Conservatories in Fez and Rabat now teach it formally, and the repertoire is also sung in North African Jewish communities.","Choose a single nuba by a Fez or Tetouan orchestra and let it run through from the first prelude to the fast finale. Then listen to the Cantigas de Santa Maria and Mozarabic chant, heard in the same peninsula at the same time, and to flamenco's cadences: all of them share a memory of this music."],"anouar-brahem-thimar":["A trio with no drums: oud, reeds and bass, playing slow, spacious pieces that sit between Arab art music and chamber jazz. The oud states a modal theme; John Surman answers on soprano sax or the woody bass clarinet; Dave Holland's bass walks, drones or sings. There is a great deal of air in the sound. The marker is the bass clarinet and oud together, two low, dark instruments in quiet conversation.","Brahem, born in Tunis in 1957, trained in the Arab classical tradition before turning to his own compositions. He began recording for Manfred Eicher's Munich label ECM in 1991, whose clean, reverberant production became part of his signature. Surman is English and Holland played on Miles Davis's electric records; the album brought North African maqam into a European jazz world.","The oud's tuning and phrasing keep the Arabic modes intact, including notes between the Western semitones; Surman and Holland adapt to it rather than the other way around. Listen for how rarely the three play at full volume together. Brahem's writing often gives each instrument its own rhythmic cycle, so the pieces seem to float rather than march.","It was well received in the jazz press and helped make Brahem the best-known oud player in the West. Some Arab listeners regard his ECM work as a softened, Europeanised form of their music; admirers answer that he never pretends to be traditional. His later records, especially Le pas du chat noir, extended the approach.","Listen late at night, at low volume. Follow one instrument through a whole track and notice where it steps back for the others. Then compare Paco de Lucía's trio with McLaughlin and Di Meola: another encounter between Mediterranean tradition and jazz, but loud and fast where this is slow and hushed."],"nusrat-fateh-ali-khan-shahen-shah":["Qawwali, the devotional song of the Sufis of Pakistan and North India, performed by the tradition's greatest modern singer and his party. Each piece starts quietly with harmonium and a sung invocation, then the rhythm enters with tabla and handclaps and the song climbs toward ecstatic climaxes. Nusrat's voice is the marker: enormous range, and cascades of rapid improvised syllables, the sargam, in which he names the notes at dizzying speed.","Nusrat came from a family of qawwals in Faisalabad with a lineage going back centuries. Qawwali is sung at the shrines of Sufi saints to bring listeners toward a spiritual state of rapture. After his appearance at the WOMAD festival in 1985, Peter Gabriel's Real World label recorded him in England; this was among the first of those albums, aimed at listeners with no Urdu or Punjabi.","The rapid solfège passages come from Hindustani classical practice, not from qawwali proper, and Nusrat's use of them in devotional song was his innovation. Listen for how a single line of mystic poetry is repeated dozens of times; the repetition is the method, since each return is meant to strip away the listener's resistance. The claps are part of the rhythm section, not applause.","Western audiences were overwhelmed, and the records made him the first South Asian voice with a global following. Some in Pakistan objected that he made sacred music into entertainment; he answered that it was taken to new listeners. Critics now agree that his studio albums are best heard alongside live recordings, where the pieces stretch to half an hour.","Start at the beginning of a long track and do not skip; the payoff is at the end. Listen for the moment the whole party joins the refrain and the tempo lifts. Then set it beside a Hindustani raga performance and hear what he borrowed."],"nusrat-fateh-ali-khan-mustt-mustt":["Nusrat's voice set within Western production by the Canadian guitarist and producer Michael Brook. The songs are shorter than traditional qawwali, carried by ambient guitar textures, bass lines and programmed beats, while his improvisations soar over the top. The title track, built on a chanted refrain in praise of the Sufi saint Lal Shahbaz Qalandar, is the one people know.","Recorded for Real World after Nusrat's appearance on Peter Gabriel's soundtrack for The Last Temptation of Christ. Brook had developed an infinitely sustaining guitar and a taste for drifting textures, and the sessions were built by layering. Soon after release the Bristol group Massive Attack remixed the title track into a trip-hop single, which reached club audiences who had never heard of qawwali.","Brook recorded Nusrat's vocal improvisations and edited them around new backing, so some passages are assembled rather than performed live. Listen for how the refrain of the title track still follows the qawwali call-and-response form, the party answering its leader, even inside a studio loop. The devotional text, praising a saint, is unchanged by the dance-floor setting.","It is often cited as a founding album of fusion between South Asian tradition and electronic music, and it influenced the Asian Underground scene in Britain. Qawwali purists find it thin beside his live work. Nusrat went on to collaborate with Eddie Vedder and to score film; his death in 1997 at 48 cut short the arc.","Hear the title track twice: first the album version, where Brook's guitar drifts under the voice, then the Massive Attack remix, where a heavy slow beat takes over and the chanted refrain becomes a hook. Then play a traditional qawwali recording of the same saint's praise with harmonium and handclaps. Three settings, one voice; for the full art, go back to the concert albums."],"amalia-rodrigues-barco-negro":["Black Boat: a woman on a beach waits for a lover lost at sea, insisting he is still alive. The rhythm is unusual for fado, a slow, insistent pulse drawn from the Brazilian original, and over it Amália's voice rises with sharp, sobbing ornaments. The sound to recognise is the Portuguese guitar, a pear-shaped twelve-string instrument, weaving bright countermelodies around the voice.","The song adapts Mãe Preta, a Brazilian song about an enslaved nursemaid; the Portuguese censor of Salazar's dictatorship reportedly found the original subject unacceptable, and the poet David Mourão-Ferreira supplied new words. Amália sang it in the French film Les Amants du Tage in 1955, which made her an international star. She was already the queen of fado in Lisbon, and the film took her to Paris, New York and beyond.","The drumlike rhythm that sets this song apart from most fados comes from its Afro-Brazilian source, a reminder that fado itself emerged in Lisbon in the early nineteenth century partly from Brazilian dances brought home across the Atlantic. Listen for the moment the voice holds a note while the guitar keeps moving; that is where she shapes the lament.","It became one of the most covered fados, and the story of its censorship has become part of its legend, though details vary between accounts. Some fado purists considered Amália's film career a betrayal of tradition; she is now recognised as having redefined the art for the twentieth century.","Listen to the pulse first, which is the song's engine, then to how she accents the words over it. Then hear Mãe Preta in its Brazilian form, and compare her later Busto album, which is more poetic and less cinematic."],"amalia-rodrigues-busto":["Known by its cover, a photograph of a sculpted bust of the singer. The songs are by the French-born composer Alain Oulman, set to serious poetry, and they change fado from within: longer melodic lines, darker harmonies, more space for the voice to speak. Amália sings with restraint, holding back the sob until it counts. The marker is that combination of guitar-and-voice intimacy with the gravity of art song.","Lisbon, 1962, under the Estado Novo dictatorship. Fado had been promoted by the regime as a national symbol, and Amália was accused by some of serving it. Oulman, a composer from a family of Jewish bankers, was himself later arrested by the secret police for opposition activity. His settings brought poets such as David Mourão-Ferreira and Pedro Homem de Mello, and older poets as well, into the fado house.","Among the songs is Abandono, often called the Fado de Peniche, about a prisoner; Peniche was the fortress where political prisoners were held, and listeners understood the reference without anyone saying so. Listen for how Oulman's melodies sometimes avoid the expected fado cadence, holding a phrase open where tradition would close it.","Traditionalists attacked Oulman's music as not true fado at all; within a decade it had become the standard for serious fado singing, and the album is now regarded as her masterwork. The question of Amália's politics has been reconsidered since her death: evidence of her quiet support for opponents of the regime has emerged, and she is honoured in the National Pantheon.","Read the poems in translation before you press play, so the words are already in your head. Then listen for her pauses: the silence before a line is where she does her acting, and the guitars wait for her. Start with Povo que lavas no rio. Compare Barco Negro to hear the film star, and Carlos Paredes for the Portuguese guitar alone."],"carlos-paredes-guitarra-portuguesa":["Instrumental pieces for the Portuguese guitar, the twelve-string instrument that accompanies fado, played here as a solo voice. Paredes plays with ferocious speed and rhythmic drive, fast repeated notes ringing like a mandolin, and then with sudden, grave lyricism. A second guitar keeps the harmony. The best-known piece, Verdes Anos, written for a film, is the melody that many Portuguese know by heart.","Paredes, from a Coimbra family of guitarists, worked for years as a hospital clerk. In 1958 he was arrested by the secret police for his Communist sympathies and spent more than a year in prison, and he lost his job. His music is not fado song but a modern concert art built on it, and in Portugal under the dictatorship it carried an unspoken sense of dignity and resistance.","Listen for the strumming patterns and ringing open strings, which come from the Coimbra school of fado, older and more academic than Lisbon's. Paredes often phrases across bar lines, so the melody seems to lean forward. He is said to have practised silently in prison, fingering pieces on his arm; the story may be embellished, but it fits.","It was recognised at once as a landmark; after the 1974 revolution Paredes became a national figure, and many consider him the greatest Portuguese instrumentalist of the century. He was reluctant to record and published few albums, which adds to their weight. Some critics compare his status to Paco de Lucía's in flamenco.","Begin with Verdes Anos, then go to a fast piece and just listen to the right hand. Compare with Albéniz or Tárrega for the classical guitar's route to Iberian song, and with Amália to hear the same instrument as accompanist."],"antologia-del-cante-flamenco":["An anthology on several LPs of flamenco song forms, each performed by a singer with a single guitar: soleares, siguiriyas, tonás sung without accompaniment, fandangos, malagueñas, bulerías and many more. The effect is encyclopaedic, a map of the whole family. The recognisable sound is the guitarist Perico el del Lunar's crisp accompaniment framing voices that crack, rasp and wail across long melismas.","Recorded in Madrid and released in 1954 by the French label Ducretet-Thomson and Spain's Hispavox. Flamenco had become dominated by commercial staged shows, and the project set out to document the older, deeper song forms at risk of vanishing. Perico el del Lunar chose the singers and the repertoire; the scholar Tomás Andrade de Silva wrote the notes. It revived the ideal behind Falla and Lorca's 1922 Concurso de Cante Jondo.","Several of the singers were not famous stars but respected artists who knew specific older styles, and some forms were reconstructed from memory. That makes the anthology partly an act of scholarship and partly an act of invention. Listen for the tonás, sung with no guitar at all, which sound closest to chant and to the Andalusi and Jewish melodic traces flamenco carries.","It won major prizes in France and became the reference collection for the flamenco revival of the 1950s and 1960s; many later singers learned forms from it. Scholars have since noted that it presents a particular, somewhat purist view, underrating the Gitano family traditions of Jerez and Utrera. It remains the starting point.","Do not play it straight through. Take one form at a time: a soleá, then a siguiriya, then a bulería, and listen for how the rhythm cycles differ. Then hear Falla's El amor brujo, whose songs imitate exactly these forms."],"camaron-la-leyenda-del-tiempo":["The great flamenco singer of his age, with his heart-torn, sandpaper voice, set among rock and jazz instruments: electric bass, keyboards, drum kit and, on one track, a sitar. Several songs set poems by Federico García Lorca. The guitar of Tomatito remains central, and the rhythms are still flamenco's own, but the sound is something new. The title track, with its slow bass-led groove, is the marker.","Spain in 1979, four years after Franco's death, was opening to every kind of experiment. The producer Ricardo Pachón gathered Camarón, then known through his classic duo records with Paco de Lucía, with young Andalusian rock musicians. Lorca, murdered by Nationalists in 1936, had been only partly accepted under Franco; singing him was itself a statement.","The song Volando voy is by the rock musician Kiko Veneno and became a flamenco standard. The Lorca texts come from his plays and his poems about Gitano Andalusia, closing a circle with the poet who had championed cante jondo in 1922. Listen for the way the drum kit plays around the flamenco rhythm cycle rather than on a rock backbeat.","It sold poorly at first and was rejected by many purists, who reportedly returned copies to shops. Within a decade it was recognised as the founding record of nuevo flamenco, and it is now widely named as the most important flamenco album. Camarón's early death in 1992, at 41, made him a legend.","Start with the title track and let the bass guide you; then play a traditional Camarón recording with Paco de Lucía to hear the voice undressed. Compare Crumb's Ancient Voices of Children, another setting of Lorca from a very different world."],"paco-de-lucia-entre-dos-aguas":["A rumba, flamenco's cheerful Cuban-derived form, played as an extended guitar improvisation over a steady two-chord vamp. Bongos and an electric bass lay down a loose, danceable groove while Paco's guitar races through ornate runs, rapid-fire picking and the percussive rasgueado strums of the right hand. The marker is the bass ostinato and the sense of a jam session: a flamenco track you can dance to.","Recorded for the album Fuente y caudal in 1973, reportedly almost by accident, when the record was short and the band improvised to fill it. Paco, born in Algeciras in 1947, was already the most admired flamenco guitarist of his generation. Released as a single, it became a surprise hit, one of the best-selling instrumental records in Spain, and brought flamenco guitar to listeners far outside the tradition.","The bongos and electric bass were almost unheard of in flamenco then; this track opened the door for the percussion and bass that became normal in later flamenco. Listen for the picado runs, alternating fingers striking single notes at speed, which Paco pushed to a precision few guitarists had approached. The title means Between Two Waters.","Purists dismissed it as commercial, a flamenco guitarist playing to the pop charts. Its success, however, gave Paco the money and the freedom for everything experimental he did afterwards. Guitarists now treat the track as a technical study, transcribed and copied note for note, and critics mark it as the moment flamenco guitar became an international solo voice in popular music rather than an accompaniment to song and dance.","Listen once for the groove, as you would any dance record. Then listen again only for the guitar's right hand, and try to follow a single run from start to finish; it is harder than it sounds. Afterwards play his Falla album, which turns the same technique toward a composer, and Camarón's records with him, where the guitar returns to serving a voice."],"paco-de-lucia-interpreta-a-manuel-de-falla":["Falla's music, chiefly from El amor brujo, arranged for Paco's flamenco group. The Ritual Fire Dance, the Pantomime and the songs become guitar pieces, with flamenco singers and palmas taking the place of orchestra and mezzo-soprano. The effect is a homecoming: themes Falla drew from flamenco returned to the players. The marker is the Ritual Fire Dance's trills and hammering chords, now struck on guitar strings.","Falla had written El amor brujo in 1915 for the Gitana dancer Pastora Imperio, and in 1922 he co-organised the Concurso de Cante Jondo in Granada to defend flamenco's old song forms. Paco, by 1978, was the tradition's most famous instrumentalist and a composer in his own right, and the album was a tribute that also claimed Falla as one of flamenco's own.","Listen for how Paco turns Falla's orchestral textures into strummed rasgueados and rhythmic palmas: every sustained string chord in the original becomes a percussive pattern. Where Falla imitates a guitar in the orchestra, the arrangement simply hands the line back to one, which reveals how guitar-like Falla's thinking was.","Classical listeners found it surprising and flamenco listeners found it scholarly; both camps gradually warmed to it. Some critics feel the arrangements lose Falla's harmonic subtlety; others hear them as proof of the music's roots. It is now seen as an important bridge between concert and flamenco worlds.","Play Falla's own El amor brujo first, ideally a recording with a flamenco-trained mezzo-soprano, then this. Listen for what disappears, the orchestral colour and the harmonic shading, and what appears, the stamping rhythm of dance. Then hear Miles Davis's Sketches of Spain for a third reading of Spanish sources, this time through jazz harmony and Gil Evans's orchestra."],"paco-de-lucia-friday-night-in-san-francisco":["A live concert by three acoustic guitarists: Paco de Lucía, John McLaughlin and Al Di Meola. They play duets and trios in which flamenco, jazz and rock-fusion technique collide, trading breakneck runs and quotations. The atmosphere is a friendly contest, with the audience cheering each exchange. The marker is Mediterranean Sundance, a Di Meola and de Lucía duet built on a rumba rhythm.","Recorded at the Warfield Theatre in San Francisco in December 1980 and released in 1981. McLaughlin had led the Mahavishnu Orchestra and the Indian-jazz group Shakti; Di Meola had come from Chick Corea's Return to Forever. Paco had been drawn to jazz since the 1970s, and this tour gave flamenco a vast new audience in the jazz and rock world.","Each guitarist has a different rhythmic mother tongue: Paco's is the flamenco compás, McLaughlin's the swing and Indian cycles, Di Meola's the straight rock beat. Listen for moments where they briefly lock and then pull apart. There are also comic quotations of familiar tunes; the jokes are part of the show.","It became one of the best-selling acoustic guitar albums ever made, and a legend among guitarists. Some critics find it too much about speed and competition; others hear it as joyful. Paco's own later groups took the jazz lesson further, with flute, sax and bass.","Choose one player and follow him through a whole track, then play it again and switch to another. Listen especially for Paco's rasgueado bursts, the fanned strums of the right hand, which neither of the others can make. Afterwards hear Shakti with John McLaughlin, a gentler cross-cultural encounter, and Anouar Brahem's Thimar for the quiet opposite of this contest."],"enrique-morente-omega":["A flamenco singer and a Granada noise-rock band, Lagartija Nick, setting Lorca's Poet in New York and songs by Leonard Cohen. Morente's voice, schooled in the oldest cante forms, rides over distorted guitars, heavy drums and flamenco guitars. The sound is harsh, grand and often thrilling. The marker is the collision in the first minutes: a flamenco cry against a wall of rock guitar.","Morente, from Granada, was a scholarly singer who knew the old forms thoroughly and also sought out avant-garde encounters. Lorca had written Poet in New York during his visit to the city in 1929 and 1930, poems of alienation. Leonard Cohen revered Lorca and had named his daughter after him; the album brought the circle together.","Cohen's songs, including Take This Waltz, itself Cohen's version of a Lorca poem, are sung in Spanish, so Lorca returns to his own language via a Canadian. Listen for the way Morente keeps the melismatic style of cante jondo even when the rhythm is a rock beat. The album is long, and deliberately unrelenting.","It was initially met with confusion and hostility from both flamenco and rock audiences. It is now regarded as one of the key Spanish records of the 1990s and has been reissued and restaged. Critics place it with La leyenda del tiempo as a turning point in flamenco's modern history.","Start with the Leonard Cohen songs, which you may already know in English, and listen for how Morente bends their melodies into flamenco ornament. Then work back to the Lorca settings, where the rock band is at its loudest. Compare Camarón's La leyenda del tiempo to hear how far the experiment travelled in seventeen years, and Crumb's Lorca settings for a concert-hall answer."],"markos-vamvakaris-frangosyriani":["A love song to a Catholic girl from the island of Syros, sung by Markos Vamvakaris in his cracked, gravel voice over his own bouzouki. The rhythm is the hasapiko, a steady, lilting dance, and the bouzouki plays spare melodic figures between verses. The marker is the rough, unpolished sound of voice and bouzouki together, closer to a tavern than a studio.","Markos, born on Syros in 1905, worked in Piraeus as a butcher and dockworker and learned the bouzouki in the hashish dens of the port. Rebetiko, the urban music of the Greek underclass, had absorbed the Anatolian music of refugees expelled from Turkey after 1922. His recordings from the 1930s were the first to make the bouzouki the centre of the style.","The girl is Catholic because Syros had a long-established Catholic community, a detail that marks the song as local and specific. Listen for the bouzouki's melodic figures, which use modes the rebetes called dromoi, close cousins of Turkish makams and Arab maqamat. The rough recording quality is part of the tradition's character.","It is one of the most famous rebetiko songs and still widely sung. Under the Metaxas dictatorship of the late 1930s rebetiko was censored for its drug references and Eastern sound; Markos's reputation recovered in the revival of the 1960s, and he is now called the patriarch of rebetiko.","Listen first for the bouzouki between the verses, then for Markos's phrasing: he often half-speaks the words rather than singing them, like a blues singer. The rough sound is not a flaw. Then compare Tsitsanis's Cloudy Sunday to hear the style refined a decade later, and hear an old recording of Misirlou for the Anatolian modes both men inherited."],"tsitsanis-synnefiasmeni-kyriaki":["Cloudy Sunday: a slow zeibekiko, the heavy, limping dance in nine beats, in which the singer compares the grey day to his own heart. The melody is simple and grave, with the bouzouki carrying the introduction and the voices entering in a mournful unison. The marker is the dragging, uneven rhythm and the bouzouki's clear, bell-like tone.","Vassilis Tsitsanis, born in Trikala in 1915, wrote the song during the German occupation of Greece, in Thessaloniki, and recorded it in 1948 once the recording industry had revived. He refined rebetiko's rough tavern sound into something more melodic and widely acceptable, bringing it to a mass audience. Greece was then in the middle of its civil war.","Written under occupation, the song's grey Sunday was heard as a veiled picture of the occupied country. Listen for the zeibekiko's nine beats, grouped unevenly, which give the song its reeling gait. The dance was traditionally danced solo by a man, improvised and introspective, not for an audience.","It became perhaps the most famous rebetiko song and a kind of unofficial anthem of the occupation years. Tsitsanis is credited with making rebetiko respectable; some scholars argue he smoothed away its rawness, while others see him as its greatest composer. Many later singers have recorded the song.","Try counting the nine beats as you listen, feeling how they fall into uneven groups that make the dance lurch and recover. Then find a film of a zeibekiko being danced, arms spread, eyes down. Compare Markos's Frangosyriani to hear the rougher earlier style, and a Turkish folk song in an uneven rhythm to hear its Anatolian cousins."],"misirlou-1927":["The title means Egyptian girl, and the song is a love song to one. Its melody, built on a mode with an augmented second, the wide, exotic-sounding gap that Western ears hear as Middle Eastern, climbs and falls in a slow, swaying dance. The earliest known recording is a Greek version made in the United States in 1927, sung with a small ensemble.","The song circulated among Greek, Arab, Jewish and Armenian musicians in the Eastern Mediterranean and in the immigrant communities of New York. Its origin is unknown; several claims of authorship exist. Recorded first as a Greek rebetiko by Tetos Demetriades, it was later arranged for big band and taken up by Jewish klezmer and belly-dance musicians.","In 1962 the surf guitarist Dick Dale, of Lebanese descent, recorded a thunderous electric version, and in 1994 Quentin Tarantino used that version at the start of Pulp Fiction. So one Eastern Mediterranean mode crosses from a New York rebetiko session to Californian surf and a Hollywood title sequence. Listen for the augmented second in each version.","It is among the most widely travelled melodies of the twentieth century, and the arguments over its origin remain unsettled. Scholars use it as a case study in how songs move between communities. Its ubiquity in film has made it a cliché of exotic sound, which some critics see as a loss of its real history.","Collect three versions: an early Greek recording from the late 1920s, an Arab or klezmer version with clarinet or violin, and Dick Dale's surf guitar from 1962. Listen for the wide augmented-second step in each, the same interval played on bouzouki, clarinet and a reverb-drenched electric guitar. Then compare Rimsky-Korsakov's Scheherazade, a European fantasy built on similar sounds."],"itri-segah-tekbir":["The tekbir is the proclamation of God's greatness, sung in Islamic worship. Itri's setting is in the makam segâh, a mode built around a note that sits between the Western semitones, which gives it a floating, suspended quality. The melody is sung in unison by male voices, slowly, with long melismatic phrases. The marker is that central note, neither major nor minor, around which the melody circles.","Buhurizade Mustafa Itri (c. 1640–1712) was the leading composer and theorist of the Ottoman court in Istanbul, in the age of Sultan Mehmed IV. He wrote secular and sacred works, many of which survive only in oral tradition. This tekbir is still sung in mosques across Turkey at the Eid festivals, and it is one of the oldest continuously performed pieces in the Ottoman repertoire.","Listen for the segâh note itself: it sits roughly a quarter-tone below a Western E natural and is the reason the mode cannot be played on a standard piano. The melody moves in a very narrow range, so small changes in pitch carry great expressive weight. It is transmitted orally; written versions came much later.","Itri is regarded as the central figure of Ottoman classical music, and this tekbir as his best-known work, so familiar in Turkey that many people who never attend a concert know it by heart. Scholars debate how accurately his pieces have survived three centuries of oral practice, and some attributions to him are doubtful. It remains part of living worship rather than concert repertoire, which is itself unusual for music this old.","Hear it sung by a mosque choir during Eid if you can; otherwise find a recording by a Turkish religious ensemble. Listen for the central note and how it seems to hover. Then compare Gregorian chant and Scelsi's Quattro pezzi, other music built on one pitch's nuances."],"dede-efendi-ferahfeza-ayin":["An âyin is the music for the Mevlevi ceremony known in the West as the whirling of the dervishes. It unfolds in four sections, the selams, each slower and then faster, while the dervishes turn. The ney, an end-blown reed flute, opens with an improvised prelude, then voices sing Rumi's poetry over the small kettle drums. The marker is the ney's breathy, hollow tone, which the Mevlevis hear as the soul longing for God.","Hamamizade İsmail Dede Efendi (1778–1846) was the leading Ottoman composer of his time and a Mevlevi dervish himself, active at the court of Mahmud II during a period of Westernising reform. The Mevlevi order, founded after Rumi's death in 1273, used music as a path to God. This âyin, probably one of his late works, uses the makam ferahfeza.","Listen for the rhythmic cycles: the drums play long patterns, some many beats long, that repeat through each section. The ney's pitch bends, controlled by the angle of breath, give it the microtonal intervals of makam. The four selams correspond to stages of the soul's journey in Mevlevi thought.","Dede Efendi's âyins are considered the high point of the Mevlevi repertoire. After 1925 the Turkish Republic closed the Sufi lodges, and the ceremony survived as a cultural performance; scholars debate how the music changed as it moved from worship to stage. It is now inscribed as intangible heritage.","Watch a film of the ceremony first, so you can picture the white skirts turning, then listen to the music alone with your eyes closed. Follow the ney through its opening improvisation and notice how the drums enter beneath it. Then compare qawwali, another Sufi music, which drives toward ecstasy where this one aims at stillness."],"tanburi-cemil-bey-recordings":["Improvised preludes, the taksims, and composed pieces played on the tanbur, a long-necked lute, and on the kemençe, a small bowed fiddle. The playing is fast, fluent and emotionally direct, moving through makam after makam with extraordinary control of the microtones. The recordings are crackly 78s; the voice of the instrument still comes through. The marker is the kemençe's sharp, singing tone.","Tanburi Cemil Bey (1873–1916) lived through the last decades of the Ottoman Empire in Istanbul and was the most celebrated instrumentalist of his age. European gramophone companies had set up in the city to record local music for local buyers, and he made many sides for them in the years just before the First World War. He was also a theorist and wrote a treatise on Ottoman instruments; he died young, in 1916.","Listen for the microtonal inflections in the taksims: Cemil Bey slides between pitches that fall between Western semitones, the heart of the makam system. His improvisations on these records were widely copied and memorised by later players. He also played the yaylı tanbur, a bowed version of the lute, which he popularised.","These recordings are now considered among the most important documents of Ottoman classical music, and players still learn his taksims by ear from them. They are also a lesson in what early recording preserved and lost: the three-minute limit of a 78 forced him to compress improvisations that in a salon might have run far longer. Their survival owes much to collectors and to careful modern reissues.","Choose a kemençe taksim first, since the bowed fiddle's singing line is the most direct way in. Then try a tanbur piece and listen for the long decaying notes. Compare Hindustani raga recordings of roughly the same era, and then Partch or La Monte Young, Western composers who went looking for the notes between the piano keys that Cemil Bey took for granted."],"asik-veysel-uzun-ince-bir-yoldayim":["I Am on a Long and Narrow Road: a philosophical song in which life is a journey between two doors, walked day and night. Veysel sings in a plain, steady voice, accompanying himself on the bağlama, whose strummed and plucked strings drone under the melody. The melody is simple and repeated for each verse. The marker is the bağlama's buzzing ring and the singer's calm, unhurried delivery.","Âşık Veysel Şatıroğlu (1894–1973), blind from childhood smallpox, came from a village near Sivas in central Anatolia. The âşıks are wandering minstrel-poets, heirs to a tradition going back centuries. In the 1930s and 1940s the Turkish Republic promoted folk culture, and Veysel taught at the Village Institutes; this song, probably composed earlier, became famous through recordings around mid-century.","The imagery of the road between two doors, birth and death, comes from the Alevi and Sufi traditions that shaped Anatolian folk poetry. Listen for the bağlama's frets, which include microtonal positions allowing the makam intervals. Veysel's steady rhythm is closer to recitation than to song.","It is one of the most famous Turkish folk songs, taught in schools and covered by rock, pop and jazz musicians. Veysel is regarded as the greatest âşık of the twentieth century, and his house near Sivas is now a museum. Some scholars question the way the young Republic's cultural policy turned him into a national symbol, smoothing away the Alevi religious dimension of his poetry.","Read a translation first, then listen without it and let the bağlama's buzzing drone and the steady strum carry you from verse to verse. Look for Veysel's own recordings rather than later pop covers; his plain, unhurried voice is the point. Then compare a Delta bluesman alone with a guitar, or Dylan's early songs: the lone singer-poet facing death is a familiar figure."],"ofra-haza-yemenite-songs":["Songs from the Yemenite Jewish tradition, many setting devotional poems by the seventeenth-century rabbi and poet Shalom Shabazi, sung by the Israeli pop star Ofra Haza. The arrangements combine traditional percussion, including hand drums and struck metal, with strings, keyboards and a modern rhythm section. Haza's voice is bright, agile and richly ornamented. The marker is Im Nin'alu, with its soaring unaccompanied opening phrase before the drums enter.","Haza, born in Tel Aviv in 1957 to parents who had come from Yemen, was already a leading pop singer in Israel when she recorded this album of the songs she had heard at home. Yemenite Jews had arrived in Israel in large numbers around 1949 and 1950, and their music was long treated as marginal beside European-derived culture. Released in 1984, the album later found an international audience through dance remixes.","In 1987 the British duo Coldcut sampled Im Nin'alu for their remix of Eric B. and Rakim's Paid in Full, and the same year M/A/R/R/S used her voice on Pump Up the Volume. A seventeenth-century Hebrew poem became a hip-hop hook. Listen for the ornamentation, which comes from Arabic and Jewish liturgical singing.","It made Haza the best-known Israeli singer internationally and introduced Yemenite Jewish music to millions who had never heard of it. Some critics complained that pop production smoothed the tradition; others credited her with restoring pride in a community's heritage. Her international career continued through the 1990s until her death in 2000, at 42, and her voice remains one of the most recognised samples in dance music.","Start with Im Nin'alu, then play Eric B. and Rakim's Paid in Full in the Coldcut remix and wait for her voice to arrive. The same phrase lives in two worlds. Then look for a traditional Yemenite recording, voices and drum alone, to hear the source, and compare Reich's Tehillim, another modern setting of Hebrew devotional words."],"javanese-gamelan-ketawang-puspawarna":["Puspawarna, 'Kinds of Flowers', is a short piece in ketawang form: a repeating cycle of sixteen beats closed each time by the deep stroke of the great hanging gong. Around a plain melody carried by the bronze metallophones, softer instruments weave their own lines: the bowed rebab, the bamboo flute, the gender with its rippling mallets, and a solo female singer who floats above the beat. Drums steer the tempo; the whole thing breathes rather than marches, slowing into its final gong.","The piece belongs to the court of the Mangkunegaran in Surakarta, central Java, where princes were also poets and patrons of music; its verses are usually credited to Mangkunegara IV, who ruled from 1853. It served to greet the prince. In 1977 a recording made at the Paku Alaman palace in Yogyakarta was chosen for the Golden Record carried by the Voyager space probes, so this court welcome is now leaving the solar system.","Listen to the gongs as a clock rather than as punctuation. The small gongs mark the halves and quarters of each cycle; the great gong arrives only at the end and is felt as arrival, not as a beat. Everything elaborate, including the singer's free-floating line, is aiming at that stroke. The tuning, slendro, divides the octave into five near-equal steps that no piano can play.","Western ears first met Javanese gamelan at the Paris Exposition of 1889, where Debussy heard it and never forgot it; for decades it was admired as exotic colour. Ethnomusicologists since Jaap Kunst and Mantle Hood have insisted on it as a classical system with its own theory, modes and repertory. The attribution to Mangkunegara IV is traditional and probably covers the text more securely than the music.","Find the Voyager recording and simply count: feel the sixteen-beat cycle, wait for the gong, and notice how the drummer draws the ensemble into the final slowdown. Then play Debussy's 'Pagodes' from Estampes and hear what a Parisian piano made of these layered bells. A full palace ensemble on a good modern recording shows the soft instruments best."],"balinese-kecak":["Kecak has no instruments at all. A large circle of bare-chested men sits around a flickering lamp and chants the syllable 'cak' in interlocking patterns, each group placed a fraction off the others, so that the sound becomes a single seething rhythm faster than any one person could produce. A leader keeps time with a hissed pulse, a singer intones a melody, and within the circle dancers enact episodes from the Ramayana: the abduction of Sita, the monkey army, the battle.","The chorus comes from sanghyang, a trance ritual in which the men's chant supported entranced dancers. In the early 1930s the dancer Wayan Limbak and the German painter Walter Spies, living in Bali, reshaped it into a staged drama with a Ramayana story, partly for the film 'Insel der Dämonen' and for the island's first tourists. It was, in effect, a modern invention built from ritual parts.","The chant is the gamelan translated into voices. The 'cak' groups reproduce the interlocking kotekan of Balinese metallophones, in which two parts each play only some of the notes and together make a continuous stream; the hissing leader stands where the gong-keeper would. Once you hear it as an orchestra of mouths, the circle's geometry makes sense.","Kecak has been accused of being a tourist confection, and it is certainly younger than its reputation. Scholars now treat it as a case study in how tradition is made: Balinese performers adopted it, varied it and made it their own, and villages still compete to perform it. Its sound reached Western composers and film-makers long before most of them knew its history.","Watch a filmed performance rather than just listen: the circle, the waving hands and the lamp are half of it. Then focus on one layer, perhaps the hissing leader, and let the rest swirl round it. Afterwards try Reich's Clapping Music, which makes a whole piece from two parts sliding against each other."],"balinese-legong-lasem":["Two young dancers in gold brocade move in exact mirror image, eyes flicking, fans quivering, to a gamelan that races and stops on a hair. The Lasem story tells of a king who abducts a princess, and of the bird of ill omen that warns him before his death in battle; a third dancer, the attendant, frames the tale. The music runs in sections of shifting speed, from glittering fast figuration to slow, suspended passages where the dancers barely move.","Legong grew up in the princely courts of south Bali, traditionally danced by girls before adolescence; one legend places its origin in a prince's dream at Sukawati early in the nineteenth century. Its orchestra, the gamelan pelegongan, is lighter than the processional ensembles. In the 1930s the Canadian composer Colin McPhee lived in Bali, transcribing this repertory, and later played his transcriptions with Benjamin Britten.","Two paired drummers, not a conductor, lead everything; the dancers' sudden freezes and head-snaps are cued by drum strokes. Listen too to the fast metallophone figuration: it is split between two players, one taking the on-beat notes and the other the off-beat notes, so that neither alone plays the tune. The instruments are also tuned in pairs slightly apart, making the whole ensemble shimmer.","European visitors in the 1920s and 1930s made legong the emblem of 'unspoiled Bali', a view that says more about them than about Bali. Balinese musicians have kept developing it, and the post-war kebyar style changed its speed and brilliance. Britten's visit in 1956 left its mark on his ballet The Prince of the Pagodas, and Reich's study of Balinese gamelan fed his own pulse music.","Seek out film, since the dance and music are inseparable. Start by watching the drummers' cues land in the dancers' bodies. Then close your eyes during a fast section and try to follow a single metallophone. Compare Britten's gamelan scenes in The Prince of the Pagodas and hear how much, and how little, an orchestra can borrow."],"gagaku-etenraku":["Etenraku is the best-known piece of gagaku, the court orchestra music of Japan. Its sound is unmistakable: the shō, a mouth organ of seventeen bamboo pipes, holds shimmering clusters of notes that swell and fade with the player's breath; the hichiriki, a short, piercing double reed, sings the melody with slides between pitches; a transverse flute doubles it. Lute and zither mark the beats with sparse plucked patterns, and a small drum and gong frame the slow cycle.","Gagaku arrived from Tang China and Korea in the seventh and eighth centuries and was organised at the Heian court in Kyoto into the form it has kept. Etenraku belongs to the tōgaku, 'music of Tang', repertory. It has been played by hereditary court musicians for more than a thousand years, and the Imperial Household Agency's music department in Tokyo still performs it.","The tempo is not fixed: the piece starts almost without pulse and gradually settles, and the gap before each drum stroke stretches in a way Western musicians find hard to copy. The shō's chords are not harmony in the Western sense but a sustained halo; they change a moment before the melody does. A popular folk song, Kuroda-bushi, is probably descended from a sung version of this melody.","Western composers heard gagaku as sound rather than tune. Hidemaro Konoye arranged Etenraku for Western orchestra in 1931, and Messiaen imitated its textures in Sept haïkaï after visiting Japan. Scholars such as Laurence Picken have argued that the modern slow tempo is itself a historical change, and that in Tang times this music ran several times faster.","Listen first only to the shō: follow its breathing clusters and let the melody pass over them. Then pick out the hichiriki's slides. Play Messiaen's 'Gagaku' movement next and hear strings and oboes impersonating these instruments. A recording by the Imperial Household ensemble is the reference."],"shakuhachi-shika-no-tone":["Two bamboo flutes call to each other across an imagined autumn valley, as a stag and a doe answer one another from distant hills. Each phrase is a single breath: a note swells from breathy noise into tone, bends down, flares up, and dies away. The second flute answers, sometimes overlapping, and gradually the calls come closer until the two voices meet and sound together. There is no pulse, only breath and the silence between.","Honkyoku, 'original pieces', were the spiritual repertory of the komusō, the begging monks of the Fuke sect of Zen who wandered Edo-period Japan in basket hats, playing the shakuhachi as meditation rather than performance. Kurosawa Kinko collected and codified many of these pieces in the eighteenth century, and his school still teaches them. When the sect was abolished in 1871, the music passed to lay players.","The 'imperfections' are the point. The rushing air of muraiki, where the breath is deliberately audible, and the pitch shading made by tilting the head, are techniques, not faults. So is the silence: the Japanese idea of ma treats the gap between phrases as part of the music, as charged as the notes. Listen for how long the silence lasts, not just the sound.","Shakuhachi players dispute whether honkyoku is music at all or suizen, 'blowing Zen'. Western interest grew after the war, fed by Zen's vogue among artists such as Cage. Takemitsu's November Steps in 1967 put shakuhachi and biwa in front of the New York Philharmonic and made the instrument's sound famous; purists argue over whether that helped or diluted the tradition.","Listen in a quiet room, at low volume, and do not wait for a tune. Follow one flute's breath from start to end, then notice how the other answers. Afterwards hear the shakuhachi in Takemitsu's November Steps and consider what an orchestra adds, and takes away."],"yatsuhashi-kengyo-rokudan-no-shirabe":["Rokudan, 'six steps', is the most famous piece for solo koto. It is built from six sections of equal length, each a variation on the same underlying frame, and it gets steadily faster from the first to the last, before a sudden slowing at the very end. The sound is plucked and bright; the left hand presses the strings behind the movable bridges to bend a note or add a quiet sigh after it is struck.","The piece is credited to Yatsuhashi Kengyō, a blind musician who reached the highest rank of the guild of blind musicians and worked in Kyoto in the seventeenth century. He is also credited with the hirajōshi tuning heard here, which gave koto music its characteristic mix of wide and very narrow steps. Instrumental pieces of this kind, danmono, were rare; most koto music accompanied song.","The acceleration follows jo-ha-kyū, the Japanese principle of a slow opening, a development and a rush to the close found in Noh and the tea ceremony alike. Each section also has the same fixed number of beats, the first slightly longer, so the structure is almost mathematical. One scholar has even proposed that the piece descends from a Gregorian Credo brought by Jesuit missionaries, a hypothesis most specialists doubt.","Every koto student learns Rokudan, and it has been recorded countless times, sometimes with shakuhachi or a second koto added. The attribution to Yatsuhashi is traditional rather than documentary, and the piece may have been shaped by several generations of blind masters. The musicologist Tatsuo Minagawa's suggestion of Christian roots has kept it unusually visible in Western scholarship, even among those who reject it.","Count the six sections by listening for the return of the opening shape, and feel each one moving a little faster. Listen for the bent notes after the pluck, the koto's signature. Then hear Miyagi's Haru no Umi, which shows how far the koto travelled in two and a half centuries."],"miyagi-michio-haru-no-umi":["A duet in three parts. The koto sets up a gently rocking ripple like small waves, and the shakuhachi floats a long melody over it, with gull calls and the creak of oars suggested in passing. A faster central section brings livelier interplay, the two instruments tossing figures back and forth, before the calm opening music returns. It is short, tuneful and immediately pictorial.","Miyagi wrote it in 1929 after a visit to the coast of the Inland Sea, and it was introduced to the wider world when the French violinist Renée Chemet, touring Japan, recorded a version with him on violin. Miyagi was a blind koto master who had grown up on Western music as well as Japanese, and led the Shin Nihon Ongaku, 'New Japanese Music', movement of the 1920s.","Listen to the koto's accompaniment: it behaves like a Western piano part, with repeated patterns and a sense of chords, something the older koto repertory avoids. The form, slow-fast-slow with a return, is Western too. Yet the melodic bends and the shakuhachi's breathy attacks remain wholly Japanese. The piece is a quiet argument that the two need not be enemies.","Traditionalists found Miyagi too Western; modernists later found him too sweet. The public never wavered: Haru no Umi is played on Japanese television and in shops every New Year and is probably the best-known Japanese instrumental piece at home. Recent writers treat Miyagi as a genuine reformer, and his invention of a seventeen-string bass koto is still in use.","Hear the koto and shakuhachi version first, then the violin version, and decide which blends better with the plucked strings. Picture the scene as you go: waves, boats, birds. Afterwards try Rokudan for the older koto style, and Takemitsu's November Steps for a very different, far harsher fusion of East and West."],"guqin-liu-shui":["The guqin is a long, low zither played on a table, its silk or metal strings producing a soft, woody sound with sliding tones and bell-like harmonics. Liu Shui paints water from mountain spring to river: quiet harmonics like droplets, then a famous central passage where the player's fingers roll and sweep rapidly across the strings to make a torrent, before the music widens into a calm current and fades.","The piece is tied to the ancient legend of the qin player Bo Ya and his friend Zhong Ziqi, who understood whether Bo Ya was thinking of mountains or of water; when Zhong died, Bo Ya broke his instrument. The tablature first appears in the Ming prince Zhu Quan's collection of 1425. The torrent section was added by the Sichuan player Zhang Kongshan in the nineteenth century.","Qin notation records strings, fingers and positions but hardly any rhythm, so every player must reconstruct the time, a practice called dapu. No two versions agree. The recording by Guan Pinghu was chosen for the Voyager Golden Record in 1977, alongside a Javanese gamelan piece, so this music about friends who understood each other is addressed to whoever finds it.","The qin was the instrument of scholars and officials, played for oneself or a friend rather than an audience, and Confucian writers considered it a moral discipline. In the twentieth century it nearly died out; recordings by Guan Pinghu and others preserved it. UNESCO recognised qin music in 2003, and there are now more players than at any time in the last century.","Find Guan Pinghu's recording and listen at low volume, as if in a small study. Wait for the torrent in the middle, then notice how quiet the ending is. Then hear Chou Wen-chung's Yü Ko, which transfers qin gestures to a Western ensemble."],"pipa-shimian-maifu":["A battle told on one lute. The pipa player musters the troops with drum-like strumming, lays out the army, springs the ambush, and then unleashes the fight: bursts of rapid tremolo, strings pinched together to make a buzzing clash, sharp cracks like arrows and clattering like hooves. The last sections describe the defeated general's flight and death. It is virtuoso programme music, loud and physical.","The story is the battle of Gaixia in 202 BC, where Liu Bang, founder of the Han dynasty, trapped his rival Xiang Yu. The music was printed in 1818 in a pipa collection edited by Hua Qiuping, though it is surely older. The pipa was the instrument of entertainers and courtesans rather than scholars, which is why its repertory is showier than that of the qin.","The clashing sound in the battle section comes from crossing two strings over each other with the left hand and striking them together, a technique almost unique to this piece. The whole work is a catalogue of pipa effects, and its section titles work like a newspaper account. The same battle ends in the Peking opera Farewell My Concubine, told from the loser's side.","Every pipa virtuoso records it; it is the instrument's calling card, and players compete in speed and violence. Some critics find it crude beside the reflective civil pieces such as Moonlight on the Spring River. Its fame reached cinema: Zhang Yimou's film House of Flying Daggers takes its Chinese title from the piece.","Read the section titles first, then follow the story as it unfolds. Listen for the crossed-string clash and for the sudden quiet of the final death scene. Then hear the pipa in Tan Dun's Ghost Opera, which places the same instrument beside a string quartet in a very different world."],"abing-erquan-yingyue":["A single erhu, the two-string fiddle held upright on the knee, plays a long, sorrowing melody that keeps returning, each time a little varied and more intense. The tone is thin and vocal, full of slides and small ornaments; there is no accompaniment, only the melody and the bow's grain. It is less a showpiece than a monologue, with the dignity of someone who has had a hard life.","Hua Yanjun, known as Abing, was a Taoist musician who went blind and lived as a street performer in Wuxi. In 1950 the musicologists Yang Yinliu and Cao Anhe recorded him on a wire recorder; he died that year. The title names a spring at Huishan in Wuxi, and was given at the recording session. The piece became a national symbol in the new People's Republic.","Only six pieces by Abing were recorded, and the recording itself is rough, with the wire's hiss audible. Listen to how freely he treats the pulse, lingering on some notes and hurrying others; later orchestral arrangements tend to smooth this out. The conductor Seiji Ozawa is reported to have said the piece should be heard kneeling.","Erquan Yingyue became a set piece for every erhu student and was arranged for Chinese orchestra, strings and even Western orchestras. Some scholars note that its 'suffering' was shaped by the political reading given to Abing's life, the blind poor man as victim of the old society. The original recording remains the benchmark.","Hear the 1950 recording first, however worn, and follow the returning melody. Then compare a modern erhu virtuoso and notice what polish adds or removes. Afterwards try Chen Gang's Butterfly Lovers Concerto for the Chinese melodic style in a Western frame."],"peking-opera-farewell-my-concubine":["The king of Chu, Xiang Yu, is surrounded; at night he hears his own soldiers' home songs sung by the enemy and knows the war is lost. His consort Yu Ji dances for him with two swords and then takes her own life. The sound is Peking opera's: a high, nasal fiddle, the jinghu, shadowing the voices; a percussion band of clappers, drums and gongs that marks every gesture; and stylised singing, the woman's role in a high falsetto.","Mei Lanfang, the greatest performer of female roles, created the opera in 1922 with the actor Yang Xiaolou, reshaping an older play. Mei's tours to Japan, the United States in 1930 and the Soviet Union in 1935 introduced Peking opera abroad, where Brecht and Eisenstein saw him. The sword dance became his signature, and the play Peking opera's best-known work.","The percussion leader, on a small drum, is the real conductor, cueing the other players and the actors' movements with strokes too fast to count. Many gongs mark an entrance, a thought, or a decision. Listen also for the change between the two main melodic systems, xipi and erhuang, which carry different moods. The whole play is the other side of the pipa piece Ambush from Ten Sides.","Peking opera was attacked as feudal in the 1960s and replaced by the 'model operas' of the Cultural Revolution, then revived. Chen Kaige's 1993 film Farewell My Concubine, about two actors who play Xiang Yu and Yu Ji through fifty years of Chinese history, brought the opera a global audience. UNESCO recognised Peking opera in 2010.","Watch a filmed performance, ideally with subtitles, and focus on Yu Ji's sword dance. Notice how each gesture is cued by the drum. Then see Chen Kaige's film, and compare Puccini's Turandot, a Western dream of China that could hardly be further from this."],"kunqu-peony-pavilion":["Du Liniang, a sheltered girl, wanders into her family's garden in spring, falls asleep and dreams of a young scholar; she wakes into longing, wastes away and dies. Years later the scholar finds her portrait, and her love brings her back from the dead. The music is Kunqu, older and softer than Peking opera: a bamboo flute, the dizi, leads, and the singing is slow and highly ornamented, with long melismas on single syllables.","Tang Xianzu wrote the play in 1598, in fifty-five scenes, in the late Ming dynasty; it is exactly contemporary with the first operas in Florence. Kunqu, from the Suzhou area, was the elite theatre of the Ming and Qing. A full performance takes about twenty hours, so it is usually staged in selections, above all the garden and dream scenes.","The rhythm of the singing follows the tones of the Chinese words, so the melody is partly dictated by the language. Listen also for the clapper: it marks strong beats, but the singer can stretch time against it. The dream scene's flower spirits were a Ming device for showing an inner state on stage, a solution to a problem Western opera solved with arias.","The play was a sensation in its own time; some readers were said to die of love for it. It was banned or censored at various points for its frank desire. Kunqu was listed by UNESCO in 2001, the first such listing, and the writer Pai Hsien-yung's 2004 'youth version' drew young audiences. Peter Sellars's 1998 staging with Tan Dun's music reached the West.","Start with the 'Interrupted Dream' scene on film. Listen to the flute and the way the voice lingers on each syllable. Then read the plot and watch the resurrection. Compare Monteverdi's Orfeo, written within a decade and also about love beyond death, to see two civilisations inventing opera at once."],"pansori-chunhyangga":["One singer, holding a fan, and one drummer tell a whole novel for up to eight hours. The voice is husky, forced, deliberately rough, swinging from speech to song to cries; the drummer on a barrel drum marks rhythmic cycles and calls out encouragement. The story: Chunhyang, a courtesan's daughter, secretly marries a nobleman's son who leaves for Seoul; she refuses a corrupt governor and is jailed, until her husband returns as a royal inspector.","Pansori grew up in the eighteenth century among travelling performers of southwestern Korea and rose to aristocratic and court patronage in the nineteenth, when the scholar Shin Jae-hyo wrote down and polished the texts. Chunhyangga is one of five surviving stories of an older twelve. Singers train for decades, traditionally by shouting against waterfalls, to build the voice's rasp. UNESCO recognised pansori in 2003.","The drummer is not an accompanist but a partner: his shouts of encouragement, chuimsae, are part of the performance, and audiences join in. The drum cycles move from the very slow jinyang to rapid patterns as the story speeds up. Singers belong to lineages and claim the particular versions of their teachers, so the 'work' exists in schools, not in one fixed text.","Once a low-status art, pansori became a national emblem in modern Korea. The first performance of a full story in one sitting was given only in 1968, by Park Dong-jin. Im Kwon-taek's 2000 film Chunhyang, built around a pansori performance, brought the form to Cannes. Debate continues over preserving old lineages versus creating new stories.","Start with a short excerpt, such as the prison lament or the love song, rather than the whole. Listen for the drummer's shouts and the rasp in the voice. Then compare Schubert's Winterreise: another single voice with one accompanist carrying a long story."],"gayageum-sanjo":["Sanjo, 'scattered melodies', is a suite for a solo instrument with drum, and on the gayageum it is the most celebrated form of Korean instrumental music. It begins in a very slow rhythmic cycle, with long, bent notes, and passes through several movements that get faster and faster, until the last section rattles along at speed. The player presses the strings behind the bridges to make notes waver, swell and fall.","Sanjo arose in southwestern Korea in the late nineteenth century, drawing on the improvised music of shaman rituals and on pansori; Kim Chang-jo is credited with its first form. Later masters developed their own versions, called ryu, and students learn a master's version note for note. The Japanese occupation and the war both nearly broke the lineages; they were rebuilt after 1960.","Behind every left-hand bend lies the singing voice: sanjo imitates pansori's range of expression, including its sobs and cries. Listen to the drummer too: the janggu's two heads, one high and one low, mark the rhythmic cycles, and the sense of acceleration comes as much from them as from the strings. Each ryu shows its master's personality.","Sanjo is seen in Korea as the peak of the classical instrumental tradition, though its origin is in the popular rather than the court world. Composers such as Isang Yun and Hwang Byungki drew on its expressive single tones. Scholars debate whether its fixed versions have lost the improvisation that gave birth to them.","Hear the whole thing through at least once to feel the acceleration. Listen for the left-hand bends in the slow opening. Then try Rokudan on the Japanese koto, a related zither with a similar speeding structure, and Isang Yun's music for how the sound moved into the modern concert hall."],"samulnori-uttari-pungmul":["Four players sit on the floor with four instruments: the kkwaenggwari, a small brass gong that cuts through everything; the jing, a large gong that booms and hums; the janggu, an hourglass drum; and the buk, a barrel drum. They start slowly and build through a series of rhythmic cycles to a crashing climax. The small gong leads, changing patterns with signals, and the others follow.","Samulnori was founded in Seoul in February 1978 by Kim Duk-soo and three colleagues, most from families of itinerant performers, who took the outdoor farmers' band music, pungmul, and made it a seated concert piece. Pungmul had accompanied village festivals, rituals and communal labour, and rapid urbanisation under military rule was destroying it. 'Uttari' refers to the central region whose rhythms this piece gathers.","The four instruments stand for weather: the small gong is thunder, the big gong wind, the hourglass drum rain, and the barrel drum clouds. Listen to how the players breathe together: Korean rhythm is often in groups of three, and the swing comes from the lengthening and shortening of the beat, not from a straight pulse.","Samulnori was an immediate success, and the group's name became the name of a genre practised by thousands of amateur and school groups. Some traditionalists objected that a moving, outdoor village practice had been frozen into a concert item, but it arguably saved the rhythms from oblivion. The group went on to collaborate with jazz musicians and Western percussion ensembles.","Watch a video first to see how the small gong leads with signals the others answer. Feel the three-beat swing under the surface, and notice the long build to the climax. Then compare Reich's Drumming, which also grows long percussion processes, and the Balinese kecak, another ensemble driven by interlocking parts."],"jongmyo-jeryeak":["Two ensembles, one on the upper terrace and one in the courtyard below, alternate through the day-long ritual at the Jongmyo Shrine in Seoul, honouring the kings of Joseon. The music is slow and grave: bronze bells and stone chimes hung in frames, the double-reed piri and the transverse flute, the bowed zither ajaeng, and singers chanting texts of praise. Lines of dancers move in formal patterns.","King Sejong, who also created the Korean alphabet, is credited with the two suites, Botaepyeong and Jeongdaeeop, composed for court banquets around 1447; his son King Sejo adapted them for the ancestral rite in 1464. The rite has been performed ever since, with interruptions during invasions and the colonial period, and is now given each May. UNESCO recognised the rite and its music in 2001.","Every piece begins and ends with the bak, a wooden clapper made of six slats, whose single crack is the signal to start or stop. Isang Yun used this sound in Réak. Listen too to the bells and stones: they are tuned in sets, and the melody moves between them, rather than as harmony.","The music is counted among the oldest orchestral traditions still performed anywhere, comparable only to Japanese gagaku. Scholars debate how far present practice reflects the fifteenth century, since invasions, the fall of the dynasty and the Japanese occupation all broke the chain of performers. The rite has also become a public spectacle in Seoul, which some see as a gain and some as a loss.","Watch a film of the ritual to see the two ensembles and the dancers. Listen for the clapper at the start and end, and the ring of the bells and stones. Then hear Isang Yun's Réak for the same sound world in a modern orchestra."],"misora-hibari-kawa-no-nagare-no-yo-ni":["A slow ballad in which Misora Hibari, at the end of a forty-year career, looks back on life as a river that flows on whatever happens. The arrangement is plain, strings and piano growing gently behind her, and the voice does the work: low and controlled, with a deep vibrato and the small catches in the tone that marked her style. It is more a quiet reckoning than a performance.","Released in January 1989, with words by Yasushi Akimoto and music by Akira Mitake, it was the last single she released in her lifetime; she died that June at 52. Misora had been Japan's biggest singer since she was a child star in the late 1940s, a symbol of the post-war years. The song was a farewell before anyone knew it.","Misora was the queen of enka, the sentimental ballad genre with its sobbing ornaments, but this song is not enka: its melody is closer to Western pop balladry. Listen for how she lets some of her enka habits in, a slight catch here, a bend there, and holds others back. It sums up her career by crossing its genres.","The song became one of the most loved in Japan, a regular at the top of polls of the greatest Japanese songs and a staple of karaoke and funerals alike, and it has been covered by many singers. Misora received the People's Honour Award posthumously in 1989, the first woman to do so. Critics now hear it as the close of the Shōwa era, which ended the same month it was released.","Listen once through without distraction, following only the voice and the way it holds back. Then compare one of Misora's earlier enka recordings to hear the style she was stepping away from. Afterwards try Teresa Teng's 'The Moon Represents My Heart', a ballad by East Asia's other great popular voice."],"kyu-sakamoto-ue-o-muite-aruko":["A bright, bouncing tune with a whistled refrain, sung by Kyu Sakamoto in a light voice with a gentle catch. The words describe someone walking with his head up so that his tears will not fall, remembering happier days; the cheerfulness of the music and the sadness of the words pull against each other. The arrangement is a small orchestra with strings and a steady swing.","Written by the lyricist Rokusuke Ei and the composer Hachidai Nakamura, it was released in 1961 and became a hit in Japan. A British label renamed it 'Sukiyaki', after a beef dish, since English speakers could not manage the title, and an instrumental cover charted in Britain. In June 1963 Sakamoto's original reached number one in the American charts, the first Asian-language song to do so.","Ei is said to have written the words after the defeat of the 1960 protests against the US–Japan security treaty, which would make it a political song hidden as a love song. The melody uses the Japanese pentatonic scale, so it sounds both folkish and modern. Sakamoto died in a plane crash in 1985.","The English title has been criticised as trivialising, a tourist menu pinned on a sad song, but the record opened a door for Asian pop in the West. It has been covered many times, including by A Taste of Honey in 1981 with new English words. It remains the only Japanese-language song to top the American charts.","Listen first to the whistle and the bounce, then notice the melancholy underneath them. Compare Elvis Presley's early ballads, which Sakamoto admired and whose vocal hiccup he borrowed. Then hear PSY's 'Gangnam Style', the Korean song that half a century later came closest to repeating the feat."],"mariya-takeuchi-plastic-love":["A mid-tempo disco-funk track with a rubbery bass line, choppy rhythm guitar, bright horns and a sweeping string arrangement, sung by Mariya Takeuchi in a warm, unhurried alto. The song describes a woman who dances and flirts through the city's nightlife to hide a heart broken by an old love, and the music matches her: glossy and danceable on top, melancholy underneath, with a long instrumental coda.","It appeared on Takeuchi's 1984 album Variety, produced and arranged by her husband Tatsuro Yamashita, at the height of Japan's economic boom, and was issued as a twelve-inch single in 1985. City pop, as the style came to be called, was the sound of a newly rich Tokyo: American soul and jazz-funk played by the best studio musicians. It was only a modest hit at the time.","The song's fame came thirty years later: in 2017 an unofficial YouTube upload of the extended version, with a photo of Takeuchi, gathered tens of millions of views, and the song became the emblem of a global city pop revival. Vaporwave and future-funk producers had been sampling such records, and Plastic Love became their anthem.","Critics once dismissed city pop as derivative, a polished copy of American records. The revival changed that, and Plastic Love is now treated as a classic; an official video followed in 2019. Debate continues about the revival's nostalgia for a Japan its new fans never knew, and about whether a recommendation algorithm can be said to have made a hit.","Listen to the extended version, with its long instrumental passages, and follow the bass line from start to finish. Notice how the strings arrive to lift the chorus. Then compare Chic's 'Good Times' and Earth, Wind & Fire's arrangements for the American models, and Yamashita's own album For You for the same studio sound."],"tatsuro-yamashita-for-you":["The album opens with 'Sparkle', a burst of clean, chopping guitar and horns that is the most recognisable sound in city pop. What follows is a set of polished songs moving between funk, soft rock and ballads, with Yamashita's multitracked harmonies and exact arrangements. The album closes with 'Your Eyes', an English-language ballad. The sound is warm, bright and expensive.","Yamashita had come up through the band Sugar Babe in the mid-1970s, whose record came out on Eiichi Ohtaki's label, and by 1982 he was one of Japan's leading songwriters and producers. For You was recorded with his regular band of studio musicians and released early that year. The cover illustration by Eizin Suzuki, a sunny, American-style scene, helped define the look of the genre.","Yamashita's rhythm guitar is the album's engine: he plays tight, muted strokes like a funk guitarist, but with a rock guitarist's attack. Listen too to his a cappella harmonies, often all sung by himself. The American soul influences are real, but the chords and melodic turns are unmistakably Japanese pop.","For You was a major hit in Japan and remains one of Yamashita's best-regarded albums, a reference point for Japanese producers. The city pop revival made it famous abroad, alongside Plastic Love, and it is now frequently reissued on vinyl. Yamashita's long caution about streaming has made his records harder to find outside Japan, which has only sharpened their mystique.","Start with 'Sparkle' and listen only to the guitar for the first minute. Then go through the album and follow the stacked harmonies. Compare the polished jazz-rock of American studio records of the same years, and his wife Mariya Takeuchi's Plastic Love, which he arranged."],"happy-end-kazemachi-roman":["A soft folk-rock album with gently strummed and picked guitars, loose, unhurried drums and the voices of Eiichi Ohtaki and Haruomi Hosono, singing in Japanese. The songs are lyrical and understated, with touches of country and psychedelia and no showing off. 'Kaze wo Atsumete', 'Gathering the Wind', is the best-known track; the others paint a Tokyo of wind, streetcars, summer and memory.","Happy End were Hosono, Ohtaki, the guitarist Shigeru Suzuki and the drummer and lyricist Takashi Matsumoto, and Kazemachi Roman, released in 1971, was their second album. They stood at the centre of a public argument about whether Japanese rock should be sung in Japanese; many insisted only English would do. Matsumoto's 'wind city' was the Tokyo of his childhood, erased by the building boom before the 1964 Olympics.","The band's model was Buffalo Springfield, and the interlocking guitars show it. But the melodies follow the rhythm and accents of spoken Japanese, which was the whole point of the project. 'Kaze wo Atsumete' was used in Sofia Coppola's film Lost in Translation in 2003, which quietly introduced the band to listeners who had never heard of them.","The album was not a big seller, but it is now regarded as the founding record of Japanese-language rock and routinely heads lists of the best Japanese albums. Its members went on to shape Japanese pop: Hosono formed Yellow Magic Orchestra, Ohtaki made the city pop classic A Long Vacation, and Matsumoto became one of the country's most successful lyricists.","Start with 'Kaze wo Atsumete', then listen through as a whole, since the album is built like a walk through the city. Compare The Band and the Byrds for the American sound it adapted. Then hear Yellow Magic Orchestra's first album to see how far Hosono travelled in seven years."],"ryuichi-sakamoto-merry-christmas-mr-lawrence":["The main theme is recognisable in two notes: a delicate, five-note melody in bell-like synthesizer tones over a slow, rippling pattern, rising with a gentle, melancholy lift. The score around it mixes electronic textures with gamelan-like percussion, sampled sounds and passages of near-stillness. The main theme has since become a piano piece in its own right, one of the most played of its era.","Sakamoto, a founder of Yellow Magic Orchestra, wrote the score for Nagisa Oshima's 1983 film about a Japanese prisoner-of-war camp in Java, in which he also acted, playing the camp commandant opposite David Bowie. He had studied composition and ethnomusicology in Tokyo, and had never scored a film. The music won a BAFTA. A vocal version, 'Forbidden Colours', was made with David Sylvian.","The melody uses the same five-note scale as much East Asian music, but the harmonies underneath are Western and romantic, and several synthesizer sounds imitate the gamelan of the film's Javanese setting. Listen for how the theme is fragmented and recoloured through the score, never quite allowed to resolve until the final scene, where it carries the film's last close-up.","The theme became Sakamoto's most famous piece and he returned to it all his life, in solo piano, trio and orchestral versions. Some critics consider the rest of the score uneven beside it. It is now seen as a pioneering electronic film score, alongside Vangelis's Blade Runner, and as proof that a pop musician could write film music of real weight.","Listen to the original main title, then to one of the solo piano versions Sakamoto played in concert late in life, and notice how the melody survives without its electronic halo. Then compare Vangelis's Blade Runner for another synthesizer score of the same years, and Yellow Magic Orchestra's BGM for where Sakamoto came from."],"joe-hisaishi-my-neighbor-totoro":["A film score for Hayao Miyazaki's story of two sisters who move to the countryside and meet forest spirits. It runs from a bouncy marching song for the opening credits to 'The Path of the Wind', a hushed, mysterious piano and strings piece. The title song, sung by Azumi Inoue, is known by every Japanese child. The sound mixes orchestra, synthesizers and simple piano.","Hisaishi had begun as a minimalist composer, influenced by Reich and Riley, before Hayao Miyazaki hired him for Nausicaä of the Valley of the Wind in 1984. Totoro was released in 1988 as a double bill with Isao Takahata's Grave of the Fireflies, a pairing of rural wonder and wartime tragedy. Composer and director would work together on every Miyazaki feature for the next four decades.","The minimalism never left: listen for short repeated patterns under the melodies, especially in 'The Path of the Wind', where a piano figure turns over and over beneath the tune. The simple songs hide sophisticated harmonies, and the Japanese countryside is painted almost entirely with Western instruments rather than koto or shakuhachi, as a child's world, not a postcard.","Totoro did modest business on release but grew into a classic, and its title character became Studio Ghibli's emblem. Its songs are sung in Japanese schools. Hisaishi's Ghibli scores are now performed in concert halls around the world, sometimes with Hisaishi conducting, and critics have come to treat him as a composer rather than just a supplier of tunes.","Start with 'The Path of the Wind', listening for the repeated piano pattern, then the cheerful title song. Watch the bus-stop scene in the rain with the music in mind. Compare John Williams's E.T., another film about children and an otherworldly friend, and then hear Reich to find Hisaishi's roots."],"utada-hikaru-first-love":["A debut album of R&B-inflected pop, sung by a sixteen-year-old in Japanese laced with English phrases. The sound is sleek and late-night: programmed beats, soft electric keyboards, gentle bass, and a voice that slides and ornaments in the manner of American R&B singers. 'Automatic', the laid-back first single, set the tone; the title track closes the album as a slow, string-lined ballad about the end of a first love.","Utada was raised between Tokyo and New York, the child of the enka singer Keiko Fuji and a producer, and wrote much of the album as a teenager. First Love was released in March 1999 and sold more than seven million copies in Japan, becoming the best-selling album in Japanese history. It marked the full arrival of American R&B in the mainstream of J-pop, just as the domestic record industry peaked.","Listen to the phrasing: Utada fits Japanese syllables to R&B rhythms, stretching and clipping words in a way many producers thought the language could not bear. Something of the mother's enka may survive in the expressive slides. The title track, the last on the record, is sung almost without ornament, as if the singer had dropped the style to say it plainly.","The album was a sensation and remains a landmark; Utada is widely seen as the defining Japanese singer-songwriter of their generation. Some critics at the time heard it as imitation American R&B, but its songwriting has outlasted that view. The title track found a new audience through a 2022 Netflix series named after it.","Start with 'Automatic' and listen to how the voice sits behind the beat, then jump to the title track. Play the album late at night, as it was clearly made to be heard. Compare TLC's CrazySexyCool and Janet Jackson's The Velvet Rope for the American models, and then Lauryn Hill for a contemporary."],"seo-taiji-and-boys-nan-arayo":["A mix of rapped verses, new jack swing beats and a soaring pop chorus, the first Korean hit to combine them. The track runs on programmed drums, sampled stabs and synthesizers, with a hard, swinging groove. In the words, a young man desperately insists on his love for someone slipping away. On stage the trio danced in the style of American hip-hop videos, which Korean television had not seen from Korean performers.","Seo Taiji had played bass in the heavy metal band Sinawe; with the dancers Yang Hyun-suk and Lee Juno he formed Seo Taiji and Boys. They debuted on a television talent show in April 1992, where the judges gave them low marks, but the song became one of the biggest hits in Korean history. South Korea had become a democracy only five years earlier, and a youth market was waiting.","Rap in Korean was widely thought impossible, since the language lacks English stress patterns; Seo solved it by chopping phrases into short, percussive bursts. The chorus, by contrast, has a very Korean melodic shape. The group later fought government censorship openly, and their protests helped end the pre-screening of song lyrics in 1996.","The song is taken as the birth of modern K-pop, the moment the idol industry became possible. Critics have debated its originality, since its American sources are obvious, and plagiarism accusations followed some of the group's later work. But its effect on Korean youth culture is not in doubt, and its members built the industry that followed.","Listen for the rapped Korean verses against the sung chorus, and notice how new the combination sounded in 1992. Then hear Run-DMC and Janet Jackson's Rhythm Nation for the American sources, and BTS's 'Spring Day' for how far the idea travelled in twenty-five years."],"psy-gangnam-style":["An electro-house track with a pounding four-on-the-floor beat, a squealing synthesizer riff and PSY's rapped and chanted verses, capped by a shouted hook anyone can join. The video shows him performing a horse-riding dance in absurd places, from a stable to a sauna to a lift. The song mocks the flashy lifestyle of Seoul's wealthy Gangnam district. It is comedy and club music at once.","PSY, Park Jae-sang, had been a well-known Korean entertainer for a decade, famous for comic songs and marathon concerts, and was signed to YG Entertainment. The video, released in July 2012, spread across the world through YouTube and social media and in December became the first video to reach a billion views. It reached number two in the United States and topped charts in many countries.","The satire is aimed at Korea's new money: Gangnam is a district of luxury shops and plastic surgery clinics, and PSY plays a man pretending to be far richer and cooler than he is, in a rundown setting. Most non-Korean listeners missed the joke entirely and heard only a party song, which is itself part of its story.","Some critics saw a novelty hit and a one-off; others saw a turning point in global pop, proof that a song in a language most listeners did not speak could spread through the internet without radio. Its success also fed debate about Western audiences laughing at, rather than with, an Asian performer. It opened doors for the K-pop wave that followed.","Watch the video first; it is half the work. Then listen for how the build-up and drop follow club music conventions. Compare Kyu Sakamoto's 'Sukiyaki', the earlier Asian-language song that reached the top of the American charts, and BTS for what came after."],"bts-spring-day":["A mid-tempo ballad that opens on a soft synthesizer pad and guitar and slowly adds a steady beat, sung and rapped in turn by the seven members. The song speaks of missing someone through a long winter and waiting for spring to bring them back. The mood is wistful rather than dramatic, rising to a warm, anthemic chorus and then dissolving again into quiet.","Released in February 2017 on You Never Walk Alone, an expanded edition of the album Wings, the song was co-written by the members RM and Suga with the group's producers. BTS were on the verge of becoming the first K-pop group with a mass Western following, and this song arrived just as their fan base, ARMY, was growing into a global force.","The song and video have been read as a memorial to the victims of the 2014 Sewol ferry disaster, in which many students died. The video includes images such as yellow ribbons, and a hotel sign referring to a story by Ursula K. Le Guin about a city whose happiness depends on one child's suffering. Once you know this, the waiting for spring sounds less like romance and more like mourning.","Many fans and critics consider it BTS's finest song, and it stayed in the Korean streaming charts for years after release, an unusual feat in a fast-moving market. It showed that K-pop could be reflective and grief-stricken as well as dance music. The members have never officially confirmed the Sewol reading, which leaves listeners free to hear their own loss in it.","Listen to how the song passes between rap and singing, and how the chorus opens up. Watch the video for its imagery, then read about the Sewol disaster. Compare Seo Taiji and Boys and PSY to trace K-pop's journey from a Seoul talent show to the world's biggest stages."],"cui-jian-nothing-to-my-name":["A rock song built on a slow, heavy groove, with a rasping, strained voice, electric guitars and a Chinese double-reed horn, the suona, which plays a wailing solo. In the words a young man tells a woman he has nothing to offer, and she laughs at him; he keeps asking when she will go with him. The mood is raw and frustrated, building from a mutter to a shout.","Cui Jian, a classically trained trumpeter who had discovered Western rock on smuggled tapes, performed the song in May 1986 at a large concert in Beijing and caused a sensation, the first real rock song most Chinese listeners had heard. It became the unofficial anthem of the student protests in Tiananmen Square in 1989, where Cui performed for the students. He has faced performance restrictions since.","The suona, used at Chinese weddings and funerals, gives the song a folk sound and makes it unmistakably Chinese rather than a copy of Western rock. Listeners in 1986 heard the lyric as more than a love song: a whole generation with nothing, facing a system that laughed at it. Cui never had to say so.","The song is regarded as the birth of Chinese rock, and Cui as its father. Its association with 1989 has made it politically sensitive in China, though Cui still performs. Western critics compared him to Dylan and Springsteen, which he has sometimes resisted, and Chinese critics now debate whether rock ever regained the urgency it had in these few years.","Listen for the suona solo and how it cuts through the guitars. Then compare Bob Dylan's 'Blowin' in the Wind' and Public Enemy's 'Fight the Power', another anthem of 1989, and Teresa Teng's 'The Moon Represents My Heart' for the soft music Chinese youth had loved before this."],"teresa-teng-the-moon-represents-my-heart":["A slow, gentle love song in Mandarin, sung by Teresa Teng in a soft, intimate voice close to the microphone. The arrangement is spare: strings, guitar and a light rhythm, leaving the voice in front. The words have the singer's lover ask how deep her love is, and she answers that the moon represents her heart. The melody is simple and swaying, easy to sing along to, which is much of its power.","Written by the composer Weng Ching-hsi with words by Sun Yi, it was first recorded by another Taiwanese singer in the early 1970s; Teng's 1977 version made it famous. Teng was a Taiwanese star with a huge following across Asia. In mainland China her music was banned as decadent in the early years of reform, but cassettes were widely copied and smuggled.","The song was a symbol of the thaw after the Cultural Revolution; a saying went that 'Old Deng rules by day, little Deng rules by night', comparing Deng Xiaoping with Teresa Teng. Listen for how softly she sings, almost whispering: after years of revolutionary songs sung at full voice, the intimacy itself felt like a political act.","It is probably the best-known Chinese popular song in the world, sung at weddings and karaoke from Taipei to San Francisco. Teng's intimate style was attacked in China as 'spiritual pollution' but shaped generations of singers there. She died in 1995, and her reputation has only grown; the song is now accepted even in the mainland.","Listen to Teng's soft voice and the way she places the words gently behind the beat. Then compare Misora Hibari's farewell ballad for a Japanese counterpart, and Cui Jian's 'Nothing to My Name' to hear what the next generation of Chinese youth shouted instead."],"messiaen-sept-haikai":["Seven 'Japanese sketches' for piano and a small orchestra with only eight violins for strings. Movements depict the park at Nara, the lake at Yamanaka, the gagaku court music, the shrine gate at Miyajima standing in the sea, and the birds of Karuizawa, framed by an introduction and coda. The sound is bright and hard, with bird calls, bells and clusters.","Messiaen visited Japan in 1962 with the pianist Yvonne Loriod, whom he had married the previous year, on a concert tour. He heard gagaku and Noh, visited temples, shrines and gardens, and notated the songs of Japanese birds, as he did everywhere. He wrote the work on his return to Paris, and it was first performed there in 1963, with Loriod at the piano.","In the 'Gagaku' movement the eight violins play high, sustained clusters without vibrato to imitate the shō, the bamboo mouth organ, while a trumpet and double reeds take the piercing line of the hichiriki. It is one of the closest imitations of an Asian ensemble by a Western composer, and it sounds nothing like the rest of Messiaen's music.","The work is performed less than Messiaen's larger pieces but is admired as a vivid travel diary, full of colour and precise observation. Some Japanese listeners have found the gagaku imitation charming but inaccurate. Scholars see it as a turning point in Western composers' engagement with Japanese music, which Takemitsu and others would explore from the other direction.","Start with the 'Gagaku' movement, then listen to a gagaku performance of Etenraku and hear what Messiaen was copying. Then play the whole work and follow the birds of Karuizawa. Compare Mayuzumi's Bugaku, written by a Japanese composer the same year, and Takemitsu's November Steps."],"lord-kitchener-london-is-the-place-for-me":["A calypso in the old Trinidadian manner: a small band with clarinet and muted trumpet trading short phrases, a guitar and piano keeping a loping two-step, and over it Lord Kitchener's warm, slightly nasal tenor, carefully enunciating every syllable. The song is a list of pleasures, a newcomer praising the city, its manners, its famous places, the feeling of being at the centre of the empire. It lasts barely three minutes and never raises its voice.","Kitchener, born Aldwyn Roberts in Arima, Trinidad, sailed to Britain on the Empire Windrush in June 1948. A newsreel crew filmed him on arrival at Tilbury singing an early version of this song unaccompanied. He settled in London and Manchester, played West Indian clubs, and recorded the finished song for the small Melodisc label in 1951, when calypso records were sold mainly to Caribbean migrants and to a few curious British jazz fans.","The song's optimism is the point of tension. Kitchener wrote it before he had lived in the city, and within a few years he was writing sharper calypsos about rent, cold and colour bars. Listen to the band too: the clarinet lines owe as much to New Orleans jazz as to Port of Spain, a reminder that calypso had long been in conversation with American records before it ever reached London.","For decades it was a period curiosity. It returned to view in 2002, when Honest Jon's Records named a series of compilations of London calypso after it, and again around the seventieth anniversary of the Windrush landing, when the newsreel clip became one of the defining images of Caribbean migration to Britain. Some listeners hear innocence; others hear a performer shrewdly flattering his new audience.","Watch the 1948 Pathé newsreel first, then play the 1951 record, and notice how a bare shipboard tune has become a dressed-up dance number. Listen for the way Kitchener lands on the ends of lines, a calypsonian's diction built to carry jokes across a tent. Follow it with Mighty Sparrow's Jean and Dinah to hear how quickly the genre sharpened."],"harry-belafonte-calypso":["Eleven songs of Caribbean origin or flavour, sung by Harry Belafonte with a guitar-led band, hand percussion and a chorus that answers him like dock workers across a yard. It opens with Day-O, the Banana Boat Song, built on a Jamaican work song, and includes Jamaica Farewell and I Do Adore Her. The sound is polished and theatrical: Belafonte acts each song, slowing and whispering, then swelling to a full-throated call.","Belafonte was born in Harlem to a Jamaican mother and a Martinican father and spent part of his childhood in Jamaica. By 1956 he was a successful nightclub and folk singer at RCA Victor. Many of the songs came from the songwriter Irving Burgie, who adapted Jamaican mento and Trinidadian calypso material. Released at the height of the long-playing record's first boom, it is widely reported to be the first LP by a single artist to sell a million copies.","Much of what America heard as calypso here is actually Jamaican mento, a rural style with banjo and rumba box, and several songs are folk material reworked and newly copyrighted, a tangle of credits argued over ever since. Listen to Day-O's structure: the call of the tallyman counting bunches of bananas is a genuine work-song form, and the chorus is doing the answering the labourers once did.","It topped the American album chart for weeks and set off a brief calypso craze in 1957, complete with imitations and a Hollywood film cash-in. Trinidadian calypsonians were often scornful, hearing a smoothed, generic product with none of the topical wit of the Port of Spain tents. Its reputation has since grown with Belafonte's own, as a civil-rights activist who used his fame to fund the movement.","Start with Day-O and listen to the chorus behind him; then Jamaica Farewell for the gentler, homesick Belafonte. Compare the album with a real Trinidadian calypso such as Mighty Sparrow's to hear the difference between a concert-hall staging and a tent performance. The original mono pressing has the most presence."],"mighty-sparrow-jean-and-dinah":["A brisk calypso with horns riffing between the lines and a bouncing rhythm meant for a carnival road march. The young Sparrow sings with a swagger that is half boast and half commentary, the tune simple enough for a whole street to shout back. The song mocks the fortunes of certain women who had made their living from American servicemen, now that the Americans are leaving, and cheers the local men who expect to win back the island.","Trinidad hosted a large United States naval base at Chaguaramas during and after the Second World War, and the base's scaling-down in the 1950s was a live political subject as the island moved toward independence. Slinger Francisco, born in Grenada and raised in Port of Spain, won both the Calypso King title and the Road March with this song at Carnival in 1956, still only about twenty, and became the dominant calypsonian of the next generation.","The song is really about sovereignty. Beneath the bawdy surface lies a sharp nationalist point: the foreign power is going, and islanders will run their own affairs. It is also a reply, a decade later, to the Andrews Sisters' 1945 hit Rum and Coca-Cola, itself lifted from a Trinidadian calypso; Sparrow is taking the subject back from America.","It made Sparrow a star overnight and set off a long rivalry with Lord Kitchener that shaped calypso for twenty years. Later listeners have been uneasy with its view of the women it describes, and scholars now read it both as anti-colonial satire and as an example of how calypso's wit often worked at women's expense. Its place in Trinidad's story of independence is not in doubt.","Listen for how the horn riff and the chorus are built for walking: this is music for a moving crowd, not a seated audience. Then hear Sparrow's later records from the 1960s to follow the growth of his band sound. Pair it with Lord Kitchener's London Is the Place for Me for the migrant's side of the same era."],"bob-marley-simmer-down":["A fast ska record: horns blaring the offbeat, a walking bass, a drummer driving hard, and three young voices in close, slightly ragged harmony. Bob Marley leads, with Peter Tosh and Bunny Livingston answering. The song is an appeal to the rude boys, the restless young men of West Kingston, to calm down before trouble finds them. It is raw, urgent and over in under three minutes.","The Wailers auditioned for Clement 'Coxsone' Dodd at his new Studio One in Kingston in 1963, and this was among their first recordings, made with the session men who were about to become the Skatalites. Jamaica had become independent in August 1962, and Dodd's records were tested first on his sound system, the huge mobile discos that played to crowds in the city's yards. The record went to the top of the Jamaican charts.","The Wailers here sound like an American doo-wop group trying to keep up with a Jamaican band. Their harmony model was the Impressions and other Chicago vocal groups, whose records reached Kingston on radio from Miami and New Orleans; listen to the way the voices stack. The horn players behind them are among the finest jazz musicians on the island, playing a pop session.","For years it was known only to collectors and Jamaican audiences; after Marley's international fame, the Studio One sides were reissued and reassessed. Critics now treat them as essential to his story, showing that the social preacher of the 1970s was already there at eighteen, and that the Wailers began as a vocal group rather than a band.","Listen first to the horns and bass, the ska engine, then to the three voices, and notice how Marley's lead already cuts through. Then play Catch a Fire, ten years later, to hear how much slower and heavier the same singers became. Compilations of the Wailers' Studio One sides give the fuller picture."],"millie-small-my-boy-lollipop":["Two minutes of bright, bouncing ska-pop: a choppy guitar on the offbeat, a shuffling beat and a sixteen-year-old singer whose high, girlish voice seems to be smiling through every line. A harmonica solo breaks in halfway. The song is a simple, sweet declaration of teenage love, and the whole record is pitched to sound like fun on a transistor radio.","Millie Small came to London from Jamaica at the invitation of Chris Blackwell, whose small Island label had been licensing Jamaican records for migrants. The song was an American rhythm-and-blues number first recorded in 1956 by Barbie Gaye. Blackwell had it arranged by the Jamaican guitarist Ernest Ranglin, and licensed it to Fontana because Island could not handle a hit. It reached number two in both Britain and the United States.","The harmonica soloist is disputed; the long-told story that a young Rod Stewart played it has been denied, and another session player is usually credited. Listen past the novelty and you hear the ska offbeat, lightly scrubbed, reaching millions who had never heard of Kingston. The money it earned let Blackwell build Island Records, the label that would later carry Bob Marley to the world.","Dismissed by many at the time as a novelty, it is now seen as the moment Jamaican music first went global, several million copies across the world. Jamaican purists heard a watered-down ska; later historians point out that without it there might have been no Island Records and no international reggae. Small herself left music within a few years and spoke of receiving little of the money.","Listen for the guitar chop on the offbeat, the one ska fingerprint left in the pop arrangement. Then play Barbie Gaye's 1956 original to hear what Ranglin changed, and the Skatalites' Guns of Navarone for what ska sounded like at home. It is short: play it twice."],"prince-buster-al-capone":["An instrumental ska stomp with a gangster-film frame. It opens with a car's screeching tyres and Prince Buster's spoken warning, then the band hits a fast, lurching groove: horns blasting a riff, piano and guitar stabbing the offbeat, Buster breaking in with shouts and asides. Most of the record is band, not singing: it is a dance track built around a character.","Cecil Campbell, a former boxer who took the name Prince Buster, started out as a bodyguard for Coxsone Dodd's sound system and then built his own, the Voice of the People, becoming one of the first producers to press records by local musicians. Released in Jamaica in the mid-1960s, Al Capone was licensed in Britain on the Blue Beat label, which sold ska so steadily to migrants and mods that 'bluebeat' became a British name for the music. It reached the UK top twenty in 1967.","The gangster theme is a mirror: American crime films played to packed houses in Kingston, and the city's rude boys borrowed their style. Buster's shouted interjections over the band are an early version of the deejay's art, talking over a rhythm, which would become toasting and, in time, rapping. The rhythm itself is a Jamaican reading of American shuffle rhythm and blues.","It became a talisman for British youth cultures, from mods to the late-1970s 2 Tone revival: the Specials built their first single, Gangsters, on it, and Madness named themselves after another Prince Buster song. Critics in Jamaica tend to rank Buster higher as producer and personality than as singer; this record catches both at once.","Enjoy the opening sound effect, then follow the horns, which carry the melody. Listen for Buster's spoken breaks as the first sign of the deejay tradition. Afterwards play U-Roy's Wake the Town to hear where talking over a record went, and the Clash's first album to hear ska's afterlife in punk."],"the-skatalites-guns-of-navarone":["An instrumental: the theme from a Hollywood war film, taken at ska speed. Horns in unison state the tune like a brass band on parade; the piano and guitar hammer the offbeat; the bass walks; the drummer punches accents on the third beat. Then the soloists take turns, jazz-style, before the full section returns. It sounds like a big band from Kingston playing a march for dancing.","The Skatalites formed in 1964 from Kingston's best session players, several of them trained at the Alpha Boys School, a Catholic institution famous for its band programme. They played on hundreds of records for Coxsone Dodd and Duke Reid. The tune is Dimitri Tiomkin's theme for the 1961 film The Guns of Navarone. The band broke up in 1965 after its trombonist, Don Drummond, was arrested for killing his partner; the record became a UK hit only in 1967.","Listen to Lloyd Knibb's drums: the accents falling where a jazz drummer would not put them, together with hits on the rim, give ska its forward lurch, and the pattern is often said to draw partly on Rastafarian burru drumming. Tommy McCook, Roland Alphonso and the others were jazz players first; their solos quote bebop phrasing. Guns of Navarone is film music turned into a dance by musicians who would rather have been playing Coltrane.","In Jamaica the Skatalites were the house band of a whole era, but their fame abroad came posthumously, through reissues and 2 Tone. Critics now rank them among the great instrumental ensembles of the Americas. There is debate over how much ska owes to New Orleans rhythm and blues and how much to mento and burru; the band's own playing suggests all three.","Follow the unison horn theme, then listen only to the drums for a chorus. Compare it with the band's other famous sides, Eastern Standard Time and Freedom Sounds, and with a New Orleans record such as Fats Domino's to hear the offbeat change places. Any Studio One compilation of the band will do."],"alton-ellis-girl-ive-got-a-date":["A slow, swaying love song. The band has cut ska's tempo nearly in half; the bass has stopped walking and plays short, melodic phrases with gaps between them; the guitar still ticks on the offbeat, but gently. Over it Alton Ellis sings a smooth, soul-inflected tenor with a small backing chorus, apologising to a girl as he leaves for someone else. It is quiet, intimate and made for close dancing.","Recorded for Duke Reid's Treasure Isle studio in Kingston in 1966, it is one of the records most often named as the start of rocksteady, the short-lived style between ska and reggae. A hot summer, it was said, made fast ska too tiring to dance to; more likely, the singers were following the slower American soul records of the day, from Curtis Mayfield and the Impressions to the Stax singers. Ellis followed it with Rock Steady, which gave the style its name.","Listen to the bass: this is where the Jamaican bass line becomes the lead instrument, and the space around it would later become dub. Four years later this very rhythm was reused by Duke Reid for U-Roy's Wake the Town, one of the first great toasting records. The same backing track thus stands at the start of both rocksteady and the deejay tradition.","Ellis was long revered in Jamaica as the godfather of rocksteady while almost unknown abroad; he spent his later years in London performing to devoted audiences. Historians still argue about which record 'invented' rocksteady, with this, Hopeton Lewis's Take It Easy and others in contention. Few doubt that Ellis was its finest singer.","Put it next to Guns of Navarone and feel the tempo drop. Listen to the bass and the space between its notes, then to Ellis's phrasing, borrowed from American soul. Follow it with U-Roy's Wake the Town to hear the same rhythm reborn. Treasure Isle compilations give the whole rocksteady world."],"the-paragons-the-tide-is-high":["A rocksteady love song in the smoothest manner: three voices in soft close harmony, a lilting rhythm, a lightly strummed guitar and a round bass line. The lead singer, John Holt, compares his stubbornness in love to someone holding on against a rising tide. The melody is short and circular, and the record floats rather than drives.","The Paragons were a vocal trio at Duke Reid's Treasure Isle, the studio that defined rocksteady's sweet, polished sound in 1966 and 1967, and a rival to Coxsone Dodd's Studio One. Holt wrote the song. Jamaican vocal groups modelled themselves on American harmony groups such as the Impressions and the Drifters, whose records came in on Miami radio. The Paragons dissolved by the end of the decade; Holt became one of Jamaica's best-loved solo singers.","The song's afterlife is larger than its first life. In 1980 Blondie's version, with steel drums and horns added, topped the charts in the United States and Britain; many listeners still do not know it began in Kingston. The Paragons' own rhythms were reused by deejays too: U-Roy toasted over their Wear You to the Ball, one of his first hits.","In Jamaica it was a moderate hit rather than a monument; its canonisation came through the Blondie cover and later through Atomic Kitten's 2002 version, which made the song familiar to yet another generation. Critics hold the Paragons' original up as an example of rocksteady's understated grace, and of how a Jamaican song could become global pop without its authors being heard.","Hear the Paragons' version first, then Blondie's, and notice what an American new-wave band kept (the melody) and what it added (a Caribbean costume). Listen in the original for the gentle bass and the three voices breathing together. Follow with Alton Ellis for rocksteady's other great voice."],"toots-and-the-maytals-do-the-reggay":["A dance-craze song that names a music. Over a skipping, choppy rhythm, faster than rocksteady and with the organ and guitar pecking at the offbeat, Toots Hibbert sings with the raw fervour of a gospel preacher, calling on everyone to join the new dance, while the two Maytals answer him. It is short, joyful and a little rough, the sound of a band excited by its own groove.","Toots and the Maytals recorded it for the Chinese-Jamaican producer Leslie Kong at his Beverley's label in 1968, as rocksteady was being pushed into a quicker, more clipped beat. Hibbert had recently come out of prison after a drug conviction, and was one of Jamaica's most popular singers. The record is generally credited as the first to use the word reggae, then spelled here with a y, in its title.","Nobody is quite sure where the word came from. Hibbert said it came from 'streggae', Kingston slang for a raggedy, everyday style; others connect it to 'raggay' or to the word for a ragged garment. Listen to the organ's doubled chop, 'bubbling' on the offbeat: that new keyboard pattern is the reggae fingerprint, and it is here in embryo.","Its importance is as a name and a threshold rather than a finished statement; its fame outside Jamaica is recent. Critics admire Hibbert's voice above all, often compared with Otis Redding's for its grain and urgency, and Toots and the Maytals remain for many the most soulful of the early reggae groups. Hibbert died in 2020, still touring.","Listen for the organ's double chop and the bass locking with the kick drum, then to Toots's voice, which is gospel through and through. Play it after Girl I've Got a Date to hear rocksteady quickening into reggae, and follow with Pressure Drop and 54-46 That's My Number."],"desmond-dekker-israelites":["A lean early-reggae record: a jaunty, bubbling rhythm, a busy bass, a chorus of backing singers, and Desmond Dekker's high, keening voice complaining of getting up early, working hard and still having nothing to eat. His family has left and his clothes are torn. The tune is catchy enough to hide the misery; the record's bounce and its sorrow run side by side.","Dekker, born Desmond Dacres in Kingston, had already had a rude-boy hit with 007 (Shanty Town). Israelites was produced by Leslie Kong and released in Britain on Pyramid, a label serving Caribbean migrants and the skinheads who adopted Jamaican music. In April 1969 it became the first Jamaican record to top the British singles chart, and it reached the American top ten.","The title draws on Rastafarian language, in which Black Jamaicans are the exiled Israelites, suffering in Babylon; the song is a complaint about poverty cast in biblical terms. Its Jamaican patois was famously misheard by British and American listeners, spawning a long tradition of mistaken versions. Listen to the rhythm guitar and the bass: they are already fully reggae, a year after the word appeared.","It was Jamaica's first big international number one, and for years the most famous Jamaican record abroad, until Bob Marley. Critics have occasionally dismissed it as pop reggae, but it is now valued as a clear-eyed song of hardship with a hook to carry it, and for how it reached British working-class youth before rock critics had noticed reggae at all.","Listen to it twice: once for the bounce, once for the words, described plainly as poverty and exile. Notice the backing chorus answering Dekker. Then play 007 (Shanty Town), from the Harder They Come soundtrack, and Toots's Pressure Drop for Kong's other great productions."],"toots-and-the-maytals-pressure-drop":["A reggae song of reckoning: a tight, loping rhythm with a stuttering guitar and organ on the offbeat, the Maytals chanting a simple, insistent refrain, and Toots Hibbert's rough, gospel-drenched voice warning someone who has done wrong that the pressure will fall on them in turn. It repeats and repeats, gaining force by insistence, like a spiritual turned into a curse.","It was another Leslie Kong production, made in 1969 at the height of early reggae's fast, lean phase. Jamaica was a young nation with deep inequalities, and the song's idea of moral retribution was part of the language of the poor. In 1972 it was chosen for The Harder They Come, the film that carried reggae to cinemas in Britain and America, and its soundtrack album made the song internationally known.","The chorus is a call-and-response pattern straight from the Revivalist church music Hibbert grew up singing, and the song's warning has the shape of an Old Testament prophecy. Its later history is a map of reggae's reach: the Clash recorded it in 1979, and it has been covered by punk, soul and rock bands ever since, often with its menace intact.","Through the film it became one of the most loved reggae songs abroad, and it is often named as Toots's finest record. Critics note that the international audience first heard it as ambience in a crime film; its gospel roots have been more appreciated since Hibbert's death in 2020 prompted a new look at his whole career.","Listen to the repeated refrain gather weight, and to how Toots cracks his voice on the high notes. Then hear it on the Harder They Come soundtrack, in context, and follow it with the Clash's version to hear what punk heard. Do the Reggay and 54-46 That's My Number complete the early Maytals."],"jimmy-cliff-many-rivers-to-cross":["A slow ballad closer to a hymn than to reggae. A church organ holds long chords, a piano rolls gently, and Jimmy Cliff sings in a clear, pleading voice about loneliness, weariness and a long road still ahead, rising near the end to a cry backed by a gospel choir. There is no offbeat skank here at all: it is soul music with a Jamaican singer at its heart.","Cliff, from rural St James in Jamaica, had his first hits as a teenager in Kingston and moved to London in the mid-1960s at Chris Blackwell's invitation. He wrote the song, he later said, out of frustration with his struggle to break through in England. It appeared on his 1969 album for Trojan and Island, and was later placed on the soundtrack of The Harder They Come, becoming one of his best-known recordings.","The record's models are American: the organ and choir come from the Black church via soul singers such as Sam Cooke and Otis Redding. Listen to the final verse, where the choir enters and Cliff lets his voice break. It has been covered many times, most famously by UB40 and Harry Nilsson, but the original's plainness is rarely matched.","It was not a big hit on release, but it grew into one of the most covered songs by any Jamaican writer. Critics have long debated whether Cliff's gift lay in reggae or in soul; this song argues that the distinction mattered less than his voice. It is now a fixture of memorial services as well as reggae compilations.","Hear it on a quiet night, and listen for the organ holding everything together. Then compare it with Sam Cooke's A Change Is Gonna Come, which it closely resembles in spirit. Follow it with The Harder They Come to hear the same singer at full, defiant speed."],"the-abyssinians-satta-massagana":["A slow, solemn reggae hymn. Three voices in close, sustained harmony float above a heavy, rolling bass and a spare, deliberate beat; a horn line answers in the gaps. The words, partly in Amharic, the language of Ethiopia, give thanks and praise to God and look toward a promised land in Africa. It moves like a procession, calm and devotional.","The Abyssinians, Bernard Collins and the brothers Donald and Linford Manning, recorded it at Coxsone Dodd's Studio One in 1969. Dodd reportedly did not see its commercial potential and shelved it, so the group bought the tape back and released it on their own Clinch label in 1971, when it became a Jamaican hit. It was among the first records to carry Rastafarian devotion so fully into the charts.","The Amharic phrase in the title is usually translated as giving thanks, a reference to Ethiopia, which Rastafarians regard as Zion, and to its last emperor, Haile Selassie. The rhythm has been reused on dozens of other records, including deejay and instrumental versions, making it one of the most recycled riddims in Jamaican music. Listen to how the harmony never hurries.","It is now regarded as one of the foundation stones of roots reggae and as its most beautiful hymn. Critics often place it beside Burning Spear's Marcus Garvey as the purest expression of the Rastafarian spirit in music. The Studio One back story is part of the legend, a warning about producers' judgment and ownership in Jamaica.","Listen to the three voices as a choir, and to the bass beneath them, and give it full volume. Then follow it with Burning Spear and Culture, the other great Rasta vocalists, and compare it with a spiritual such as Amazing Grace, from a different diaspora of the same continent."],"u-roy-wake-the-town":["A deejay record: U-Roy's voice, excited, rhythmic and full of whoops and catchphrases, talking and half-singing over a rocksteady backing track, with snatches of the original singer allowed through between his interjections. He calls the whole town to wake up and listen. The effect is of a party host commenting live on a record he loves, turning it into something new.","Toasting, talking rhythmically over records on a sound system, had been a feature of Kingston dances for years. Duke Reid, the Treasure Isle producer, had U-Roy toast over his rocksteady backing tracks, including Alton Ellis's Girl I've Got a Date, and released the results in 1970. U-Roy's records reportedly held several top chart places in Jamaica at once. The same period saw the arrival of 'versions', instrumental B-sides made to be talked over.","Listen for Alton Ellis's voice peeping through: U-Roy is performing a duet with a four-year-old record. A few years later a Kingston-born teenager called Clive Campbell, who had moved to the Bronx, began playing records at parties as Kool Herc, and brought the Jamaican practice of talking over breaks to New York; hip-hop grew from that seed. U-Roy's records are part of that line.","It put deejays on record for the first time as stars and made U-Roy the originator of a lineage running through Big Youth and I-Roy to dancehall. Critics have debated how far hip-hop descends from Jamaican toasting; the Kool Herc connection makes the link concrete, though American traditions of rhyme and radio patter mattered too. U-Roy died in 2021.","Play Alton Ellis's Girl I've Got a Date first, then Wake the Town, and hear how the deejay treats the original as a stage. Notice his timing, often landing just off the beat. Then play Rapper's Delight and Grandmaster Flash to hear the Bronx version of the idea a decade later."],"jimmy-cliff-the-harder-they-come":["A defiant, driving reggae song: a bright organ, a choppy guitar, a steady beat and Jimmy Cliff singing about a poor man who refuses to be crushed and would rather fight than bow down, the chorus warning that the bigger they are the harder they fall. It sounds more like a rock anthem than most reggae of the time, with a strong, upbeat melody meant for a big screen.","The song is the title theme of Perry Henzell's 1972 film The Harder They Come, the first Jamaican feature film, in which Cliff played Ivan, a country boy who comes to Kingston, records a hit, becomes an outlaw and is shot by the police; the story drew on a real Jamaican gunman of the 1940s. The soundtrack album, on Island, collected songs by Cliff, Toots and the Maytals, Desmond Dekker, the Melodians and others.","The film is also about the music business: Ivan's record is bought from him for a pittance by a producer, a sharp portrait of how Kingston studios worked. The soundtrack's other songs, Rivers of Babylon, Johnny Too Bad, 007, amount to a greatest hits of early reggae. Listen to Cliff's delivery, which borrows the swagger of American soul singers.","The film played for years on the American midnight-movie circuit and became a cult classic, and many critics credit the soundtrack album with doing more than any single record to bring reggae to North America. Some hear the song as reggae adjusted for rock audiences; others argue its defiance was the real thing. It remains Cliff's defining statement.","Watch the film, then play the soundtrack album from beginning to end: it is the best one-disc introduction to early reggae. Listen in the title song for the organ riff and for Cliff's refusal to soften. Follow it with Bob Marley's Catch a Fire, released within months."]});